and the membership is each scroll's whole
content — a merge would lose which items
stand in which family, which is the only
claim either scroll makes.
(ii) 1.2.1173, the permanent-graph decision
bound, into 1.2.1174, weighted counting for
bounded-occurrence 3-CNF — the two major
theorem consequences of Chapter 124, both
resting on the same imported premise
(Anthropic's announced VP ≠ VNP). Held
apart. The held scroll files the decision
consequence: a 2^{Ω((log m)^{4/3})} lower
bound for deciding permanent-graphs in an
algebraic computation-tree model, its
strongest form scoped to a bounded
coefficient norm. The newer scroll files
the counting consequence: a like bound for
weighted counting on bounded-occurrence
3-CNF, the strongest form scoped to bounded
weights, its provenance a research branch
rather than a paper. A shared premise is
not a shared claim: the premise is imported
by both and proved by neither, and what
each scroll holds is its own conclusion —
merged, the scroll could not state which
conclusion the premise buys, in which
model, at which bound.
(iii) 1.2.1175, the Erdős–Sauer partial
note, into 1.2.1178, the Erdős–Oler partial
log. Held apart. Both are notes standing in
the collection's Partial results section,
carrying no solved claim and no label — and
what they share is that status, which is
not a claim. The held scroll files the
hypergraph partition problem with its
defect D(G) = |E(G)| − r·ν(G), its solved
boundary cases (D = 0, D = 1, the
intermediate case, r ≤ 3) and its open
middle. The newer scroll files the disk
packing problem in a triangle of side
L + √3, its sharp cases and the general
conjecture standing open behind them, the
objection at Q485 riding on it. The
problems share no statement, no method and
no object; a merge would lose which problem
stands open in which form, and the audit's
own remarks differ between them (the note's
catalogue placement against the log's
partial heading).
(iv) 1.2.1163, the Erdős 607 line, into
1.2.1166, the Burstein enumeration. Held
apart. Both scrolls file an enumeration
recomputed by the house and reproduced
against its OEIS b-file — the line-size
sets' F(1..24) against A401948 by two
routes; the Burstein counts by three,
against A218225. The shared shape is the
audit's method, not a mathematical claim:
the enumerations count different objects
(line-size sets of a hypercube against
binary strings avoiding two pattern
classes), and each verification record is
its own. The held scroll additionally holds
the provenance correction — the solved form
is Kotesovec's refinement, with the
Szemerédi–Trotter settlement behind it —
which the newer scroll cannot state; the
newer holds the scope exclusion (the
paper's Conjectures 6.1 and 6.2 unclaimed)
which the held cannot. Neither scroll's
claim can be stated in the other's terms.
