- Pass #48 (2026-11-25; standalone
pass — no taking-in; concept-count
trigger — 1,025 canonical concepts
cross the 1020 line set by Pass #47,
crossed at Paper 091's filing):
merges weighed, five, and held apart,
each on its record.
(i) 1.2.1008, Casselman's comparison
theorem at a minimal parabolic, into
1.2.947, Casselman's comparison
theorem at the minimal parabolic —
the nearest pair this pass has
weighed: one theorem standing in two
statements, each with its own proof.
Held apart. The newer scroll files
the theorem in the form the field
held it first: Theorem 1 of Paper
090, stated there as Casselman's,
unpublished — for a connected
semisimple real Lie group with finite
center and a Harish-Chandra module M
of an admissible representation, the
map on u-homology from M to its
smooth globalization is an
isomorphism, and the smooth side's
homology is in particular
finite-dimensional. Its warrant is
the remark's own deduction: the
reduction (1.2.1011) from the
minimal-globalization comparison
(1.2.1010), whose three warrants run
through the D-module side of the
theory — the authors' own 1993
n-homology theorem, Bratten's more
general comparison, and Bunke and
Olbrich's through Schmid and
Kashiwara. The held scroll is the
same case proved again a generation
later (Li, Liu and Yu, Theorem 5.2):
for a real linear reductive group
and a smooth Frechet representation
V of moderate growth, the inclusion
V_K -> V induces isomorphisms
H_i(n, V_K) = H_i(n, V) for every
i >= 0 — no completion taken on
either side — and dually the
inclusion V' -> V_K^* induces
H^i(n, V') = H^i(n, V_K^*), a
cohomological form the newer scroll
nowhere states. Its proof uses
distribution theory alone, the
authors expressly setting aside the
D-module machinery the newer
scroll's warrant chain runs on, and
descends through the two-filtration
machine (1.2.952) to the scalar
comparison (1.2.953). Filing the
remark's statement inside the later
proof would lose the form the field
held first and the deduction that
relocates the labour onto the
embedding (1.2.1011); filing the
later proof's statement inside the
remark would lose the dual form and
the no-completion claim — the
homology of the module itself, not
of any completion of it.
(ii) 1.2.1012, the principal-series
reduction, into 1.2.947, Casselman's
comparison theorem at the minimal
parabolic: the same proposition
standing in two filings — Proposition
3 of the remark, filed at 1.2.1012 as
the step the remark's deduction
consumes, and named in the held
scroll's own text as a load-bearing
piece of its proof ('Hecht-Taylor's
reduction ([12, Proposition 3])').
Held apart. The newer scroll files
the proposition in the remark's own
numbering, as the step of the
reduction (1.2.1011): each
Harish-Chandra module embeds into a
generalized principal series module
(1.2.1013), resolutions by such
modules transport to both
completions by the exactness of the
two globalization functors
(1.2.1009), and the isomorphism for
M follows from the isomorphism for
the resolution's terms — and it
records the held text's boundary:
the publisher's preview stops inside
this proof, at the resolutions
sentence. The held scroll spends the
same proposition inside a different
architecture entire: duality
([8, Lemma 5.11]), the Jacquet
module's exactness (1.2.950) and its
regular-density form (1.2.951), the
two-filtration descent (1.2.952) and
the scalar comparison (1.2.953) —
machinery the newer scroll nowhere
holds, in service of the theorem of
(i). Filing the proposition inside
the theorem would lose the remark's
deductive architecture — premise
(1.2.1010), move (1.2.1011), step
(1.2.1012) — and the record of where
its text stops; filing the theorem's
use inside the proposition would
import a whole descent into a step's
scroll.
(iii) 1.2.1009, the two canonical
globalizations, into 1.2.500, the
smooth completion (-)^infinity —
Casselman-Wallach globalization: the
pair standing as one frame against
its smooth member standing as one
functor. Held apart. The newer
scroll is the frame of Paper 090's
first page: among the many
globalizations of a Harish-Chandra
module, two are canonical and
functorial — the minimal, the space
of analytic vectors in any Banach
globalization, constructed and
investigated by Schmid, and the
smooth, the space of smooth vectors
in any Banach globalization, defined
and studied by Casselman and
Wallach — and both correspondences
are exact functors, the exactness
the principal-series reduction
(1.2.1012) runs on. The held scroll
is one functor with its uniqueness:
for a Harish-Chandra module W,
W^infinity is the unique
Casselman-Wallach representation
(1.2.496) whose K-finite vectors are
W, guaranteed by the
Casselman-Wallach globalization
theorem — and in Paper 036 the
functor is spent on homology, the
hinge of the comparison (1.2.495)
and of its Tor and Ext forms
(1.2.501, 1.2.502). Filing the frame
inside the functor would lose the
minimal globalization and the pair's
exactness; filing the functor inside
the frame would lose the uniqueness
theorem in the exact form the
comparison spends it. That the
uniqueness is now also proved in
the book's form, as an equivalence
of categories (1.2.1017), is a
further claim, filed at its own
scroll.
(iv) 1.2.1019, the minimal
globalization, the book's form, into
1.2.985, the four canonical
globalizations: a construction
against the chain whose small end
carries its name. Held apart. The
newer scroll builds the completion:
for an admissible (g,K)-module V,
V^min is the completion whose
conjugate dual — the (g,K)-module
built from the continuous linear
functionals on a completion
satisfying the moderate-growth
bound — recovers V, its K-finite
vectors the double conjugate dual of
V; right exactness is its character,
quotients of V completing to
quotients of the completion. And
the scroll carries the record of
the book's own argument, which its
author later called 'rather
convoluted', with 'misprints in
unfortunate places' (Q409), the
exposition rebuilding it through
the conjugate-dual construction and
an elementary proof of its key
lemma, which it locates at the
book's pp. 91-93. The held scroll
builds nothing: it is the nested
chain of completions of an
irreducible admissible module —
V^omega, the analytic or minimal
globalization; V^infty, the smooth;
a Hilbert space globalization;
V^-infty, the distribution
globalization; V^-omega, the
hyperfunction or maximal — each
depending only on the infinitesimal
equivalence class of the module, and
its load-bearing clause is the
forms clause of Theorem 1.5: an
invariant Hermitian form on the
module extends uniquely to
continuous forms on V^omega and on
V^infty, and, unless the module is
finite-dimensional, admits no
continuous extension to V^-infty or
to V^-omega — the fact the
unitarity problem (1.2.986) is
organized around, proved as Theorem
9.16 and filed at 1.2.996. The
chain's small end is characterized
by its place in the chain and by
the forms' reach; the book's
minimal globalization is
characterized by its construction
and its exactness. Filing the
construction inside the chain would
lose the conjugate dual and the
repair record; filing the chain's
end inside the construction would
lose the forms clause and the four
completions it stands among.
(v) 1.2.1022, moderate growth and
the category HF^mod(G), into
1.2.496, the Casselman-Wallach
representation: the condition and
the category against the class
defined through them. Held apart.
The newer scroll states the estimate
itself — a smooth Frechet
representation (pi, H) is of
moderate growth when there is an
exponent d and for each x in U(g) a
constant with ||pi(g)xv|| bounded by
C||g||^d|v| across G — and names
HF^mod(G) the category of such
representations whose K-finite
vectors are admissible and finitely
generated, with continuous
G-morphisms: the smallest analytic
category in which the Harish-Chandra
modules can be completed, the
category the book's equivalence
(1.2.1017) is stated over, and the
seminorm content Paper 082 cites
this book for ('fundamental to the
theory of representations of
moderate growth'). The held scroll
is a class of representations with
a determination claim: a
Casselman-Wallach representation is
a smooth Frechet representation of
moderate growth whose K-finite
vectors form a Harish-Chandra module
(1.2.499) and which is the
Casselman-Wallach globalization
(1.2.500) of that module — the
analytic object and the algebraic
object determine each other — and
it is the universe the founding
chapter quantifies over, pi ranging
over it in the comparison (1.2.495),
in the Tor and Ext comparisons
(1.2.501, 1.2.502) and in the
closedness results (1.2.504). An
object of HF^mod(G) is not by that
standing alone the globalization of
its own module; the determination
is a further claim, and it stands
in the held scroll, not the newer.
Filing the category inside the class
would lose the estimate and the
equivalence's stated home; filing
the class inside the category would
lose the determination the founding
chapter's theorems quantify over.
Splits: none — no concept of the
eighteen filed by Papers 090-091 is
one claim in two seats: 1.2.1009's
two globalizations are two functors,
a pair filed as one frame because
the remark sets them side by side,
not one construction stated twice;
1.2.1018's maximal and 1.2.1019's
minimal globalization are a dual
pair, left exact against right
exact, joined by their agreement at
1.2.1020 (goodness) as 1.2.992 and
1.2.995 were joined by Serre duality
at Pass #47; 1.2.1024's leading term
and 1.2.1025's Plancherel chapter
are two chapters' records, each
holding its own chapter's contents
and, at 1.2.1025, its own recorded
defect. The split of 1.2.884,
declined at Passes #43, #44, #45,
#46 and #47, was re-weighed and
stands declined: the filter's text
has not changed since Paper 077 and
nothing in Papers 090-091 touches
it — no new evidence reopens it.
Retired: none. The standoff
companions at 1.5.4 stand at 108.
State machines stand at 351 (314
stated, 37 inferred), unchanged — no
taking-in. Registry entries 2,333
before and after; the pass's trace
is this record and the path entry at
1.5.5. Next
restructuring pass due at Paper 120
