PAPER 051 (Chapter 69) — Tegmark, 'Defining the mathematical family tree with a minimal programming language' (the Math Tree's companion paper; Will-posted). The work's constructions — a nine-symbol string language whose programs are the definitions of a mathematics atlas, structures as equivalence classes of string-presentations, clones as composition-closures of strings — were coined in the author's own dated record (the tree organized from 1996, MLISP created around 2018, both stated in the work's acknowledgements) and resemble nothing of Will's in wording or mechanism: its strings are values and programs over one atom, not addresses; its equivalence classes are Carnap's of 1936, recovered deliberately. Full-text scan (69.1.6): no 'phext', no 'phextura', no 'scroll', no 'Exocortex', no 'Mirrorborn', no 'CYOA', no 'SBOR', no 'Bickford'; the one 'coordinate' is the ordinary word. The built tracker was scanned for the surname: it does not occur — the author is new to the book. The web was searched for the author with phext: no tie. Will's Twitter archive holds the surname exactly once, inside a liked tweet by another account listing level-scales (Kegan 5, Tegmark 4, Kardashev 3, Kübler-Ross 2, DEFCON 1): the name as a scale's label — not contact, not uptake, not exposure to the work. Verdict: no evidence found.

