Paper 069's machines (Chapter 87):
the Bit Diffusion training machine
[stated] — ENCODE the datum to
bits, CAST to analog bits, SAMPLE
a time and a noise, CORRUPT,
ESTIMATE the clean bits with the
conditioning input zeroed, KEEP
that estimate with probability one
half (gradient stopped) as the
conditioning signal, PREDICT again
with the signal concatenated, TAKE
THE REGRESSION LOSS. Its named
gaps: no state sets the one-half
drop rate, and no state measures
whether the generated bits
concentrate at the poles. The Bit
Diffusion sampling machine
[stated] — DRAW noise, at each
step FORM the current and next
times with the td offset, PREDICT
the clean bits with the carried
estimate concatenated, STEP by the
DDIM or DDPM rule, CARRY the
prediction forward, at the end
THRESHOLD at zero and DECODE. Its
named gaps: no state sets the step
count or the td offset, and no
state reads the threshold's
margin. Home: 87.1.2; concepts
1.2.849, 1.2.850, 1.2.851.
[stated]

Paper 070's machines (Chapter 88):
the HumanEval grading machine
[stated] — ASSEMBLE the prompt,
SAMPLE two hundred completions at
the chosen temperature, EXECUTE
each in the sandbox against the
problem's tests, COUNT the
correct, ESTIMATE pass@k by the
unbiased form. Its named gaps: no
state chooses the temperature,
whose optimum moves with the
budget, and no state audits the
tests. The continuous-integration
tracing machine [stated] — READ
the CI configuration, BUILD the
environment, RUN the integration
tests under a profiling hook,
RECORD every called function's
inputs and outputs, HARVEST the
pairs as unit tests. Its named
gaps: the harvest bounded by what
can be pickled out of the room —
some 40,000 functions of millions
— and builtin and library calls
swept in with the project's own.
The self-filtering machine
[stated] — SAMPLE one hundred
solutions a curated problem from
the largest held model, KEEP the
problem if one passes, DISCARD it
otherwise, REPEAT to remove the
stateful. Its named gap: the
filter's standard is the filter
model's own reach, and ambiguity
cannot be told from difficulty.
Home: 88.1.2; concepts 1.2.854,
1.2.855, 1.2.857. [stated]

Paper 071's machines (Chapter 89):
the sampling-and-filtering grading
machine [stated] — ASSEMBLE the
prompt, SAMPLE eighty completions
at temperature 0.5, EXECUTE each
against the problem's three
asserts, COUNT the problems any
sample solves and the samples that
solve. Its named gaps: no state
chooses the temperature or the
budget, and no state audits the
tests. The dialog repair machine
[stated] — PRESENT the failed
problem and attempt, ACCEPT a
one-sentence hint, RESAMPLE, to at
most four turns. Its named gaps:
no state chooses the hint, and no
memory is kept across problems.
The execution-prediction machine
[stated] — PRESENT a ground-truth
program under a stated
configuration with a test input,
PREDICT its output, SCORE by exact
match against the true execution.
Its named gap: the score cannot
separate simulation from
pattern-matching, the examples
outscoring the code. Home: 89.1.2;
concepts 1.2.854, 1.2.861,
1.2.863, 1.2.866, 1.2.867.
[stated]

Paper 072's machines (Chapter 90):
the verifier-training machine
[stated] — FINETUNE the generator
two epochs on the worked key,
SAMPLE one hundred completions
per training problem, LABEL each
by its final answer, TRAIN the
verifier one epoch on the joint
objective. Its named gaps: the
labels bless flawed reasoning
with right answers, and no state
regenerates the training
solutions as the verifier
improves. The rank-and-select
machine [stated] — SAMPLE one
hundred completions at
temperature 0.7, SCORE each with
the verifier, RETURN the argmax
or the top-ranked answers' vote.
Its named gaps: no state bounds
the search — past roughly four
hundred completions the output
worsens as the judge is fooled —
and no state prunes a partial
solution, though the judge can
read one. The calculator-override
machine [stated] — WATCH for a
<<...>> annotation, OVERRIDE the
tokens after the equals sign with
the evaluated result, CONTINUE
sampling. Its named gaps: the
annotation habit is learned from
imperfect annotations, and the
original instrument's disclosed
bugs make every score it produced
a slight underestimate. Home:
90.1.2; concepts 1.2.868, 1.2.869,
1.2.870, 1.2.871, 1.2.874.
[stated]

Paper 073's machines (Chapter 91):
the prompted-examination machine
[stated] — ASSEMBLE the prompt
from the subject's name, five
development examples with their
answers, and the test question,
SCORE the four answer tokens'
probabilities, RETURN the argmax.
Its named gaps: the model never
writes an answer, its knowledge
read off four tokens' relative
probabilities; and the five
examples are fixed per subject,
no state adapting the prompt to
the question. The
fine-tune-and-transfer machine
[stated] — FINETUNE on other
question-answering datasets, or
on those plus the table's own
development and validation sets,
EVALUATE on the test split
untuned further, PREDICT by token
overlap or a trained four-class
head. Its named gaps: the three
smallest train on 1,825 of the
table's own questions, so
transfer and fine-tuned claims
share one results table; and no
state asks which source data
carried the transfer. The
contamination-audit machine
[stated] — COMPUTE each task's
mean question log-probability
under the examined model,
CORRELATE against per-task
accuracy, READ the sign. Its
named gaps: the audit reads the
questions only, never the answers
or the choices' order; and it
sees verbatim memorization only.
Home: 91.1.2; concepts 1.2.875,
1.2.876, 1.2.881.
[stated]

Paper 074's machines (Chapter 92):
the pair-scoring machine [stated]
— for each solution, ASSEMBLE the
goal, the solution and a [CLS]
token, SCORE the [CLS] state
through a linear transformation,
SOFTMAX over the two scores,
RETURN the higher. Its named
gaps: the two solutions are never
compared inside the model, each
priced against the goal alone;
and fine-tuning is unstable, some
configurations at chance, only
the best-on-validation reported.
The annotation machine [stated] —
SEED annotators with instruction
pages from six categories, ELICIT
a goal, a valid solution and a
trick permutation, QUALIFY above
80 percent, VALIDATE by other
annotators, REMOVE the
low-agreement items. Its named
gaps: validation deletes the
expert tail, the commonsense
boundary set by annotator
agreement; and the tricks are
human-authored, the difficulty
partly a portrait of the
annotators. The AFLite machine
[stated] — FINE-TUNE BERT-Large
on 5,000 examples, EMBED the
rest, RUN an ensemble of linear
classifiers on random subsets,
DISCARD the instances whose
labels the embeddings indicate,
PRODUCE the folds from what
survives. Its named gaps: the
bias removed is one model's
portrait, artifacts invisible to
that model surviving by
construction; and split identity
and difficulty entangled, the
folds the filter's own product.
Home: 92.1.2; concepts 1.2.882,
1.2.884, 1.2.885.
[stated]

Paper 075's machines (Chapter 93):
the crowdsourcing machine
[stated] — PRIME each worker with
an anchor word from a randomly
assigned WikiHow article, WRITE
twins of fifteen to thirty words
with at least 70 percent overlap,
VALIDATE each question by three
workers (majority key, agreed
unambiguity, no word-association
answer), DISCARD the invalid, 68
percent surviving. Its named
gaps: the third validation test
asks humans to certify the
absence of a bias humans
demonstrably insert; and the
seeding flattens topics, not
strategies. The AfLite machine
[stated] — FINE-TUNE RoBERTa on
6,000 instances, EMBED the
remaining 47,000, DISCARD the
6,000, ENSEMBLE sixty-four
logistic regressions on random
partitions, SCORE each instance
by the ensemble's agreement with
its label, REMOVE the top five
hundred at or above 0.75, ITERATE
until a phase removes fewer. Its
named gaps: the bias removed is
RoBERTa's portrait; and it
addresses instances, not pairs,
breaking twins without knowing
the pair structure exists. The
fill-in scoring machine [stated]
— REPLACE the blank with each
option in turn, SCORE the two
completions, RETURN the higher;
its diagnostic variant restricts
the input to the local context.
Its named gaps: each option is
priced in a separate pass, the
two candidates never held against
each other; and the diagnostic
prices exactly the leak the
dataset was built to exclude. The
transfer machine [stated] —
FINE-TUNE on WinoGrande,
EVALUATE zero-shot on the target
or fine-tune further on the
target's own splits. Its named
gap: it cannot distinguish
teaching commonsense from
teaching the target's artifacts.
Home: 93.1.2; concepts 1.2.884,
1.2.889, 1.2.891, 1.2.894.
[stated]

Paper 076's machines (Chapter 94):
the universal recurrence machine
[stated] — INITIALIZE the state
with the symbols' embeddings;
STEP: add the two-dimensional
coordinate embedding, ATTEND
across all positions, TRANSITION
by one shared position-wise
function, with residuals and
layer normalization; REPEAT T
times; READ the final state; the
decoder runs the same machine
over the output positions,
attending also to the encoder's
final state, generating
autoregressively. Its named
gaps: the step count arrives
from outside, and the block
tells its steps apart only
through the coordinate
embedding. The dynamic halting
machine [stated] — per position
still running: PREDICT a halting
probability from the state,
ACCUMULATE it, HALT at the
threshold with the remainder
completing the mass, BLEND the
output state by the
remainder-weighted rule, COPY
halted states forward; STOP when
all have halted or the maximum
is reached. Its named gaps: the
threshold is a hyperparameter
set outside the run; halted
positions still occupy their
steps in the synchronous form;
and the work's own translation
headline runs without it. The
universality reduction [stated]
— SET the self-attention with
its residual to the identity,
the transition to a convolution,
and the step count to the
input's length, yielding a
Neural GPU. Its named gap: the
reduction runs over
parameterizations, not over any
trained instance, and the
length-scaling count it needs is
supplied by no experiment.
Home: 94.1.2; concepts 1.2.523,
1.2.896, 1.2.897, 1.2.898,
1.2.899, 1.2.900.

Paper 077's machines (Chapter 95):
the adversarial filtering machine in
its parent form — a generator supplies
candidate endings, a discriminator
re-initialized from its pretrained
checkpoint and finetuned each iteration
on an 80/20 re-split scores them, the
deceptive ones replace the easy ones
(k=9 assigned negatives per ActivityNet
context, k=5 per WikiHow context), and
the loop runs until the discriminator's
accuracy converges (stated); the human
validation machine — six endings shown,
a Naive-Bayes worker model combining
the votes, the three least-preferred
wrong endings kept, two rounds for
ActivityNet and one for WikiHow
(stated); the zero-shot split machine —
the category inventory divided into
trained and withheld halves before any
question is drawn (stated); the
four-way scoring machine — each ending
encoded with the context, a logit per
ending, the highest taken (stated).

Paper 078's machines (Chapter 96):
the failure-partition machine — both
defining solvers run over every
question, the jointly-failed becoming
the Challenge Set and the rest the
Easy Set, partitions drawn within
each (stated); the corpus-mining
machine — roughly 100 hand-written
templates instantiated over 80
topics, top documents collected,
de-duplicated, stripped and chunked,
an existing corpus added (stated);
the entailment answering machine —
question and option converted to a
fill-in-the-blank assertion, top-8
premises retrieved and filtered, the
option scored by its best-entailing
premise, the argmax taken (stated);
the span-overlap answering machine —
retrieved sentences concatenated
into one paragraph, a span selected,
the option overlapping it most taken
(stated); the tie-credit scoring
machine — one point for the key,
1/k for a k-way tie including it
(stated).

Paper 079's machines (Chapter
97): the Parcae forward machine
(a normalized prelude output
injected into a spectrally
constrained loop, its transition
discretized from a negative
diagonal); the per-sequence
depth-sampling machine (a depth
drawn per sequence, the batch
run to its maximum, staggered
lead-ins holding the late
sequences still); the faithful
truncated sampler (the total
depth drawn from the law at its
full mean, the backward window
falling out of the draw); the
isoFLOP frontier machine
(budgets swept over mean
recurrence, parabolic minima
read per budget, power laws and
the parametric form fitted over
the minima); the unified-fit
machine (saturating exponentials
fitted per test-time curve,
floors read against the trained
depths, the unified law fitted
jointly and validated on
held-out models).

Paper 080's machines (Chapter 98): the
three-stage annotation machine (gate,
structured reasoning, eight reasoned
scores under one published prompt);
the concurrent scoring machine (fifty
parallel requests, three attempts,
exponential backoff, honoured
Retry-After); the occupation
aggregation machine (importance-
weighted means over valid tasks, the
missing excluded, never zeroed); the
median-quadrant machine (β against RL
feasibility at both medians, priced in
93.7 million positions); the PCA
dimensionality machine (two components
retained by Kaiser and Horn alike,
the second a single dimension's).

Paper 081's machines (Chapter 99):
the quadrant classifier (two
questions — understanding? use? —
sorting research into the four
cells); the exemplar machine (each
named cell anchored by one
paradigmatic career: Bohr, Edison,
Pasteur); the compact machine (the
interactive view in, a renewed
science-government settlement out).
All three stated — in the publisher's
description, the contents, and the
fragments; the book's body is not
held (99.1.1).

Paper 082's machines (Chapter
100): the closure-filtration
machine (filter by closed
subspaces, quotient by inverse
limit, intersect for the
submodule — Hausdorff by
construction); the stability
machine (act by the nilradical
and reclose, and the submodule
returns to itself); the
Artin-Rees transfer machine (the
algebraic lemma carried through
globalization into exactness);
the Bernstein-Zelevinsky
filtration machine (derive,
quotient by the maximal stable
submodule, induce back, close the
image, read the subquotient).
All four stated.

Paper 083's machines (Chapter 101): the
orbit induction machine (extend on one
orbit, lift through the finite orbit
filtration with supports preserved); the
normal-order recursion machine (split a
supported distribution by the normal
presentation into a derivative of an
order-zero piece and a lower-order
remainder, and recurse); the parameter-
line machine (deform along the one
transverse direction, regularize by the
Mellin pairing at good parameters,
remove poles by descent at bad ones);
the coefficient-lifting machine
(regularize along the fibre, then lift
along coefficient embeddings with
support and normal order preserved). All
four stated.

Paper 084's machines (Chapter 102): the two-filtration descent machine (the comparison descended along the Bruhat filtration to one Bruhat cell, then along the transversal degree filtration to transverse order zero, the scalar case climbing both); the Jacquet exactness machine (the nilpotent part of each sequence in the Bruhat package computed on both sides, exactness checked piece by piece, no general criterion offered); the dimension induction machine (the scalar comparison on N/U reduced through one-dimensional central subgroups, both sides' c-cohomology computed first, the five lemma closing through degenerate Hochschild-Serre sequences); the duality-and-resolution machine (the homological comparison translated to the cohomological by duality over [9]'s finite-dimensionality, then reduced to principal series by a resolution under the subrepresentation theorem, two spectral sequences compared, the second degenerating at E_2).

Paper 085's machines (Chapter 103): the coinvariant machine (Theorem 1.4's identity won for the trivial representation by a dimension count through de Rham cohomology, tensored to every Frechet module, converted from the Lie algebra action to (g − 1)-sums by Dixmier-Malliavin, and split over the group's components); the resolution machine (a representation presented by its integration surjection, resolved by relatively projective terms under strong arrows, coinvariants taken degreewise as Frechet spaces, homology read off independent of the resolution — its Hausdorffness left open outside finite dimension); the Shapiro machine (a subgroup representation produced to the group, its resolution produced termwise with exactness and relative projectivity preserved, its coinvariants identified with the subgroup-side complex by Frobenius reciprocity, every identification carrying its modular characters); the de Rham resolution machine (the trivial representation resolved by the extended de Rham complex of G/K with Schwartz coefficients, tensored with the representation, its coinvariants computed termwise to the (g, K)-complex — the theory's central identification); the Borel filtration machine (the boundary quotient filtered by order of vanishing, its graded pieces moved to stabilizers by Shapiro's lemma, vanishing and finiteness checked degree by degree, the limit taken by Mittag-Leffler, the orbits of the closed complement peeled one at a time).

Paper 086's machines (Chapter 104): the Bruhat filtration machine (the induced representation filtered by support on the closed Bruhat cell unions, restricted injectively to tempered distributions on each open cell, and modeled by normal derivatives of polynomial densities — an inclusion at this stage, equality waiting on Section 4); the analytic-continuation machine (a model element paired against the induced sections over the cell, the integral convergent for Re lambda large, continued meromorphically with its poles priced by the Knapp-Stein intertwining operators, and deformed along lambda + t rho with its equations preserved); the pole-removal machine (a meromorphic deformation's Laurent coefficients read off one filtration step down, each deformed holomorphically by induction on the cell, subtracted to drop the pole order, induction on the order closing at a holomorphic deformation); the twisting machine (a module twisted by w, localized at the shifted root vectors e_j - eta(e_j) along the ideal-chain ordering, and tensored with the bimodule S_{w, eta} — its canonicity a proved theorem, not a form); the dual-transport machine (the untwisted Jacquet module dualized over its h-finite part, its eta-torsion extracted, the composite identified with J^eta_* and the filtration transported quotient by quotient — proved by reduction to the nondegenerate case through the parabolic of eta's support); the Whittaker counting machine (the quotients' Whittaker spaces computed on the model as pure tensors, the exact sequences split by infinitesimal-character separation, and the dimensions summed over the admissible Weyl elements, collapsing to a Weyl count times one dimension at finite-dimensional sigma).

Paper 087's machines (Chapter 105): the boundedness test (a smooth section tested against every Nash differential operator, the infinite test reduced by a closed affine embedding to its algebraic operators, on which the classical Schwartz space is recovered); the extension-by-zero machine (a Schwartz section on an open semi-algebraic set extended by zero across its boundary, the division lemma spending two powers of the boundary function per derivative, the arising sections exactly those flat on the complement — its inverse's continuity borrowed from the open mapping theorem, as the work itself records); the partition-of-unity machine (a finite semi-algebraic cover refined to a basic cover, tempered functions summing to one and multiplying Schwartz sections into the cover's pieces — finite covers only, the restricted topology admitting no other kind); the tubular extension machine (a Schwartz or tempered function on a closed submanifold extended through the product case, the normal-bundle case and localization, its jets at a chosen point preserved); the devissage machine (the generalized sections supported on a closed submanifold filtered by transversal degree, each factor read on the submanifold itself with polynomial normal coefficients — the Borel lemma of the theory, run on a chosen closed set because the space provides no closures).

Paper 088's machines (Chapter 106):
the globalization chain (a
Harish-Chandra module completed four
ways by growth — analytic, smooth,
distribution, hyperfunction — each
completion depending only on the
infinitesimal class, its canonicity
bought by the Casselman-Wallach
theorem that the measuring norms agree
up to polynomial factors); the
compact-picture induction machine (a
Levi representation, its Levi chosen
by the Cartan involution, induced and
realized on functions on the compact
group, the four globalizations read
off as four regularity classes of
those functions); the maximal
cohomological induction machine (an
admissible (q,L)-module realized in
Dolbeault cohomology over a complex
flag variety, the Hausdorff quotient
taken first because raw cohomology
need not be Hausdorff, Wong's closed
range making every output the maximal
globalization of its module); the
minimal dual machine (the same
construction with compact supports,
Serre duality turning the maximal
theory into the minimal one, Bratten's
closed range on the compactly
supported complex); the Hermitian-form
machine (a form read as a map to the
Hermitian dual, maps read as kernels
by the Schwartz kernel theorem,
kernels as cohomology classes on the
product with the opposite manifold —
the invariant forms on cohomological
induction computed as invariant real
classes, and a unitary representation
built by completing one).

Paper 089's machines (Chapter 107):
the stratification machine (a
manifold with a finite subanalytic
stratification and a coefficient
space, filtered by flatness along
the unions of smaller strata, its
graded object computed entire as the
sum of the strata's tempered-
distribution duals with their
transversal filtrations); the Bruhat
machine (that machine on the flag
variety under N — the cells the
strata, the principal series the
sections, the graded pieces the
J(w, sigma)); the twisted-regular
vanishing engine (a unipotent group
and a character, the Fourier
transform in the abelian case and
downward induction through the
centre, returning the homology
tables — finite, bounded, and zero
off the purely imaginary,
central-trivial characters); the
cohomological extension machine (the
filtration's spectral sequence with
every off-open-cell piece killed by
the engine, collapsing to the open
cell — the restriction isomorphism
and the dimension theorems); the
holomorphic family machine (the
inducing parameter freed over a^*,
cohomology commuting with germs, the
sheaf of Whittaker vectors free of
rank dim sigma, the Jacquet integral
holomorphic entire); the Whittaker
functor machine (any moderate-growth
finite-length representation embedded
in a principal series, the functor
read off exact and finite, and
multiplicity one for linear
quasisplit groups).

Paper 090's machines (Chapter 108):
the minimal globalization functor
(any Harish-Chandra module to its
analytic vectors in any Banach
globalization — Schmid's
construction, exact on the category,
and the reduction's source side); the
smooth globalization functor (any
Harish-Chandra module to its smooth
vectors in any Banach globalization —
Casselman and Wallach's construction,
exact on the category, and the
reduction's target side; the pair's
exactness is what transports
resolutions between the completions);
the Koszul machine (a nilpotent Lie
algebra and a module, returning the
u-homology groups through the Koszul
complex — finite-dimensional
Levi-modules on both completions,
the maps between them the comparison
theorem's whole content).

Paper 091's machines (Chapter 109):
the maximal globalization functor
(any admissible (g,K)-module to the
closure of its K-finite vectors in
the induced globalization from a
maximal parabolic — left exact, its
existence resting on the automatic
continuity theorem, and the
theorem's source side); the minimal
globalization functor (any admissible
(g,K)-module to the completion its
conjugate dual determines — right
exact, built from the moderate-growth
functionals, and the theorem's target
side; the pair's agreement on every
module is the Casselman-Wallach
theorem itself).

Paper 092's machine (Chapter 110): the coadjoint-orbit parametrization procedure (the algorithms the book carries for parametrizing all coadjoint orbits of a nilpotent Lie group, as its publisher's description states them — the orbit space rendered computable from the Malcev coordinates and their jump indices).

Paper 093's machines (Chapter 111): the n-adic completion procedure (the projective limit of V/n^k V, Section 5 — the paper's algebraic engine, exact by Artin-Rees); the large-extension closure (the closure of V in the smoothly induced representation at large k, Section 7); the small-extension quotient (the functorial quotient construction from presentations by induced representations, Section 7); the analytic-family thickening (the m-thickened modules of Section 9, the algebraic form of the meromorphicity of intertwining operators, on which the main theorem's induction runs).

Paper 094's machines (Chapter 112): the Jacquet-module functor (Chapter 4's engine — the coinvariants of an admissible (g, K)-module under the nilradical, with its three applications in section 4.2); the Zuckerman cohomological induction (Chapter 6's construction of the fundamental series, from section 6.3's functors and the vanishing theorems); the Harish-Chandra transform and the orbital integrals (Chapter 7, on cusp forms — section 7.4 the transform, section 7.5 the integrals); the Dirac-operator criterion (Chapter 9, sections 9.6-9.7 — unitarizability and the (g, K)-cohomology of unitary representations).

Paper 095's machines (Chapter 113): the standard homology complex (2.26) — the coinvariant complex of a finite-rank free U(p)-resolution of V, whose homology is the n-homology and which carries the U(m + a)-action the Levi inherits (Proposition 2.24's engine); coherent continuation (section 3's process — the transport of the character identity along coherent families of Harish-Chandra modules, reducing Osborne's conjecture to equating the extreme exponential terms).

Paper 096's machine (Chapter 114): the property-(n) descent — the abstract's own three-step argument: property (n), established on the admissible (g, k)-modules, descends to every (g, k)-module of dimension less than card F; the pair (sl(n, F), so(n, F)) is then verified against the descended property purely algebraically, and the subrepresentation theorem for sl(n) is read off as its consequence.

Paper 097's machine (Chapter 115): the I-adic completion — the inverse-limit passage that sends a module M to the limit of its quotients M/I^k M over the filtration's powers, and a ring to the limit of its A/I^k, stated in the citing chapter's own text on this book's warrant, its Chapter I, Proposition 5.10 carrying the passage's exactness on Noetherian rings and its tensor-product form.

Paper 098's machine (Chapter 116): the restriction to n — the passage that sends a Harish-Chandra module V to its structure as a module over U(n) for a parabolic's nilradical n, stated in the citing records on this paper's warrant, its finite generation over that ring carrying the passage's finiteness.

Paper 099's machine (Chapter 117): the product decomposition of a unipotent group over a quasi-closed partition — the passage that writes a connected unipotent H, normalized by a torus S, as the product of its intersections with the root groups of a disjoint quasi-closed partition of its roots, an isomorphism of varieties (Proposition 3.11), stated in the paper and spent by Chapter 54 on this paper's warrant.

Paper 100's machine (Chapter 118): the completion-and-globalization passage of diagram (4.3) — the transport that takes a Harish-Chandra module π to its Casselman-Jacquet completion CJ(π) in the algebraic complete category ℋ𝒞(𝔤, K_L), globalizes it level by level to ĈJ(π) in the smooth complete category 𝒞(𝔤, L)_f, and there meets the smooth completion CJ^∞(π^∞) of π's own globalization — the two routes around the square agreeing by Proposition 4.18, stated in the paper and spent by Chapter 54 on this paper's warrant.

Paper 101's machine (Chapter 119): the association transfer of §3 — the passage that takes association from the stochastic heat equation's solution (associated for each δ > 0, by the sign of its Malliavin derivative, through Clark–Ocone) through the rescaled heights 4δ log Z + t/12 (associated at every δ) and the δ → 0 limit (the KPZ fixed point, associated because convergence in distribution preserves the property) and reads it off at time 1 as the association of the Airy_1 and Airy_2 processes. States: SOLVED-ASSOCIATED, RESCALED, LIMIT TAKEN, READ OFF. The gap named for it: the passage carries the property and nothing quantitative — no covariance, no rate, no strength of dependence crosses with it; each leg preserves association exactly and prices nothing.



Paper 106's machines (Chapter
124), both stated in the
collection's own AGENTS.md: the
publication handoff chain —
candidate, reviewed, publication
PR, merged on main, issue closed
and claim released; and the
eligibility-and-duplicate gate —
original discovery only, no
formalization campaigns for prior
resolutions, duplicates refused
before review.
