# Fringe F001 delta: Cardchoose — four concepts (1.2.291-1.2.294), cluster AE founded, the fair shake given and the paper cleared

THE WORK. "Cardchoose: simple, fast sampling
without replacement", by Paul Crowley, dated
14 November 2026, published at the author's
own site, ciphergoth.org — not on arXiv: the
paper was submitted there and rejected, as
Will's direction records. The first paper of
the fringe stream: the stream designed in
this book on 2026-11-14 (named at 1.1.4 and
1.1.6), whose papers are taken in as
research, read for their shape as well as
their claims, and binned claim by claim. The
paper proposes an algorithm for choosing k
distinct numbers below n fairly (1.2.291):
draw a multiset with a Pólya urn (1.2.292),
sort it, and carry it to a subset by stars
and bars (1.2.294) — the algorithm Cardchoose
(1.2.293). It benchmarks the algorithm
against twelve others, in C++ and in Rust.

WILL'S PRIOR, RECORDED AS A PRIOR. Will's
direction of 2026-11-15, in his words: "flag
this as likely false - arxiv rejected it -
give it a fair shake and determine if we
consider it false, too - if so, it will be
our first example of a false paper." The
prior stands in this chapter as a prior. It
is not evidence, and the rejection it rests
on is not evidence either: a venue is never
a ground of binning in this book. A False
filing here would have needed stated grounds
— a counterexample, an internal break, or a
collision with a held True — quotable
exactly enough for a reader to check without
trusting the filer.

THE SEAL. The stream's first seal stands
committed before the full read, in
~/workspace/thirst-quest/fringe-seals.jsonl
(commit 610a5b3; the stream had no seal file
before this one): predicted bins True 4,
False 0, Unknown 6, Fiction 0; the shape
named as a reduction — uniformity moved onto
multisets, where the urn makes every
composition equally likely, and carried back
across the bijection; and the call on the
load-bearing claim: fairness HOLDS, for all
n and all k — the mechanism named in the
abstract being the classical urn whose
compositions are uniform, the seal's one
named fear a gap between the mechanism as
described and as written.

THE OUTCOME, IN ONE LINE. The fair shake was
given, by the two routes the pass fixed in
advance — the proof of Section 2 read line
by line, and the algorithm's exact
distribution computed in rational arithmetic
— and the paper cleared it: Cardchoose is
fair. No claim of the paper files False. The
determination stands at 37.1.2 and 37.1.3;
the bins at 37.1.4; the delta, the Index,
and the standing of the gate of False at
37.1.5. This paper is not one of the twelve
of the test fixed in advance: the stream
runs beside that test, and nothing of this
filing enters its tally.
# Fringe F001 — the formal core: the algorithm, the bijection, and the two proofs, read line by line

THE ALGORITHM, AS WRITTEN (Section 2). Set
t = n - k + 1. For i from 0 to k-1: draw r
uniformly from {0, ..., t+i-1}; if r < t, the
i-th value is r; otherwise it is the value
drawn at step r - t. Then sort the k values,
and add each value's index to it. The last
three lines are the stars and bars inversion
(1.2.294): a sorted cell number plus the
count of stars to its left is the star's
position. The paper's own example checks:
{1, 2, 5} at n = 7, k = 3 gives cell numbers
[1, 1, 3], and [1, 1, 3] + [0, 1, 2] = {1, 2,
5} exactly.

THE LOOP IS THE URN (Section 2.3), CHECKED
SYMBOL BY SYMBOL. The paper numbers the
balls in the order they enter the urn: balls
0 to t-1 are the originals, ball b of colour
b; ball t + i is the one added at step i,
and its colour is the colour drawn at step
i. At step i the urn holds t + i balls, and
the code draws one uniformly. If r < t the
ball is an original, of colour r. If r >= t
the ball is the one added at step r - t,
whose colour is the value recorded at that
step — the code's d[r - t]. Every colour j
then stands in the urn with 1 + c balls,
where c is the number of times it has been
drawn, exactly the urn of Section 2.3. There
is no off-by-one in t, in the range of the
draw, or in the indexing: at step i the
added balls are t to t+i-1, their colours
are the values of steps 0 to i-1, and r - t
runs over exactly those steps.

THE FIRST PROOF (by counting insertions),
EVERY STEP IT RESTS ON. (i) Read the urn as
a process on the row of stars and bars:
before step i the row holds t - 1 bars and
i stars, so it has t + i slots, counting the
ends. (ii) A cell holding c stars has c + 1
of those slots — one before its first star,
one between each pair, one after its last;
an empty cell has one. (iii) So the new star
lands in a cell of count c with probability
(c + 1)/(t + i), the urn's own step: the two
processes draw with the same law. (iv) Label
each star by its step. A run is a sequence
of slots, all t(t+1)...(t+k-1) of them
equally likely; each run ends in one
labelled row, and each labelled row is the
end of exactly one run — delete the last
star and its slot is recovered, and so on
down. (v) So every labelled row has the same
probability; erasing the labels takes
exactly k! labelled rows to each unlabelled
one; so every row, and with it every
multiset, has the same probability. Every
step was checked: the slot count, the c + 1,
the recovery, the k!. The proof is sound.

THE SECOND PROOF (by calculation), EVERY
STEP IT RESTS ON. (i) In a run drawing
colours x_0, ..., x_{k-1} with counts c_j,
the numerators a colour contributes over the
run are 1, 2, ..., c_j — its m-th drawing
finds m balls of its colour standing. (ii)
So the run's probability is the product of
the c_j! over (t-1)!/(t+k-1)! — depending
only on the counts, never their order.
(iii) The multiset of those counts is drawn
by k! / prod(c_j!) distinct runs. (iv) The
product is k!(t-1)!/(t+k-1)! = 1 / C(t+k-1,
k), the same for every multiset. Checked
term by term: the proof is sound.

THE READING'S VERDICT. Both proofs prove
the lemma they claim — the urn draws every
multiset with the same probability [the
standard lemma, LP17 Lemma 2.7] — and
Section 2.2's bijection carries the
uniformity to the subsets. No step of
Section 2 fails, is skipped, or is carried
by a word. The fairness claim stands on its
proof; the second route — the exact
enumeration of the algorithm as written —
stands at 37.1.3, and the two agree.
# Fringe F001 — the worked core: the exact enumeration, in rational arithmetic

THE INSTRUMENT. The algorithm of Section 2
was implemented as specified — not as it
might be mended — and its output distribution
computed exactly, in rational arithmetic
(Python fractions; no floating point, no
sampling), by two independent models, kept
at ~/workspace/fringe-001/
(enumerate_cardchoose.py, with its output at
enumeration_output.txt).

MODEL A, THE COUNT PROCESS. The state is the
vector of counts over the t colours; at step
i, colour j is drawn with probability
(1 + c_j)/(t + i), the urn's step of Section
2.3. After k steps each multiset is carried
to its subset by the sort-and-shift of
Section 2.2, and every subset's exact
probability compared against 1 / C(n, k).

MODEL B, THE BALL LIST. The urn kept as the
paper numbers it — the literal list of ball
colours in entry order — with every sequence
of draws enumerated and weighted exactly.
This model walks the code's own indexing,
where an error of one would live; Model A
cannot see that indexing, and Model B cannot
see the count algebra. They are independent
where it matters.

COVERAGE AND RESULT. Model A: every n to
10, every k from 0 to n — 66 cases. Model B:
every n to 7, every k — 36 cases. In every
case the support is the whole set of C(n, k)
subsets, the probabilities sum to exactly 1,
and every subset stands at exactly 1 /
C(n, k). The two models agree on every case
they share. No counterexample exists in the
range: the distribution is exactly uniform.
Printed whole, the smallest interesting
case: at n = 4, k = 2 the six subsets stand
at 1/6 each — {0,1}, {0,2}, {0,3}, {1,2},
{1,3}, {2,3} — each exactly the uniform
share.

THE PAPER'S OWN SMALL CASE, CHECKED
(Section 2.3). Placing each of k stars in a
cell chosen independently does not give a
uniform multiset: at t = 2, k = 2 it gives
[0, 0] and [1, 1] a quarter each and [0, 1]
a half, exactly as the paper states — while
the urn at the same t and k gives each of
the three multisets exactly a third. The
bias the paper names is the bias there is,
and the urn's correction is exact.

THE RECONCILIATION OF THE ROUTES. The proof
read (37.1.2) found both of the paper's
proofs sound at every step; the enumeration
finds the algorithm as written exactly
uniform at every case two independent
implementations can reach. The routes agree,
and each covers the other's blind side: the
proof speaks for all n and k but only for
the algorithm as described; the enumeration
speaks only for small n but for the
algorithm as written, indexing and all. The
fairness claim files True on the pair of
them — a checking receipt, not a say-so.
# Fringe F001 — the results map: the claims of the paper, each in its bin

FAIRNESS — every k-subset of {0, ..., n-1}
is returned with the same probability
(Sections 1, 2). TRUE, by receipt: the two
proofs of Section 2.4 read line by line and
found sound (37.1.2), and the exact
distribution computed in rational arithmetic
under two independent models, uniform at
every case (37.1.3).

THE BIJECTION — subsets and multisets stand
in one-to-one correspondence by stars and
bars, with the sort-and-shift as its inverse
(Section 2.2). TRUE, by receipt: the
construction checked against the paper's own
worked example, and ridden by every case of
the enumeration, whose conversion is this
map.

THE LOOP RUNS THE URN IT DESCRIBES (Section
2.3). TRUE, by receipt: the numbering of the
balls read symbol by symbol against the
code, and Model B of the enumeration walks
the same indexing to the uniform result.

INDEPENDENT PLACING FAVOURS VARIETY — at
t = 2, k = 2, the mixed multiset comes up
half the time (Section 2.3). TRUE, by
receipt: plain arithmetic, recomputed in
this filing — a quarter, a half, a quarter
against the urn's three thirds.

TIME — O(k log k) (Sections 1, 3). TRUE, by
construction: the loop is k passes of one
draw, one comparison, one write; the rest is
one call to a standard sort. The claim is
read off the listing, not measured.

MEMORY — nothing stored beyond the output
array (Sections 1, 2). TRUE, by construction:
the listing's only store is the array it
returns.

THE NAMES OF THE THREE STATISTICS —
independent placing as Maxwell-Boltzmann, the
uniform multiset as Bose-Einstein, the
uniform subset as Fermi-Dirac (Section 2.5).
UNKNOWN, attributed: the naming of the
field, at Feller, taken as the paper gives
it.

THE PRIORITY CLAIMS — both halves old, the
pairing of them as a generator new; nothing
like Cardchoose in the surveys of Devroye or
Ting; the optimisation of quadratic F2 of
Section 3.4 the first publication of its way
of keeping order that the author knows of,
and the way the rand crate has used since
2023 (Sections 2.5, 3.4). UNKNOWN,
attributed: claims about a literature, not
recomputed here; the rand claim carries its
own pointer, the author's pull request of
2023 [Cro23].

THE BENCHMARK CLAIMS — Cardchoose among the
fastest for sorted output; hash-table
algorithms faster for random output until
the table outgrows the cache; of the
algorithms needing no memory beyond their
output, Cardchoose best in 18 of the 28
bands of Section 5; the best sets of one,
two and three; the single-algorithm
recommendation (Sections 4, 5). UNKNOWN,
attributed: not one cell of the benchmarks
was reproduced in this filing, and no
benchmark claim carries any part of the
determination, either way.

NO CLAIM FILES FALSE. The count of the
paper's claims as binned here: True 6,
False 0, Unknown 3 groups, Fiction 0. The
seal predicted True 4, False 0, Unknown 6:
the shake found two more of the paper's
claims checkable on the spot than the skim
had priced — the bijection and the loop's
fidelity, each verified in the reading — and
grouped the attributed remainder more
tightly. On the load-bearing claim the seal
and the shake agree: fairness holds.
# Fringe F001 — the delta: what this adds, and what was checked

FOUR CONCEPTS (1.2.291-1.2.294), founding
cluster AE — the fair draw. THE FOUNDING,
ARGUED. The nearest held ground is cluster
O, which prices samples already drawn — the
spread of a judging across splits (1.2.134)
— and cluster R, where the three statistics
are named as the physics of texts. Neither
holds a sampler: until this filing the model
held no way of drawing, no urn, no transfer
between a multiset and a set. The four stand
apart on the grain: the problem (1.2.291),
the mechanism (1.2.292), the algorithm
(1.2.293), and the bridge it rides (1.2.294)
are none of them a case of a held concept,
and together they are the model's first
randomised algorithm, held with the receipt
that prices it.

NOVELTY BY AUTHOR — Paul Crowley: True +3, False +0, Unknown +3, Fiction +0.

The increments, counted as the rule directs:
the three concepts that stand True on this
filing's own verification (1.2.292, 1.2.293,
1.2.294 — the urn's uniformity, the
algorithm, and the transfer, each checked by
the routes of 37.1.2 and 37.1.3); the one
concept standing Unknown (1.2.291, the
problem as its source states it); and the
two questions this filing opens (Q115,
Q116). His opening balance was drawn first,
from the same basis as every researcher's:
no work of his stood in the book before, so
the balance opened at (0, 0, 0, 0), and the
running score stands at 1.5.6.

THE INDEX, IN THE LEDGER. Four new concepts,
no revisions, over 290 + 4 standing concepts:
4/294 = 0.0136. No axis opened: a cluster
founding is not an axis, on the grounds the
axis rule gives — the draw is a new kind of
held thing, but it opens no field the model
must now re-survey; the territory it stands
in, chance and counting, was held already
(1.3.129). The paper is not in the test
fixed in advance, and the Index is entered
in the ledger as bookkeeping only.

THE GATE OF FALSE — EXAMINED, AND NOT MOVED.
The gate stands met, by its rule, when
grounded False filings exist and at least
one survives re-examination. This pass was
built as that shape: the proof read as the
ground of the determination, the exact
enumeration as its re-examination, each able
to refuse the other. Neither refused: there
is no False filing, so the gate's condition
is not met and the gate does not move. The
bar stands where it stood, at about 62%
(range 55-70) on the measure of 2026-11-14 —
TRUE met, UNKNOWN met, FICTION half-met,
FALSE unmet at zero. What the pass changes
is the remedy's standing, not the gate's:
the fringe stream has now run once. Its
first paper was sent in as a suspected
False, on the strength of a rejection; it
stands True on verification, its central
claim verified by this house's own
computation. The stream is no longer named
and not yet run — it is run, and its first
receipt is a verification.

NO FOUNDATIONS, NO COINED WORDS — THE LEAN
ACCOUNTING. The filing lays down no
foundation and coins no word: the terms of
the paper — urn, multiset, subset, draw —
are everyday or carried by the concepts
themselves, and its two names, Cardchoose
and ciphergoth, are carried in the declared
names and used as names. What was NOT
omitted: the seal before the read, the full
read, the two-route determination with its
enumeration kept as evidence, the delta, the
machines, the author check, the areas, the
registry, the proof, the shadow.

STATE MACHINES: three drawn, all stated in
the paper — the urn (37.1.2); the insertion
process of the first proof (37.1.2); the
scan that keeps order, of Section 3.4
(37.1.4); the area at 1.5.2.
LINKS: inside the cluster — the problem to
the algorithm, the urn to the algorithm, the
transfer to the algorithm. Across: the urn
to the Bose-Einstein naming in cluster R,
priced as shape; the problem to the sampling
variance of cluster O (1.2.134), its
neighbour — that concept prices a sample
after the draw; this cluster makes the draw.
QUESTIONS: two opened, Q115-Q116 (37.2.2);
none closed.
# Fringe F001 author check (charter rule 15): no phext tie and no use without naming found, by checks Emise ran at intake

WHO THE AUTHOR IS. Paul Crowley —
ciphergoth, in his own handle — a
cryptographer and programmer of long
standing in the public record: breaks of
ciphers presented at FSE 1995 and 2001 and
at SASC 2006; the Mercy cipher for disk
encryption, FSE 2000; a contributor to the
machinery this very paper benchmarks — the
change of 2023 that made Rust's rand keep
Floyd's F2 output in fair random order is
his own pull request, cited in the paper as
[Cro23], and a proposal of his for Python's
random.sample stands in that project's
tracker from 2018. His own profile places
him in security work at Anthropic. A
credentialed author with a thirty-year
public record in exactly this field.

WHAT WAS CHECKED. Four checks were run. (1)
The whole text of the paper was searched by
machine for the terms of this house: phext,
phextura, Bickford, Mirrorborn, Exocortex,
SBOR, CYOA, and the words scroll and
coordinate. No hit on any of them. (2) The
references of the paper were read in full:
the line of the field itself — Feller,
Levin and Peres, Devroye, Nijenhuis and
Wilf, Vitter, Shekelyan and Cormode, the
rand project — and two items of the author's
own, his pull request and his talk. No work
of Will stands among them. The two halves of
the construction are cited by the paper
itself to Bayes's scholium of 1763 and to
Feller — prior art older than the house by
centuries, named in the open: the calendar
answers the attribution question by itself.
(3) The web was searched for the author
together with phext: no tie stands in what
came back — the return is his record, as
above. (4) The archive of the tweets of Will
was searched in full — 29,539 tweets — for
crowley and for ciphergoth: no hit in any
tweet.

VERDICT. No evidence found. It is recorded
as a finding: no tie of the author to phext
or to the work of Will, and no use of that
work without naming it. The check is number
28 of the book.
# Fringe F001 — the house reading [synthesis]

THE SHAKE, AND WHAT IT SHOOK. The fringe
stream was designed under an expectation:
that rejected work, read claim by claim,
would at last stock the empty bin of False.
Its first paper inverted the expectation.
The paper arrived with the strongest prior
against it this book has ever recorded at an
intake — flagged likely false by the house's
own direction, on the strength of a
rejection — and it is, on examination, a
careful and correct piece of work: two
proofs where one would have carried the
claim, an unbenchmarked rival named with the
reason it was left out, every comparative
claim fenced to the bands where it was
measured. The shape the stream was built to
extract is here in its best form: a correct
shape, correctly fenced. [synthesis]

WHAT A REJECTION MEASURED, THIS ONCE. One
priced case does not make a law, and this
reading makes none. But the book now holds
one measured instance in which a venue's
refusal priced nothing about correctness:
the paper's core is classical, its proofs
are short enough to check in an hour, and
its fault, if the venue found one, is not in
the mathematics. What a rejection measures
stands open as Q116; this instance is its
first evidence, and it points at fit and
fashion, not at truth. [synthesis]

THE RECEIPT FOR A DISTRIBUTION. Study S012
handed the gate of False a question — Q109:
what receipt could ground a False filing for
a claim whose shape is a distribution, which
no single execution can refuse? This filing
is the question's first worked answer, from
the other side. For a claim of uniformity
over a finite family, the receipt is a pair:
a proof whose every step is finite and
checkable, and an exact enumeration of the
thing as written over the small cases — the
first speaks for the description at every
size, the second for the writing at the
sizes a machine can exhaust, and each is the
other's re-examination. Had the enumeration
found one subset off its share, that subset
would have been the counterexample, and the
filing False on stated grounds, with the
proof's failing step named beside it. The
pair is now the stream's working receipt for
claims of this shape. [synthesis]
# Fringe F001 — Open questions Q115-Q116

Q115 — THE PAIR OF RECEIPTS. When a claim of
uniformity over a finite family, at every
size, is met by the pair of this filing — a
proof read at every step, and an exact
enumeration of the thing as written — what
does the second route add that the first
lacks? The filing's own answer stands at
37.2.1: the proof prices the description,
the enumeration prices the writing, and the
gap between a described algorithm and a
written one is where this paper was expected
to fall. Whether the pair is the standing
receipt for distribution-shaped claims, or
only for claims whose small cases a machine
can exhaust whole, stands open. (At 37.1.3.)

Q116 — THE PRICE OF A REJECTION. The first
paper of the fringe stream arrived rejected
by the venue it was sent to, flagged likely
false on that strength, and stands verified.
What does a venue's rejection measure, that
an assessment should price — the work's fit
to a venue's shape, the fashion of its
field, the cost of checking it — and how
would an assessment tell the three apart
before its own reading? The question stands
beside Q113, on where an obvious defect ends
and a judgment of correctness begins: a gate
that refuses on obvious defects, as Hexagon
does, is pricing rejections of its own.
(At 37.1.1.)
# Standoff references — Fringe F001 (Chapter 37)

In reading order:

1.3.129 — chance and probability. The ground
of the fairness claim and of the urn
(1.2.292, 1.2.293): a fair draw is a claim
about what comes next, priced by the rule
this foundation states.

1.3.85 — state machine: the ground of the
three machines drawn from this chapter at
1.5.2.

Sources: the paper itself — "Cardchoose:
simple, fast sampling without replacement",
Paul Crowley, 14 November 2026, at
ciphergoth.org (www.ciphergoth.org/
sansreplace/cardchoose.pdf), read entire
2026-11-15; the seal of the skim, committed
before the read at ~/workspace/thirst-quest/
fringe-seals.jsonl (commit 610a5b3); and the
