# Paper 024 — synthesis: a judge whose certificate does not hold [synthesis]

[synthesis] Cluster O was founded on judges that work: a
rule run on logged data, priced by what another rule would
have earned, with a correction that takes out the judge's
own echo. This paper is the cluster's first priced failure
of a judge in daily use — and the failure's shape is the
one the cluster's own founding should have predicted. The
own-observation effect (1.2.112) bends a grade because each
point's echo stands in its own prediction; here the bend
runs through the calibration set itself: every p-value is
read off the same draw D, so the judge's inputs move
together, and the guarantee — proved where inputs are
independent or positively dependent — fails by exactly the
covariance the shared draw creates. The identity at
1.2.332 is the own-observation effect grown up: not a bias
to subtract but a covariance to price.

[synthesis] The two mechanisms are the book's two kinds of
second sight. Curvature (1.2.334) is a failure with no
villain: symmetric fluctuations, a bent function, an excess
nobody chose. The decoy switch (1.2.335) is a failure with
an author: a block placed where the fluctuation is selected
for. That the same broken guarantee exhibits both — the
honest bend at second order, the engineered kink at first —
is why the paper prices them apart, and why this book files
them apart.

[synthesis] For the house itself, one line is worth
carrying: the paper's practical wisdom is that a guarantee
is a claim about a procedure's inputs, not its intentions —
BH did not change when the weights arrived; its premises
did. The tracker prices its own skim the same way: the
seal's premises are title and abstract, and its misses
(41.1.5) are the covariance between what an abstract shows
and what a reading finds, collected one paper at a time.
# Paper 024 — Open questions Q127-Q129

Q127 — the constants. The proved upper constant of the
excess bound (1.2.336) is 1.612; the best lower constant
found (1.2.338) is c(q), 0.054 at q = 0.1 — a factor of
about thirty apart. Of that factor, at most about 3.1 is
lost inside the calibration-conditional reduction (about
1.64 in the Chernoff tails, at most about 1.9 in the union
over grid points), and the reduction's own constant is
numerically at least about 9.6 times c(q). So either the
true constant stands well above c(q), or closing the gap
needs an upper bound that does not pass through E[M_D]
(1.2.333). Which? (Opened by Paper 024, section 7.)

Q128 — finite n. The lower half of the rate lives in the
Poisson-limit model (1.2.337); its convergence from the
finite-n FDR is proved only for the global null (Lemma 44
of the paper), not for the non-null structures, and the
fixed-distribution theorem (1.2.339) gives no explicit n_0.
An explicit n_0, and the convergence with a rate, would
carry the rate to large finite n at fixed kappa, as Lemma
44 does in the global null. (Opened by Paper 024,
section 7.)

Q129 — the global-null constant. Three pieces stand open
together: the positive-curvature condition of Appendix D
(verified in exact arithmetic for many (m, q), not proved),
on which Proposition 19's conditional sharpness rests;
Conjecture 20, that the linear-regime constant of the
global-null price (1.2.340) equals (1/2)(1-q) over all
configurations; and an m-free bound on beta_m(rho), which
would make Theorem 17's upper bound linear in m and meet
Theorem 16's lower bound in order. (Opened by Paper 024,
sections 5 and 7.)
