# Paper 025 — The Chabauty-Kim dimension inequality via
noncommutative Iwasawa theory (Zehavi)

Source: arXiv:2611.18218 [math.NT], submitted 13 Nov
2026; read in full (the HTML form) 2026-11-17. One
author: Sa'ar Zehavi, of the Max Planck Institute for
Mathematics. Posted by Will in the MOAT chat as a bare
link, 2026-11-17, and taken in as a paper on the
standing direction of that chat.

THE TEST. The paper counts in the test of the skim as a
paper posted by Will — the third of the four of that
kind, the sixth of the twelve of the test — and no seal
of the skim stands for it: the paper was posted into
the conversation and read there, and the delta stands
on the reading alone, on the precedent of Paper 022
(the delta speaks once, at 42.1.5). The Index the
filing gives is entered in the ledger as every paper's
is.

THE PAPER. Kim's conjecture for curves, open since
2009 outside special cases, is proved for smooth
projective geometrically connected curves over Q of
genus at least 2: the dimension inequality holds for
all sufficiently large n, at any odd prime of good
reduction, with no condition on the Jacobian's
endomorphism ring or on the Galois image, and neither
of the conjectures Kim's earlier deduction assumed.
The engine is a bound on second Galois cohomology,
proved for every representation of the ambient
generalized symplectic group at once, through
noncommutative Iwasawa theory. The paper carries an AI
declaration (its section 1, after the
acknowledgements), recorded here as part of the
record: the general strategy is the author's, or
perhaps Coates and Kim's; the reduction of H^2
dimensions to kernels of Iwasawa operators was carried
out independently by the author about a year before
this draft; the development of the nontrivial kernel
bound and its application to the power-saving bound
was carried out through a lengthy back-and-forth
interaction with GPT 6 Astra (OpenAI, Ultra mode),
after which a dense shorter draft was generated with
Astra's help; and this draft was later written by the
author without any use of LLMs, including without
consulting the older version, except perhaps for
discussions of standard ambient theory and the
gathering of references. The tracker files the paper's
claims as the author's, attributed, as it files every
claim.

THE DELTA. Sixteen concepts, founding cluster AI
(1.2.344-1.2.359): the dimension inequality, proved in
the projective case (1.2.344); the kernel bound on
second Galois cohomology (1.2.345); the power-saving
bound (1.2.346); the reduction of H^2 to kernels of
Iwasawa operators (1.2.347); the uniform splitting
(1.2.348); the torsion Iwasawa module (1.2.349);
noncommutative Weierstrass preparation and the bad
weights (1.2.350); the central reduction, faithful
(1.2.351); the Hilbert-series bound (1.2.352); Filip's
character formula (1.2.353); the multiplicity bound
(1.2.354); the Casimir second-moment estimate
(1.2.355); the growth of the graded pieces (1.2.356);
the de Rham lower bound (1.2.357); the conjugation
eigenspace (1.2.358); the ambient-group generality
(1.2.359). Twelve foundations (1.3.291-1.3.302) and
one defined word coined (1.3.303). No held concept
revised; no held scroll stated again. FOUR state
machines drawn — three stated in the paper, one
inferred by the house (42.1.2, 42.1.3; the area at
1.5.2). Four questions opened, Q130-Q133 (42.2.2).
The author check stands at 42.1.6.
# Paper 025 — the formal core: the setting, the tower,
and the reduction to kernels

THE SETTING (section 1 of the paper; concepts 1.2.344,
1.2.359). C over Q is smooth, projective, geometrically
connected, of genus g at least 2, with a rational
basepoint b; p is an odd prime of good reduction; Sigma
contains p, infinity, and the primes of bad reduction.
Kim's diagram sends the S-integral points of the model
of C into the p-adic points, and below, the global
Selmer scheme H_f^1(G_{Q,Sigma}, U_n^et) by localization
at p into H_f^1(G_{Q_p}, U_n^et), which the Bloch-Kato
logarithm identifies with U_n^dR / F^0. The dimension
condition (2) is dim H_f^1 < dim U_n^dR / F^0. When it
holds, a nonzero algebraic function on U_n^dR / F^0
vanishing on the image of the Selmer side pulls back by
Kim's period map to a Coleman function on each residue
disk, whose zeros are finite — Kim's finiteness
argument, reproduced in the paper from Kim's section 3.
The same section gives the reduction the whole paper
serves: with L = gr Lie(U^et), the Selmer side is at
most the sum over k up to n of dim H^1(G_{Q,Sigma}, L_k)
(the closed embedding of H_f^1 into H^1 and induction
along the descending central series), and the Euler
characteristic formula (Milne, cited) prices each
dim H^1(L_k) as dim H^2(L_k) + dim L_k^-, the H^0
vanishing by purity of weight. Everything therefore
rests on a power-saving bound for dim H^2(G_{Q,Sigma},
L_n), and on the two side estimates (1.2.357, 1.2.358).

THE AMBIENT SETTING (sections 1-2; concepts 1.2.345,
1.2.348). For the H^2 bound the paper works with a
principally polarized abelian variety A over Q of
dimension g, good reduction outside S (in the
application, the Jacobian of C). Its Tate module
V = T_p A tensored with Q_p carries the Weil pairing,
so the Galois representation factors through
GSp(V, omega) = GSp_{2g}(Q_p). The irreducible
representations V_{lambda,s} of GSp_{2g} — lambda a
highest weight for Sp_{2g}, s the central weight — are
the coefficients of Theorem 1.1. The tower
K_a = Q(A[p^a]), K_infinity the union, F = K_c for the
fixed large c of the uniform splitting (1.2.348), has
Gamma = Gal(K_infinity / F) = Z_c x H_c, and over it
the Iwasawa algebras Omega = Z_p[[Gamma]] and
Lambda = Omega[1/p], noetherian domains (Lim, cited).

THE MODULE AND THE REDUCTION (section 2; concepts
1.2.349, 1.2.347). The Iwasawa cohomology module
cal X = H^2_Iw(K_infinity / F; Z_p(1)) is finitely
generated (Proposition 2.14) and torsion (Proposition
2.17, by Lim's criterion and the Brauer-group
vanishing). Its rational form X, through the duality
of Theorem 2.18 and the Hochschild-Serre step of
Corollary 2.19, turns every dim H^2(G_{Q,Sigma}, W)
into a space of Gamma-maps out of X, and the choice of
generators with annihilators delta_i embeds that space
into the sum of the kernels ker delta_i on W (display
(13) of the paper). The trivial bound of Theorem 1.1 —
dim H^2 at most a constant times d_lambda, for every
weight — is Proposition 2.20, read off the embedding.

STATE MACHINES IN THIS PART. The Iwasawa tower: states
are the finite layers E between F and K_infinity; the
moves are corestriction maps from each layer's H^2 to
the layer below; the machine's rest state is the
inverse limit, the module cal X, in which every layer's
cohomology stands at once. Stated in the paper (its
section 2.2, the definition of Iwasawa cohomology).
The descending central series tower: states are the
truncations U_n = U / U^{n+1} of the pro-unipotent
fundamental group, for U^et and U^dR alike; the moves
are the quotient maps U_{n+1} to U_n and the maps they
induce on Selmer schemes and de Rham quotients; the
dimension inequality is a property the machine acquires
at some state n and keeps at every later state. Gap:
the paper gives no effective state at which the
acquisition happens — no n_0 is computed, and the gap
is filed as Q130. Stated in the paper (its Definition
1.2 and section 1.1).
# Paper 025 — the formal core: the kernel bound, the
character theory, and the assembly

THE NONTRIVIAL BOUND (sections 2.3 and 3; concepts
1.2.350, 1.2.351, 1.2.352). Theorem 3.1 prices
ker eta on V_{lambda,s} at (1 + ||lambda||)^{D-g-1} /
d_lambda, for eta prepared by the noncommutative
Weierstrass preparation (1.2.350) so that eta(t) is
nonzero at every specialization; off the finite
bad-weight set E, ker delta = ker eta. The proof runs
in five steps (the paper's section 1.2.1): (I) the
preparation itself; (II) the Lazard comparison and the
central reduction, injective by affinoid Verma
faithfulness, so eta_{lambda,s} is nonzero (1.2.351);
(III) clearing p-powers, the reduction of theta lies in
filtration degree at most d, independent of (lambda, s)
(Lemma 3.28); (IV) Jacobson density turns the kernel's
dimension, times d_lambda, into a difference of
filtration pieces of R-bar_lambda (Proposition 3.29);
(V) the Hilbert series of the graded ring, of Krull
dimension D - g, prices each piece at
(1 + ||lambda||)^{D-g-1} (1.2.352). Section 3.3
assembles Theorem 1.1: E the union of the E(delta_i),
finite; for s outside E both bounds hold.

THE CHARACTER THEORY (section 4.1; concepts 1.2.353,
1.2.354, 1.2.355, 1.2.356). L is the free Lie algebra
on V modulo the one quadratic relation dual to the
cup product (Corwin-Zehavi, cited), so L_n is pure of
weight n and splits into the V_{lambda,n} with
multiplicities m_n(lambda). Filip's character formula
(1.2.353) gives chi_n on USp(2g) from chi_V; from it,
the multiplicity bound (1.2.354) by orthogonality and
the first-moment estimate, the Casimir second moment
(1.2.355), and the growth b_n asymptotic to alpha^n / n
with alpha = g + sqrt(g^2 - 1) (1.2.356). The proof of
Theorem 1.4 splits the sum over lambda at
||lambda|| = R sqrt(n): below the split, Theorem 1.1's
nontrivial bound with the multiplicities costs
b_n R^{D-1} / sqrt(n); above it, the trivial bound with
Rankin's trick on the second moment costs b_n R^{-2};
at R = n^{1/(2(D+1))} the two meet at
b_n n^{-1/(D+1)}.

THE ASSEMBLY (section 4.2; concepts 1.2.357, 1.2.358,
1.2.344). The de Rham side is at least the sum of the
b_k less g^n (1.2.357); the Selmer side, by the Euler
characteristic formula, is the sum of dim H^2(L_k) plus
the sum of dim L_k^-, the second of these the half-sum
(1/2) sum b_k to main order (1.2.358). Since
alpha > g, the de Rham side's main term is the full
sum; the Selmer side's is the half-sum plus a term the
power saving makes negligible; so the inequality (2)
holds for all large n — Corollary 1.5, Kim's conjecture
in the projective case, and with it the finiteness of
the Chabauty-Kim loci and Mordell over Q through
Chabauty-Kim.

STATE MACHINES IN THIS PART. The weight specialization:
states are the weights s in Z; at each state the central
coordinate specializes to t_s = (1 + p^c)^s - 1 and the
operator eta acts as eta(t_s) in Z_p[[H_c]]; the finite
set E of bad weights is the set of states at which
ker delta and ker eta part company, and outside E the
machine's reading is the kernel bound. Stated in the
paper (its Corollary 2.22 and section 3.3). The
two-bound balance: states are the split radii R; below
R sqrt(n) the nontrivial bound prices the pieces, above
it the trivial bound and the second moment price the
tail; the machine rests where the two costs meet, at
R = n^{1/(2(D+1))}, and the resting cost is the
exponent of Theorem 1.4. The paper runs the balance
twice (its sections 1.2.2 and 4.1) without drawing it
as a machine; the drawing is the house's. [inferred]
# Paper 025 — the results map

Each claim of the paper, in its standing here. Attributed
to Paper 025 unless a check is named. No claim of this
paper was recomputed or re-proved by the house; the paper
contains no computation to recompute (42.1.5).

- Kim's conjecture holds in the smooth projective
  geometrically connected case over Q, genus at least 2,
  rational basepoint, any odd prime of good reduction:
  the dimension inequality for all sufficiently large n;
  the Chabauty-Kim loci finite at all sufficiently large
  levels; Mordell over Q recovered through Chabauty-Kim
  (Corollary 1.5; the abstract). [Unknown — attributed]
