  Dan-Cohen-Kim-Wewers, cited in the paper's footnote
  3); an effective n_0, an explicit E, or any effective
  constant; the case p = 2; the quasi-projective case of
  Kim's conjecture. These stand open at 42.2.2 as
  Q130-Q132, with the paper's own textual discrepancy
  at Q133.
# Paper 025 — the delta: what this adds, and what was
checked

THE FILING. Sixteen new canonical concepts
(1.2.344-1.2.359), founding cluster AI — the paper's
centre of gravity is arithmetic geometry, ground the
model has never held: no held cluster holds a curve, a
Galois group, or a cohomology bound, and the sixteen
are filed together as a new country, on the precedent
of the annexes (clusters E and N) and of cluster AA.
Twelve foundations (1.3.291-1.3.302) and one defined
word (1.3.303). No held canonical scroll revised; no
held scroll stated again. The axis judgment, written
here at filing, under the axis rule of the protocol:
NO axis — (a) is recorded against the judgment
plainly: the paper's centre of gravity IS a field for
which the model holds no slot, and that ground alone
would argue an axis. The second and third grounds
decide it the other way. (b) No held concept is merely
re-read to receive the paper, and none must be
re-measured: the quantities the inequality compares
are dimensions, bounds, kernels and growth rates of
held kinds of space, and the new concepts are built
from those kinds on new objects. (c) Its bridges to
held ground are analogies of shape, not shared
machinery — the symplectic form beside cluster E's
area rule, the fundamental group in another category,
dimension beside cluster O's judging quantities — and
nothing the book already runs is an input to this
proof, nor does this proof re-price any held claim. A
cluster founding is not an axis, and a new territory
slotted by a founding is the model's ordinary way of
growing; if a later paper makes held quantities be
re-measured in arithmetic terms, the axis question
reopens at that filing.

THE FILING RULES AT WORK (the protocol's five).
Instrument rule: Filip's character formula (1.2.353)
and the reduction to kernels (1.2.347) each stand as
concepts — the formula is used at two distinct points
of the argument (the multiplicity bound and the
conjugation eigenspace), and the reduction at every
point after its section. Split rule: the two side
estimates are filed apart (1.2.357, 1.2.358) because
the paper prices them apart — different sides of the
inequality, different inputs, different sections;
Theorem 1.1 and Theorem 1.4 are filed apart (1.2.345,
1.2.346) because they price different objects — a
representation of GSp against a graded piece of a Lie
algebra — by different machinery. Grain rule: the
uniform splitting (1.2.348) and the conjugation
eigenspace (1.2.358) stand as concepts though the
abstract subsumes both: each is a stated result the
proof loads on. Revision rule: no revision is counted
— the tracker held no Chabauty-Kim, Iwasawa or Mordell
claim, and the held judges and geometers stand beside
this paper's claims, not under them. Folds recorded:
the Hochschild-Serre step of Corollary 2.19 folds into
the reduction (1.2.347); the trivial bound of
Proposition 2.20 folds into Theorem 1.1 (1.2.345); the
finite generation and torsionness of Propositions
2.14 and 2.17, with Lim's criterion, fold into the
module (1.2.349); Lemma 3.28's uniform degree, the
Jacobson density step and Claim 3.32 fold into the
Hilbert-series bound (1.2.352); the first-moment
estimate folds into the multiplicity bound (1.2.354);
the Casimir eigenvalue formula folds into the second
moment (1.2.355); the locus finiteness and the Mordell
recovery fold into the headline concept (1.2.344).

LINKS. Twenty links are counted in this filing:
seventeen inside the new ground (the inequality to the
kernel bound and to the power saving; the inequality
to each side estimate; the bound to the reduction and
to the Hilbert series; the reduction to the module;
the splitting to the module and to the preparation's
specialization; the preparation to the central
reduction; the central reduction to the Hilbert
series; the power saving to the multiplicity bound and
to the second moment; the character formula to the
multiplicity bound and to the eigenspace; the growth
to the de Rham bound; the generality to the kernel
bound, whose route it is) and three across to held
ground, each priced as shape or kind, none as shared
