symplectic ground (the area rule's group, with a
similitude factor; the annex's topology is not used);
the pro-unipotent fundamental group to the held
fundamental group (foundation 1.3.12 — the same name in
another category, its loops replaced by Galois
symmetries); and the dimension inequality to cluster
O's judging quantities (two dimensions compared, the
verdict finiteness — an exact comparison, not an
estimate, and no held judge revised by it).

WHAT WAS CHECKED. Nothing of this paper was recomputed
or re-proved by the house, and the delta says so
plainly: the paper contains no computation to recompute
— no worked example, no computed case, no number its
claims rest on — and the house does not hold the
machinery to re-prove a Chabauty-Kim argument. Its
proofs were read for structure, in full; every claim
stands attributed to the paper, as filed. Where the
proof's load-bearing steps rest on cited prior work,
the filing names it at the step: the whole Chabauty-Kim
setup, the reduction to H^2, and the F^0 bound are
Kim's [20]; the strategy of the kernel bound is
Coates-Kim's [9]; the Iwasawa formalism and the torsion
criterion are Lim's [24] and [25]; the Harish-Chandra
computation, the Lazard comparison and the Verma
faithfulness are Ardakov-Wadsley's [1] and [3]; the
two Galois-image facts are Bogomolov's [7] and Pink's
[32]; the character formula is Filip's [15]; the
quadratic presentation of L and Olsson's comparison
come through the author's own Corwin-Zehavi [11]; the
Euler characteristic formula and the finiteness of H^2
with finite coefficients are Milne's. The reading did
check the paper against itself, and found one
discrepancy: Theorem 1.4's exponent is printed
-1/(2(D+1)) at its restatement in section 4.1, against
-1/(D+1) in the statement of section 1, in the summary
of section 1.2.2, and in both computations of the
balance; the filing takes the proved exponent and the
discrepancy stands open as Q133. A smaller slip, the
error term of Proposition 4.16 written in the proof's
variable, is noted inside its concept (1.2.358); its
content is unambiguous and no question is opened on it.

STATE MACHINES. Four drawn in this chapter: the
Iwasawa tower (42.1.2), the descending central series
tower (42.1.2), and the weight specialization (42.1.3),
all stated in the paper; and the two-bound balance
(42.1.3), inferred — the paper runs the balance twice
without drawing it as a machine. Tracker totals after
this filing stand at 1.5.2.

QUESTIONS. Four opened — Q130 (effectivity: an
explicit bad-weight set and an effective n_0), Q131
(the prime 2), Q132 (the quasi-projective case of
Kim's conjecture), Q133 (the exponent discrepancy) —
at 42.2.2. None closed.

