stands against the name anywhere the searches
reached; a same-name undergraduate profile at
another university is a different person and was
not conflated. No tie found.

LEG THREE — Will's own archive. Will's Twitter
archive was searched for the author's name and
mailbox: no occurrence of Doan, Lam Doan or ldoan5
anywhere in it.

VERDICT. No evidence found on any leg: no recorded
tie between Lam Doan and phext or Will's work, and
no use of Will's published work in the paper, named
or unnamed. The check is clean — the thirty-fifth
of the register, and the third in a row to close
with no follow-up owed.

# Paper 027 — synthesis: the cone is known by its
resolution [synthesis]

[synthesis] Every paper so far has priced its
object directly. This one never touches its object
at all. The cone is singular at its vertex and its
link sits at infinity; the proof learns both by
building a second object — the self-expander, the
cone smoothed by its own flow — and reading the
first object off the second: the link is a level
set of the expander's squared distance, the space
of links is retracted by the expander's sections.
Existence says the resolution is always there;
uniqueness, bought by smallness of entropy, says it
is canonical; and canonicity is what turns a
construction into a definition — the retraction of
Theorem 1.2 is only a map because there is THE
expander of a link, not an expander. The book has
met this shape before in other clothes: Paper 025
priced a curve by a Galois group built to resolve
it, and Paper 024's certificate failed exactly
where its resolution stopped being canonical. The
pattern the synthesis names: a singular or distant
object becomes tractable when a canonical smooth
resolution exists, and the price of the whole
method is the smallness that buys the canonicity —
here, entropy within epsilon of its floor. What the
model gains is a country, not a tool: cluster AJ is
the book's first extrinsic geometry, and its
bridges to held ground are words — entropy,
curvature, minimal — each naming a different
quantity on the new ground than on the old. The
founding records those calls as shape, so that a
later paper which makes one of them machinery will
be visible as the event it is.

# Paper 027 — Open questions Q137-Q140

Q137 — diffeomorphic, or only topological? Theorem
1.1's statement concludes that the link is
diffeomorphic to S^{n-1}; the abstract, section 3's
own statement of the result, and its conclusion
after Step 3 all say the link is a topological
sphere. The proof's last step applies the Morse
lemma to the smooth squared-distance function on
the expander, whose regular level sets are smooth
spheres — which reads as the stronger conclusion —
but the paper does not remark the difference. Which
does the argument as written deliver, and does any
step (the reading of the link off at infinity, say)
cost the smoothness the level sets have? (Opened
by the filing's own reading of the paper; the
precedent is Q133, Paper 025's unproven direction.)

Q138 — is the retraction a deformation retraction?
The paper's closing remark: the homotopy Psi(·, t)
is built from sphere-sections of the expander, and
nothing in the argument shows the intermediate
sections are links of low-entropy cones — Psi(·, t)
may leave L(epsilon) for 0 < t < 1. If it does not
leave, (L(epsilon), E) is a deformation-retract
pair and the homotopy type of the link space is
RP^n's outright. Does the section of a low-entropy
cone's expander at finite radius always arise as
the link of a cone of entropy below 1 + epsilon —
and if not, what is the first obstruction?

Q139 — is epsilon(n) effective? The existence of
epsilon(n) is proved by contradiction and
compactness (Step 1), so the paper computes no
value and no rate. Can the argument be made
effective — an explicit epsilon(n), or an explicit
rate at which the curvature bound kappa follows the
entropy excess — and if the compactness is
essential, is there a cone sequence showing no
effective bound of the natural form exists?

Q140 — higher codimension. The entropy is defined
for submanifolds in R^{n+m}, and cones, links and
expanders all make sense there, but every theorem
of the paper is codimension one: White regularity,
the Bernstein-Wang theory and the expander equation
are spent in their hypersurface forms. Does a
small-entropy rigidity hold for higher-codimension
cones — the link forced to be a sphere, the link
space retracting — and which step of the present
proof is the first that fails off codimension one?

