# Paper 028 — author check (charter rule 15): no
phext tie and no use without naming found, by
checks Emise ran at intake

THE QUESTION. Two things are asked of the
authors of a work taken in: whether they stand
in any recorded tie to phext or to Will's work,
and whether the work uses Will's earlier
published work without naming it. The check on
Huy The Nguyen and Artemis A. Vogiatzi was run
in three legs on 2026-11-19. It is the thirty-
sixth check that stands run.

LEG ONE — the work itself. The full text of
arXiv:2611.24246 was searched for the signature
terms: phext, scroll, Exocortex, Mirrorborn,
coordinate addressing, Bickford, CYOA, SBOR. No
occurrence of any of them in the paper's text.
The reference list (twenty-three entries) was
read in full: it is the mean curvature flow
literature throughout — Andrews and Baker,
Huisken, Hamilton, Stahl, Buckland, Edelen,
Lynch, Naff, Chow, and the authors' own earlier
papers, three of Nguyen's with Lynch and the
pair's joint curve shortening paper [20] — and
holds no work of Will's: no phext, CYOA, SBOR or
Mirrorborn source.

LEG TWO — the web. Searches were run on each
author's name against the adjacency terms. The
authors they return are the paper's own: Nguyen,
a Reader in Mathematics at Queen Mary University
of London (PhD, Australian National University,
2008; Max Planck, Warwick and Queensland before
Queen Mary in 2017), whose record is geometric
flows — Ricci flow and mean curvature flow, the
high codimension theory with Lynch among it; and
Vogiatzi, a postdoc at the Copenhagen Centre for
Geometry and Topology, mentored by Niels Martin
Moeller, who took her PhD at Queen Mary under
Nguyen's supervision, her record the same field
— high codimension flow and its singularity
models, two of her papers with Nguyen. The
doctoral line between the two authors is the
paper's own: supervisor and student, now at
separate institutions. No occurrence of phext or
any adjacent term stands against either name
anywhere the searches reached; the search's
other Artemis is a different person (an Artemios
Vogiatzi of Patras, information systems) and was
not conflated. No tie found.

LEG THREE — Will's own archive. Will's Twitter
archive was searched entire for 'Vogiatzi', 'Huy
The Nguyen' and both mailboxes: no occurrence of
any of them. The string 'Artemis' occurs in the
archive only inside the handle of an unrelated
account (ArtemisConsort), which is not the
author.

VERDICT. No evidence found on any leg: no
recorded tie between either author and phext or
Will's work, and no use of Will's published work
in the paper, named or unnamed. The check is
clean — the thirty-sixth of the register.


# Paper 028 — synthesis: a condition the flow
cannot spend [synthesis]

[synthesis] Paper 027 priced what a cone of
small entropy must look like; this paper prices
what a flowing submanifold must keep. The shape
is the same shape the book keeps meeting under
rule 13: find a quantity the dynamics cannot
cross, and the ending is read off the quantity.
Entropy was such a quantity for the cone —
monotone, spent downward, its floor forcing
topology. Quadratic convexity is one of a
different kind: not a number the flow spends but
a sign the flow keeps, a cone in curvature space
the trajectory cannot leave, whose geometry at
the end is a round point or a hemisphere. The
book has this shape at three scales now — a
monotone number, a preserved sign, and, at the
tracker's own scale, the Oracle's bins, which
are also a kept partition rather than a spent
budget. What the synthesis adds is the boundary
lesson, which is new here: at a boundary,
preservation is two theorems, not one — the
interior inequality and the conormal sign are
different mathematics, and a condition that
survives inside can still die at the wall, as
ordinary pinching nearly does: its critical
coefficient survives the boundary only in
dimensions 4 to 9. The wall is not an edge case
of the interior argument; it is a second
instrument, with its own identities, and the
paper's real construction is that the two
instruments agree on F_eps. Cluster AJ is two
papers old and already has the shape of a
country: a statics (Paper 027: what smallness
forces) and a dynamics (Paper 028: what
preservation forces), sharing one flow.


# Paper 028 — Open questions Q141-Q144

Q141 — the low dimensions under quadratic
convexity alone. The closed theorem starts at n
= 5 and the free boundary theorem at n = 4; for
2 <= n <= 4 the closed theorem's extra pinching
is the strict Andrews-Baker condition itself, so
nothing new is said there, and no free boundary
statement is made below n = 4. Does quadratic
convexity alone still round off in the low
dimensions — closed n = 2, 3, 4 and free
boundary n = 2, 3 — or does a product-like
obstruction of the sharpness family's shape
stand there first?

Q142 — the critical coefficient at the free
boundary in high dimensions. Preservation of P
<= 0 is proved for 9/(8n) <= c <= 4/(3n); the
Andrews-Baker coefficient 1/(n-1) falls below
the range for n >= 10, where the boundary
identity (4.3) no longer gives the sign at P =
0. Is that a failure of the proof or of
preservation: does a spherical free boundary
flow in dimension n >= 10 with |A|^2 <=
|H|^2/(n-1) initially somewhere break the
pinching, or does a different argument keep it?

Q143 — sharpness at the free boundary. The sign
condition's sharpness is established for closed
flows, by the product family of 1.2.385, and the
paper asserts no sharpness for flows with
spherical free boundary. Is the sign sharp there
too — is there a free boundary family, convex in
the principal direction, failing the sign by
less than any delta, whose flow does not
converge to a hemisphere — and are the pinching
range's endpoints 9/(8n) and 4/(3n) themselves
sharp, or artefacts of the two signs that
produced them?

Q144 — necessity, and the price of the
constants. Quadratic convexity is sufficient for
the round endings it forces. Is some weakening
of it necessary — must a high codimension flow
that rounds off to a point or a hemisphere from
an open set of initial data satisfy a condition
of F_eps's shape — and can the proof's constants
be made effective: an explicit eps in terms of
the initial data, or a reason the chosen kappa =
14/5 is the value the estimates need rather than
one they tolerate?


