The piece: Tommy He, 'tufazip: Learning to
Compress a Billion Bytes of Wikipedia', Tufa
Labs research post, 2026-10-14.
tufalabs.ai/research/tufazip/ — read in full
2026-11-20. The artifact: the tufazip
repository, public code, linked from the
post. The benchmark: Matt Mahoney's Large
Text Compression Benchmark,
mattmahoney.net/dc/text.html — its table,
updated 2026-11-19, read by Emise 2026-11-20;
row 13 lists tufazip v2 at payload 100,958,324
plus decompresser 4,430,029, total
105,388,353 — the receipt under the verified
leg of 1.2.456.

Frame and ancestry as the piece cites them:
Legg & Hutter (2007), a measure of machine
intelligence; Chollet (2019), on the measure
of intelligence; Kumar, Clune, Lehman &
Stanley (2025), the Free Energy Ranking
hypothesis — the piece's lineage for
compression as a measure. Mahoney (2009),
'Rationale for a Large Text Compression
Benchmark'. Delétang et al. (2023), 'Language
Modeling Is Compression'. Huang et al.
(2024), compression as understanding.
Manheim & Garrabrant (2018), categorizing
variants of Goodhart's Law — the rider's
source. Tao (2026), on exploring space,
cited for the search over configurations.
Turing (1950), for the test the framing
argues from. The construction's named
ancestors: Bellard's NNCP, the neural
compressor the piece starts from ('Starting
from NNCP, I improved...'), and Knoll's cmix
(v21: 2,077 mixed predictors), the hybrid
contrast — the entries above tufazip on
today's table are hybrids of that kind.

Foundations and words held standing, cited
where they stand: the token (1.3.36); the
state machine (1.3.85); prior and
conditioning (1.3.89) — the book's older,
Bayesian sense of prior, stood apart here
from this chapter's offline-trained prior:
the same word, two senses, each fixed at its
own coordinate. The Transformer machinery is
cluster J's, used as found; the piece's own
description of its model stands at 49.1.1.
