# Paper 032 — author check

Run 2026-11-20, the fortieth check of the
series. The piece names its author by its
address rather than a byline: the remark
hangs on the personal site of Levent Alpöge,
whose heading gives him as of Anthropic (PBC)
and a junior fellow of the Harvard Society of
Fellows. He is a working number theorist —
arithmetic statistics is his published
ground, his papers listed on the same site —
and the piece's thanks run to the field's
centre: Bhargava, Radziwill, Sarnak among
them. Claude, the piece's collaborator
throughout, is an AI system and not a
researcher entry; the novelty of this filing
is carried by Alpöge as the work's human
author of record, the piece's own framing of
the division of labour recorded at 50.1.1.

THE THREE LEGS. (1) The piece and its
artifact: the remark's text read in full — it
cites no bibliography, naming its ancestry
inside the prose (Stepanov, Weil, Deligne,
Katz, Chowla–Selberg, Siegel); the word phext
occurs nowhere in it. The artifact's 175,187
source files were searched entire: the single
character-match for 'phext' is the substring
inside the file name GraphExtend, which is
not the word; no scroll, no Exocortex, no
coordinate addressing, no CYOA, no SBOR, no
work of Will's appears in either text. The
construction the piece claims descends from
Stepanov's, in its own words. (2) The web:
searches joining the author's name to phext
returned his research record and the day's
reporting alone; nothing in his public
material connects him to phext or to Will.
(3) Will's Twitter archive, searched entire:
'Alpoge' and 'Alpöge' — neither occurs. No
follow, no reply, no mention, in either
direction.

VERDICT: NO EVIDENCE FOUND that the author
has engaged with phext or with Will's work,
and no use of Will's prior art without naming
— the artifact's ancestry is the classical
literature it names, and its machinery is
mathlib's, cited by revision in its own
lakefile. The standing of this scroll: the
checks were run, and their results are as
recorded.
