pdfTeX), SHA-256
e039d3f6cf0a4b14f03c7edb85097752dd65c0a2fe543c76fe992be234f97bf5
as fetched — posted the same day by Ravi
Vakil on his blog Math 216 (post of
2026-11-20 18:18:42 UTC), the file hosted on
the same site. Taken in as Paper 033 at
Will's direction in the MOAT chat, 2026-11-21
— outside the test of the skim fixed in
advance: the Will-posted stratum of that
sample closed at Paper 029, so no seal stands
for this paper and no score is computed. The
filing is made in the ordinary way; its axis
judgment stands written at filing in the
chapter (51.1.5), and its Novelty Index is
computed from the filed delta and entered in
the ledger, as every paper's is.

HOW IT WAS FOUND. Paper 032's chapter opened
the question Q166 — the second proof of the
same day — and its reception scroll (50.1.4)
recorded the pointers: a human-written draft
claiming the Generalized Riemann Hypothesis
by Alessandro Masullo, surfaced by Ravi Vakil
and privately vetted for months by Brian
Conrad, as reported by DataCamp. From those
pointers the house went to Vakil's own post,
read it entire (including its update, added
about eight hours later), and followed its
link to the draft itself. The draft was
fetched once, from the post's own address,
and read before anything was filed.

THE AUTHOR, AND THE ACCOUNT THE DRAFT GIVES
OF ITSELF. Masullo is an independent
mathematician based in Rome; he took his PhD
at Stanford in 2019 under Brian Conrad
(dissertation: Arithmetic Deformations of
Crystalline Cohomology, per the Mathematics
Genealogy Project). The acknowledgments of
the draft give its history in the author's
own words: the work began near the end of his
first year as a graduate student at Stanford
under Conrad, and took nearly twelve years to
assemble; arrangements were under way for a
year at Stanford with Conrad to polish the
writing, and in light of the current climate
he was advised to release the current version
as it is. Two sentences of that account
matter to the filing and are recorded exactly
as written: The math is complete, and only
filling in some writing remains on several
parts across this long paper; and, a few
lines later, The introduction itself is
unfinished, and Brian Conrad and I are aware
of several glitches here and there that I was
in the process of addressing. The
introduction carries, as its sections 1.7 and
1.8, excerpts from a letter to Peter Sarnak
sent in mid-October 2026 giving an overview
of all the methods; the acknowledgments
record a four-and-a-half-hour blackboard
discussion with Sarnak at the Institute for
Advanced Study on the Sunday before the
release.

THE SHAPE OF THE DOCUMENT. Fourteen chapters
and five appendices: adelic theta functions;
idelic zeta functions; periodic Schauder
bases on modulated groups; the spaces of
those functions, with three structure
theorems; local-global structures, with two
more; Schauder bases of idelic zeta
functions, polar multiplicities, nuclearity;
periodic orbits and fixed points;
Hardy-Sobolev topologies; the spectral side
of the trace formula; the shell structure;
the geometric side of the trace formula,
ending in the main theorem (1.2.466); a
one-page thirteenth chapter, Zeros of Hecke
L-functions; and complements —
multiplicities, the function-field case, the
higher-rank case. The extracted text runs to
about 4.1 million characters and 305 numbered
statements. The house's reading: the full
text extracted and read — the front matter,
acknowledgments, and introduction entire; the
method and trace-formula chapters at the
level of their statements throughout;
chapters 12 to 14 entire; the passages the
filing turns on read closely and quoted. The
house does not referee nine hundred pages of
machinery, and the chapter says plainly, at
51.1.3, what was checked and what was not.


# Paper 033 — the formal core: what the draft states, and the machines it runs

THE MAIN THEOREM, AS STATED. Theorem 12.5.1.1
(Trace Formula): for a number field F and a
test function on the idele class group
(1.3.347) vanishing on its norm-one part, the
geometric side of the trace — a sum over all
places of F, the real places contributing
(1/2) times a principal-value integral of the
test function weighted by |g| to the 1/2 over
|1 minus g|, the complex places the same with
1 over 2 pi, the finite places a sum weighted
by log(q_v) — equals the Mellin transform of
the test function at |.| to the 1/2 and at
|.| to the minus 1/2, minus a sum over the
zeros c of the Hecke L-function in the strip
around the line, each weighted by its
multiplicity N(c) and by the Mellin transform
at c. The theorem is filed as a concept
(1.2.466); the draft argues it in its twelfth
chapter out of the machinery of the eleven
before it.

THE ROUTE OF THE OVERVIEW TO THE ZEROS. The
introduction (its section 1.4) sketches how
the zeros' location was to follow. Take the
holomorphic family of one-sided test
functions, t to the minus z for t above 1 and
0 below, mollified and cut off (section
1.4.2); the finite-place sum then tends to
the logarithmic derivative of the finite
Hecke L-function. Section 1.4.3 asserts the
Archimedean integrals over this family are
uniformly bounded and applies Montel's
theorem to extract a holomorphic limit (the
assertion is refuted at 1.2.468). Section
1.4.5 draws the consequence: the logarithmic
derivative is holomorphic wherever the real
part exceeds 0, the point 1/2 excepted for
the principal character — so the L-function
has no zeros to the right of the line, and by
the functional equation none to the left.
This completes the overview of how to deduce
the main results, the draft writes. The
formal deduction was reserved to the
thirteenth chapter, whose main theorem is not
in the document (1.2.465).

THE RESTATED FORMULA AND THE CAP. Chapter 13
states the trace formula again as its Theorem
13.3.1.1 with one change that carries a
concept (1.2.469): the spectral sum counts
each zero not by its multiplicity N(c) but by
the capped count min of N(c) and [F : Q]. The
hypotheses of the restatement add a family of
vanishing conditions on the test function,
one for each derivative order up to [F : Q]
minus 1, at each character's central point.

MACHINE M1 — the trace-formula machine
[stated in the draft]. States: a smoothened
geometric kernel on the idele class group;
its trace computed along the diagonal in two
forms — GEOMETRIC (orbital integrals, place
by place) and SPECTRAL (the zeros, weighted)
— the machine's output being their identity.
Moves: smoothen the kernel along the
diagonal; differentiate in the sense of
Lebesgue along it; deconstruct the global
kernel into its S-local pieces; compute the
S-local trace; put the whole back together.
The gap named for the machine: its identity
is proved for test functions in a coarse
topology and vanishing on the norm-one part —
the machine's output speaks about the zeros
only through the test functions it can be
fed, and the feeding of the one family that
would locate the zeros is M2's business,
where the break is filed (1.2.468). Home:
here; concept 1.2.466.

MACHINE M2 — the step-by-step degeneration
[stated in the draft]. States: the global
convolution operator; the same operator after
k Euler factors have been removed, one place
at a time, by replacing the factor 1 at a
place outside S with the unit-circle factor;
and at the limit, the S-local operator of
Connes' calculation. Moves: one replacement
at a time, the loss of information accounted
at each step, against the earlier method's
single infinite iteration in a topology fine
enough to make the infinite product converge.
The gap named for the machine, in the framing
of the draft itself: the fine topology that
makes the limit easy also makes every zero's
point-mass continuous, and the machine's
output collapses to Weil's explicit formulas
— the whole design of the draft is to walk
the same path in a coarse topology and keep
the spectral side selective (1.2.467). And
the machine has a state it cannot pass, found
by the house's check: at a real Archimedean
place, as the cutoff approaches the identity,
the integrals the walk must carry diverge
(1.2.468). Home: here; concept 1.2.467.


# Paper 033 — the house's check record

THE FETCH. Vakil's announcement post was read
entire on its own page, its update included;
the draft it links was fetched once from the
post's own address (math216.wordpress.com,
the file the-music-of-the-primes.pdf),
6,756,996 bytes, SHA-256
e039d3f6cf0a4b14f03c7edb85097752dd65c0a2fe543c76fe992be234f97bf5;
its internal creation stamp reads 2026-11-20
18:13:46 UTC, five minutes before the post
that released it. The full text was extracted
(4,096,938 characters) and read as recorded
at 51.1.1.

CHECK ONE — THE COMPLETENESS CENSUS. The
thirteenth chapter of the draft, Zeros of
Hecke L-functions — the chapter its table of
contents assigns the main theorems on the
location of the zeros — was read entire. It
is one page. Its sections 13.1 (Recollections
on the Explicit Formulas) and 13.2
(Holomorphic families of test functions) are
headings with no body under them. Its section
13.3 states the trace formula again (Theorem
13.3.1.1) and then reads, verbatim: Upon
combining Theorem 13.3.1.1 and Theorem ??, we
obtain the following: Theorem 13.3.1.2
(Divisor of L(s, chi)). No statement follows.
The theorem's footnotes are the author's
working notes, verbatim: Correct statement.
Insert statement. State and prove the Main
Theorems, also explaining how to deduce them
by approximation as Re(s) exceeds 1. The
cross-reference to the second theorem is an
unresolved mark in the text. The census
around it: section 1.6 of the introduction
(How this work was conducted, and why) is
likewise headings without bodies; the
extracted text holds 17 unresolved ??
reference marks and 2 Finish! notes of the
author's left standing in section 1.4; a note
on the same page of the draft asking that the
statement and proof of the main theorem about
zeros be added was reported independently
(XenoSpectrum's account) and matches what the
house read. Against this census stands the
acknowledgments' sentence — the math is
complete, and only filling in some writing
remains. The house's finding: the missing
piece is not writing. A main theorem whose
statement is absent is not an exposition gap;
the deduction from the trace formula to the
zeros, which the chapter existed to carry, is
not in the document. This check grounds the
filing at 1.2.465.

CHECK TWO — THE BOUNDEDNESS COMPUTATION. The
house recomputed the load-bearing estimate of
the deduction of the overview. Section 1.4.3
asserts the Archimedean integrals over the
approximating family of the draft are bounded
above by a common constant A greater than 0
independent of z in K and independent of the
specific test function, and rests Montel's
theorem on it. Specialize as the critics did:
F = Q, the trivial character, the real place,
z = 2. The family of section 1.4.2 — bump
functions valued in [0, 1], mollified — is
nonnegative and converges pointwise to t to
the minus 2 for t in (1, 2). The real-place
integral is (1/2) times the integral over R
greater than 0 of f(t) t to the 1/2 over |1
minus t|, against dt over t; on (1, 2) the
integrand is f(t) t to the minus 1/2 over (t
minus 1). Fatou's lemma gives the liminf of
the integrals at least the integral of the
limit, t to the minus 5/2 over (t minus 1) on
(1, 2); that integral diverges, since t to
the minus 5/2 is at least 2 to the minus 5/2
throughout the interval and the integral of 1
over (t minus 1) diverges at 1. The integrals
are therefore unbounded along the family: no
common A exists, and the assertion as stated
is false. The computation is elementary and
the house stands on its own running of it; it
agrees with the two counterarguments
published on the day of the release (Nielsen;
Zeraoulia — 51.1.4), which were arrived at
independently. This check grounds the filing
at 1.2.468. Its blast radius is stated there:
the deduction route of the overview falls;
the trace formula (1.2.466) is not touched by
it.

WHAT WAS NOT CHECKED. The proof of the trace
formula itself — some five hundred pages of
machinery under Theorem 12.5.1.1 — was read
at the level of its statements and its
architecture and not verified; it stands
attributed (1.2.466), and whether it survives
expert checking is Q170. Nielsen's second
counterexample (a norm-preimage
identification in the divisorial strata
discussion) was read as reported and not
verified (Q168). The further proposition a
reader's video account reports as false as
stated was not verified (Q169). One point in
favour of the draft, recorded because the
record is the record: where the draft argues
conditionally it says so — its Proposition
14.1.2.3 assumes the Grand Riemann
Hypothesis, the Grand Simplicity Hypothesis,
and Global Langlands in its statement, and
draws only the consistency conclusion those
assumptions buy. The over-claim is located at
the completeness claim and at the one bound,
not spread through the book.

THE SCROLL'S OWN STANDING. This record's
claims are the checks themselves, each with
its receipt named above; they stand verified
as run. The claims they price stand at
1.2.465 and 1.2.468, refuted on the grounds
given; the rest of the chapter files at the
bases its scrolls state.


# Paper 033 — the release, and the day it landed on

THE RELEASE. Vakil's post (Math 216,
2026-11-20 18:18:42 UTC) is short and is
recorded in substance: he writes from the
American Mathematical Society's Executive
Committee and Board of Trustees meeting,
about to chair an hours-long meeting, because
this is urgent. Alessandro Maria Masullo has
a proposed proof of GRH, and Brian Conrad has
been looking closely at it for months and
gave extensive feedback which is being
incorporated, and it is so far looking good
to him. It is all human, with no AI. The hope
was to have Brian work with Alessandro for a
year to polish the writing and then announce
it after running it by enough people; now it
seems best to put out the current draft. I
hope people quickly check it, formalize, fill
any small gaps if they occur. I have great
faith in Brian's judgement. Here it is — and
the file of the draft follows. About eight
hours later the same post gained an update:
Several people have contacted me to report
problems in the proposed proof, identified
with the assistance of different AI models.
As is usual, all issues need to be resolved
to the satisfaction of the community before
this proof would be accepted as true. (I make
no claim as to the correctness of the proof
strategy.)

THE VETTER'S POSITION, AS REPORTED. Conrad's
months of close reading and extensive
feedback stand on Vakil's word and on the
acknowledgments of the draft itself. His
public position, as reported the same day
(Scientific American; Science): judging
whether the proof holds will require more
final revision — the paper is undergoing
major revision — and the choice to release
was between two options, neither of them
good, silence setting the other claim's
narrative if nothing was said. Science's
account adds, on Conrad's authority, that an
earlier attempt by Masullo several years ago
had failed to withstand scrutiny, and that
the new proof draws on a substantially
different set of methods.

THE COUNTERARGUMENTS, AS PUBLISHED. Two
arrived on the day itself, each aimed at the
same joint: the uniform boundedness the
Montel step of the overview needs. Jenny
Lorraine Nielsen's note (PhilArchive,
archived 2026-11-20) exhibits a
counterexample to the uniform boundedness
assertion used in the concluding argument —
for the trivial character over Q the
approximants produce an Archimedean integral
that diverges as the cutoff approaches the
identity, so the application of Montel's
theorem in section 1.4.3, repeated in section
1.8.4.3, is invalid — and a second
counterexample, to a norm-preimage
identification in the discussion of
divisorial strata. Rafik Zeraoulia's note
(2026-11-20) proves the same divergence as
its Proposition 1, by Fatou's lemma, and
identifies the sign of the associated prime
sum. The house ran the computation itself and
agrees (51.1.3); the filings stand at
1.2.468. A reader's video account of the same
evening reports checking one further
proposition of the draft and finding it false
as stated; the house has not verified that
report (Q169).

THE FIELD'S TEMPERATURE, AS REPORTED. Terence
Tao's post of the day ranks three results by
how digested they are and puts the Riemann
claims last: the artifact an enormous
Lean-only proof, the digestion to take some
time; he endorses neither claim, and neither
does anyone else of standing in the day's
record. The Clay Mathematics Institute still
lists the hypothesis as unsolved, per the
day's reporting.

THE OTHER CLAIM'S STATE, AS REPORTED — FOR
THE CONTRAST AT 51.1.5. Andrew Sutherland's
verification page for the artifact of Paper
032 (read 2026-11-21, page updated 12:55 UTC)
reports, as of that hour: all 175,187 modules
compile without errors; the five theorems'
axioms print as the three standard axioms
only; the Lean kernel's replay of every
module passes; two independently written
kernels accept the full exported proof; and
an independent implementation whose statement
matching confirms all five theorems against
the challenge — with the end-to-end
comparator run and the Lean kernels' replay
of the export still in progress. The same
page is plain about scope: GRH there means
Dirichlet L-functions; Dedekind zeta
functions, Hecke L-functions, and automorphic
L-functions in general are not among the
statements checked. All of this stands as
Sutherland's report, not the house's receipt:
the house's own build of the cone, launched
2026-11-20, was still running when Paper 032
was filed, and 1.2.460 stands as that chapter
filed it. What the house itself ran for this
chapter stands at 51.1.3, and governs what
this chapter files, and at what standing.


# Paper 033 — the delta: what the model takes in

WHERE THE PAPER LIVES. Cluster AO — the
Riemann country — is EXTENDED, not founded
again: concepts 1.2.464-1.2.469 join
1.2.458-1.2.463 on the same ground (zeta, the
L-functions, their zeros), and foundations
1.3.347-1.3.348 join 1.3.345-1.3.346. The
argument against a new cluster is the
founding argument of Paper 032 read the other
way: a cluster is founded on its ground, and
the ground of this draft is the identical
territory — the same functions, the same
line, the same open question — claimed in the
other form. What changes inside AO is its
centre of gravity: the country now holds both
species of Millennium claim, the artifact
that prices itself when run and the argument
that must be read to be priced, and the
contrast between them is this chapter's main
taking-in, stated below. The neighbours
weighed at AO's founding stand as they stood;
nothing at the borders moves.

THE CONTRAST, STATED PLAINLY — the point of
the filing. Scope: the draft aims wider. Its
claim is the Hecke form (1.2.464) — every
number field, every unitary Hecke character
at once — where the artifact of Paper 032
proves, and the community's checks cover, the
Dirichlet form alone (1.2.459); the wider
statement is exactly the part of the draft
that is not written. Evidence form: the
artifact's claim can be checked by a machine
against a fixed statement, and its checking
is a matter of runs completing; the claim of
the draft can be checked only by reading, and
its reading has already produced, within a
day, two published counterarguments the house
confirms and one internal absence the house
has verified. Standing: the artifact's proof
claim stands attributed at 1.2.460, one house
build from the verified bin, its statements
already house-verified as the genuine
statements. The completeness claim of the
draft stands refuted at 1.2.465, and the
load-bearing bound of its overview refuted at
1.2.468. Failure shape: the draft fails in
the open — its gaps are printed in its own
footnotes, and a reader can find them in an
evening; the artifact, if it fails, fails
silently until a kernel speaks. The asymmetry
is not a ranking of the two works: the
programme of the draft (1.2.467) may yet be
the one a human can learn from, and its trace
formula (1.2.466) stands unverified rather
than fallen. It is a pricing of what each
claim, today, has earned.

THE AXIS JUDGMENT, WRITTEN AT FILING. NO
AXIS. The axis criterion is a re-reading: a
held concept or price changes its meaning
under the new piece, with the re-pricing
named. Nothing held is re-read here. The
Hecke form is filed beside the Dirichlet form
as a new concept (1.2.464); 1.2.459's text
and standing are untouched. The proof claim
of Paper 032 (1.2.460) is neither stepped nor
repriced by anything in this draft — the two
claims do not bear on each other's evidence,
which is itself the synthesis at 51.2.1.
Question Q166, opened by Paper 032 for this
draft, is answered and closed by this
chapter; closing a question is not a
re-pricing. The Oracle's FALSE gate moves
(below) — a gate movement prices the
tracker's binning reliability, not the
model's geometry, and is not an axis event.
Paper 032's judgment named the condition
under which it would open again — a step of
1.2.460 in either direction — and this
chapter does not meet it.

WHAT THE PIECE DID NOT MOVE. No held concept
revised; no cluster founded or redrawn;
1.2.460 unmoved; the TRUE, UNKNOWN, and
FICTION gates unmoved.

NOVELTY, AS FILED. New canonical concepts: 6
(1.2.464 the Generalized Riemann Hypothesis,
Hecke form; 1.2.465 the proof as a human
draft; 1.2.466 the trace formula over the
idele class group; 1.2.467 topology-priced
spectral selectivity; 1.2.468 the uniform
boundedness assertion; 1.2.469 the
multiplicity cap). Revisions of held
concepts: none. Links as filed: 6 inside the
filing (464 to 465 and 466; 465 to 466; 466
to 467; 467 to 468; 469 to 465) and 4 to
foundations (464 to 1.3.348; 465 to 1.3.348;
466 to 1.3.347; 467 to 1.3.347), and 3 to the
chapter before (464 against 1.2.459; 465
against 1.2.460; 467 against 1.2.461);
cross-cluster links none. Foundations laid
down: 2 (1.3.347-1.3.348). Words coined:
none. Novelty Index: 6 new of 469 canonical
