# Paper 035 — Gumbel fluctuations of the maximum of the Airy_1 process (Chen & Pu)

Source: 'Gumbel Fluctuations of the Maximum
of the Airy_1 Process', by Le Chen (Auburn
University) and Fei Pu (Beijing Normal
University) — arXiv:2611.23658, version 1,
submitted 18 Nov 2026, in the arXiv class
for probability (math.PR). Taken in as
Paper 035 of the test of the skim fixed in
advance: slot 11 of 12 of the sample, the
second pick of the far stratum — the paper
stood as the declared mechanical far
candidate of the scan of 19 November 2026
(0 held clusters touched), and the preserved
far-pool order carried it to this taking-in.
The seal stands committed blind in the quest
log (commit 7f839da) before any full-text
access: ten new concepts foreseen (interval
6 to 15), no revision, the axis at
probability 0.15, the Novelty Index at
0.0204 (interval 0.012 to 0.030), thirteen
links within and none across, three
questions, and the Oracle held at about 62%
(55-70) as ledger bookkeeping. The score is
computed by script in the scoring pass and
reported both ways on the sensitivity filing
recorded at 53.1.5. Restructure Pass #23
runs inside this taking-in, on the standing
trigger (Paper 035).

THE READING. The paper was read entire in
its HTML form at its arXiv address: the five
sections, the two appendices (the continuum
determinant limit; the weighted operator
estimates), and the eighteen references. The
house's check record stands at 53.1.3, and
states plainly what kind of checking this
pass holds: the two cancellations of Lemma
5.1 verified by reading, and no house
recomputation — no claim of this filing
stands by receipt.

THE SHAPE OF THE WORK. The paper prices the
fluctuation of a maximum whose first-order
growth was already known. Its route is the
classical one of extreme-value theory,
rebuilt for a dependent process: first a
two-term expansion of the tail of the
maximum over a fixed interval (1.2.487),
produced by a trace identity inside a
Fredholm determinant (1.2.488); then the
joint exceedances of unit blocks priced in
every range of separation — touching
(1.2.490), short gaps (1.2.491), mesoscopic
gaps (1.2.492), and the far range surrendered
to mixing (1.2.493); then the whole assembled
by big blocks and small blocks (1.2.494)
into the Gumbel limit (1.2.483). The paper
notes, in its Remark 5.3, that the
persistence estimates of Ferrari and Liu
also imply its theorem, with level-uniform
error bounds; its own proof is the direct
local description of exceedances, and the
chapter files the proof the paper gives.
# Paper 035 — the formal core: what the paper states, and the machines it runs

THE OBJECT AND THE THEOREM. The Airy_1
process A_1(x) (1.2.481) is stationary, with
one-point law P{A_1(0) <= s} = F_1(2s), the
GOE Tracy-Widom law. M_N (1.2.482) is its
maximum on [0, N]. Theorem 1.1 (1.2.483):
with kappa = 4 sqrt(2)/3, gamma =
kappa^{-2/3}, the centering a_N and scale
b_N of 1.2.484-1.2.485, (M_N - a_N)/b_N
converges in law to the Gumbel distribution
(1.3.352).

THE FIXED-INTERVAL TAIL. pi_T(M) and rho(M)
stand at 1.2.486, with rho(M) ~ 8M p_pt(M)
over the point tail. Proposition 1.2
(1.2.487) expands pi_T to two terms. Its
proof (the paper's Section 2) starts from
the continuum-statistics determinant of
Quastel and Remenik: P{A_1 <= M on [0,T]} =
det(I - B_0 + Lambda^M P_{-T} B_0), where
B_0 has kernel Ai(x+y) (1.3.351) and Lambda
is the heat kernel times the Brownian-bridge
non-crossing probability 1 -
e^{-(x-M)(z-M)/T} below M. The weighted
realisation A~ = V W (exponential weights
U_a, a polynomial weight Phi; admissible
weights cancel in the determinant) gives the
trace identity (1.2.488), and the trace-norm
bound ||A~||_1^2 = o(rho/M) disposes of the
rest of the determinant series by Simon's
bound. The identity is the fixed-interval
counterpart of Ferrari and Liu's first trace
identity.

THE JOINT PRICES. Corollary 1.3 (1.2.490)
prices touching blocks by inclusion-
exclusion off the two-term expansion.
Proposition 1.4 (1.2.491) prices separated
blocks through a two-interval determinant:
its cross terms are Laplace-type integrals
whose exponents are minimised at the action
J_M(r) (boundary minimisers; a Hessian lower
bound of 1/(2L)), and its negative-Airy
pieces are superexponentially small
(e^{-c a^4}). Proposition 4.3 (1.2.492)
prices the mesoscopic range by mesh,
oscillation, and Newman's inequality, on
the association of A_1 (Pu) and the
covariance law of Basu, Busani and Ferrari,
Cov(A_1(0), A_1(d)) = exp{-(4/3 + o(1)) d^3}.

THE ASSEMBLY. With H(u) = kappa u^{3/2} -
(1/4) log u - log C_*, Lemma 5.1 calibrates
H(a_N) = log N + o(1) and Lemma 5.2 reads
the block rate at u_N(z) = a_N + b_N z off
1/H'(a_N) ~ b_N, giving N P(Y_0 > u_N(z)) ->
e^{-z}. Anti-clustering (the paper's (5.6))
sums the three joint prices; the big-block /
small-block machine (1.2.494) closes the
squeeze, on the cubic-exponential mixing of
Chen and Pu (1.2.493).

MACHINE M1 — the gap-range dispatch
[stated in the paper, its Figure 1]. States:
a pair of blocks at one of four ranges —
TOUCHING, routed to Corollary 1.3; SHORT
GAPS, 1 <= r <= chi sqrt(2M), routed to
Proposition 1.4; MESOSCOPIC, to
O(sqrt(M)), routed to Proposition 4.3;
DISTANT, routed to the mixing bound. Moves:
each range is priced by its own instrument
and the four prices sum into the single
anti-clustering estimate (5.6). The gap
named for the machine: the ranges meet at
cutoffs set by the proof's needs — the
chi^3 > 1/2 condition is where two of its
instruments exchange custody — and no state
of the machine prices a pair that falls on
a boundary except by the instrument that
claims it. Home: here; concepts
1.2.490-1.2.493.

MACHINE M2 — the big-block / small-block
assembly [stated in the paper]. States:
BLOCKED — the interval cut into cores of
length about N/k with discarded gaps of
length q_N between them; PRICED BELOW and
PRICED ABOVE — Bonferroni bounds per core;
SQUEEZED — (1 - tau/k)^k below and
(1 - tau/k + delta_k/k)^k above; CLOSED —
k -> infinity sends both bounds to e^{-tau},
with real N squeezed between floor(N) and
floor(N)+1. The gap named for the machine:
the discarded gaps are thrown away by
count, not priced — the machine trusts the
mixing rate (1.2.493) to make what it
discards harmless, and no state measures
the discarded mass directly. Home: here;
concept 1.2.494.

MACHINE M3 — the mesh refinement of
Section 4 [stated in the paper]. States:
MESHED — each unit interval cut at size
h_M = M^{-4}; QUIET or OSCILLATING — the
oscillation event D_M(I), of probability at
most C M^4 e^{-cM^2} (Lemma 4.1); REDUCED —
off D_M, an interval exceedance forces a
mesh point above M-1; PRICED — the mesh
points' pairs bounded by Newman's
inequality on association and the covariance
law. The gap named for the machine: the
reduction loses a full unit of level (M to
M-1) at the mesh points, a loss the tail's
steepness absorbs but no state of the
machine prices on its own. Home: here;
concept 1.2.492.

MACHINE M4 — the dyadic continuum limit of
Appendix A [stated in the paper]. States:
MESHED AT n — the two intervals cut by a
dyadic mesh; BARRIERS SET — the discrete
non-crossing events, decreasing in n;
LIMIT TAKEN — the barrier events decrease
to the continuum event and the discrete
determinants converge to the continuum
determinant (1.3.353), the convergence
upgraded to trace norm by telescoping and
domination (Lemma 3.1). The gap named for
the machine: the domination that carries
the limit is an analytic bound, not a state
— a mesh whose barrier probabilities failed
to dominate would leave the machine with a
limit it cannot certify, and no state
checks the domination. Home: here; concept
1.2.491.
