(arXiv:2611.23658, version 1) on 2026-11-23:
the five sections, the two appendices, and
the eighteen references.

WHAT THIS PASS HOLDS, STATED HONESTLY. This
pass carries no house recomputation. No
derived number of the paper was recomputed
by the house in arithmetic of its own, and
no claim of this filing stands by receipt:
the chapter's concepts stand attributed, as
the registry records them. What the house
did beyond the reading is one verification
by reading, recorded here for exactly what
it is.

THE VERIFICATION BY READING — LEMMA 5.1.
The calibration of the centering (1.2.484)
rests on the identity H(a_N) = log N + o(1),
where H(u) = kappa u^{3/2} - (1/4) log u -
log C_*. The house followed the proof's
expansion of H at a_N term by term, as
printed, and confirms the two cancellations
the lemma stands on: the coefficient
gamma/9 in the second-order term of a_N is
the value that cancels the log log N term
the leading term generates, and the constant
log(3/(2 pi^3)) in the same term is the
value that cancels the constant term. The
cancellations are verified as read — the
algebra followed on the page and found as
printed — and are NOT counted as a receipt:
no independent recomputation stands behind
them, and the filing's novelty lines at
53.1.5 count no True increment for them.

WHAT WAS NOT CHECKED. Everything else. The
determinant estimates, the Laplace bounds,
the oscillation estimate, and the mixing
input are the paper's and its cited works',
filed as their claims at the chapter's
concepts. What checking the load-bearing
legs would take can be stated from the
paper itself: an independent evaluation of
the trace identity's Airy asymptotics
(1.2.488), and a re-derivation of the
action's lower bound in Proposition 1.4
(1.2.491) — neither run in this pass.

THE SCROLL'S OWN STANDING. This record's
claims are the reading and the followed
proof, each named above; they stand as the
record of the pass, at the basis the
registry gives this scroll. The chapter's
claims stand attributed at its concepts; the
author check (53.1.6) stands on its own legs
as the checks run there.
# Paper 035 — the results, as claimed, and where the paper places itself

THE RESULTS, AS THE PAPER REPORTS THEM.
Theorem 1.1 (1.2.483): the maximum of the
Airy_1 process over [0, N], centred at a_N
and scaled at b_N, converges in law to the
Gumbel distribution. Proposition 1.2
(1.2.487): the fixed-interval tail expands
to two terms, pi_T(M) = T rho(M) +
rho(M)/(4M) + o(rho(M)/M), with the explicit
signed remainder of the published form.
Corollary 1.3 (1.2.490): touching blocks
exceed together with probability about
rho(M)/(4M), a conditional exceedance of
about 1/(4M). Proposition 1.4 (1.2.491):
separated blocks are priced by the action
bound, summed to o(rho(M)) over its range.
Proposition 4.3 (1.2.492): the mesoscopic
range sums to o(rho(M)) as well. Together
with the mixing input (1.2.493), these are
the whole content of the theorem: a
first-order law that was already known —
M_N/(log N)^{2/3} -> gamma almost surely
(Pu), with the almost-sure extrema laws of
Basu and Bhattacharjee beside it — now
carries its fluctuation law.

THE SECOND ROUTE, AS THE PAPER RECORDS IT.
Remark 5.3 sketches how the persistence
estimates of Ferrari and Liu also imply
Theorem 1.1: their bounds are uniform in the
level, with beta(c) <= 0.46/7 bounding
alpha_0 = 14 e^{-97 sqrt(3)/49} < 0.46. The
tracker files the remark as the paper's:
the theorem has two roads to it in the
record, and this chapter's concepts file the
road the paper itself builds — the direct
local description of exceedances — because
that road's instruments (1.2.487-1.2.488) are
the filing's new ground.

THE LIMITS THE PAPER STATES. Two, in its
Section 1.5, filed here as the paper's own
account of its edges. The separated-block
estimate is an upper bound only: no matching
asymptotic across growing gaps is proved
(Q176). And the unit-interval maxima do not
capture the joint local profile of the
process near several extreme peaks (Q177).
The tracker prices the claims as they stand:
attributed, with no house receipt behind any
of them this pass (53.1.3).
# Paper 035 — the delta: what the model takes in

WHERE THE PAPER LIVES. Cluster AQ is
FOUNDED — the country of extremes of a
dependent process: concepts 1.2.481-1.2.494,
on ground the book had not entered. The
founding argument is the ground itself. The
book held no KPZ process, no Airy process,
and no extreme-value limit for anything:
its tails were prices inside other
countries' arguments, never the object. This
paper's object — the highest point of a
stationary dependent process over a growing
interval, and the law of its fluctuation —
was held by nothing, and its instruments
(a two-term tail expansion whose second term
is a joint law; a trace identity inside a
continuum determinant; anti-clustering
priced range by range) are filed where they
are used, as the cluster's own concepts.

THE AXIS JUDGMENT, WRITTEN AT FILING,
AGAINST THE PROTOCOL'S CRITERIA. NO AXIS.
(a) The centre of gravity — the extreme
fluctuations of a KPZ-class process — is
ground the model held no slot for: this leg
points toward an axis. (b) But every move
the paper makes is of a held kind: a limit
theorem with explicit normalisation, a tail
expansion, joint-exceedance bounds, mixing
estimates, a blocking assembly — the model's
kinds receive the paper without re-reading
any held concept: this leg stands against.
(c) Its bridges to held ground are analogies
of shape — tails, thresholds, blocks —
rather than shared machinery: this leg
stands against. Two of the three legs
against; and a cluster founding is not by
itself an axis (the precedent of Papers
027, 029 and 030). Filed: axis = no.

WHAT THE PAPER DID NOT MOVE. No held concept
revised; no held standing moved; no cluster
but the new one touched. No concept of this
filing stands by receipt: the Lemma 5.1
cancellations were verified by reading
(53.1.3), not recomputed, and are counted
nowhere as a receipt.

NOVELTY, AS FILED. New canonical concepts:
14 (1.2.481 the Airy_1 process; 1.2.482 the
maximum over a growing interval, M_N;
1.2.483 the Gumbel limit theorem; 1.2.484
the centering a_N; 1.2.485 the scale b_N —
split from the centering under the split
rule, the paper using it apart as 1/H'(a_N);
1.2.486 the fixed-interval tail pi_T(M) and
rho(M); 1.2.487 the two-term tail expansion;
1.2.488 the trace identity — filed apart
under the instrument rule, used at two
distinct points; 1.2.489 the block maxima
Y_j; 1.2.490 the touching-block asymptotic;
1.2.491 the two-interval bound, with the
action J_M(r); 1.2.492 mesoscopic
anti-clustering; 1.2.493 cubic-exponential
strong mixing and the gap scale q_N;
1.2.494 the big-block / small-block
assembly). Revisions of held concepts: none.
Links as filed: 18 inside the filing (481
to 482; 482 to 483, 486 and 489; 483 to
