# Paper 035 — synthesis: the second term is the law [synthesis]

[synthesis] The shape this filing leaves in
the model is a lesson about where laws hide.
The paper's headline is a limit theorem, but
its load-bearing object is a correction: the
second term of a tail expansion, of relative
size 1/(4M), which a one-term reading would
discard as polish. That term is, exactly,
the joint law of touching blocks — the first
anti-clustering price — because when two
intervals are priced together the leading
terms cancel and only the correction
survives. The tracker files the shape
beside its kin: a result whose content
lives in the term the headline rounds away.
The skim foresaw the expansion's two loads;
the reading showed the second load was not
a refinement of the first but a different
kind of statement — a dependence price, not
a tail price — and the filing's instrument
and split decisions (1.2.487-1.2.488,
1.2.484-1.2.485) both turn on that
difference.

THE DEPENDENCE, PRICED IN RANGES. Read as
one argument, the paper is a tariff on
dependence, charged range by range: at
touching range the price is algebraic
(1/(4M)); at short gaps exponential in the
gap (e^{-2Mr}); at mesoscopic range it is
paid by the covariance's cubic death; at
long range by mixing at the same cubic rate.
The Gumbel law at the end is the statement
that the total tariff is negligible — the
dependent maximum inherits the independent
limit because dependence, at the extreme
level, is too weak to cluster. The model's
other extreme-value ground is all of the
independent kind; cluster AQ files the first
priced account of what dependence costs an
extreme, and where the cost is charged.

THE OPEN SEAM. The tariff is one-sided: the
joint prices are upper bounds, and the
paper says so (Q176). Whether separated
peaks repel exactly at the action's rate —
or merely no more than that — is the
country's first open question, and it is
the shape of question the far stratum was
meant to buy: a precise seam, named by the
paper itself, at the edge of a finished
theorem.
# Paper 035 — Open questions Q176-Q179

Q176. The matching lower bound (the paper's
Section 1.5; 1.2.491). The separated-block
estimate is an upper bound only: across
growing gaps, joint exceedance is at most
the action's price, and no matching
asymptotic is proved. Do two blocks at gap r
exceed together at exactly the rate
e^{-J_M(r)} the bound charges — is the
action the true price of a separated pair
of peaks, or only a ceiling on it? The
paper names the gap itself; a lower bound
of the same shape, or a counterexample rate
below it, decides.

Q177. The joint local profile (the paper's
Section 1.5; 1.2.489). The filing prices
unit-interval maxima; the process near
several extreme peaks — the heights and
shapes of the peaks themselves, jointly —
is not captured by the maxima. What does
A_1 look like, rescaled, around its highest
points when two or more of them stand near
the extreme level at once? A point-process
account of the peaks, if one exists, would
carry the profile; the maxima alone cannot.

Q178. Universality across the class
[synthesis] (1.2.483). The Gumbel law is
proved for Airy_1, the flat case. Does the
same law, with the same (log N)^{2/3}
centering shape and (log N)^{-1/3} scale,
govern the maximum of Airy_2 and of other
KPZ-class processes over growing intervals —
and where the exponents differ, what in the
process sets them? The paper's instruments
are Airy_1-shaped (its determinant, its
covariance law); a class-wide statement
would need the tariff of 53.2.1 repriced
process by process.

Q179. The rate of convergence [synthesis]
(1.2.483). The limit is stated without a
rate: how fast does the centred, scaled
maximum approach the Gumbel law — are there
second-order corrections to the convergence
itself, of the kind the centering already
carries at second order? The calibration
machinery (Lemma 5.1's exact cancellations)
suggests the paper's estimates could be
pushed one order further; nothing in the
paper prices the approach.
