# Paper 036 — synthesis: completion is invisible to homology [synthesis]

[synthesis] The shape this filing leaves in
the model is a commutation: two passages
that look opposite — completing a module
along a nilpotent ideal, and globalizing it
from algebra to analysis — turn out to be
the same passage as far as the nilradical's
homology can see. The tracker files the
shape beside its kin in other countries: a
measurement that cannot distinguish the
algebraic object from its analytic shadow,
proved not by computing either side but by
building a chain of intermediate objects
(a completion, a weight-by-weight
globalization, a smooth Jacquet module)
each of which the homology provably cannot
tell from the next. The proof's real engine
is a reduction of trust: every hard step is
moved to a place — a finite level, a single
orbit, a scalar distribution — where it
becomes checkable, and the filtrations are
the roads between those places.
# Paper 036 — Open questions Q180-Q182

Q180. Beyond the linear case (the paper's
own limit, stated at 54.1.4; 1.2.495). The
comparison is proved for real LINEAR
reductive groups. Does it hold for general
real reductive groups, and in the full
generality of Vogan's Conjecture 10.3 for
canonical globalizations? A proof, or a
counterexample representation, decides.

Q181. A derived unification (1.2.501,
1.2.502). The homology, Tor and Ext
comparisons are proved as three families
over the filtration F_k. Is there a single
derived or spectral-sequence statement —
one isomorphism of complexes or of derived
objects — of which all three are shadows,
with the compatibility across k as its
differential? The paper's naturality
suggests the shape; no such statement is
made.

Q182. Effectivity (1.2.498). The comparison
is an isomorphism with no quantitative
content stated: are there vanishing ranges,
finite-generation bounds, or growth
controls for H_i(u, pi) that the comparison
transfers between pi_K and pi — degrees in
which the homology must vanish, priced from
the algebraic side? Nothing in the paper
prices one.
