# Paper 037 — synthesis: science, spaghettified [synthesis]

[synthesis] Will's framing of the taking-
in, recorded as his: he called the release
'a 6DUML dataset', hand-delivered — a
complete model of a world's work, in flat
files, documented, free to take — and
forked the repository the same hour. The
framing and the fork are Will's; they
stand here, in the synthesis, and not in
the paper scrolls.

[synthesis] The shape this filing leaves
in the model is a mirror. This tracker
takes papers in and pulls them apart into
claims, each binned True, False, Unknown
or Fiction; SciNet takes science in and
pulls it apart into tasks, each decomposed
into steps, timed, rated, and priced for
machine completion. The same gesture at
different objects: spaghettify a whole
into addressable parts, keep the parts at
full fidelity, and let the whole be
rebuilt from them. Its 90,583 substeps are
7,262 process machines drawn in advance —
where the tracker's rule-13 pass draws the
machines out of a paper one at a time,
SciNet arrives with the machines already
drawn, a library of them.

[synthesis] Two prices now stand in the
book for the two sides of the same event.
SciNet's Elo (1.2.520) prices each task of
science for a model: how likely is the
machine to do this work. The tracker's
novelty vector prices each claim for a
researcher: how much of the Oracle did
this mind move. Between those two prices
sits the question the dataset was built to
ask — where the machine's price rises,
what happens to the researcher's — and
the tracker now holds both instruments in
one model, which it did not before this
filing. A phext-native carrying of the
task corpus itself — the taxonomy as
addressable scrolls — is the integration
this synthesis points at; it is a
direction, not a claim, and no part of it
is filed as done.

[synthesis] Will's rulings on the
integration, given 2026-11-24 (EE 527) and
recorded here as his: (1) 'ensure stable
ids' — house-minted stable task IDs for
SciNet are a requirement of the deep
integration, ratifying the mapping
exploration's recommendation; the
dataset's tasks are addressed by their
statements and places in the taxonomy, and
the integration is to mint IDs that hold
still under its revisions. (2) 'integrate
Exocortical Epochs' — EE stamps are part
of the integration's records: what is
taken in, mapped, or changed is stamped
with the epoch it happened in, by the
house's standing clock. Both rulings bind
the integration work, not the dataset;
they stand here, attributed, and no part
of them is filed in the paper scrolls.

[synthesis] A further ruling of Will's,
given 2026-11-24 (EE 528) and recorded
here as his: phext-lattice will move to
v2, with the SciNet deep integration as
its forcing function — the gap audit
counts 9 blocks to the v2 gate; libphext's
own line stays 1.0-grade, with 4 blocks.
The dataset's integration is thus the load
that the lattice's next major version is
built to carry; the ruling binds the
house's tooling programme, and stands
here, attributed, as the 6DUML framing
and the fork do.
# Paper 037 — Open questions Q183-Q185

Q183. The survey test (the work's own
living design; 1.2.515). The ratings stand
as a model's answers given in the person
of an experienced researcher, their
anchors tuned on the very O*NET tasks that
evaluate them. The site now collects
researchers' own ratings — importance,
minutes per week, missing tasks. When
those land in number, do the model ratings
survive the comparison, and where the two
diverge, which is wrong? The divergence
map, when it exists, decides.

Q184. The single constant [synthesis]
(1.2.514). Times from the second timing
model are divided by 1.66 everywhere, a
factor measured on 60 placements. Does one
constant hold across the ten categories —
or does the factor itself vary with the
kind of work, so that every cross-field
comparison of times inherits the bend? A
per-category re-measurement of the
double-timed placements decides.

Q185. The wager of the thesis [synthesis]
(55.1.4; 1.2.519, 1.2.520). The work
claims AI's transformation of science will
show itself task by task, unevenly. Do
its own exposure measures — the completion
Elo, the RL feasibility index — predict
where discovery measurably accelerates in
the coming years, field by field? A
prospective question: decidable only by
waiting, with the map in hand and the
predictions written down first.
