THE WORK. Lexiang Tang, Weihao Gao,
Bingchen Zhao, Lu Ma, Qiao Jin, Bang
Yang and Yuexian Zou (Peking University;
Zhao at the University of Edinburgh),
'Thinking by Subtraction:
Confidence-Driven Contrastive Decoding
for LLM Reasoning', arXiv:2602.18232v1
[cs.CL], 20 February 2026 — read entire
in HTML form (appendices included) on
2026-11-24. Tang and Gao are co-first
authors; correspondence to Yang and Zou.
Code is promised at
github.com/bolo-web/CCD; the house has
not examined it.

THE METHOD (the paper's Sections 2-3).
Reasoning uncertainty is localized
(1.2.539): a small subset of
low-confidence tokens drives errors and
output expansion. The method, CCD
(1.2.537), estimates token-level
confidence online (1.2.538), keeps the
last W confidences in a sliding window,
and takes both of its thresholds as
quantiles of that window (1.3.368). A
position inside the reasoning region
whose confidence falls below the
window's q_cd-th quantile is an
LC-token (1.2.540); only there does the
method intervene. The intervention is a
contrast (1.2.525): a reference
distribution is read from a contrastive
context in which the high-confidence
tokens — the semantic anchors (1.2.541)
— have been replaced by a semantically
minimal placeholder, and the final
logits are (1+alpha) times the main
logits minus alpha times the reference
logits. The reference lives in a dual
KV cache (1.2.544), updated by its own
rule, so the main trajectory is never
disturbed. Before the window fills, the
thresholds are set so nothing intervenes
and nothing is masked: the method warms
up as plain decoding. The paper's
Algorithm 1 states the loop entire.

THE TWO STATE MACHINES the filing takes
from the work, both stated. (i) The CCD
decoding loop: WARM-UP (window not yet
full; thresholds inert) gives way to the
per-token cycle ESTIMATE (confidence
read from the main logits, window
updated), GATE (window quantiles
computed; the position classed LC,
HC or ordinary), CONTRAST (at LC
positions only, the reference logits
read from the contrastive cache and
fused), EMIT (the token drawn from the
final distribution; both caches
updated). (ii) The dual-cache
maintenance: the main cache appends the
emitted token as usual; the contrastive
cache appends the placeholder in place
of any high-confidence token inside the
reasoning region, and the real token
otherwise.

THE THEORY (Section 2.4). High-confidence
tokens act as semantic anchors: large
attention weights, large Jacobians.
Masking an anchor perturbs the hidden
state substantially, and by the
data-processing inequality can only
reduce the context's information about
the next token — so the reference's
conditional entropy is no smaller and
its top-logit margin smaller in
expectation (1.2.545). A flatter
reference is what a subtraction needs:
contrast sharpens the main distribution
only against a less confident pole.

THE EXPERIMENTS (Section 3). Four
competition mathematics benchmarks —
AIME 2024, AIME 2025, BRUMO 2025, HMMT
2025 — under Mean@8 accuracy, a single
trajectory per input, no ensembling.
Models: Qwen3-4B, 8B, 14B, 32B (the main
table reports 4B-14B), the MoE
Qwen3-30B-A3B, and DeepSeek-R1-Distill-
Qwen-1.5B, all without fine-tuning.
Baselines: standard sampling and MTI.
CCD improves on the base decoder in all
twelve dense-model cells of the main
table (gains 1.66 to 5.00 points) and
stands comparable to MTI (eight cells
won, one tied, three lost, on the
house's count at 57.1.3). On AIME 2025
the MoE model gains 2.92 points and the
distilled 1.5B gains 2.49. Ablations:
the gains hold across four threshold
settings and transfer across benchmarks
without retuning; masking high-confidence
tokens induces a higher-entropy
reference than masking mid- or
low-confidence ones; semantically
minimal placeholders give the most
stable signal. Analyses: the mean
confidence of intervened positions rises
from 6.355 to 6.673; correct and
incorrect traces separate further after
intervention (1.2.546); responses on the
AIME sets grow substantially shorter and
thinking-token frequency falls, while
HMMT and BRUMO lengths are preserved.
A case study (Appendix B.1) shows the
base decoder settling on a wrong answer
(271) where CCD's trace reaches the
right one (821) on an AIME 2025
combinatorics problem.

THE LIMITATIONS, as the paper states
them (Section 4): the evaluation is
mathematical reasoning only, and
general-domain effectiveness remains
unexplored; the models are text-only,
and the multimodal extension is future
work. An impact statement notes the
method inherits its base models' biases
and could make wrong content more
convincing if deployed irresponsibly.
THE HOUSE CHECK (run by Emise
2026-11-24; the paper's printed
arithmetic recomputed from its printed
tables; the check script and its run
record are kept with the taking-in).
Table 1 (Mean@8, Qwen3-4B/8B/14B across
AIME24, AIME25, BRUMO25, HMMT25): all
twelve CCD-over-Base gains are positive
as the paper claims, running from +1.66
(Qwen3-14B, HMMT25) to +5.00 (Qwen3-8B
AIME24 and Qwen3-14B AIME25). Against
MTI in the same table, CCD wins eight
cells, ties one (Qwen3-14B AIME24, 81.67
each) and loses three — the paper's
'comparable to MTI' stands as printed.
Table 2's two printed deltas are exact:
85.00 - 82.08 = +2.92 (MoE, AIME25) and
24.57 - 22.08 = +2.49 (distilled 1.5B).
Table 3 (threshold ablation, AIME25):
eleven of the twelve configuration
cells stand strictly above their Base
row and none below; the exception is an
exact tie, recorded at 57.1.4. Section
3.4's printed means are internally
consistent as differences: the
low-confidence positions' mean confidence
rises by 0.318 (6.355 to 6.673); correct
traces rise by 0.09 and incorrect traces
fall by 0.55, so the separation the
paper reports widens by 0.64 in total.
The paper's claims agree with its tables
at every point checked, bar the two
prose loosenesses recorded at 57.1.4.
CAVEATS ON THIS FILING. (i) Two
prose-versus-table loosenesses, found by
the house check at 57.1.3 and filed
here, neither rising to a False filing.
Section 3.2.1 states that on Qwen dense
models from 4B to 14B 'CCD improves
Mean8 accuracy by approximately 3-5%
over the Base method': eight of the
twelve cells do fall in 3-5, but the
14B row runs +2.09, +2.16 and +1.66 on
three of its four benchmarks and the 8B
BRUMO cell +2.92 — the family's true
range is 1.66 to 5.00, and 'approximately'
carries the claim only loosely.
Section 3.3 states that under all four
threshold settings the configurations
'consistently outperform the Base
decoding' on AIME25: eleven of the
twelve cells do, but the (2,5) setting
at Qwen3-8B ties Base exactly at 67.91 —
a tie at printed precision, not an
outperformance. (ii) The evaluation is
four mathematics benchmarks and nothing
else; every accuracy claim in this
chapter is a claim about competition
mathematics, and the paper itself names
the general-domain question as open
(Q192). (iii) The method's thresholds
were fixed at their best AIME25 values
and then frozen for the other
benchmarks — a stated design the paper
reports openly, which makes the
cross-benchmark numbers a transfer
result rather than a tuned one, and is
recorded here so the filing does not
read them as either more or less than
that. (iv) The results are the paper's
own runs, attributed; the house checked
the paper's arithmetic against its
tables, not the benchmarks themselves,
and has not examined the promised code.
(v) The theory at 1.2.545 is the paper's
derivation, filed as stated; the house
has not re-derived it.
QUESTIONS OPENED AT THIS CHAPTER.
Q190: the two gates of cluster AT — do
they mark the same positions? Paper
038's adaptive form sets its strength
from the unguided prediction's top-two
margin (1.2.529); Paper 039 gates on the
mean confidence of the top-k over a
sliding window (1.2.543). Where the two
signals disagree about a position, which
gate is right — and does a gated
trajectory contrast (LoopCD fired only
at low-confidence positions) keep its
gains at still lower cost? Q191: the
anchor account (1.2.545) is tested in
one direction only — masking anchors in
the reference helps. The same account
predicts that masking them in the MAIN
branch should hurt, by the same
mechanism. The paper does not run that
control; until someone does, the anchor
theory rests on the direction that
flatters it. Q192: does the method's
gain survive off mathematics — code,
factual question-answering, open-ended
reasoning? The paper names the question
itself as its first limitation; its
thresholds and its placeholder are
tuned on competition mathematics, and
nothing in the filing answers for other
ground. Q193: the widened trace-level
confidence separation (1.2.546) is
offered by the paper as a signal for
downstream verification and reranking,
and not used for either in the paper.
Does selecting on post-intervention
confidence beat DeepConf-style filtering
on the confidence of the unintervened
trace — the comparison that would make
the separation a tool rather than an
observation?

