THE WORK. Alperen Keles (Datadog, Inc.;
the blog's named contact,
alp.keles@datadoghq.com), 'Ephyra: A New
Agentic Property-Based Testing Library'
— Datadog engineering blog, published
2026-11-24 — read entire on 2026-11-24.
Companion paper, read entire in HTML
form with it: 'Ephyra: A Property-Based
Testing Framework for the LLM Era',
arXiv:2611.23282v1 [cs.SE], submitted 23
October 2026, single author, slated for
the Future of Property-Based Testing
workshop at POPL 27 (January 2027). The
repository, datadog-labs/ephyra (MIT),
was read at its README and its Lean
case-study index.

THE PROBLEM, as the work states it.
Coding agents write a growing share of
software, and verification has not kept
pace: review misses subtle interactions,
and hand-built testing is overwhelmed by
the volume. Property-based testing
(1.3.370) is the established technique
the work builds on; its historical
bottleneck is the generator, whose
construction 'takes significant time and
expertise'. The work's answer is to give
the generator to the agents (1.2.562).

THE LIBRARY (blog; paper Sections 1-3).
Ephyra (1.2.561) is designed on three
principles: components should be
synthesized where they are verifiable
(1.2.565); customizability is first-class
— the loop is composed from generate,
mutate, shrink and run, after the
programmable-PBT design of Keles et al.
(2026), the author's own prior work; and
observability is a first-class
primitive, coverage and statistics
feeding the agent that steers (1.2.564).
The paper's two hypotheses: future PBT
workloads interleave generation,
enumeration and mutation rather than
sampling one distribution; and generators
can be synthesized against arbitrary
functional predicates where type-based
derivation and randomness-parsing cannot
reach. The starter form the blog ships
is a prompt: hand the repository to a
coding agent and let it write and
optimize tests autonomously.

THE TWO STATE MACHINES the filing takes
from the work, both stated. (i) The
property run (the library's composed
runner): GENERATE (an input drawn from
the generator) gives way to EXECUTE (the
property checked against the system
under test); on violation, SHRINK (the
input reduced to its minimal reproducer,
1.2.566); and RECORD (the failure
remembered by seed in the regression
file, and replayed first on the next
run). (ii) The generator-optimization
loop (blog; paper Sections 2.3 and 4):
WRITE (a generator authored — by the
engineer, or by the agent from a short
description), MEASURE (code coverage and
input statistics collected from the
executions), REWRITE (the generator
changed against the measurements — a
feature added to its vocabulary, a
weighting moved), and RE-RUN; the
steering is the engineer's in the open
loop and the agent's own in the closed
loop (1.2.574).

THE CAMPAIGNS, as each source states
them. The paper (October snapshot):
fourteen systems; 349 bugs found, 300
reported. Open loop — interactive agent
sessions on six systems, 167 confirmed
bugs: SQLite 63, Turso 72, PostgreSQL
16, ada-url 12, Cedar 2, QED 2, run
largely on the literature's own oracles
— TLP and NoREC (1.2.567) — and on a
generator that constructs SQLite files
directly (1.2.568). Closed loop
(1.2.574): five systems in parallel,
about eight hours, $100 of agent budget
each; 80 verified bugs for $330 all-in,
setup, triage and write-up priced
separately in its Table 1; then 72 more
in continued running, and 30 in a later
trial on compilers (binaryen, OCaml,
OxCaml). The blog (November): the
campaigns grown to 26 systems, 535
unique previously unknown bugs, all
reported upstream, 373 confirmed by
maintainers, 313 already fixed — the
repository's case studies carry the
reports per target, and its README
counts 70+ bugs in SQLite, 20 in
PostgreSQL and 25 in Lean. The found
bugs' lifetimes run from one day to 24
years.

THE NAMED FINDS (blog, each with its
upstream report). SQLite: three DELETEs
leave one byte of an interior page
unaccounted, and a third reports the
database malformed — found by the
file-constructing generator, reported by
Keles on the SQLite forum (9d039d4ca7)
and answered there the same day by drh.
OCaml: a non-exhaustive integer match
crashes instead of raising Match_failure
(1.2.570; ocaml/ocaml issue 15185).
Lean 4: a match on Float patterns treats
5 and 5.0 as different patterns, and
decide proves a true equation false
(1.2.569; leanprover/lean4 issue 16198).
PostgreSQL: nested BETWEEN doubles parse
work per level until a short query needs
gigabytes, uncancellably (1.2.571; BUG
#19758). QED: decimal constants read as
float32 make distinct constants equal to
the prover (1.2.572; qed-solver/prover
issue 13). LiteLLM: streaming changes a
tool call's finish_reason to 'stop', and
the agent silently skips the call
(BerriAI/litellm issue 48716). DuckDB: a
LIMIT 1 query returns three rows
(duckdb/duckdb issue 27714).

THE LIMITATIONS, as the work states
them. The blog: the experiments 'provide
evidence that autonomous generator
optimization is an effective testing
strategy' but 'do not yet validate
Ephyra's design choices' — the classical
loop used so far is reproducible in any
PBT library with handwritten generators
and shrinkers; controlled clean-room
experiments on reliability, cost and
generality are future work, as is agents
optimizing the whole loop (swarm testing,
power scheduling) rather than the
generator alone. The paper (Section 5)
adds the loop's own hazard: reward
hacking (1.2.573) — 'designing metrics
that resist gaming, and detecting the
reward hacking remains an open problem'.
THE HOUSE CHECK (run by Emise
2026-11-24; the work's public record
verified against the upstream reports
themselves and the repository's own
indexes; the check script and its run
record are kept with the taking-in).
Four of the seven named upstream reports
were read entire.

(1) LEAN (leanprover/lean4 issue 16198,
read entire; its tracker record read by
API). The issue, filed 2026-11-02 and
marked 'Found by an automated
differential tester (ephyra)', states
the mechanism exactly as the blog does:
float literal patterns are matched by
the literal as written, not by its
value; the comparisons sit in the match
compiler (values.contains; v == value);
and — the issue's own sentence — 'The
elaborated matcher is wrong, so #eval,
compiled code and the kernel all agree
on 0.' Reproduced on 4.33.0, 4.35.0 and
a 4.37.0 nightly; float literal patterns
were unsupported in 4.29.0. The record
shows the issue CLOSED on 2026-11-03,
the day after it was filed — the blog's
'fixed the day after we reported it'
holds. The defect's class is stated
precisely at 1.2.569: a pattern-
compilation correctness bug, upstream of
both evaluator and kernel, which inherit
the same wrong matcher and so agree.
That is a different failure class from
the kernel soundness holes behind
Chapter 50's criterion (issues 14806,
14807, 14838, where the kernel itself
accepted what it should reject): here
the kernel checked faithfully a matcher
it was handed already wrong. The filing
therefore records the find as a
cross-reference to Chapter 50's thread —
an instrument (property-based
differential testing) that prices the
agreement surface between elaborator,
evaluator and kernel — and expressly not
as evidence about 1.2.460, whose step
the addendum at 50.1.7 governs and which
this chapter does not move.

(2) SQLITE (forum post 9d039d4ca7, read
entire). Reported by Alperen Keles on
2026-10-05; the reproducer is the blog's
script. drh — SQLite's author — replied
the same day, confirmed the bug and
root-caused it on the record: a 3-byte
minimal tuple is rounded up to 4 bytes
on a leaf; copied up to an interior page
as a divider its size is miscomputed as
8 rather than 7, leaving one byte of the
page unaccounted, which a later DELETE's
free-space accounting meets as
corruption. His dating: 'The problem
goes back until at least February 2014'
— a maintainer's bound of twelve-plus
years, where the blog says 'nearly 13
years'. Fixed by a check-in the same
day (2026-10-05). Keles's account of the
method is on the same thread: a library
that 'constructs valid random SQLite
files' directly, then generates random
interactions against that state
(1.2.568). The paper's appendix lists
the bug as fixed.

(3) OCAML (ocaml/ocaml issue 15185, read;
its tracker record read by API). The
report's program is the blog's (the
report calls f 4 false directly; the
blog wraps the 4 in Sys.opaque_identity);
the contract is quoted from the
reference manual ('the exception
Match_failure is raised'); the results,
checked 2026-10-21 in the official
images, are an illegal instruction or a
segmentation fault on both OCaml 5.4.1
and 4.14.4, bytecode and native. The
record shows the issue closed on
2026-10-28. The blog's '24 years' dating
is not stated in the report and stands
attributed to the blog.

(4) POSTGRESQL (BUG #19758, read
entire). Logged by Alperen Keles. The
report gives the mechanism
(transformAExprBetween copies the left
operand with copyObject; two copies per
level, four for SYMMETRIC; no
CHECK_FOR_INTERRUPTS anywhere in the
parser, so cancels wait for the whole
transform), a 521-byte proof of concept
at depth 20, and the measurements: 1.445
s and about 1.65 GB at depth 20, doubling
per level. The blog's larger case — a
670-byte query, 26 levels, nearly 100
GB — follows the report's own doubling
law (1.65 GB x 2^6 is about 105 GB): the
house verified the law and the depth-20
point, and the far point is consistent
with them, not independently measured.
The report also carries the reporter's
own mitigation (a CHECK_FOR_INTERRUPTS
in the transform recursion), with
measurements.

(5) THE COUNTS, recomputed where a
recount is a bounded act. The paper's
arithmetic: the open-loop per-system
counts (63 + 72 + 16 + 12 + 2 + 2) sum
exactly to its stated 167; the
closed-loop Table 1's bug counts sum
exactly to 80; the appendix's
decomposition (167 + 80 + 72 + 30) sums
exactly to the stated 349 found. Table
1's dollar figures carry a rounding
grain: four of five row totals and two
of three column totals differ from the
sum of their printed cells by exactly
one dollar, in alternating directions —
the signature of totals computed from
unrounded values — and the grand total,
$330, is consistent by both routes. The
repository: its case-study table lists
26 target systems, matching the blog's
'26 well-tested systems'; its Lean index
was counted entire — 25 bug rows, 13
marked fixed, 11 open, 1 reported by
others — matching the README's '25 bugs
in Lean', and the index carries the
Float-pattern bug as fixed. The blog's
totals — 535 found, 373 confirmed, 313
fixed across the 26 systems — were NOT
recounted: the complete list lives in
26 per-target indexes, and an exhaustive
recount is not a bounded act at a polite
pace; the totals stand attributed to the
blog, with the paper's earlier snapshot
(349 found, 300 reported, fourteen
systems) standing attributed beside
them.

(6) NOT READ. Three named reports — QED
(qed-solver/prover issue 13), LiteLLM
(BerriAI/litellm issue 48716) and DuckDB
(duckdb/duckdb issue 27714) — were not
fetched, the fetch budget being spent on
the four above; their existence is
corroborated inside what was read: QED
stands in the paper's open-loop counts
and its case-study list, and LiteLLM and
DuckDB stand as harness targets in the
repository's case-study table. They are
filed on the blog's naming, at 59.1.4.
CAVEATS ON THIS FILING. (i) The blog's
headline totals outrun the companion
paper's, and the two sources count
differently. The paper (October) reports
349 found and 300 reported across
fourteen systems, keeping 'found' and
'reported' apart, with statuses —
duplicate, won't report, not yet
reported, declined — that the blog's
phrasing ('uncovered 535'; 'reported 535
bugs upstream') collapses. The blog
(November) is the later snapshot of a
campaign still running, and nothing in
either text is contradicted by the
other; but only the paper's figures are
internally recomputed by the house
(59.1.3), and the blog's totals stand
attributed. (ii) The bug lifetimes are
the authors' datings. For SQLite the
maintainer's on-record bound ('at least
February 2014') is weaker than the
blog's 'nearly 13 years' though
consistent with it; the OCaml report as
read does not date its bug's
introduction, and the PostgreSQL
report's neither — the 24-year and
21-year figures stand attributed to the
blog. (iii) The blog's PostgreSQL case
(670 bytes, 26 levels, nearly 100 GB) is
larger than the report's measured proof
of concept (521 bytes, depth 20, about
1.65 GB); it is consistent under the
report's doubling law, and the house
verified the law and the measured point,
not the extrapolated one. (iv) The
work's own concession binds the filing:
the results 'do not yet validate
Ephyra's design choices', and the loop
used is reproducible in any PBT library
with handwritten generators and
shrinkers — the concepts at this chapter
accordingly price the loop and its
artifact (1.2.562-1.2.565, 1.2.574),
which the evidence exercises, and not
the library's particulars beyond what
the record shows. (v) The results are
the authors' campaigns, attributed; the
house verified the public record around
them — four upstream reports read
entire, two indexes counted, the paper's
arithmetic recomputed — and ran no
campaign of its own.
QUESTIONS OPENED AT THIS CHAPTER.
Q198: the agreement surface. Ephyra's
differential tester found a defect that
Lean's evaluator and kernel shared
because both consumed the same wrongly
elaborated matcher (1.2.569) — a surface
Chapter 50's criterion does not price,
since independent-kernel checking
verifies the kernel, not the agreement
of elaborator, evaluator and kernel on
one elaboration. Can property-based
differential testing be made a
systematic instrument for that surface —
properties over pattern compilation
itself, run against a proof assistant's
own DecidableEq instances as the oracle
the issue itself cites — and what would
its findings do to the price of a clean
axiom printout? Q199: reward hacking
(1.2.573) is named by the work as an
open problem, not a solved one. Is there
a steering metric for generator quality
that an agent cannot climb without
gaining testing power — a held-out
measure, say bugs found per dollar on
systems the loop never saw — or is every
metric legible to the agent that reads
it gameable in principle, leaving human
audit as the only backstop? Q200: the
counts. The blog's 535 / 373 / 313
across 26 systems stand attributed, the
full recount left undone as unbounded at
a polite pace (59.1.3). Does a complete
recount of the repository's 26
per-target indexes reproduce the blog's
figures, and under what deduplication
rule — the same upstream defect found
under two harnesses, or already covered
by another reporter's issue (the
appendix's 'duplicate' status), counts
how many times? Q201: direct state
construction (1.2.568) reached, in one
campaign, states that years of stateful
interaction testing had not. For which
systems does constructing valid states
dominate interacting toward them, and
can the reachable-state gap be measured
before the campaign is run — priced from
the validity predicate's own structure —
rather than discovered from its finds?

NOVELTY BY AUTHOR. Alperen Keles, the
work's single author, carries this
filing's increments in full: True +1
(the house's verification of the work's
public record, 59.1.3 — one claim carried
whole), False +0, Unknown +18 (the
fourteen new concepts standing
attributed, 1.2.561-1.2.574, and the
four questions, Q198-Q201), Fiction +0.
The running vector stands at 1.5.6.
