THE WORK. Jian Yang, Shawn Guo, Wei
Zhang, Tianyu Zheng, Yaxin Du,
Haau-Sing Li, Jiajun Wu, Yue Song, Yan
Xing, Qingsong Cai, Zelong Huang, Chuan
Hao, Ran Tao, Xianglong Liu, Wayne Xin
Zhao, Mingjie Tang, Weifeng Lv, Ming
Zhou and Bryan Dai (Beihang University;
IQuest Research; Langboat; Renmin
University of China), 'LoopCoder-v2:
Only Loop Once for Efficient Test-Time
Computation Scaling', arXiv:2606.18023v1
[cs.LG], 16 June 2026 — read entire in
HTML form (appendices included) on
2026-11-24. The experiments consumed
1M GPU hours by the paper's own account
(its Section 3.1).

THE ARCHITECTURE (the paper's Section
2). The parallel loop Transformer
(1.2.576, PLT; Wu et al. 2025) attacks
the two costs of sequential looping —
latency multiplying by R, cache growing
as O(R*L*S*d) — with cross-loop
parallelism (1.2.577) and shared-KV
gated sliding-window attention
(1.2.578), leaving latency near a
single block pass and the cache
independent of R (its Table 1). The
price is structural and stated by the
paper itself: at every loop boundary
each token is refined from its
neighbour's state, not its own.

THE STUDY (Sections 3-4). LoopCoder-v2
(1.2.575): one 7B configuration (14
layers, hidden size 5120, 40 heads,
FFN 27,648, vocabulary 76,800 — its
Table 5) trained from scratch on 18T
tokens and instruction-tuned, at R = 1,
2, 3, 4 under the matched loop-count
comparison (1.2.586). The macroscopic
result is the non-monotonic loop-count
effect (1.2.580). The microscopic
result, from the per-loop diagnostic
lenses (1.2.585) over 500 held-out
samples: loop 2 as the principal
refinement site (1.2.581),
effective-rank narrowing across loops
(1.2.583) and oscillatory refinement
(1.2.584) past it, and the intrinsic
offset cost (1.2.582) roughly fixed
while the gains collapse — the
gain-cost view of loop count (1.2.579)
with both sides measured. Section 4.3
adds latent-explicit complementarity
(1.2.587): at R = 2 an explicit
reasoning trace compounds with the
latent loop, super-additively on the
paper's reading.

THE TWO STATE MACHINES the filing takes
from the work, both stated. (i) The PLT
forward pass (its Appendix A,
Algorithm 1): FIRST LOOP (the shared
block applied once with full attention;
its keys and values written to the
shared cache), OFFSET HANDOFF (each
later loop's input formed as the token
embeddings plus the previous loop's
states shifted one position), PARALLEL
LOOPS (each loop's block evaluation
proceeding concurrently across the
token index, attending by the gated
fusion of the frozen cache and its own
window), READOUT (the head applied to
the final loop's states). (ii) The
loop-count selection diagnostic (its
Sections 3.2 and 5): MEASURE (the
per-loop lenses run over held-out
samples — gain as output shift and
re-routing, cost as the offset
measure), LOCATE (the loop where
effective rank peaks and the gain
collapses), SELECT (the loop count set
at the peak, one offset tax paid, no
more) — the machine that turns the
gain-cost view into a deployment
decision without an exhaustive sweep.
THE HOUSE CHECK (run by Emise
2026-11-24; the paper's printed
arithmetic recomputed from its printed
tables by script; the check script and
its run record are kept with the
taking-in). Table 2 (the main results;
eighteen model rows, ten benchmarks and
a printed average the caption defines
as the mean over available benchmarks,
dashes excluded): seventeen of the
eighteen printed averages are exact at
printed precision under the caption's
own rule — the four LoopCoder rows
among them at R = 1 (38.0), R = 2
(46.5) and R = 3 (36.9). The exception
is the R = 4 row: its ten cells average
35.7 against the printed 34.3, a
difference of 1.4 points, beyond any
rounding grain; which side is wrong —
a cell or the average — the text does
not determine, and no conclusion of
the paper rests on the value (60.1.4).
The headline deltas are exact:
SWE-bench Verified 43.0 to 64.4 is
+21.4, Multi-SWE 14.0 to 31.0 is +17.0,
and the three-loop SWE-bench figure,
27.6, stands 15.4 below the baseline
as the text states. Table 4 (thinking
against instruct at R = 2): all five
printed deltas are exact (+26.9, +6.6,
+3.9, +2.7, +2.7), and its instruct row
agrees with Table 2's R = 2 row on the
two benchmarks the tables share
(LiveCodeBench 35.4, MultiPL-E 73.9).
Section 4.3's supporting claim — the
loop alone yields only single-digit
gains — verifies on those same two
benchmarks (LiveCodeBench +8.0,
MultiPL-E +4.4, R = 1 to R = 2). Table
3 (the four-loop model's per-loop
signatures): the column maxima for the
output shift (1.75) and the effective
rank (174.6) stand at loop 2, as the
table's underlines mark; the step
size's maximum is loop 4's 1014, not
the underlined 846, and the angular
change's largest value is loop 4's
0.04, loop 2's -0.72 being largest only
in magnitude (60.1.4). Table 6 (the
code-data composition): the eleven
shares sum to 99.9, the rounding grain
of eleven cells printed at 0.1.
Appendix B's configuration recomputes
to about 7.2B parameters with a tied
head (7.6B untied) against the printed
approximately 7B — consistent. The
diagnostics' plotted quantities (the
30-45x cost-to-gain ratio, the
refinement shares) are read from the
paper's figures, not recomputed; the
check covers the printed tables.
CAVEATS ON THIS FILING. (i) The R = 4
row of Table 2 is internally
inconsistent: its cells average 35.7
under the caption's stated rule; the
printed average is 34.3 (60.1.3). The
direction of the error is undetermined
— a misprinted cell or a misprinted
average — and the filing rests nothing
on it: the four-loop variant stands
last on the agentic pair under either
value, and the non-monotonic claim
(1.2.580) is carried by the per-benchmark
cells, whose own averages at R = 1, 2
and 3 are exact. (ii) Table 3's
caption states that underlining marks
the largest value in each column; the
marks in fact sit on loop 2's row
throughout. They are right for the
output shift and the effective rank,
right for the angular change only
under a magnitude reading (loop 2's
-0.72 against loop 4's 0.04), and wrong
for the step size, whose maximum is
loop 4's 1014. The filing reads the
underlines as marking the paper's
protagonist row and the caption as
overstated; the loop-2 finding itself
(1.2.581) rests on the two columns
where the marks are exact and on
Figure 7's shares, not on the caption.
(iii) The abstract's compression —
'variants with three or more loops
regress' — is looser than its own
table: the three-loop variant improves
six of Table 2's ten cells against the
baseline, and its average falls on the
collapse of the agentic pair alone
(SWE-bench Verified -15.4, Multi-SWE
-3.0). The introduction's 'regresses
on many tasks' is the same looseness
at one remove. The filing's 1.2.580 is
worded from the table, not from the
abstract. (iv) Super-additivity
(1.2.587) is argued on two tabulated
legs and one asserted one: the joint
gain and the loop-alone gain are
printed (Tables 4 and 2); the
explicit-CoT-alone leg — 'does not
improve the non-looped model on these
tasks' — appears in prose without a
table. The concept carries the claim
as the paper's reading. (v) All results
stand attributed: 18T-token pretraining
and a stated 1M GPU hours are beyond
house reproduction, and the released
checkpoint is the two-loop variant
only, so the three- and four-loop
models exist for the house only as the
paper's tables. No False filing: the
one wrong number is a derived display
cell whose direction is undetermined
and whose correction moves nothing.
QUESTIONS OPENED AT THIS CHAPTER.
Q202: the saturation's owner. The gain
collapse after loop 2 is measured only
inside PLT, whose every extra loop pays
the offset tax (1.2.582). Sequential
looped models in the paper's own
lineage gain across dozens of loops
(Geiping et al., to 50). Is the gain
curve's peak at loop 2 a fact about
looping — refinement itself is spent
after one extra pass — with the fixed
tax merely making the crossing visible
at 2, or a fact about PLT, whose
neighbour-shifted states degrade the
gain itself? A matched sequential
family under the same lenses
(1.2.585) would separate the two; the
paper runs PLT only. Q203: the adaptive
offset. The paper names adaptive offset
mechanisms as future work. If the shift
were learned per token or per loop — a
gate between a token's own state and
its neighbour's, priced like the G-SWA
gate (1.2.578) — would the tax fall as
the gain shrinks, and would the
saturation point move past 2, or does
the gain collapse for reasons the
offset never touches (1.2.583), leaving
the crossing where it stands? Q204:
the diagnostic's range. The
effective-rank trajectory is proposed
as a loop-count instrument (1.2.583):
rank falling signals saturation. Does
the rank peak predict the performance
peak outside this family — in
sequential looped models, in
mixture-of-experts loops, in
retrofitted loops (1.2.547) — or only
inside PLT, where the offset gives the
rank's decline a second cause? A
diagnostic that works only where it
was found is a description of this
family, not an instrument. Q205: the
complementarity's address.
Latent-explicit complementarity
(1.2.587) is measured at R = 2, the
operating point where the latent loop
is net-positive. At R = 3 and beyond
the latent loop alone is net-negative
on the agentic benchmarks: does an
explicit trace compound there too —
can the textual channel rescue loops
the latent channel wastes — or is the
super-additivity itself a property of
the single-extra-loop point?

