# The standoff — Paper 042 (Chapter 60) against the book

Paper 042 extends cluster AT for the
third time, and the standoff is first
inside the cluster, now four papers
wide. The three earlier members each
spend the trajectory (1.2.524): LoopCD
reads its states as predictions of
rising strength and contrasts them at
decode (1.2.526, 1.2.530); CCD damages
the context to make a second reference
and gates the contrast by confidence
(1.2.537, 1.2.543); Paper 040's wrapper
manufactures the trajectory on a frozen
checkpoint and integrates it to its
trained endpoint (1.2.547, 1.2.550).
This paper spends nothing. It trains
the trajectory's architecture for
parallelism and then audits the
trajectory loop by loop — what each
pass adds, what each pass costs — and
its yield is a law about the
trajectory's length rather than a use
of it: refinement concentrates at the
first extra loop and the rest is tax
and oscillation (1.2.581, 1.2.582,
1.2.584). [synthesis] The cluster's
centre of gravity moves again: two
papers on reading the trajectory, one
on making it, and now one on pricing
it. And the pricing retro-reads the
others: the founding papers' depth
halving at held accuracy (1.2.533) and
the wrapper's depth fraction rule both
assume the useful trajectory is short;
this filing measures why, on a
neighbour-shifted trajectory, short is
where the gain lives. The composition
nobody has run grows by one term: a
PLT trajectory, manufactured
training-free, read contrastively —
three of the cluster's four mechanisms
stacked, each now with a measured
price. The offset cost is also the
first per-step price the cluster has
held for a trajectory: the earlier
trajectories were same-token, their
steps free of structural mismatch, and
their costs were compute. PLT's steps
are neighbour-token, and the mismatch
is intrinsic — parallelism itself is
what is priced.
Neighbours weighed and held apart, as
at the cluster's founding. J (the look
back inside a run) is the sharpest
pressure this filing brings, and it is
recorded plainly: G-SWA's frozen
first-loop cache (1.2.578) is state
kept across the whole run and read
back by every later loop — nearer to
J's country than anything the cluster
has held. Held apart still, on the
founding's distinction: J keeps states
to be read as a reference — a memory
the run consults — while the shared
cache is not consulted as a reference
but consumed as an input, the second
loop's raw material; and the filing's
claims are not about the cache but
about the trajectory the cache serves
— its per-loop gains, its saturation,
its price. The trajectory remains the
object; the cache is one of its
mechanisms. AU (the generator as the
optimized artifact) is held apart: the
loop count looks like an artifact under
optimization, but no agent rewrites
anything here — each count is a
separately trained model, and the
selection is a diagnosis performed
once, by the authors, on measurements.
AP (reasoning kept as code) is held
apart: nothing is built, kept or
executed between runs. AC (search over
executions) is held apart: there is no
search and no thin oracle; the runs are
the models' own, and the verdicts are
benchmarks and internal measurements.
B (a harness reshaped while running) is
held apart: the harness is fixed; the
architecture under study is the model.
The deep-equilibrium line, cited and
not held as a country, stands as the
paper's own contrast: PLT's loops are
not a fixed-point solve, and its
updates past loop 2 are oscillatory
(1.2.584) where an equilibrium model's
would converge by construction.
