THE WORK. Hanwen Du, Yuxin Dong and Xia
Ning (Ohio State University), 'Latent
Thinking Optimization: Your Latent
Reasoning Language Model Secretly
Encodes Reward Signals in Its Latent
Thoughts', arXiv:2509.26314v1 [cs.CL],
30 September 2025 — read entire in HTML
form (appendices A-K included) on
2026-11-24. A version 3 stands (24
February 2026) and the work appears at
ICLR 2026; the filing reads v1 (62.1.4).
The paper studies Huginn-3.5B, the
recurrent-depth latent reasoner of
Geiping et al. (2025), and asks what its
latent thoughts know.

THE DIAGNOSIS (the paper's Sections
3.1-3.2, Appendix C). Latent
trajectories that end in correct answers
are geometrically unlike those that end
in incorrect ones: correct trajectories
are compact and convergent as the
thinking steps proceed, where incorrect
ones remain dispersed; and across
entropy, effective rank, anisotropy and
intrinsic dimension, correct thoughts
stand higher in entropy and intrinsic
dimension, lower in effective rank,
higher in anisotropy — richer and less
noisy, in the paper's reading. The
differences are present from the early
steps and grow over the trajectory:
correctness separability of latent
trajectories (1.2.602).

THE INSTRUMENT (Sections 3.3 and 5,
Appendix D). A lightweight sequence
classifier is trained on trajectories
the base model sampled on training
problems, each labelled by its answer's
correctness. Read at a prefix of t steps
it predicts the finished answer's
correctness — prefix probing (1.2.606):
ROC-AUC near 1.0 on SVAMP and near 0.8
on MBPP for the recurrent-depth
reasoner. Spent as a reward, the
classifier is the latent reward model
(1.2.601): a scalar price on a thought,
available before the thought is finished
and where no answer can be checked.

THE METHOD (Section 5, Appendix E).
Latent Thinking Optimization (1.2.600)
maximizes expected LRM reward under a KL
penalty against the reference policy.
The optimum is closed-form — the
reward-tilted latent policy (1.2.603),
the paper's Theorem 1 — and Algorithm 1
realizes it exactly by acceptance by
reward gap (1.2.604), its Theorem 2,
never estimating the reference density.
Theorem 3 bounds the price of an
imperfect reward model: the reward-error
bound on selection (1.2.605). The
operating point is small: N = 20 sampled
trajectories, M = 1 accepted, T = 32
thinking steps, beta = 1e-3 by default.

THE STUDY (Section 6). On the
recurrent-depth reasoner across five
datasets, LTO is best in every column of
Table 1 — over the base model by +0.059
on GSM8K, +0.040 on GSM-Symbolic, +0.021
on SVAMP, +0.017 on CommonsenseQA,
+0.021 on MBPP — with latent-reward
weighted voting (1.2.610) the strongest
baseline in every column. Read through
layer states as a latent chain
(1.2.607), LTO improves four general
LLMs (OLMo-7B, Llama-2-7B, Llama-2-13B,
Mistral-7B) in all twenty model-dataset
cells of Table 2, by up to 103 percent
relative. Latent reward models transfer
across domains, and one generalist
latent reward model (1.2.608) trained on
the pooled domains matches the
domain-specific ones. The instrument is
cheap: LRM training runs 0.85 to 6.12
hours at about 10.4 GB on one A100, and
a reward computation costs about 0.076
seconds against 6 to 43 seconds of base
inference per sample. The limits are the
authors' own (Appendix H): the reward is
binary correctness only, and the method
selects — it cannot improve a pool whose
samples are all incorrect, the selection
ceiling (1.2.609) the paper also
measures directly (Appendix G.4).

THE TWO STATE MACHINES the filing takes
from the work, both stated. (i) The LTO
sampler (its Algorithm 1): PROPOSE (N
trajectories drawn from the reference
policy and scored by the latent reward
model, the pool's maximum reward
recorded), FILTER (candidates drawn
uniformly from the pool and accepted
with probability exp((r - r_max)/beta)
until M stand accepted), ANSWER (the
answer decoded from an accepted
trajectory). (ii) The latent correctness
probe (its Sections 3.3 and 5, Appendix
D): SAMPLE (trajectory-answer pairs
drawn from the base model on training
problems), LABEL (each trajectory
labelled by its own answer's
correctness), FIT (the sequence
classifier trained by binary
cross-entropy on trajectory prefixes).
THE HOUSE CHECK (run by Emise
2026-11-24; the paper's printed
arithmetic recomputed from its printed
tables by script; the check script and
its run record are kept with the
taking-in). Table 1 (the main results on
the recurrent-depth reasoner, five
datasets): LTO's row is the maximum in
all five columns, and latent-reward
weighted voting is the strongest
baseline in all five, as the text claims
— the gains over the base row are +0.059
(GSM8K), +0.040 (GSM-Symbolic), +0.021
(SVAMP), +0.017 (CommonsenseQA), +0.021
(MBPP). Table 2 (the general LLMs): LTO
is the maximum in all twenty
model-dataset cells, and the headline
relative gain is exact — OLMo-7B on
GSM8K, 0.124 to 0.252, is +103.2 percent
against the printed 'up to 103%'.
Appendix G.4 (the selection ceiling's
measurement): the correct-pool splits
sum to 1000 at 16, 24 and 32 steps, and
the printed upper bounds recompute
exactly at 16 steps (0.573) and 32 steps
(0.546), and at 24 steps from the
section's own stated split (446/468/86),
as (468+86)/1000 = 0.554. Appendix A5:
the reward-computation speedup over base
inference recomputes between 80x and
566x across the five datasets, as the
efficiency claim requires. Appendix A3's
step sweep is internally ordered as
printed, and its 32-step row's
difference from Table 1's is explained
by its own stated protocol (62.1.4). The
three theorems were read, not
recomputed: the closed form (Theorem 1)
is the standard KL-regularized optimum;
the sampler's exactness (Theorem 2)
follows by the cancellation the proof
exhibits; the bound (Theorem 3) composes
Jensen and Pinsker in the order the text
states. The script's two failures are
one-unit internal inconsistencies:
Appendix G.4's worked 24-step formula
prints (468+85)/1000 = 0.554, its second
addend one below the 86 of its own split
and bound; and the MBPP training set
stands at 374 problems in Appendix F.1
against 'only 373' in Appendix F.2 (both
at 62.1.4). Of forty-six scripted
checks, forty-four pass and two fail in
single units, neither touching a
headline claim. The plotted quantities
(the probe's ROC curves, the trajectory
visualizations) are read from the
paper's text, not recomputed; the check
covers the printed tables.
CAVEATS ON THIS FILING. (i) Appendix
G.4's worked 24-step formula prints an
addend of 85 where the section's own
split (446/468/86) and its own bound
require 86; the bound itself recomputes
exactly. A one-unit slip inside a worked
example, the illustrated quantity
unharmed; the filing does not choose
between the formula and the split beyond
noting the bound sides with the split.
(ii) The MBPP training-problem count
stands at 374 in Appendix F.1 and 'only
373' in Appendix F.2. Nothing in the
results rests on the difference of one
problem; the filing does not choose
between them. (iii) The base model is
spelled 'Huggin-3.5B' throughout the
held text; its makers name it Huginn. A
spelling, recorded because the filing
quotes the paper's usage; no claim turns
on it. (iv) All accuracies, AUCs and
timings are the paper's experiments,
filed attributed: the house recomputed
the printed arithmetic (62.1.3) and did
not re-run a single training or
evaluation. The probe's ROC-AUC values
for the recurrent-depth reasoner are
reported in the paper's figures; the
tabulated AUCs the house could read
stand in Appendix A2 for the general
models. (v) Table A3's 32-step LTO row
differs from Table 1's LTO row on shared
datasets. Checked and resolved, not a
tension: A3's own protocol trains its
reward model on mixed-step trajectories,
a different instrument from Table 1's,
and the filing reads the two tables as
the two protocols they declare
themselves to be. (vi) The filing reads
version 1 of the paper (30 September
2025), per the house's precedent. A
version 3 stands (24 February 2026), and
the work appears as a conference paper
at ICLR 2026; the filing does not read
them, and any change they carry is
outside this chapter's evidence. In the
held HTML the proof of Theorem 3 prints
its Pinsker step without the radical the
final combination requires; the filing
reads this as a rendering loss in the
copy — the combination itself is
consistent — and files no caveat against
the proof.
QUESTIONS OPENED AT THIS CHAPTER. Q210:
the difficulty question — does the probe
price the thinking, or the problem? The
separability study samples trajectories
per problem, where difficulty is held;
the reward model is trained across
problems, where easy problems produce
both compact trajectories and correct
answers. Does the latent reward signal
survive difficulty controls — a probe
trained and spent within problem, or
within difficulty strata — at the
strength the pooled instrument shows, or
is part of the reward a price on the
question rather than on the thought?
Q211: the gaming question — where does
selection pressure turn into reward
hacking? The tilt leans on a learned
proxy; as beta shrinks or the pool
grows, selection can find trajectories
that merely look correct to the probe,
the latent form of reward hacking of the
guiding metric (1.2.573); and the
paper's own bound loosens as beta
shrinks (1.2.605). At what selection
pressure, measured against an
independent correctness check, does the
gain stop following the bound's promise?
Q212: the composition question — do
alignment training and selection
compose? Chapter 61's LOTUS trains the
trajectory into alignment with a gold
chain; this paper selects trajectories
by an internal signal. Trained into
alignment first and selected second,
does the probe still find signal, or
does alignment collapse the trajectory
diversity that the selection ceiling
(1.2.609) feeds on, so the two methods
spend the same resource? Q213: the
other-axes question — is correctness the
only readable axis? The reward here is
binary correctness, and the paper's
limitations name the absence of safety
and helpfulness dimensions. Are latent
trajectories separable along those axes
by the same probing, and can several
latent rewards tilt one policy at once
without the tilts interfering?

