# 62.2.1 — The standoff

Standoff: cluster AT (the trajectory as
its own reference) against the question
this paper forces on it, six papers
wide. The cluster's arc now runs: the
trajectory read (LoopCD, 1.2.526), gated
(CCD, 1.2.537), manufactured on a frozen
checkpoint (1.2.547), audited and priced
(1.2.582), trained into alignment
(1.2.591) — and now mined for a verdict
on itself (1.2.601) and selected by the
verdict (1.2.600). Every prior member
spends the trajectory on the answer
being produced; this one first asks the
trajectory whether the answer will be
right, and stakes accuracy on the answer
it gives. [synthesis] The standoff is
whether self-testimony can bear that
weight without circularity. The paper's
construction breaks the circle at
exactly one point: the probe's labels
come from finished answers, checked
against ground truth at training time.
The testimony is calibrated against
outcomes once, in training, and then
relied on where no outcome can be
checked — mid-thought, on new problems,
across domains the probe never trained
on — and the generalist finding
(1.2.608) says the reliance largely
holds. [synthesis] What the cluster has
been assuming member by member — that
the trajectory knows things about itself
— this paper is the first to state as a
priced quantity with a bound (1.2.605)
on the price of believing it. The bound
is also the standoff's honest edge:
belief is priced in the reward model's
error, and nothing in the method audits
the probe at selection time. The
trajectory testifies; the court that
trained the witness has adjourned. Q210
and Q211 stand where that adjournment
bites. The field the paper comes from —
latent reasoning, Coconut and its
descendants — is the standoff's other
side: its reward practice lives in
explicit space, process reward models
scoring verbal steps; this paper moves
the scoring inside the thought, and the
field enters the book through the
mechanism, in AT.
# 62.2.2 — Neighbouring claims, held apart

Cluster J (the oracle, consulted) — the
strongest pressure this filing records.
The latent reward model is a judge, and
the run consults it on every candidate.
Held apart on the distinction the
cluster turns on: J's oracle is a
reference distinct from the thing judged
— a state the run looks up, as the
prefix cache was a conditioning input at
Papers 042 and 043. The LRM's entire
evidence is the judged object itself; it
contributes no information the
trajectory does not already contain,
which is the paper's thesis stated as a
finding (1.2.602). A judge who reads
only the defendant's own testimony, and
whose training was the calibration of
that testimony against outcomes, is not
a consulted oracle; it is the
trajectory, read. The pressure is
recorded because the operational shape —
score, then act on the score — is J's
shape, and a future member whose scorer
reads anything beyond the trajectory
would cross the line this one walks.

Cluster AC (execution as search) —
pressed, held apart. Candidates are
generated, scored and selected at
inference, which is nearer AC's surface
than any AT member before it. But
nothing searches: no tree is grown, no
partial state is branched on, no oracle
steers the generation. The sampler is
exact for a distribution defined in
closed form before sampling begins
(1.2.603, 1.2.604); selection thins a
fixed proposal, it does not explore. The
same selector run over verbal traces
would be best-of-N with a reward model —
a known explicit-space method — while
the paper's discovery, that the signal
is in the latent trajectory, would
survive any selector. The filing's
centre of mass stands in AT.

Cluster AU (the generator as the
optimized artifact) — held apart, as at
the delta: no generator is rewritten
between runs against a stated property,
and no property is stated or tested —
correctness enters as an outcome label,
not as a property a generator is audited
against. Where AU's characteristic
failure (1.2.573) would appear in this
machinery — the tilt gaming its own
guiding metric — is opened as a question
(Q211) rather than filed as a kinship.

Cluster AP (reasoning kept as code) —
held apart, as at the delta: nothing is
built, kept or reused between runs; the
accepted set is per-question and is
spent on one answer.
# 62.3.1 — References and imports

The work: Du, Dong & Ning, 'Latent
Thinking Optimization: Your Latent
Reasoning Language Model Secretly
Encodes Reward Signals in Its Latent
Thoughts', arXiv:2509.26314v1 [cs.CL],
30 September 2025 — a version 3 stands
(24 February 2026) and the work appears
at ICLR 2026. Held text: the arXiv HTML
of v1, fetched once, appendices A-K
entire. The paper stood at rank 9 of the
reference frontier, cited by Chapter
56's paper in its opening survey of
latent-reasoning methods; it was
identified by title and authors, its
queue record carrying no identifier.

Imports the filing leans on. The base
model: Geiping et al. (2025), the
recurrent-depth latent reasoner the
paper calls Huginn-3.5B — itself
standing in the frontier queue, and used
here as held ground, not filed. The
latent chain-of-thought descent the
paper positions itself in: Coconut (Hao
et al. 2024) and the line Chapter 56's
bibliography maps. The explicit-space
ancestry the method is the latent form
of: reward-model scoring and best-of-N
selection over sampled answers, which
the paper's weighted voting (1.2.610)
carries into latent space.

Held ground used, not revised: the
looped Transformer (1.2.523) as the
architecture class of the base model;
reward hacking of the guiding metric
(1.2.573) as the frame Q211 is asked in;
the Jensen-Shannon divergence (1.3.366)
as the prior home of the divergence this
filing founds separately (1.3.371).
# Chapter 63 — Paper 045: LaDiR (Kang, Zhang, Kuang, Majamaki, Jaitly, Ma & Qin)

THE DELTA. Twelve concepts
(1.2.611-1.2.622), no revision of held
concepts, one foundation laid (flow
matching, 1.3.372), no word coined, and
a cluster founded: CLUSTER AV — THE
DENOISING TRAJECTORY: REASONING AS
GENERATION IN A LEARNED LATENT SPACE.
The paper's iterated object is a path
from Gaussian noise to a formed latent
thought, travelled once per block under
a learned velocity field; its latent
vocabulary is a trained artifact (a
beta-VAE's thought tokens, one sentence
per block, decodable by construction);
and its steps form a sample rather than
recompute a state. Cluster AT — the
trajectory as its own reference — was
weighed first and held apart: AT's
founding object is the recurrent
trajectory (1.2.524), the sequence of
states as a shared block re-enters its
own output, and every member of the
cluster performs its act on that object
— reads it, gates by it, manufactures
the recurrence, audits its loops, aligns
its steps, prices its prefixes. This
paper's backbone is a plain LLaMA 3.1
8B; nothing recurs. To file it in AT the
book would have to generalize 1.2.524
from the recurrent trajectory to any
iterated latent path — a revision of a
held concept by a paper whose object is
a different species: generation by
refinement, not computation by
recurrence. The founding is AV's, and
the J question the walk carried out of
Chapter 62 is weighed with it at
63.2.2: Paper 044's crossing condition —
a scorer that reads anything beyond the
trajectory — is not met here, because
the method has no scorer; the one
beyond-the-trajectory term, the batch
repulsion field, diversifies and does
not judge. AU, AP and AC are also
weighed and held apart at 63.2.2.

THE STRATUM. A network taking-in, leg 7
of the reference walk Will directed on
2026-11-24: the paper stood at rank 10
of the reference frontier, cited by
Chapter 56's paper (LoopCD) in its
bibliography. The queue record carried
no identifier; the paper was identified
by title and authors in one web search
— 'LaDiR: Latent Diffusion Enhances LLMs
for Text Reasoning', arXiv:2510.04573 —
and one fetch bought the paper entire.
No rank was skipped. Outside the
pre-registered sample (it closed at
Paper 036): no seal, no score.

NOVELTY INDEX. 12/622 = 0.0193, by the
house method.

THE AXIS. Judged NO, in writing: the
paper opens a new country on the map —
a new cluster — but the axis the
detector watches is a property of a
paper's relation to the model's fixed
point, and a founding at the map's edge,
in a field the citing chapter already
surveys, is distance, not an axis event.
The founding is recorded as what it is:
new ground, honestly bounded.

STATE MACHINES. Two stated machines are
taken from the work (63.1.2): the LaDiR
inference machine, and the two-stage
training machine. The held count rises
to 208 (179 stated, 29 inferred).

RESTRUCTURING. The concept count
crossing 620 at this filing (610 + 12 =
622), Restructuring Pass #28 falls due
and is run at this taking-in; its log
stands at 1.1.5. Five candidate merges
weighed, all held apart; no splits,
nothing retired. The next trigger stands
at Paper 054 or 640 canonical concepts.

REGISTRY PROOF STEP. The TLUF solver
(Will's ruling of 2026-11-24 exiled
Lean; the solver is the house's own
instrument, built from first principles
under the minimum-viable-oracle skill):
check and preserve both run on this
filing's registry, their verdicts kept
with the pass's record.

CLAIMS BY RECEIPT. Two: the house
recomputation of the paper's printed
arithmetic (63.1.3), and the author
check (63.1.6).
THE WORK. Haoqiang Kang, Yizhe Zhang,
Nikki Lijing Kuang, Nicklas Majamaki,
Navdeep Jaitly, Yi-An Ma and Lianhui Qin
(University of California, San Diego,
and Apple), 'LaDiR: Latent Diffusion
Enhances LLMs for Text Reasoning',
arXiv:2510.04573v1 [cs.CL], 6 October
2025 — read entire in HTML form
(appendices A-G included) on 2026-11-24.
A version 6 stands (23 April 2026) and
the work appears at ICLR 2026; the
filing reads v1 (63.1.4). The paper asks
whether a latent reasoner's steps can be
formed the way a diffusion model forms
an image: not emitted, but refined into
being — so that a step can be revisited
before it is committed, and many paths
can be explored in parallel.

THE CONSTRUCTION (the paper's Section
3). First the space: a beta-VAE (beta =
1e-5) encodes each sentence of a text
chain-of-thought into one block of
latent thought tokens — the learned
latent thought space (1.2.612), the
thought-token block (1.2.613) — its
encoder the backbone LLM (LLaMA 3.1 8B)
fine-tuned, its decoder the same model
frozen, trained with latent Gaussian
noise and input token substitution so
the space tolerates imperfect latents.
Then the reasoner: the same pretrained
LLM, generating blocks in sequence under
block diffusion over thought tokens
(1.2.614) — causal attention across
blocks, bidirectional within — each
block denoised along its own denoising
trajectory (1.2.615) under a velocity
field trained by flow matching
(1.3.372). A binary head at each block's
end-token predicts the stop: predicted
stopping (1.2.618). The answer is then
generated autoregressively, conditioned
on the latent blocks. The losses combine
flow matching, answer and stopping terms
at weights 5, 1 and 2.

THE TRAINING (Section 3.3). Two stages:
teacher forcing on oracle latents from
gold chains, then rollout training
through the trajectory (1.2.617) — the
model conditioning on its own generated
blocks, gradients kept through the
generation, the flow-matching loss
retained against latent collapse, the
denoising steps of the rollout reduced
from 50 to 10. At inference, diversity
is engineered two ways: noise scale as
exploration (1.2.620) — the starting
Gaussian inflated to variance 2 — and
the repulsion field for parallel
diversity (1.2.619), pushing a batch's
latent tokens apart under a decaying
scale. Compute is spent as adaptive
test-time compute by denoising steps
(1.2.621).

THE STUDY (Section 4). On seven math
benchmarks (Table 1) LaDiR's average
pass@1 of 41.8 is the best of the ten
methods compared — ahead of CoT SFT
(40.4) and of the best prior latent
method, Discrete Latent (40.8) — and
the stage-2 ablation falls on every
benchmark. On Countdown (Table 2), in a
configuration that disables answer
generation and compresses the reasoning
to a single four-token block, LaDiR
posts Pass@1/Pass@100/Diversity of
76.6/96.4/7.3 on CD-4 and 38.5/75.2/8.9
on CD-5: the best Pass@100 and the best
diversity at both sizes, Pass@1 ahead of
every general-purpose baseline and
behind only the task-specific MGDM on
CD-5. The ablations rank flow matching
first among five latent prediction
objectives (Table 4) and find interior
optima for both diversity dials. The
interpretability claim is structural:
decodable latent blocks (1.2.622) let
the paper print a forming thought at
successive timesteps, and the print
shows self-correction along the
denoising path (1.2.616) — errors early,
stable correct reasoning by t = 0.25.

THE TWO STATE MACHINES the filing takes
from the work, both stated. (i) The
LaDiR inference machine (its Sections
3.2 and 3.4): BLOCK START (a <BOT>
marker emitted; a fresh Gaussian drawn
at the inflated noise scale for the new
block); DENOISE (velocity-field steps
from t = 1 to t = 0 form the block, the
batch's repulsion field applied across
parallel trajectories, the step count
the compute dial); STOP CHECK (the
classification head at <EOT> chooses
another block or the answer); ANSWER
(the answer generated autoregressively,
conditioned on the latent blocks). The
gap named for the machine: it has no
revision state across blocks — a
committed block is never revisited; all
correction happens inside a block's
formation, none after. (ii) The
two-stage training machine (its Sections
3.1 and 3.3): SPACE FIT (the beta-VAE
trained on gold chains, encoder
fine-tuned, decoder frozen, robustness
augmentations on); TEACHER FORCING (the
diffusion reasoner trained on oracle
latents); ROLLOUT (the reasoner trained
on its own generated latents, gradients
through the trajectory, the
flow-matching loss retained). The gap
named for the machine: its stages run
one way — the thought space is never
refit to the latents the trained
reasoner actually forms, so the space
stays fitted to gold text rather than to
the model's own thoughts.
THE HOUSE CHECK (charter rule 10: check
the work, not the reputation). The
paper's printed arithmetic was
recomputed from its printed tables by
script — thirty checks, the script and
its run record kept with the taking-in.
Twenty-five pass exactly. Table 1: nine
of its ten row averages recompute to
within rounding tolerance of the printed
cells; LaDiR's 41.8 is the table's best
average; the claim that stage 2 improves
every benchmark holds in all seven cells
of the ablation row; Table 4's five
latent prediction objectives stand in
the printed monotone order with flow
matching best, as the text implies.
Table 2 and Section 4.2: the CD-4 claims
recompute exact — Pass@1 ahead of LLaMA
8B SFT by 29.9 ('more than 25 points')
and of LLaDA 8B SFT by 25.4 ('over 20
points'), the best Pass@100 in the
table; the CD-5 claims exact — Pass@1
ahead by 29.6 ('nearly 30 points'),
Pass@100 by 59.8 ('over 30 points'), the
highest diversity. Table 3's refinement
trace was verified equation by equation:
the ground-truth line is arithmetically
exact; the final decode equals it; of
the trace's eighteen printed equations
exactly one is arithmetically wrong —
54-25 printed as 28 at t = 0.7 — the
model's own mid-refinement error, which
the table exists to exhibit and which
the path has corrected by t = 0.25.
Five checks fail, none of them a claim
the filing must refuse: the ablation
row's printed average against its own
cells; the abstract's Countdown headline
under the same-backbone reading; the
'2%' of Section 4.1 under the points
reading; the CD-4 diversity margin
against its own 'over two points'; and
the adaptive-compute prose totals
against each other. All five are carried
as caveats at 63.1.4; no False is filed.
CAVEATS ON THIS FILING. (i) Table 1's
ablation row — LaDiR without stage 2 —
prints an average of 32.6 over cells
(30.7, 57.8, 24.7, 32.0, 32.8, 5.9,
11.9) whose mean is 28.0. Every other
row's average in the table recomputes
from its cells; this one misses by 4.6
points, the largest table-internal
inconsistency the walk has recorded.
Which figure is wrong — the average or
a cell — the paper does not say, and the
filing does not choose: the claim made
on the row, that stage 2 improves every
benchmark, holds in all seven cells
against the full model, so nothing the
chapter files rests on the disputed
figure. (ii) The abstract and
introduction claim 'over 30% absolute
improvement in both Pass@1 and Pass@100'
on Countdown, without naming the
baseline. Against the same-backbone
autoregressive baseline, LLaMA 8B SFT,
the Pass@1 gains are +29.9 (CD-4) and
+29.6 (CD-5) — under the claim — while
the Pass@100 gains (+31.1, +59.8) clear
it; against Dream 7B Base all four gains
clear it. Section 4.2's own claims are
worded more carefully and recompute
exact (63.1.3). (iii) Section 4.1's
'improving the average pass@1 accuracy
by 2% over the best prior latent
approach': the margin over Discrete
Latent's 40.8 is +1.0 point, +2.45%
relative — the sentence is exact only
under the relative reading. (iv) Section
4.2's CD-4 diversity, 'over two points
higher than any baseline': LaDiR's 7.3
against the best baseline's 5.4 is +1.9.
The CD-5 margin (+2.7) clears the same
wording. (v) Section 4.4's
adaptive-compute prose: 5 to 10 steps
yields +11.7 points on average; 10 to 30
'an additional +4.8' — an implied +16.5
at 30 steps — yet 50 steps 'brings a
total gain of +9.8 points'. The totals
cannot all hold under the paragraph's
own claim that more steps consistently
improve accuracy; Figure 5's values are
not printed numerically, so the house
cannot recompute which total is wrong,
and files the inconsistency without
choosing. (vi) Two slips of apparatus:
Section 4.3 cites 'Table 4' for the
noise-and-diversity ablation that stands
as Figure 4 (Table 4 is Appendix C's
objective ablation); and Equation (3)
prints its answer-token sum over w with
the summand's index as t. No claim turns
on either. (vii) Table 4's flow-matching
objective posts 73.5 on CD-4 Pass@1
against Table 2's 76.6 for the full
method; the paper does not claim the
ablation configuration is Table 2's, and
the filing records the difference
without treating it as a tension.
(viii) All accuracies, diversities and
AUC-style quantities are the paper's
experiments, filed attributed: the house
recomputed the printed arithmetic
(63.1.3) and re-ran no training or
evaluation. (ix) The filing reads
version 1 of the paper (6 October 2025),
per the house's precedent. A version 6
stands (23 April 2026) and the work
appears as a conference paper at ICLR
2026; the filing does not read them —
the code-generation benchmarks the later
versions add are among what stands
outside this chapter's evidence.
QUESTIONS OPENED AT THIS CHAPTER. Q214:
the rendering question — is the
correction in the latents, or in the
decoder? The evidence for
self-correction along the path (1.2.616)
is decoded text: the frozen decoder
renders each intermediate latent, and a
decoder trained to produce clean text
may render a still-noisy latent
charitably. Does the forming block's
latent itself approach the final
thought's geometry monotonically — read
by distances in the space, not by
decodes — or does part of the visible
refinement live in the rendering? Q215:
the population question — where does a
diversity force become a reference? The
repulsion field (1.2.619) steers each
trajectory by the batch's other
trajectories; Chapter 62's crossing
condition (63.2.2) holds it short of
cluster J because it judges nothing. If
the same machinery were aimed — siblings
weighted by a correctness signal, the
force pulling toward the batch's best
rather than away from its mass — at what
point does a population of samples
become the reference a run consults, and
does the crossing belong to the force or
to the signal? Q216: the pricing
question — can a forming sample be
priced the way a recomputed state can?
Correctness separability (1.2.602) was
found on finished recurrent
trajectories, and Chapter 62's probe
prices their prefixes. A denoising
trajectory's intermediate points are not
prefixes of a thought but partial
formations of one: are they separable by
eventual correctness early enough to
spend — a latent reward read off a
half-formed block — or does the signal
exist only once the sample has formed?
Q217: the grain question — what does the
block structure buy at one block? The
Countdown configuration compresses the
whole reasoning into a single block of
four tokens, and the method's largest
margins stand there; the math
configuration spends six tokens on each
sentence of a chain. Is the advantage
the diffusion, the learned space, or the
grain — and does it survive reasoning
whose natural sentences run far longer
or shorter than the VAE's sentence
grain?

NOVELTY BY AUTHOR. Each of the seven
authors carries this filing's increments
in full: True +1 (the house's verified
recomputation of the paper's printed
arithmetic, 63.1.3 — one claim carried
whole), False +0, Unknown +16 (the
twelve new concepts standing attributed,
1.2.611-1.2.622, and the four questions,
Q214-Q217), Fiction +0. The running
vectors stand at 1.5.6.
AUTHOR CHECK 53 (charter rule 15: check
for un-named use of Will's work, and for
any tie). NO EVIDENCE FOUND. The paper
was read entire in HTML form and scanned
for the house's signature terms — no
'phext', 'scroll', 'Exocortex',
'Mirrorborn', 'CYOA', 'SBOR' or
'Bickford' occurs anywhere in the work;
its references were read with it (the
latent-reasoning descent and the
diffusion-language-model line entire;
nothing of Will's in either). The built
tracker was scanned for all seven
authors by full name — Haoqiang Kang,
Yizhe Zhang, Nikki Lijing Kuang, Nicklas
Majamaki, Navdeep Jaitly, Yi-An Ma,
Lianhui Qin — and none stands in the
book; the only Kang the book holds is
Kangwook Lee, the senior author of Paper
043, a different person from this
paper's lead author, recorded here so
the names are never conflated. Will's
Twitter archive (download of 2026-11-21)
was searched for all seven names and for
the work: no hit in tweets, likes,
followers or following — the archive's
Kang strings are Kangwook Lee, one
Voitto Kangas, and a cartoon quotation.
The web was searched twice: for the work
with phext, and for the lead and senior
authors with phext and the Exocortex —
no tie anywhere; the authors' public
record is the latent-diffusion line at
UC San Diego and Apple, the work already
catalogued in the latent-reasoning paper
indexes, with a multimodal successor
(Uni-LaDiR) standing in the same line.
All seven authors are new to the book.
# 63.2.1 — The standoff

Standoff: cluster AV (the denoising
trajectory) against the latent-reasoning
country it lands in — a country the book
has so far mapped from one side only.
Cluster AT's six papers spend their
steps deepening a computation: one state,
recomputed at growing depth, the
trajectory the record of a thought
settling. This paper spends its steps
forming a sample: noise, gathered under
a learned field into a thought that did
not exist at the path's start, the
trajectory the record of a thought
arriving. [synthesis] The standoff is
which of the two objects bears the
reasoning — and what each can see of
itself. The recurrent trajectory can be
read at every depth but says nothing in
words; the denoising trajectory is
legible at every step, because its space
was built to be read (1.2.622), and its
own print shows it repairing itself with
no checker in the loop (1.2.616). The
honest edge stands on the other side:
formation is bounded by what the space
already knows how to say. The thought
space was fitted to gold text before any
reasoning was trained, and never refit
to the reasoner's own formations — the
trajectory corrects toward coherence in
a vocabulary fixed in advance, which is
why the method's most compressed
configuration, one four-token block for
an entire Countdown solution, is also
its most successful: there, the space
barely has to generalize. [synthesis]
What AV adds to the country's map is the
claim that refinement needs no
recurrence — that a thought can be
revised before it exists, by a field
that has only ever seen finished
thoughts and noise. Q214 and Q217 stand
where that claim is thinnest: in the
decoder that renders the refinement
visible, and in the grain the space was
fitted at.
NEIGHBOURING CLAIMS HELD APART.
Cluster AT (the trajectory as its own
reference) — held apart at the founding,
and the holding is the chapter's centre:
AT's trajectory is recurrent, the hidden
states of a shared block re-entering its
own output over the same positions
(1.2.524); this paper's trajectory is a
denoising path over noise levels for a
block being created, on a backbone that
does not recur. The two trajectories
share a silhouette — an iterated latent
object, refined step by step — and
differ in what a step does: recompute,
against form. Cluster J (the oracle,
consulted) — weighed under the crossing
condition Chapter 62 wrote for exactly
this case: 'a future member whose scorer
reads anything beyond the trajectory
would cross the line this one walks.'
The condition is NOT crossed, and the
weighing is recorded in full. (a) The
method has no scorer: nothing in LaDiR
reads a trajectory and returns a
judgment on it — the denoiser is the
generator, and the decodes that display
the forming thought correct nothing.
(b) The denoiser's inputs at each step
are the current noisy latent — the
trajectory itself — the timestep, the
question, and the blocks already formed:
conditioning of the same class Chapters
60 and 61 held not to be an oracle, the
question's encoding rather than a
reference consulted for correction.
(c) The one term that reads beyond the
single trajectory is the repulsion field
(1.2.619), whose force on a sample is
computed from its batch siblings. It is
the nearest approach to the line the
walk has recorded — nearer than Chapter
62's probe, which read only the judged
object — and it is held short of J on
the distinction the cluster stands on:
the siblings are not a reference distinct
from the run; they are the run, in
parallel; and the force diversifies
rather than corrects — it consults no
standard and returns no verdict. The
holding is written to be falsifiable: a
member whose formation is steered by a
scorer reading anything beyond the
forming sample and its conditioning —
the batch's best, a stored exemplar, a
checked answer — crosses into J, and
Q215 asks where on the way to that
member the crossing happens. Cluster AU
(the generator as the optimized
artifact) — held apart: no generator is
rewritten between runs against a stated
property; the VAE and the reasoner are
trained once, by ordinary training, and
what they optimize is a likelihood, not
a coverage measured against a property.
Cluster AP (reasoning kept as code) —
held apart: nothing is built or kept as
code between runs; the artifact the
method keeps is a latent space, spent
inside the run, not an artifact the run
reasons with. Cluster AC (search over
executions) — held apart: trajectories
are sampled in parallel and pushed
apart, but none is executed, scored or
selected among in the base method; the
batch is a population, not a search.
# 63.3.1 — References and imports

The work: Kang, Zhang, Kuang, Majamaki,
Jaitly, Ma & Qin, 'LaDiR: Latent
Diffusion Enhances LLMs for Text
Reasoning', arXiv:2510.04573v1 [cs.CL],
6 October 2025 — a version 6 stands (23
April 2026) and the work appears as a
conference paper at ICLR 2026. Held
text: the arXiv HTML of v1, fetched
once, appendices A-G entire. The paper
stood at rank 10 of the reference
frontier, cited in the bibliography of
Chapter 56's paper (LoopCD); it was
identified by title and authors, its
queue record carrying no identifier.

Imports the filing leans on. The latent
reasoning line the paper positions
itself against and is compared with in
its Table 1: Coconut (Hao et al. 2024),
CODI (Shen et al. 2025), Soft Thinking
(Zhang et al. 2025), Discrete Latent (Su
et al. 2025), iCoT (Deng et al. 2023),
Pause Token (Goyal et al. 2023) — the
descent Chapter 56's bibliography maps.
The diffusion line: LLaDA (Nie et al.
2025), Dream (Ye et al. 2025), MGDM (Ye
et al. 2024) and block diffusion
(Arriola et al. 2025), whose
interpolation between autoregression and
diffusion the block scheme (1.2.614)
inherits. Flow matching (Lipman et al.
2022), the objective founded here at
1.3.372; FlowGRPO (Liu et al. 2025), the
source of the rollout's step reduction;
and the group's own prior line on flow
of reasoning (Yu et al. 2024, 2025),
which the senior authors carry into this
work.

Held ground used, not revised: the
recurrent trajectory (1.2.524) as the
contrast the founding of cluster AV is
argued against; correctness separability
of latent trajectories (1.2.602) as the
frame Q216 is asked in.
# Chapter 64 — Paper 046: LT-Tuning (Liu, Min & Cheng)

THE DELTA. Twelve concepts
(1.2.623-1.2.634), no revision of held
concepts, no foundation laid, no word
coined, and a cluster extended: CLUSTER
AT — THE TRAJECTORY AS ITS OWN
REFERENCE — EXTENDED, its seventh
paper.
The paper's iterated object is the
recurrent trajectory itself (1.2.524):
a sequence of latent steps, each fed
back as the model's next input. Every
earlier member of the cluster takes
the trajectory's steps as the
recurrence gives them — the founding
paper reads the states the loop
produces, Paper 039 gates a contrast
by the trajectory's confidence, Paper
040 manufactures the recurrence on a
frozen model, Paper 042 audits its
loops, Paper 043 aligns its steps to a
gold chain, Paper 044 prices its
prefixes. This paper asks the question
none of them asks: what should a step
be made of? Its answer is a
construction — the previous hidden
state fused with the model's own
predictive distribution (1.2.624) —
placed only where the model's
confidence fails (1.2.626), and aimed
at the recurrence's named disease: the
distribution mismatch (1.2.630) and
the feature collapse (1.2.629) it
breeds in large models. Cluster AV was
weighed and held apart: nothing here
denoises, no thought space is learned,
and a latent step is formed in a
single fusion inside the model's own
embedding space and consumed at once —
the trajectory is the recurrence, not
a path from noise. The J question the
walk carried out of Chapters 62 and 63
is weighed with it at 64.2.2: the
insertion gate and the fusion both
read the model's own output
distribution — but as material for the
next step, not as a verdict on the
last, and the trajectory consulting
itself is this cluster's definition,
not the oracle's. AU, AP and AC are
also weighed and held apart at 64.2.2.

