THE WORK. Jonathan Williams and Esin
Tureci (Department of Computer
Science, Princeton University),
'Prioritize the Process, Not Just the
Outcome: Rewarding Latent Thought
Trajectories Improves Reasoning in
Looped Language Models',
arXiv:2602.10520v1 [cs.LG], 11 February
2026 — read entire in HTML form
(appendices A.1-A.9 included) on
2026-11-24. A version 2 stands (12
February 2026), a version 3 (28 May
2026) and the ICML 2026 proceedings
text (PMLR 306); the filing reads v1
(65.1.4). The paper asks why outcome-
reward reinforcement learning, which
works on ordinary language models, had
failed on looped ones — the Ouro
authors themselves report RL bringing
no gains over SFT — and answers with a
credit-assignment diagnosis: the
objective rewards the terminal loop
only, while the architecture computes
a whole latent trajectory per token.
RLTT is the repair built from the
diagnosis: keep the outcome reward,
change where it lands.

THE CONSTRUCTION (the paper's Sections
3 and 4). A LoopLM's sampling policy
reads only the terminal loop's
distribution; the intermediate loops'
distributions — the latent thought
distribution trajectory (1.2.636) —
are computed and discarded. RLTT
replaces the terminal log-probability
in the policy gradient with a weighted
sum over all loops (1.2.639), the
rollout's group-normalized advantage
unchanged, and adds a KL penalty to
the frozen pre-RL policy, the penalty
itself computed on the terminal
distribution. Three loop weightings
are defined — uniform, progressive,
and exit-probability weighting
(1.2.640), the last crediting each
loop the probability of the model's
own learned exit head — and the
experiments run the exit-probability
form. The framework's only assumption
is that a model's per-loop
distributions can be extracted; its
only extra arithmetic is the weighted
sum, and its practical cost is memory:
the per-loop log-probabilities must be
retained, halving the tokens packed
per GPU in the paper's runs.

THE STUDY (Section 5 and appendices).
Trained on MATH alone, on
Ouro-2.6B-Thinking, under conditions
matched to GRPO's step for step, RLTT
beats GRPO on all eight benchmarks:
the mathematical average rises 18.8
points (MATH-500 +14.4, AIME24 +16.6,
BeyondAIME +10.0, GSM8K +34.3) and the
non-mathematical average 6.6, GPQA
nearly doubling — transfer from
training that never saw a non-math
problem. The dynamics tell the
mechanism's story: reward separates
within forty steps and widens; the
responses shorten as they improve,
with no brevity term in the reward
(token-efficient reasoning, 1.2.641);
entropy falls in tandem, and the
Pass@k analysis acquits the fall of
collapse (controlled confidence,
1.2.646). The appendices harden the
claim from four sides: the advantage
persists across decode budgets from
1024 to 4096 tokens (1.2.645) and at
every loop count from one to four
(1.2.644); it survives all three loop
weightings nearly unchanged; it is
significant under sampled re-evaluation
on all eight benchmarks; and its
gradient signal is measurably richer
exactly on the hardest benchmarks
(1.2.643). The theory appendix prices
the shortening: trajectory credit pays
a strictly larger per-token uncertainty
cost than terminal credit, and a larger
cost selects a weakly shorter optimal
length (1.2.642).

THE TWO STATE MACHINES the filing takes
from the work, both stated. (i) The
RLTT training machine (its Algorithm 1
and Section 4.2): SAMPLE (g rollouts
per prompt under the terminal-loop
policy); SCORE (each rollout's binary
outcome reward against the ground
truth); NORMALIZE (group advantages
from the rollout rewards); CREDIT (the
per-loop log-probability of every
emitted token recorded at every loop;
the trajectory-weighted objective
formed with the loop weights and the
KL term); STEP (one optimizer update).
The gap named for the machine: its
credit is exactly as fine as its
weights and no finer — the reward is
one bit per rollout, and nothing in
the machine distinguishes a trajectory
that reasoned to its answer from one
that stumbled into it; both are
credited whole. (ii) The LoopLM
generation machine (its Section 3.1,
with Ouro's halting as the work uses
it): LOOP (the shared blocks re-applied
to the hidden state, a next-token
distribution readable at every loop,
the exit head quoting a halting
probability per loop); EMIT (the next
token sampled from the terminal loop's
distribution alone). The gap named for
the machine: generation discards what
it computes — every intermediate
distribution is produced and thrown
away at inference, and only the
training machine ever sees the
trajectory the generation machine
walked.
