CAVEATS. (i) Appendix A.6's prose
prices MATH-500's two-loop improvement
at +14.4 points; its own Table 9 gives
81.2 against 66.2, a difference of
15.0. The one-loop and four-loop
figures in the same paragraph
recompute exactly (+5.0, +14.4), so
the paragraph's middle number stands
against its table by 0.6 — which is
wrong, the prose or a cell, the paper
does not say, and the filing does not
choose; nothing filed rests on either
reading. (ii) Table 1's RLTT row does
not reconcile at its printed grain:
21.1 minutes per step over the stated
140 steps is 49.23 hours, against the
printed total of 49.05 — the total
implies a mean of 21.02, which prints
as 21.0, not 21.1. GRPO's row
reconciles within rounding. The
direction of the error is
undeterminable from the text; the
row's conclusion (RLTT the cheaper
run) holds under both readings.
(iii) The same table's headline, a
'10.0% reduction in training time',
matches neither of its own bases at
the printed grain: on the printed
totals the reduction is 9.87%, on the
printed means 9.44%. A rounding-up of
the totals' figure, recorded as such;
the relative cost 0.90x itself
recomputes exactly. (iv) Appendix
A.3's summary — that across all
benchmarks the variation induced by
the loop-weighting choice is small
relative to the RLTT-GRPO gap — fails
on one benchmark of eight: on MBPP
the three weightings span 5.5 points
(59.1 uniform against 64.6) while
RLTT's margin over GRPO there is 3.3.
On the other seven the claim holds,
GPQA's 7.1-point spread against an
18.7-point gap among them; the
sentence's 'across all' is the
overstatement, and the filing records
the exception rather than the False —
the claim is a summary of a table the
paper prints in full, and the table
is exact. (v) All accuracies,
entropies, gradient ratios and timing
figures beyond the recomputations at
65.1.3 stand attributed to the work;
the house re-ran nothing. The paired
t-tests of Appendix A.2 are reported
without their p-values in the text the
house holds (the significance markers
ride in the table's bolding), so the
significance claims stand attributed,
their means recomputed only for
consistency. (vi) The byline: the
queue record named three authors; the
version filed (v1) carries two —
Williams and Tureci — with
Russakovsky thanked in the
acknowledgments for support. The
ICML 2026 proceedings version (PMLR
306) carries all three as authors.
The filing follows the held text:
novelty is credited to the v1 byline,
and Russakovsky's position is
recorded at 65.1.6 so the frontier's
record and the paper's are both on
the page. (vii) Version 1 is filed,
as the walk's precedent files the
version the queue met; version 2 (12
February 2026) and version 3 (28 May
2026) were not read. Version 3's
abstract already shows the
experiments revised — a second scale
(Ouro-1.4B) and different headline
gains (+5.8% mean on the 1.4B,
+10.9% on the 2.6B) — and nothing
here speaks for it or for the
proceedings text.
QUESTIONS. Q222, the credit-truth
question: RLTT credits every loop of a
rewarded rollout alike, up to the
weights — the trajectory is believed
whole, including a trajectory that
reached its right answer by degenerate
loops. Does trajectory-level credit
systematically reinforce such paths,
and could a per-loop signal — Chapter
62's probe read as a weight rather
than a selector — sharpen the credit
without becoming the consulted
reference Q215 asks about? Q223, the
weighting question: three very
different weightings tie within noise
on seven benchmarks and split on two.
Is the insensitivity a property of the
trajectory (its loops already near the
answer, so any apportionment works) or
of the benchmarks (too coarse to see
the difference)? What measurement
would show a weighting mattering —
and on MBPP, where the spread exceeds
the method's own margin, which
weighting is right? Q224, the halting
question: the filing trains at fixed
loop depth and spends the exit head
only as a weighting, while the
architecture's native gift is adaptive
halting. Can trajectory-level credit
be composed with learned halting — the
trajectory's length itself trained —
without the exit-probability weighting
teaching the head to halt where the
credit is, rather than where the
thought is done? Q225, the transfer
question: gains earned on mathematics
alone appear on logic, recall,
multi-domain questions and code. Is
what transfers the trajectory
discipline — early internal
convergence, portable across domains —
or general computation the math
training happened to build? A
non-math-trained RLTT run would
separate the two; none stands.

