# 68.2.1 — The ancestor filed after the descendant

THE ANCESTOR FILED AFTER THE DESCENDANT.
[synthesis] The walk reached this paper
twelfth among its network legs and
fiftieth in the book, but the paper is
older than most of what the walk filed
before it: September 2024, the Fan–Lee
line's length-generalization paper,
standing in Chapter 56's bibliography
and under Fan's name in her researcher
entry since Paper 043. Its descendant
has been in the book for seven legs —
LOTUS (Chapter 61), Fan and Lee's later
work, which supervises every latent step
against a gold chain. Read in the walk's
order, the line runs backwards: first
the descendant, which spends supervision
on every step; then the ancestor, which
spends it on none — one final answer,
one step count, and the middle left to
be invented. The field's usual arrow
(methods grow more supervised as they
scale, because scale punishes invention)
is visible only because the book met the
pair out of order; in the field's own
order the arrow is the finding: the same
laboratory, given two more years and a
harder target, chose to label the middle
it had previously left free. What the
ancestor proves is that the free middle
can be enough — on tasks whose iteration
is already known (Q236). What the
descendant concedes is that on tasks
whose steps are not known in advance,
'enough' had not been shown. The seam
between the two filings is exactly Q235:
who supplies the count, and who supplies
the steps, when neither is known. The
book now holds both ends of that seam,
filed in reverse, and the seam is the
sharper for it.
# 68.2.2 — Cluster placement

Cluster AT is EXTENDED — its tenth
paper. The iterated object is the
recurrent trajectory (1.2.524), and the
paper's act is on its training: it
schools the trajectory. Where Paper 043
(LOTUS, Chapter 61 — Fan and Lee's own
later work) supervises every latent step
against a gold chain, this paper, the
earlier member of the same line,
supervises nothing but the final answer,
delivered after exactly the task's own
step count; the trajectory is left to
invent its intermediate steps, and the
invention is what generalizes. Against
the cluster's other acts — Paper 039
read the trajectory, 040 manufactured
it, 042 priced its loops, 044 priced its
prefixes, 046 constructed its steps, 047
rewarded its outcomes, 048 pretrained it
— this one teaches it an algorithm by
withholding everything except when to
stop and what done looks like.

Cluster AV held apart: no denoising, no
learned thought space; the loop runs in
the model's ordinary embedding space on
algorithmic tasks. Cluster AW held
apart: the survey of Chapter 67
describes this family — this very paper
stands in its bibliography — but this
paper performs an act; it does not
survey. Clusters AU, AP and AC held
apart: no artifact is optimized, no
population is run, no anatomy is
performed.

The J crossing condition (62.2.2) is
weighed a seventh time. The condition:
the mechanism consults a reference
distinct from the thing judged, for a
verdict. This paper's two stopping rules
divide on the condition's two sides, and
neither crosses. The oracle rule
(1.2.677) reads the task's step law T(n)
— a property of the problem, known
before the run begins; it is not
consulted about this run, and it returns
a count, not a verdict. The maximum-
confidence rule (1.2.678) reads the
run's own decoded outputs and keeps the
step the run itself scores highest — a
signal drawn entirely from the judged
object, the same side of the line as
Paper 048's convergence exit. No
reference beyond the trajectory and the
task's own law is consulted. NOT
CROSSED, stated falsifiably: a variant
whose stopping rule queried a held-out
verifier, a second model, or a stored
table of correct answers would cross,
and this seam stays open (Q234, Q235).
