each read with the work. Held text: the
arXiv v1 HTML, read entire — Sections
1-6, Appendices A-E, and the full
reference list.
# Chapter 69 — Paper 051: Defining the mathematical family tree with a minimal programming language (Tegmark)

THE DELTA. Fourteen concepts
(1.2.683-1.2.696), no revision of held
concepts, no foundation laid, one word
coined (first strings, 1.3.375), and a
cluster founded: CLUSTER AX — FIRST
STRINGS: MATHEMATICS AS STRINGS THAT
RUN — FOUNDED, its first paper. No
restructuring pass falls due: 696
canonical concepts stands short of the
700 line set at Pass #31, and Paper
057 is not reached; the next pass
falls due at Paper 057 or 700
canonical concepts.

The paper's object, filed where Will's
direction for this taking-in puts it
(2026-11-24: 'we do not wish to observe
presentations — we do not care about
human legibility — crumble math to
first strings'): not the family tree
the work draws, but the string corpus
the tree is drawn from. A Turing-
complete language of nine single
characters (1.2.683) in which a value
is the string that builds it (1.2.684);
six operations and three addresses
(1.2.685); a corpus that grows by
prefix definitions (1.2.686), from
seventeen strings of arithmetic
(1.2.687) up through integers and
rationals to whole presentations whose
sets and functions are alike strings
(1.2.689); finite structures held as
tables, infinite ones as programs, the
triple-dot tableau refused (1.2.688).
Over the corpus, two identifications:
structures as equivalence classes of
presentations under relabeling
(1.2.690), and the coarser clone
(1.2.691), one rung of an observer's
ladder of four (1.2.692); and the
corpus counted — structures and clones
by canonical form (1.2.693), sameness
decided by saturation (1.2.694). The
seven-symbol packing (1.2.695) shows
the alphabet itself is negotiable; the
tree (1.2.696) is the derived view.
The house check executed the corpus:
sixty-nine scripted checks, all exact
(69.1.3) — the strings compute what
the paper says, and the advertised
counts (88 structures, 65 theories)
reproduce from the work's own data
files; the remaining counts and the
Lean claims stand as caveats at
69.1.4.

Its provenance: posted by Will in the
MOAT chat (2026-11-24) as a bare link
to the interactive atlas,
tegmark.org/mathtree; the companion
paper is the held text (mathtree.pdf,
last updated 2026-11-23; 984,766
bytes, sha256 a8e0885a…), read entire.
The work is a Will-posted taking-in
outside the pre-registered sample and
outside the network queue; no seal.

Axis judgment: no axis event. The
paper's claims are about definability,
equivalence and counting; its frame-
level material — mathematical
structures as what physical systems
are, the hierarchy of well-definedness
from finite to uncountable — descends
from the author's earlier mathematical-
universe work and is carried here as
the motivation of Section 3.5-3.6, not
as a claim this filing opens a
dimension for. The string ground is a
new country (cluster AX), not a new
axis: the book has filed languages,
formats and anatomies before; what it
had not filed is a field whose objects
are the strings themselves.
# 69.1.2 — State machines

(M1) THE MLISP EVALUATOR (stated by
the work, its Section 2.4 and Table 2).
States: a stack of binary trees over
the one atom, with the program's
remaining suffix and the bindings of
the inputs i, j, k and of every symbol
the prefix definitions have minted.
Transitions: one per command (1.2.685)
— PUSH 0 (the atom); CONS (c: pair the
top two); TAKE (a or b: replace the
pair i by its first or second part);
DECIDE (d: inspect the first of three
values and continue with exactly one
of the other two, the unchosen branch
never evaluated); RECUR (f: re-enter
the function being defined on the
current arguments); MINT (a prefix
definition X<program># binds X before
the program runs). Terminal state: one
value on the stack. The gaps named for
the machine: it has no error state —
a or b applied to the atom is
undefined, and the machine returns no
value rather than a wrong one (the
prime string at input 1, outside its
stated domain, stops exactly there);
and it has no halting state for a
recursion whose decide never reaches
its base branch — such a string runs
forever, which is the price of the
Turing-completeness the corpus needs.

(M2) THE CLONE-SATURATION MACHINE
(stated by the work, its Section 3.4
and Appendix A.3). States: a set of
generated functions, held as tables,
seeded with the projections and the
presentation's constants.
Transitions: COMPOSE — apply every
generator entrywise to the tables
present, a unary generator to one
table, a binary generator to a pair;
ADD — admit every table not seen
before. HALT: a COMPOSE round that
adds nothing — the set is the clone
(1.2.691), and two presentations
generate each other exactly when their
saturated sets coincide up to
relabeling. The gap named for the
machine: its state space is finite but
not bounded by anything humane — the
binary part of a four-element magma's
clone can hold some 4.3 billion
tables, and the work leaves that row
uncomputed at a cost above 10^5
CPU-years (Q240); the machine decides
in principle what it cannot always
reach in practice, and the work says
so in its own footnote.
THE HOUSE CHECK — the strings executed
and the corpus counted, by script
(~/workspace/thirst-quest/p051/
check_execution.py; run record
check_run.txt): sixty-nine checks,
sixty-nine exact, no failures. The
paper's two worked traces run as
printed: the successor string 0ic on
the input 3 leaves 4; the minus string
jibjbfid — the very program the
abstract names — on (3, 1) leaves 2.
The whole of Table 3's ladder executed
through the reference implementation
(mlisp.py, held from the site): the
constants 1 and 2; successor of 5;
3 + 4 = 7; 5 - 2 = 3; 3 * 4 = 12;
2 ^ 3 = 8 and 3 ^ 2 = 9; 4! = 24 and
0! = 1; equality, not, and, or;
2 < 3 and not 3 < 2; 7 % 3 = 1 and
17 % 5 = 2; divisibility of 12 by 3
and not by 5; P at (7, 3); the prime
test true at 2, 7 and 13, false at 8.
Figure 3's raw strings verified by
construction: the natural 3 as
0000ccc, the integer 2 as 000cc0c,
the integer -3 as 00000cccc, the
rational -2/3 as 0000ccc0000cccc. The
ladder's upper storeys executed from
the paper's own Section 2.3 tableaus:
integer addition (2 + -3 reducing to
the standard form of -1) and integer
subtraction (5 - 2 = 3) through the
helper strings A, R and S, and the
congruence-mod-2 relation (3 ~ 5,
4 ~ 6, not 3 ~ 4) through its strings
E and the equality rung. Figure 5's
isomorphism pair executed: the string
f carries the unary strings of 0, 1,
2, 3, 5 and 6 to exactly the binary
strings the tableau's grammar gives,
and its inverse g carries each one
back. The seven-symbol packing
executed (mlisp7.py): 3 + 4 = 7,
7 - 2 = 5, the prime test at 7 and 9.
The C implementation (mlisp.c,
compiled by the house) agrees with
the Python on every computation run
in both: addition, the minus string,
multiplication, the prime test at 13,
5! = 120. All seventeen definition
bodies of the paper's Table 3 agree,
character for character, with Table 1
as the Python implementation carries
it. The corpus counted from the
work's own data files: the model
atlas enumerates exactly 88
structures; the theory tree's 75
nodes resolve, under the paper's own
classification — seven foundational
concepts in the data, Functions a
foundational concept in its Figure 1,
Triple Fields styled a specific
structure, Quadruple Fields marked
inconsistent — to exactly 65 theories.
The paper's internal numbers: the
19,683 binary operations on three
elements (3^9); the reference sequence
[1] to [41]; Table 5's group row
against A000001 as printed. A speed
spot check on 7!: the C implementation
runs the computation in about 0.9 ms
against the Python's about 0.1 s
including its startup — the same order
as the site's claim of roughly eighty
times faster, which itself stands
attributed (69.1.4). The script's own
first-run failures were its misstated
expectations — a binary zero built by
the general rule where the tableau
gives the atom itself, a prime input
below the table's stated domain, a
reference count taken over the whole
text layer — each corrected against
the held text before filing, and kept
in the run record.
# 69.1.4 — Caveats and the state of the text

No claim of the work failed as stated;
what follows are the boundaries of the
house check at 69.1.3, carried as
caveats. (1) The counts of 34 formal
systems and 14 formal languages —
carried in the work's own summary of
itself — could not be reproduced from
any held enumeration: the site's
formal-system family names six systems
(trivial, empty, ZF, Peano arithmetic,
the Hilbert-Ackermann propositional
calculus, Nicod's), and no data file
the house holds lists 34 systems or 14
languages. They stand attributed, not
verified; the 88 and the 65 beside
them were counted exactly. (2) The 65
theories reproduce only under the
paper's own classification, stated in
full at 69.1.3: a boxes-only count of
the same data file gives 66, the
difference being the node the paper's
figure draws as a foundational concept
(Functions) rather than a theory.
(3) Every Lean claim of the work —
that the definitions, key theorems and
model relations are verified in Lean,
that the public code verifies MLISP's
correctness, and that it includes 43%
of the theorems of Mathlib as of
2026-10-11 — stands attributed. The
house runs no Lean: Lean is exiled by
Will's ruling of 2026-11-24, and these
claims are of exactly the kind the
exile removes from the house's
instruments. They are filed as the
work's claims about its code, neither
endorsed nor examined. (4) The claim
of a 195-character MLISP interpreter
written in MLISP stands attributed:
the string is not printed in the
paper, so there was nothing to execute
(Q241). (5) The prime test's stated
domain begins at 2; at input 1 the
string applies b to the atom and the
reference implementation returns no
value — consistent with the stated
domain, recorded so the ladder's edge
stands on the record. (6) The site's
roughly-eightyfold speed claim for the
C implementation stands attributed;
the house's single-workload spot check
(69.1.3) is consistent with its order
and is not a measurement of the claim.

The state of the text: the paper is a
living document (last updated
2026-11-23) rather than a versioned
preprint; the version filed is the one
held, sha256 a8e0885a…, and the site's
data files were held the same day —
the counts verified are the counts of
that state, and the atlas may grow
under them. The acknowledgements
record that Codex and Claude Code
assisted with plots, tables, the Lean
proofs, Appendix A and proofreading,
that the tree was organized without AI
starting in 1996, and that the author
created MLISP and the programs of
Table 3 around 2018. The paper's own
motivation for the small trusted base
is worth the record: it cites, as its
reference [17], the postmortem of a
Lean kernel soundness bug that enabled
proving false propositions — the work
distrusts large checkers for the same
reason the house was directed to.
QUESTIONS. Q238, the normal-form
question (the work's own, from its
conclusions): is there generally a
canonical normal form — a simplest
presentation — defining a mathematical
structure or a closed mathematical
structure? The counting machine
(1.2.693) picks the lexicographically
least relabeling as representative,
which is a convention of the census,
not a simplest string; whether a
structure has a shortest defining
string, and whether it is unique, is
open. Q239, the group-order question:
two non-isomorphic groups of order
260 are known to generate each other,
and none below 24 can; what is the
lowest order between 24 and 260 at
which two different groups are
compositionally equivalent (1.2.691)?
Q240, the counting question: the
four-element magma row of the clone
census stands uncomputed — the binary
part of one clone can hold some 4.3
billion functions and the work prices
the row above 10^5 CPU-years. Is there
a method (the work names Rosenberg's
completeness criterion as a candidate)
that brings the row within reach, and
do the closed-structure sequences
enter OEIS beside their open
counterparts? Q241, the self-
interpreter question: the work claims
a program implementing and running
MLISP can be written in 195 characters
in MLISP itself, but does not print
the string. Where does it stand — in
the public code, or in the author's
keeping — and when it is produced,
does it run (69.1.4)?

NOVELTY BY AUTHOR. The filing's
increments, per author — the work has
one author, and carries them whole.
Max Tegmark: True +1, False +0,
Unknown +18, Fiction +0. (True: the
executed strings and counted corpus,
69.1.3. Unknown: the fourteen new
concepts standing attributed,
1.2.683-1.2.696, and the four
questions, Q238-Q241.)
