ensemble-theory paper the tree
extends) and 2014 (Our Mathematical
Universe); Carnap & Bachmann 1936,
via Schiemer 2020 (the definition of
structure this filing carries at
1.2.690); Post 1941, Hall via Cohn
1965, Hodges 1993 (the clone, 1.2.691);
Jablonskij 1958 and Slupecki 1939 (the
saturation shortcuts, 1.2.694);
Kearnes & Szendrei 2004 and Zádori
1997 (the group boundary, Q239);
Calkin & Wilf 2000 (the rational
enumeration, 1.2.689); Bishop 1967
(the constructivist ground); de Moura
2026 (the Lean kernel postmortem the
work itself cites, 69.1.4) — each read
with the work.
# Chapter 70 — Paper 052: AIME 2025 (OpenCompass)

THE DELTA. Twelve concepts
(1.2.697-1.2.708), no revision of held
concepts, no foundation laid, no word
coined, and a cluster founded: CLUSTER
AY — THE ANSWER KEY: THE MEASURE AS A
FIXED TABLE — FOUNDED, its first paper.
Restructuring Pass #32 falls in this
taking-in: 708 canonical concepts
crosses the 700 line set at Pass #31;
five merges weighed and held apart, no
splits, none retired; the next pass
falls due at Paper 057 or 720 canonical
concepts.

The work is not a paper but a benchmark
artifact: the American Invitational
Mathematics Examination of February
2025, transcribed by OpenCompass — the
Shanghai AI Laboratory's evaluation
platform — into two data files, one
per sitting, fifteen rows each, every
row a question string and an answer
string. The book has reported numbers
against this key for six chapters —
Chapter 56 evaluates on it, Chapter 57
builds a case study on one of its
problems, Chapter 65 reports gains on
its sibling set — and had never filed
the key itself. What the filing adds is
the instrument's own anatomy: the
integer answer contract that makes its
verdicts mechanical (1.2.698), the key
as a stored oracle (1.2.699), the
exact-match verdict (1.2.700), the two
sittings (1.2.701), the transcription
from a human contest (1.2.702), the
freshness the set is already spending
(1.2.703) — its rows stand in training
corpora in the wild (1.2.704) — the
thirty-problem grain under every score
(1.2.705), the bare row (1.2.706), the
sitting order (1.2.707), and the key's
borrowed authority (1.2.708). The house
check re-derived the key from the
problems: twenty-nine scripted checks,
all exact — twenty-two of the thirty
answers recomputed from their problem
statements (70.1.3); the one malformed
answer string and the eight
unrecomputed answers stand as caveats
at 70.1.4.

Its provenance: rank 16 of the
reference frontier built at leg 1 of
the network walk — the benchmark
artifact cited by Chapter 56's paper
in its evaluation — taken in as leg
13. The queue record carried the title
and the organization but no identifier;
identified by title and organization in
one search, to the dataset's home on
Hugging Face, and held entire at repo
HEAD a6ad95f6 (last modified
2025-02-25): the card, and both data
files (5,764 and 6,285 bytes). The work
is a network taking-in outside the
pre-registered sample (closed at Paper
036); no seal, no score.

Axis judgment: no axis event. The
artifact advances no claim about any
dimension of the models it measures; it
is an instrument pointed at them. Its
one frame-adjacent property — that its
scores are commensurable across models
and years because the table is fixed —
is the examination's design, filed as
concepts of the instrument (1.2.697,
1.2.700), not as a dimension the book
opens.
# 70.1.2 — State machines

M1, the scoring machine [synthesis]:
the loop every harness runs against
this artifact. A RESPONSE is EXTRACTED
to a single answer string; the string
is COMPARED with the key's entry by
equality (1.2.700); each agreement is
COUNTED into the paper's statistic —
pass@k, mean@k over the samples. NAMED
GAPS: no state for a response whose
answer is right but unextractable — it
falls to the miss count, invisibly; no
state for a malformed key row (II-5's
string, 70.1.4), which a strict scorer
counts as a miss for every response,
right ones included; and no state in
which the key is doubted — the machine
has no appeal. Home: 70.1.2; concept
1.2.700. [inferred]

M2, the circulation machine
[synthesis]: the artifact's life,
read off its held state and its
history in the book. An examination is
FRESH at issue (February 2025) and the
artifact inherits the freshness at
publication (2025-02-25); it passes to
CIRCULATING as downloads accumulate —
12,318 at the held state — mirrors
spread and harnesses bundle it; and
rows pass to ABSORBED when they stand
inside a training corpus, a state this
pass observed in the wild (1.2.704).
NAMED GAPS: no transition back — no
state of the artifact records its own
decay, and the rows of a spent measure
are byte-identical to the rows of a
fresh one (1.2.703); absorption is
invisible from the artifact, visible
only from the corpora that hold the
rows. Home: 70.1.2; concepts 1.2.703,
1.2.704. [inferred]
# 70.1.3 — The house check

THE HOUSE CHECK — the key re-derived
from the problems, by script
(check_answers.py, kept with this
taking-in; run record check_run.txt):
twenty-nine checks, twenty-nine exact,
no failures. The artifact's structure
first: fifteen rows in each sitting;
every row's keys exactly question and
answer; thirty distinct questions;
twenty-nine answers bare integers in
[0, 999] — the thirtieth is II-5's,
whose string carries its unit and
whose numeric content the same script
verifies below (70.1.4); the repo's
inventory exactly the card, the two
data files and the attributes file;
the card's two configs naming the two
files, its licence MIT as the card's
front matter states. One cross-check
against the book itself: Chapter 57's
case study names 821 as the right
answer, against a base decoder's 271,
on an AIME 2025 combinatorics problem
— and the pairing problem of sitting I,
the set's one combinatorics problem of
that shape, carries 821 in the key.

Then the key itself, re-derived from
the problem statements, twenty-two of
thirty. Sitting I: the base sum (b + 7
divides 56, the bases 21 and 49, their
sum 70); the heptagon, whose given
quadrilateral fixes sin of the apex
angle at 6/13 and whose area then reads
588 exactly in radicals; the flavor
assignments (2,016 in all, 16 mod
1000); the lattice pairs of the
quadratic (117, counted whole); the
permutations divisible by 22 (N − 2025
= 279); the tangential trapezoid
(r² + s² = 504 from r + s = 24 and the
leg 12); the pairing probability over
all 10,395 pairings of twelve letters
(821 as m + n); the tangent condition
on the complex system (the two k values
23/8 and 123/8, m + n = 77); the
rotated parabola's fourth-quadrant
intersection (a + b + c = 62); the
Sudoku band's count, factorized
(a-weighted sum 81); the sawtooth's
thirty-five intersection ordinates,
summed exactly to (1 + 5√185)/68
(a + b + c + d = 259); and the cube
triples modulo 3^7, counted by residue
convolution (735). Sitting II: the
collinear area (G at height 24 over
the base BE = 39, area 468); the
divisor sum (n + 2 divides 39, giving
1 + 11 + 37 = 49); the grid colorings
(82 of 4,096); the telescoping log
product (106); the nine-point circle's
arcs (DE + 2·HJ + 3·FG = 336 degrees,
the numeric content of II-5's
malformed string); the divisor-lcm
subsets (27,904 of 32,768, m + n = 237);
the greedy coin count (greedy optimal
for 610 of the thousand values); the
trigonometric zeros (149 — the sine's
zero level contributing nine roots in
the interval, not ten, its endpoints
excluded); the chair subsets (907 mod
1000); and the 24-gon's equal-length
matchings (113, the diameter step
contributing exactly one). The eight
answers not re-derived stand at 70.1.4
on the examination's authority. The
script's first run failed four of the
twenty-nine — every failure its own: a
probability taken over 2^15 − 1 subsets
where the sample space is all 2^15;
the negative-sine branch of II-9
missing its period offset, six roots
short; the diameter step of II-11
counted as 2^12 matchings where the
step graph is itself the single
matching; and the sawtooth sum's
constant term dropped in extraction,
one short of 259. Each was corrected
against the problem statements and the
run re-made clean before filing; the
failures stand in this record.
# 70.1.4 — Caveats and the state of the text

One answer string in thirty is
malformed: II-5's stored answer is
'336^\circ' — the numeric content with
its unit attached — where the integer
answer contract (1.2.698) and the other
twenty-nine rows carry bare integers.
The value is right (the house
recomputed 336 at 70.1.3); the form is
defective, and a scorer comparing raw
strings would fail every correct
response on that row. Which
normalization the consuming harnesses
of Chapters 56, 57 and 65 applied is
not in the artifact (Q243).

Eight of the thirty answers stand
unrecomputed by the house (I-12, I-13,
I-14, II-6, II-12, II-13, II-14,
II-15). They stand on the
examination's authority (1.2.708),
attributed, and Q245 asks after
completing the audit. Nothing in the
artifact would show a wrong entry:
the rows carry no solutions and no
provenance (1.2.706), so the key can
only be audited from outside, as at
70.1.3.

The two sittings' parity — I and II
as equal measures of one standard —
is the examination's claim (1.2.701),
carried attributed. The house
verified each key within its sitting,
not the equivalence across sittings,
which no answer audit could show.

The state of the text: the artifact
is a living repository, not a
versioned preprint — held at HEAD
a6ad95f6, last modified 2025-02-25,
its card recording 12,318 downloads
at the held state, that figure the
platform's counter, attributed. The
scores filed in Chapters 56, 57 and
65 were computed against states of
this artifact the house did not hold;
nothing in the artifact versions its
rows, and a silent correction to a
key entry would leave no trace in any
of them. The examination behind the
artifact is the Mathematical
Association of America's AIME of
February 2025; its problems and
official answers are the
examination's, and the artifact's
card says no more than that it
contains them.
# 70.1.5 — Open questions, and novelty by author

Q242. THE SEAM QUESTION. 70.2.2 files
the answer key as a thing in its own
cluster and holds the J crossing
un-crossed, because the condition
governs mechanisms and the key is none.
When a filed mechanism consults a key
inside its computation — not at its
grading — who files the crossing: the
mechanism's chapter, amending its own
placement, or this chapter, whose
object the mechanism consumed? And
does the key's chapter change when the
crossing is filed — does a consulted
key become part of the crossing work's
country, or stay AY's?

Q243. THE MALFORMED-ROW QUESTION. II-5's
stored answer carries its unit as a
string (70.1.4). What normalization did
the harnesses behind the AIME 2025
numbers in Chapters 56, 57 and 65
apply to that row — and if none, does
every AIME 2025 figure in this book
carry a silent one-row handicap, the
same for every model, invisible in the
comparisons because it cancels?

Q244. THE DECAY QUESTION. The set's rows
stand as training samples in the wild
(1.2.704). Is there a detectable date
after which a score on this set
measures memory of the set more than
reasoning over it — and could a
held-out twin, a fresh examination of
the same standard kept unpublished,
measure the decay directly? Until one
exists, every post-circulation reading
mixes the two in unknown proportions.

Q245. THE UNRECOMPUTED QUESTION. Eight
of the thirty key entries stand on the
examination's authority alone (70.1.4).
What would re-deriving them take — the
house's recomputation stopped where
exact derivation from the problem
statement by hand ceased to be cheap —
and does the key's standing in the
book change when the audit completes,
or is a key the kind of object whose
authority is borrowed however much of
it is repaid?

