# 71.2.1 — The substrate describing itself

THE SUBSTRATE DESCRIBING ITSELF.
[synthesis] The walk has run on this
family's weights since Chapter 56:
LoopCD's trajectories, CCD's, LOTUS's,
RLTT's were rolled out on Qwen3
models, and every AT number in the
book is a measurement taken on an
instrument whose construction the book
had never filed. This chapter files
the makers' own account of that
construction — the filters of the cold
start, the 3,995 pairs, the fusion,
the judge — and the account changes
what the earlier numbers can be read
as: not model behaviour in the
abstract, but the behaviour of a
specific recipe, whose choice points
are now on the record. Where the
recipe's choices were invisible, the
numbers looked like properties of the
models; with the recipe filed, they
are properties of the making.

The crossing happened here, and its
address is worth the record: not in a
trajectory paper, where the walk has
watched for it since Chapter 62, but
in a family report's reward taxonomy —
a three-way list in the post-training
section, the middle item of three,
stated in one sentence and never
flagged by its authors as anything
but a practical choice (71.2.2). The
seam the book has kept open through
ten AT chapters was crossed by a
training recipe's plumbing. And the
report is, in the same pass, the
heaviest consumer of the instrument
Chapter 70 filed: its headline numbers
are readings against keys — the AIME
key among them, taken at sixty-four
samples a question — so the book now
holds both the yardstick and the
largest set of readings ever taken
with it, each auditing the other's
standing in a way neither could alone.

The inversion against Chapters 69 and
70 completes a small triptych. There
the territory testified about the map
in its own language, and the measure
was mute and had to be re-derived by
hand. Here the measured thing speaks —
at length, in its makers' voice — and
what it says about its own making is
exactly as checkable as its tables and
no further: the arithmetic verifies,
the scores stand attributed, and the
recipe's claims about itself (why the
fusion holds, what the budget means)
stand where all self-description
stands, one receipt short of proof.
# 71.2.2 — Cluster placement

Cluster AZ is FOUNDED — THE FAMILY
REPORT: THE SUBSTRATE DESCRIBING
ITSELF — its first paper. The founding
is made at the level of the work's
kind: a flagship model family's
technical report — the makers' own
account of how the family's models are
built, trained and measured — and the
cluster is the country of such
accounts: the field's substrate
describing itself, at the scale of a
whole family's practice.

Cluster AT held apart, and weighed
first because Chapter 56 — the citing
paper — founds it. AT's papers perform
acts on recurrent trajectories, and
they perform them on this family's
models: Qwen3 is the substrate the AT
line runs on. But the report performs
no act on a trajectory; it
manufactures the trajectory-generators
themselves — the weights, the regimes,
the template. To file the factory
among the journeys would confuse the
substrate with its traffic: every AT
trajectory in the book is downstream
of this document's recipe.

Cluster AW held apart. AW surveys a
field's papers — latent reasoning as
practised by many hands. This report
surveys one family's own practice: its
twenty-three benchmarks are
measurements of its own models, its
related work a positioning, its tables
a self-portrait. A survey of one is
not a survey of a field, and the
report's centre of gravity is the
making, not the mapping.

Cluster AY held apart. The report is
the heaviest consumer of AY's country
in the book — its headline numbers are
readings against stored keys, Chapter
70's AIME key among them — but a
consumer of tables is not a table: the
report holds no key of its own, and
its scores are claims, where AY's
object is the instrument the claims
are read from.

Cluster AX held apart. The family's
models are trained on strings, and
the report's corpus is strings at
thirty-six trillion tokens — but the
work is not a string corpus, and its
strings do not run: they are read, by
the models the report builds. AX's
ground is a corpus whose strings
compute their content; this is a
factory whose product consumes
strings, a different country.

Cluster AV held apart, briefly:
nothing is denoised, no latent space
is traversed into an output; the
report's latent spaces are the
models', and they are trained, not
sampled from.

Cluster J weighed — and CROSSED, the
first crossing of the walk. The
standing condition (62.2.2, restated
68.2.2): a mechanism consults a
reference distinct from the thing
judged, for a verdict whose evidence
lies beyond the judged object; its
falsifiable forms name a held-out
verifier, a second model, or a stored
table of correct answers. The walk's
prior weighings held every candidate
short on the same ground — the
reference turned out to live inside
the judged object or in the task's own
law: LTO's latent reward model reads
only the trajectory it scores (62.2.2);
RLTT's outcome reward was held short
at 65.2.2 as one bit per rollout,
inspected on the emitted answer alone
— and Chapter 65's own text names the
crossing shape: a method that decodes
intermediate states and has an
external model grade them is a
consulted reference. This report's
general RL contains the device in its
second reward kind (1.2.717):
Qwen2.5-72B-Instruct — a second model,
distinct from the policy judged — is
handed a reference answer it did not
produce and scores the policy's
response against it, and the verdict
is consumed inside the training loop
as the reward the policy climbs
(1.2.716). Reference distinct from the
judged object: yes. Evidence beyond
the judged object: the reference
answer, which the response is measured
against and does not contain. Verdict
consumed by the mechanism: the reward
itself. CROSSED, on the second-model
form. The first reward kind — the
rule-based verifier — is held short on
Chapter 65's own grounds: an outcome
checked against a key is the AY
pattern at grading, and the crossing
is not rested on it. The third kind,
the preference reward model, is not
needed for the crossing and is noted
only as standing on the same side of
the line by the condition's form. The
crossing is filed by the mechanism's
own chapter — Q242's first branch, in
this instance — and Chapter 70's
placement does not change: the
references consumed here are the
report's own training answers, not
AY's key. The chapter's placement
stays AZ: the crossing is one
mechanism inside a family report, and
J gains a resident mechanism (1.2.716),
not a paper; whether a crossing can
ever pull a chapter is Q246.
# 71.3.1 — References and imports

Qwen Team, 'Qwen3 Technical Report',
arXiv:2505.09388v1 [cs.CL], submitted
14 May 2025 — the held text, read
entire, references included: the arXiv
HTML rendering (986,245 bytes, sha256
db251452…). The report's byline is the
team's; sixty core contributors are
named, An Yang first, with a further
contributor list of some one hundred
fifty names; the models are released
under Apache 2.0. The queue record
that sent the walk here: rank 17 of
the reference frontier, cited by
Chapter 56's paper (LoopCD) — the
family's models are the trajectory
substrate of the AT line (Chapters
56-68) and stand in the comparisons of
Chapters 59, 61 and 67 besides.
Imports the filing leans on: the J
crossing condition (62.2.2, restated
68.2.2) and Chapter 65's naming of the
crossing shape (65.2.2), both met at
71.2.2; pass@k (1.3.365), the statistic
of the report's Table 21, laid at
Paper 038; the AIME answer key
(Chapter 70, cluster AY), consumed by
the report's Tables 11-20 under its
own protocol (thirty questions, 64
samples, mean accuracy); and the
adaptive loop count (1.2.676),
contrasted at 1.2.711 — the looped
line's meter is held inside the run,
this family's budget is held outside
it, and the contrast is the concept's
edge.
# Chapter 72 — Paper 054: ALW: Adaptive Layer-Wise Contrastive Decoding (Zhou, Zhou, Zhang, Li & Zhang)

THE DELTA. Eleven concepts
(1.2.722-1.2.732), no revision of held
concepts, no foundation laid, no word
coined: CLUSTER AT EXTENDED — its
eleventh paper, and its first whose
trajectory is a single forward pass.
The founding papers contrast states of
a recurrence; this one contrasts
depths of a plain stack — the final
layer's readout against an early
layer's, both through the shared
output head (1.2.722) — and spends its
novelty on choosing the contrasted
layer per input: labelled offline by
a quantification strategy that scores
gold answers under every candidate
contrast (1.2.725), stored as a
prompt–layer database (1.2.726), and
predicted at inference by a
lightweight classifier (1.2.727) or,
tuning-free, by similarity to a
stored prompt (1.2.729). Cluster J
weighed at 72.2.2 and NOT joined: the
contrast's reference is the judged
network's own shallow readout — the
reference lives inside the judged
object, the ground on which the walk
has held every candidate of this
shape short since Chapter 62.

The work: a decoding framework for
reasoning, from the Soochow group.
Its premise is layer noise (1.2.723):
interference that accumulates across
depth, read as the entropy of each
layer's readout, high in the shallows
and low at the top. Against it, the
self-contrast (1.2.722), gated to the
final layer's plausible set; the
search for each input's optimal
contrastive layer (1.2.724) bounded
to the noisy half of depth (1.2.731),
with the no-contrast label (1.2.728)
for the inputs the final layer
already handles. Measured on five
LLaMA models across eight reasoning
benchmarks against greedy decoding,
DoLa and two-model contrastive
decoding: the method's row leads its
table at every scale, the gains
shrinking as the models grow (a 48%
relative rise on the smallest model's
arithmetic against 9% on the
largest). The house check recomputed
the paper's printed arithmetic from
the held text: forty checkfile
declarations run through the
pre-processor's harness — thirty-
eight pass, two at the rounding
grain, none failing (72.1.3); three
tensions stand as caveats at 72.1.4,
and every benchmark score stands
attributed — the house cannot rerun
the evaluations.

Its provenance: rank 18 of the
reference frontier built at leg 1 of
the network walk — cited by Chapter
56's paper (LoopCD), whose own
contrast ALW predates and parallels —
taken in as leg 15, the walk's first
pre-processed pass: the census, the
registry arithmetic and the check
harness ran through the house
pre-processor (moat-prep) rather than
by hand. The queue record carried
title, authors and year but no
identifier; identified by title and
authors in two searches, to the
Findings of ACL 2025 (pages
8506-8524) — the work has no arXiv
listing — and held entire: the
proceedings PDF, 1,294,842 bytes,
references and appendices included.
The work is a network taking-in
outside the pre-registered sample
(closed at Paper 036); no seal, no
score.

Axis judgment: no axis event. The
paper opens no dimension: it moves a
known contrast along an axis the book
already holds (depth in place of
recurrence) and adds a choosing
machine where the field used a fixed
rule. The choosing, not the contrast,
is the filing's news, and it is filed
as concepts of the work, not as a
dimension of the space.
# 72.1.2 — State machines

M1, the quantification-and-decoding
pipeline [stated]: the method's two
lives, joined at the database. In its
offline life a training prompt is
SCORED under every candidate contrast
of the noisy half (1.2.731); the
winning layer is LABELLED, or the
prompt is labelled NO-CONTRAST where
the final layer already suffices
(1.2.728); the pairs ACCUMULATE into
the prompt–layer database (1.2.726);
a lightweight classifier is TRAINED
on the pairs (1.2.727). In its
inference life each new prompt is
CLASSIFIED (or matched by similarity,
1.2.729); the forward pass runs once;
where the label is a layer, the two
readouts are CONTRASTED under the
plausibility gate and the token is
DECODED from the difference; where
the label is -1 the final layer
decodes alone. NAMED GAPS: no
transition from inference back to
labelling — a prompt the predictor
mislabels is never re-quantified, and
the database does not grow from use;
and no state records which prompts
the gate, rather than the contrast,
decided. Home: 72.1.2; concepts
1.2.725, 1.2.726, 1.2.727, 1.2.728.
[stated]

M2, the hesitation machine
[synthesis]: the life of one token
inside the contrast. The final layer
stands CONFIDENT or HESITANT (1.2.730)
over its next token. HESITANT, the
shallow readout's lean can tip it:
the subtraction REMOVES the shallow
candidate's support and the token
SETTLES on the deep one. CONFIDENT,
the subtraction passes through
without effect — the ranking is
unchanged whether the confidence is
right or wrong. NAMED GAPS: no state
for a token made hesitant by the
contrast itself — the subtraction can
create the near-tie it then settles,
and the machine does not distinguish
a found hesitation from a made one;
and conviction has no appeal — a
confident error exits the machine
exactly as a confident truth does
(1.2.732). Home: 72.1.2; concepts
1.2.730, 1.2.722. [synthesis]
# 72.1.3 — The house check

THE HOUSE CHECK — the paper's printed
arithmetic recomputed from the held
text, by declaration (p054.check, run
through the moat-prep harness; run
record check_run.txt): forty checks,
thirty-eight PASS, two TENSION at the
rounding grain, none FAIL. Table 1's
averages first: the printed Average
of every one of the table's eighteen
rows — five models against greedy
decoding, DoLa, two-model contrastive
decoding and the method — recomputes
as the mean of its eight benchmark
cells, seventeen rows within the
grain and one (the method's smallest-
model row, 44.7 printed against
44.775 from its rounded cells) at it.
The headline gains: the arithmetic
rise on the smallest model is 6.5
points on 13.6, a ratio of 0.478 —
the abstract's 48%; on the 30B model
9.1 on 32.6, 0.279 — the text's 28%;
on the 65B model 4.5 on 50.8, 0.089 —
the text's 9%; and the 65B model's
BBH rise is exactly the abstract's
5.2 points (43.1 - 37.9). The latency
ratios from Figure 4's printed
milliseconds: the method at 1.068 of
greedy, DoLa at 1.096, two-model
contrast at 1.369 — the text's +10%
and +37% for the baselines, and the
method's own figure against its prose
at 72.1.4. Table 9's error
proportions sum to their printed
totals on both sides of the contrast
(86.4 before, 79.5 after). The
ablation tables' delta annotations
recompute exactly: Table 3's six
(BERT-base and RoBERTa-large against
the RoBERTa-base row the paper
recommends), Table 6's five (the most
frequent layer against the
predictor), Table 8's four (a random
layer against the predictor) — every
printed difference equal to the
difference of its cells. The transfer
claim of Table 4: a predictor trained
on MMLU alone scores 40.4 on BBH,
0.969 of the in-domain 41.7 — the
text's 96%. Appendix K's two prose
averages verify from Table 14's
cells: penguins_in_a_table at 82.6
(+53.4) and snarks' rise at 13.1. The
small counts: thirty-two layers plus
the embedding are the text's
thirty-three early exits; the
classes [-1, 16] are eighteen; the
proceedings run 8506-8524, nineteen
pages; and RoBERTa-base's 110 million
parameters stand at a sixty-fourth of
the 7B model's, as the appendix
claims. The predictor's own
distribution (Table 11) sums to
14,307 predictions, its no-contrast
count the text's 5,621.

Two of the harness's shapes could not
be fed from this paper's text, and
the fallbacks are recorded here as
the instrument's backlog, not hidden:
the reference list is author–year
(ACL style), which the pre-processor's
refs shape does not parse — the
frontier classification (six of
thirty-nine references already queue
nodes: DoLa at rank 20, two-model
contrastive decoding at rank 31, the
GSM8K paper at rank 36, MMLU at rank
37, PiQA at rank 38, ARC at rank 42)
was classified by hand against the
queue; and Table 1 reached the
harness as a hand-built tables file,
the paper having no HTML form for
the extractor to read — its rows
cross-verified between Table 1 and
Table 7, which restates the greedy
and method rows entire. What the
check cannot reach stands attributed
at 72.1.4: the check recomputes the
paper's arithmetic, not its
evaluations.
# 72.1.4 — Caveats and the state of the text

Three tensions, each verified in
exact shape at 72.1.3. First, the
latency claim: the text gives the
method's decoding cost as 'only a 6%
increase' over greedy decoding, and
the appendix as '1.06 times' — but
Figure 4's own printed milliseconds
give 161.57 against 151.25, a ratio
of 1.068, an increase of 6.8%, which
rounds to 1.07, not 1.06. The
baselines' printed ratios (+10%,
+37%) recompute fairly from the same
figure; only the method's own is
rounded in its favour, by eight
tenths of a point. Second, the case
study's labels: Table 5 marks its
Case 2 with a cross, and the
generation it shows ends in the wrong
answer, while the prose of the same
section attaches '(Case 2)' to the
claim that suppressing noise
consistently yields the correct
answer. The table's own ALW row does
show the correct answer, so the
method's demonstration stands; the
case labels and the prose's use of
them do not agree, and a reader
following the prose into the table
will find the second case failing
where the text says it succeeds.
Third, a precision note on the
predictor: the text says its accuracy
is 'almost always greater than 50%'
in all settings; Table 2's grid holds
four cells of forty at or below half
— 46.1 (30B, MathQA), 43.6 and 40.8
(65B, MathQA and BBH), and one at
exactly 50.0 (65B, ARC). The hedge
'almost always' carries the sentence,
and the exceptions cluster where the
method's gains are smallest, at the
largest scale; recorded so the
sentence is read with its exceptions
counted. All three are filed as
caveats, not False filings: the
registry's two False filings are
load-bearing claims of a proof,
refuted at the claim's own level;
these are summary prose and labels
against tables that print the true
values beside them.

Rounding grain: two of Table 1's and
Table 14's printed averages differ
from the mean of their printed cells
by 0.07 and 0.06 — the expected noise
of averages computed before their
components were rounded for print,
recorded as grain, not error.

What stands attributed. Every
benchmark score in the paper: the
house recomputed the arithmetic among
printed numbers; it did not and
cannot rerun the evaluations, and the
scores stand on the authors'
harness. The claim that a random
contrastive layer beats greedy
decoding in only 25% of cases rests
on the bolding of Table 8, which the
held text does not preserve; the
cells and their deltas verify
(72.1.3), the count of bold cells
does not. The entropy readings of the
preliminary figures — about ten in
the shallows, about one at depth —
are figure readings, stated in prose
and standing as such. The sub-task
tables beyond the two rows verified
at 72.1.3 stand as printed.

The state of the text: a proceedings
paper, Findings of the Association
for Computational Linguistics: ACL
2025, pages 8506-8524, held as the
ACL Anthology PDF (1,294,842 bytes,
sha256 9a53b507…); the work has no
arXiv listing. The byline is five
names of one institution, Soochow
University; the code is public, at
the repository the paper names. The
scores other chapters of this book
report on related methods were
computed by those chapters' own
harnesses, not read from this paper;
this filing changes nothing in them.
# 72.1.5 — Open questions, and novelty by author

Q250. THE PREDICTOR'S CEILING
QUESTION. The layer predictor
(1.2.727) is right only somewhat more
than half the time, against eighteen
classes, yet the method gains almost
everywhere it is applied. How much of
the gain survives a wrong label — is
the contrast forgiving of the layer,
so that any shallow layer subtracts
most of the noise, or do the
predictor's errors fall on the inputs
where the choice matters least? The
paper's random-layer ablation bounds
the question from below (a random
layer loses), but the distance
between random and perfect is
unmeasured, and it is the distance
the predictor is paid for.

Q251. THE MADE-HESITATION QUESTION.
The hesitation machine (72.1.2, M2)
names a gap: the subtraction can
create the near-tie it then settles.
Does any measurable part of the
method's gain come from manufactured
hesitations — cases where the final
layer was confident and right, the
contrast made it hesitate, and the
settling happened to land well? A
count of sign flips at steps the
final layer had already decided,
split by whether the flip helped,
would answer it; the paper's tables
do not separate the two populations.

Q252. THE DEPTH-AXIS QUESTION. This
filing extends cluster AT along a new
axis — depth inside one pass, where
the founding papers contrast along
recurrence. Is the axis a real
boundary in the territory, or one
country seen from two sides? The
looped line's own record holds that a
loop's inner layers are not exits and
cannot serve as references (Paper
038's filing), while a dense stack's
layers can: the difference is whether
the layers were trained as exits.
What decides it is a contrast method
that treats a looped model's
completed iterations and a dense
model's layers in one frame, and
measures where each reference stops
working.

Q253. THE WRONG-PATH RESIDUE
QUESTION. The right answer on a wrong
path (1.2.732) survives denoising at
a rate the paper shows but does not
bound. Is the residue a fixed
fraction of correct answers — a
property of the answer distribution —
or does it shrink as the contrast
sharpens? The question matters beyond
this paper: every chapter in cluster
AT reports answer accuracy, and the
residue is the part of every such
number that the reasoning did not
earn.

NOVELTY BY AUTHOR — Yuechi Zhou: True
+1, False +0, Unknown +15, Fiction
+0. Chuyue Zhou: True +1, False +0,
Unknown +15, Fiction +0. Jianxin
Zhang: True +1, False +0, Unknown
+15, Fiction +0. Juntao Li: True +1,
False +0, Unknown +15, Fiction +0.
Min Zhang: True +1, False +0, Unknown
+15, Fiction +0. (Each author's
increments in full, per the house
rule: the house check at 72.1.3 for
the True; the eleven concepts
1.2.722-1.2.732 and Q250-Q253 for the
Unknown.) All five are new to the
book; their vectors are drawn fresh
at 1.5.6. The near namesakes stand
apart: Jianxin Zhang is not Jianxin
Ma or Jianxin Yang of Paper 053's
contributor list — the book's Zhangs
are many, and none of them is merged
on a surname.
