# 73.2.1 — The axis gets its instruments

THE AXIS GETS ITS INSTRUMENTS.
[synthesis] Cluster AT's founding
papers each found one thing a
trajectory could do. This chapter's
paper is the first to treat the
trajectory's axis — recurrent depth —
as an economy to be administered:
a prior over depths (1.2.735), a
learned distribution that spends
them (1.2.736), a cashier's rule for
the spending (1.2.737), a second
schooling for the spender from the
run's own losses (1.2.738), fitted
laws that price the whole axis
(1.2.734), and a memory economy that
discards all but the last state's
cache (1.2.745). Chapter 66 pre-trained
the trajectory; this chapter pre-
trains it again at seven times the
tokens and surrounds it with the
instruments of a going concern. The
cluster's centre of effort moves a
second time: Chapter 72 moved it from
finding a reference to choosing one;
this chapter moves it from the
trajectory's existence to its
administration.

The mechanism account is the
chapter's second gift to the cluster,
because it tells every other member
what its gains are made of. Storage
is not purchasable by looping
(1.2.739): the bits live in the
parameters, and the loop holds no
more of them. Manipulation is what
the loop buys (1.2.740) — composition
of what is stored, at a gradient that
favours exactly the reasoning-heavy
categories the cluster's papers
report their gains on. Read backward
through the cluster, the split
reframes the founding results: the
contrast papers, the length-
generalization paper, the rewarded
trajectory — each was spending
manipulation it could not name. The
book now holds the name, and a
construction (1.2.741) that gives
the name a shape: search, with a
frontier that doubles per step.

And the split at the axis's far end
(1.2.742) is the chapter's warning
label. Depth past training is where
the cluster's intuition — more loops,
more thought — stops being true in
the simple direction: the benchmarks
fall while safety rises, and the
paper that built the axis's best
instruments cannot say what the
extra loops are doing. The axis is
administered now; it is not yet
understood past its trained end,
and Q255 keeps the question where
the next deepening paper will meet
it.
# 73.2.2 — Cluster placement

Cluster AT is EXTENDED — THE
TRAJECTORY AS ITS OWN REFERENCE —
its twelfth paper, and the second of
its members to pretrain the
trajectory itself: where Chapter 66
(Huginn) pre-trains a recurrent-depth
model at a trillion tokens and reads
what its trajectory does, this work
pre-trains at 7.7 trillion and builds
the trajectory's administration —
the prior, the gates, the exit rule,
the scaling laws — into the
pre-training itself. The kinship is
not thematic but mechanical: the
same architecture family, the same
question (what does depth bought by
recurrence earn?), answered here at
the scale the earlier paper pointed
to. Against the extension: the work
is a family release, nearer in kind
to Chapter 71's report than to the
cluster's method papers. Held: the
report shape serves the trajectory
here — every section of the work is
an act on, a measurement of, or an
instrument for the recurrent run —
and AZ's founding is for the
substrate describing itself as an
end; this substrate is described in
the service of the loop.

Cluster AZ held apart on that same
ground, stated plainly: this is a
flagship family's release document,
and had its object been the family's
practice it would found a second
member of AZ. Its object is the
loop: the family exists in the work
as the loop's vehicle, two sizes of
it, and the work's own analyses
(capacity, manipulation, depth,
safety) are analyses of looping, not
of the family's making. The border
is recorded so the next family-scale
looped release meets both readings.

Cluster AW held apart: the survey of
Chapter 67 maps the latent-reasoning
field across many hands; this work
is one hand's construction, surveyed
by no one. Cluster AY held apart:
the work consumes answer keys in its
benchmarks, BeyondAIME among them,
but holds no key of its own and
files no table. Cluster AX held
apart: its strings are training data,
read and not run.

Cluster J weighed — and NOT joined.
The standing condition (62.2.2,
restated 68.2.2): a mechanism consults
a reference distinct from the thing
judged, for a verdict whose evidence
lies beyond the judged object. This
work's candidates were weighed one
by one. The exit gates (1.2.736,
1.2.738): the gate reads the judged
trajectory's own hidden states, and
its Stage-II targets are computed
from the judged model's own detached
losses — the reference, if the word
applies at all, is the trajectory's
own past performance, the Chapter 65
shape, inside the judged object. The
built-in draft model (Section 7.3):
an earlier step's readout proposes
and the final step's readout
verifies — but both are the same
trajectory at different depths, the
verifier the judged run deepened,
the shape Chapter 66 filed as the
speculative verifier (1.2.656) inside
AT, not across J's seam. The safety
screening of the same section names
no external reference at all — checks
over logits, beams and candidate
spans — and is a proposal, not a run
mechanism. The RLVR attempts
(Section 4.5) come nearest in kind
and fail on fact: verifiable rewards
were computed, but the attempts 'did
not yield significant performance
gains', the adaptive variant failed
outright, and no RL-trained model
was released — a mechanism attempted
and abandoned is not a resident of
the work, and its verdicts were in
any case rule-based checks of the
judged outputs against the tasks' own
answers, the near side of the line
since Chapter 68. The evaluation
judge of Section 5.2 is the harness's
instrument, outside the work's act,
where Chapter 72's weighing already
placed such spendings. 1.2.716
remains J's only resident mechanism;
this chapter adds none, and files the
weighing beside Chapter 72's, so the
next candidate meets a refusal for
the gate shape, the draft shape, and
the abandoned-reward shape together.
