# Chapter 76 — Paper 058: AIME 2024 (HuggingFaceH4)

THE DELTA. Seven concepts
(1.2.769-1.2.775), no revision of held
concepts, no foundation laid, no word
coined: CLUSTER AY EXTENDED — its
third paper, and its first year pair.
No restructuring pass falls due in
this taking-in; the next falls due at
Paper 060 or 780 canonical concepts.

The work is not a paper but a
benchmark artifact: the American
Invitational Mathematics Examination
of February 2024, sittings I and II,
transcribed into one data file of
thirty rows by HuggingFaceH4 —
Hugging Face's in-house research
team — as a derived benchmark
(1.2.769): the card names its source
as AI-MO's aimo-validation-aime,
ninety problems from AIME 2022-2024,
of which this artifact is the 2024
slice. Where Chapter 70's rows were
bare, these rows are furnished:
every one carries its problem, its
worked solution (1.2.770), its
answer, the Art of Problem Solving
wiki page it descends from, its year
and an identifier inherited from the
parent set (1.2.771) — and the
sittings stand in one file, their
partition carried by the data
(1.2.775). The book has reported
numbers against this key as well —
Chapter 56 evaluates on it beside
the 2025 set, Chapters 71 and 73
name it — and had never filed it.
What the filing adds is the year's
own anatomy, and, with Chapter 70,
the pair (1.2.773): two artifacts of
one examination, read together so
that what the examination fixes can
be told from what each transcription
chose. The house check re-derived
the key entire: fifty-six checks,
all exact — the artifact's structure,
its census arithmetic, and all
thirty answers recomputed from
their problem statements (76.1.3) —
where the 2025 audit stopped at
twenty-two of thirty (70.1.3). The
texts' defects, and the parent set's
unaudited remainder, stand at
76.1.4.

Its provenance: rank 22 of the
reference frontier built at leg 1 of
the network walk — the benchmark
artifact cited by Chapter 56's paper
in its evaluation, sibling to the
set Chapter 70 filed from rank 16 —
taken in as leg 19, the walk's fifth
pre-processed pass. The queue record
carried the title and the team but
no identifier; identified by title
and team in one search, to the
dataset's home on Hugging Face, and
held entire at repo HEAD 2fe88a2f
(last modified 2025-01-26): the card,
and the one data file (81,670
bytes). The work is a network
taking-in outside the pre-registered
sample (closed at Paper 036); no
seal, no score.

Axis judgment: no axis event. The
artifact advances no claim about any
dimension of the models it measures;
it is an instrument pointed at them.
Its one frame-adjacent property —
that its scores are commensurable
across models and years because the
table is fixed — is the examination's
design, filed as concepts of the
instrument (1.2.697, 1.2.773), not as
a dimension the book opens.
# 76.1.2 — State machines

M1, the scoring machine [synthesis]:
Chapter 70's machine, restated for
this artifact because its gaps moved.
A RESPONSE is EXTRACTED to a single
answer string; the string is COMPARED
with the key's entry by equality
(1.2.700); each agreement is COUNTED
into the paper's statistic — pass@k,
mean@k over the samples. NAMED GAPS:
no state for a response whose answer
is right but unextractable — it falls
to the miss count, invisibly; no
state for NORMALIZATION — seven of
this key's entries are zero-padded
(1.2.772), and an extraction that
arrives unpadded fails those rows
without a trace, where the 2025 key's
single malformed row was at least
visible as an anomaly; and no state
in which the key is doubted — the
machine has no appeal, though this
artifact's rows, alone among the
book's keys, carry the means of doubt
in their own solutions (1.2.770),
which the machine never reads. Home:
76.1.2; concepts 1.2.700, 1.2.772.
[inferred]

M2, the derivation machine
[synthesis]: the artifact's
manufacture, read off its card and
its rows. The examination's problems
and the community's solutions are
COLLECTED on the Art of Problem
Solving wiki; ninety of them, three
years' worth, are TRANSCRIBED into
AI-MO's validation set (1.2.774);
the thirty of 2024 are SLICED out of
it (1.2.769) and RE-ISSUED by
HuggingFaceH4 as one parquet file,
the rows keeping their parent
identifiers, their source urls and
their solutions (1.2.771, 1.2.775).
NAMED GAPS: the slice's rule is
stated in the card's one sentence —
the 2024 problems, from the named
parent — and no more: what checks
the transcription ran at either
hand-off is nowhere recorded; and no
step in the chain re-derives an
answer, so the key descends from the
examination through two
transcriptions with no audit at any
hand-off — the audit at 76.1.3 is
the first the key has had. Home:
76.1.2; concepts 1.2.769, 1.2.771,
1.2.774. [inferred]
# 76.1.3 — The house check

THE HOUSE CHECK — the key re-derived
from the problems: fifty-six checks,
fifty-six exact, no failures. The
derivations stand in kept scripts
(check_answers.py, check_answers2.py,
check_answers3.py; run record
check_run.txt) — domain computations,
outside the pre-processor's harness
shapes, on Paper 052's precedent —
and the census arithmetic stands by
declaration through the harness
(p058.check: fifteen declarations,
fifteen PASS). The artifact's
structure first: thirty rows; the
columns exactly id, problem,
solution, answer, url, year; the
identifiers 60 to 89 without a gap;
the sittings fifteen and fifteen in
order, I before II, told apart by
their urls (1.2.775); every row
carrying a solution and an Art of
Problem Solving url; every answer a
bare integer in [0, 999] as a string
— thirty of thirty, where the 2025
key managed twenty-nine of thirty —
and seven of the thirty zero-padded
to the contract's full width
(1.2.772). The repo's inventory is
exactly the card, the one data file
and the attributes file; the card's
own account — thirty problems, the
parent set named at ninety problems
from 2022-2024 — matches the
artifact row for row.

Then the key itself, re-derived
entire, thirty of thirty. Sitting I:
the walk and the coffee shop (the
shop's minutes cancel between the
two timed walks; the third takes
204); the tangent-secant length (AP
= 100/13 by coordinates on the
circumcircle; 113); the octagon's
256 colorings counted whole (115
favourable; 371); the sawtooth
graphs' intersections (385, counted
below); the least prime whose square
can divide a fourth power plus one
(17, and its least root 110); the
disphenoid set in its box (the edges
give the half-box 4 by 5/2 by 2; the
inradius 20√21/63; 104); the boxes
of surface 54 and volume 23 (two
sides equal at the extreme, 2, 2 and
23/4; r² = 657/64; 721); the
logarithms (the two equations force
xy = 25 outright, and a solution
exists to exhibit); the token game
(the losing seats counted to 2024;
809); the lottery (1 in 115 once the
prize is given; 116); the two
rectangles (the second hanging below
the shared line, e² + 184e − 561 = 0
giving e = 3; 104); the grid paths
(294, of the 12,870 counted whole);
the complex maximum (324 cos θ −
432 sin θ, amplitude 540); the rows
of tangent circles (the two rows
agreeing on BC forces cot(B/2) +
cot(C/2) = 1190/11, and the inradius
192/5; 197); the rhombus on the
hyperbola (the second vertex's line
meets the hyperbola only when
6d² > 5c², so BD² stands above 480
and never reaches it). Sitting II:
Aimeville (the named ownerships sum
to 1,124 against 437 owning one and
234 owning two; 73); the right angle
at the incenter (AI² = 156 by
Euler's formula and Pythagoras, the
product of the sides closed by the
area; 468); the triples (601 of the
45,451 compositions, counted whole);
the uncovered point (the envelope
touches AB at (1/8, 3√3/8), OC² =
7/16; 23); the thirteenth roots (the
product is (1 − (1+i)^13)(1 − (1−i)
^13) = 8,321; 321); the b-eautiful
squares (counted base by base; the
least base with more than ten is
211, holding fifteen); the
dodecagon's rectangles (315, counted
exactly in Q(√2, √3) over the
sixty-six lines through its vertex
pairs); the list (the partitions of
30 searched whole; the sum of
squares is unique at 236); the grid
of digits (45); the log system
(25/8; 33); the equilateral hexagon
(its two direction angles solved
numerically, the side reading
80.0000 from all three of the
triangle's sides independently; 80);
Bob's list (2024 in binary has ones
at 2^3, 2^5, 2^6, 2^7, 2^8, 2^9 and
2^10, so A is forced; 55); the digit
changed to 1 (the greatest such
number is 5,694; 699); the torus on
the sphere (33/4 − 33/7 = 99/28;
127); the chips (maximality forces
every row and column occupied, the
placement is then its colouring, and
the colourings count 30 × 30 + 2;
902).

Three instruments deserve their
names. The sawtooth count (I-4) is a
root count of X(Y(x)) − x on [0, 1]
at four-million-point resolution —
384 sign changes and the endpoint
root at x = 1 — cross-checked from
the other graph, Y(X(y)) − y, which
counts the same 385; the six
near-zero minima the scan flagged
were each verified adjacent to a
genuine crossing, none an isolated
touch. The dodecagon count (II-7)
is exact arithmetic, not sampling:
every line through two vertices of
the 12-gon, every pair of
perpendicular directions, every
four-corner test against the closed
polygon, in the field Q(√2, √3).
And two configurations were fixed
by the artifact's own solution
strings where the problem text
underdetermines the figure — I-14's
row of circles resting on BC, and
II-14's internal and external
tangencies (76.1.4); the derivations
from those figures are the house's
own, and both close exactly.

The year pair's census, from the
two held artifacts: the keys' sums
are 8,762 (2024) and 8,344 (2025),
their means 292.07 and 278.13, their
medians 207.5 and 220.5; sitting
I's sum exceeds sitting II's in both
years (4,573 to 4,189; 4,383 to
3,961); the value 104 stands twice
in the 2024 key, both times in
sitting I, and no value repeats in
2025's; four values stand in both
keys (104, 113, 204, 468). The
magnitudes are the keys' shapes, not
the years' difficulties — an
answer's size is the setters'
arithmetic, not their intent — and
the census is filed as the pair's
shape, nothing more (76.2.1). The
scores other papers report on this
set were not rerun and could not be
from this desk; they stand
attributed at 76.1.1. What stands
verified here is the whole key: this
is the first benchmark key in the
book to be re-derived entire, and
the 2025 key's eight standing
entries (70.1.4) are now the pair's
only unaudited remainder (Q268).
# 76.1.4 — Caveats

One problem's text is defective as
stored. II-14 names the outside
resting twice — 'when T rests on
the outside of S' is printed for
both r_i and r_o — while its own
solution string computes the two
cases as internal (the torus inside
the sphere, r_i = 33/4) and external
(r_o = 33/7), which is also what the
key's 127 requires. Read literally,
the stored problem cannot yield its
key; read with its solution, it does
(76.1.3). The defect is the
artifact's — whether the parent's
transcription made it or the slice
inherited it cannot be told from
this filing, the parent's rows for
this problem standing unheld.

A second problem's text
underdetermines its figure. I-14
names two circles tangent to AB and
BC and a chain of further circles
tangent to AB and AC, and nowhere
says that the row rests on BC with
its ends closing at the two sides;
the configuration must be supplied
before the problem has a unique
answer, and the artifact's solution
supplies it. The house's derivation
follows that figure and closes
exactly — but a reader holding only
the problems holds an ambiguous
instrument at that row, and the
ambiguity is the transcription's,
not the examination's.

No licence is stated. The card's
front matter carries its dataset
information and its configurations
and no licence field, where Chapter
70's card stated MIT outright. The
artifact's terms of reuse are
nowhere in the artifact; the
platform may carry terms the card
does not, and the filing records the
card, nothing more.

The parent set stands unaudited
beyond the slice. The filing
re-derived the thirty answers of
2024 entire; the parent's other
sixty rows — the 2022 and 2023
problems, identifiers 0 to 59 — and
its transcription choices stand
outside the audit, and the slice's
provenance (1.2.771) is the
publisher's claim, read as strings
and not fetched (Q269).

And the text's state is a living
one: a repository held at HEAD
2fe88a2f, last modified 2025-01-26,
with 42,925 downloads at the held
state — the platform's counter,
attributed — and no versioning of
its rows. The scores in Chapters 56,
71 and 73 were computed against
states of this artifact the house
did not hold; they stand as reported.
The examination is the MAA's AIME
of February 2024: the problems and
the official answers are the
examination's; the solutions in the
rows are the Art of Problem Solving
community's, carried from the wiki
the urls name and unsigned per row.
# 76.1.5 — Open questions, novelty

Q267, THE PADDING QUESTION. This
key's seven zero-padded strings
(1.2.772) make a scorer's
normalization discipline worth seven
rows in thirty: a pipeline that
parses both sides to integers never
meets the hazard, and one that
compares raw strings fails those
rows for every model it runs. The
scores this book holds against this
set (Chapters 56, 71, 73) do not say
which discipline their pipelines
used. Is there a reading of AIME
2024 in the book whose margin is
smaller than the padding tax — and
if the tax were found to have been
paid, would the reading change, or
only its standing?

Q268, THE COMPLETED AUDIT QUESTION.
The 2024 key now stands re-derived
entire — thirty of thirty (76.1.3) —
while eight of the 2025 key's
entries still stand on the
examination's authority alone
(70.1.4, Q245). The year pair
(1.2.773) is this book's drift
instrument, and its two halves now
stand at different strengths. Is a
comparison across the pair sound
while one key is audited whole and
the other in part — and would
completing the 2025 audit change any
reading the book has taken from the
pair, or only the pair's own
standing?

Q269, THE PARENT QUESTION. The
slice's rows descend from AI-MO's
ninety-problem set, whose 2022 and
2023 rows this filing did not hold
and did not audit. The slice's
answers stand re-derived, but its
texts — the problems as worded, the
solutions as carried — were checked
only against themselves, and one of
them is defective as stored
(76.1.4). Does the derived
benchmark's standing in the book
depend on its parent's, and would
taking the parent in — as a chapter
of its own, or as this chapter's
appendix — change anything filed
here?

Q270, THE VALIDATION QUESTION. The
parent set was built to validate
(1.2.774): systems for the AIMO
challenge were developed against
these ninety problems before the
slice was re-issued as a test, their
developers' readings taken
repeatedly, by design, as tuning
signals. When the slice's scores
are now reported as measurements of
finished systems, how much of that
validation life travels into the
test reading — and is there a system
whose AIME 2024 score in this book
descends from a lineage the
validation set helped to shape?

NOVELTY BY AUTHOR — HuggingFaceH4
(Hugging Face's in-house research
team, entered as a company, the
sixth in the book): True +1 (the
house check, 76.1.3), False +0,
Unknown +11 (the seven concepts
1.2.769-1.2.775, and Q267-Q270),
Fiction +0. HuggingFaceH4 is new to
the book: its name occurs nowhere in
the tracker before this chapter —
the artifact entered the queue from
Chapter 56's bibliography without
its publisher being carried into the
chapter scrolls — and its vector is
drawn fresh at 1.5.6.
# 76.1.6 — Author check #66

Four legs, all clean. (1) The held
material was scanned entire: the
card and the data file's text dump
hold no occurrence of phext,
phextura, scroll, Exocortex,
Mirrorborn, CYOA, SBOR or Bickford;
'coordinate' occurs seventeen times,
each the ordinary mathematical word
inside a problem or its solution.
(2) The built tracker was scanned
for 'HuggingFaceH4': zero occurrences
before this chapter; 'AIME 2024'
occurs five times, every one a
citing chapter's score report, the
publisher's name carried nowhere; the
platform's name occurs as the home of
other artifacts (Chapters 70 and 75)
— the team is not the platform.
(3) The Twitter archive (export of
2026-11-21) was searched entire:
'HuggingFaceH4', 'aime_2024' and
'AIME 2024' occur zero times in
tweets and likes; 'HuggingFace'
occurs five times in the likes,
every one generic — the platform's
tools, other teams' models — none
touching the H4 team or this set.
(4) The open web was searched twice
— the team with phext, and the team
with Bickford, Exocortex and
Mirrorborn: no tie in either; the
one Bickford the second search
surfaces is Douglas Bickford, a
different person, kept apart under
the house rule and recorded here
only so the collision stands on the
record. HuggingFaceH4 is the H4 team
of Hugging Face, the company's
in-house research team; nothing in
its public record names phext, Will,
or any Mirrorborn work. No evidence
of use without naming, and no
evidence of contact. The author is
new to the book.
# 76.2.1 — Synthesis: the pair on the table

[synthesis] Chapter 70 filed the
instrument at its minimum: thirty
bare rows, a key that says and
cannot show. This chapter files the
same instrument a year earlier and a
publisher richer — rows that carry
their solutions, their sources and
their parent-set numbers (1.2.770,
1.2.771) — and the pair (1.2.773) is
the filing's real object. What the
pair shows first is how much of a
benchmark is its publisher. The
examination is one: two sittings of
fifteen problems, the integer
contract, the three-digit ceiling.
Everything else in the two artifacts
differs — the file form, the row's
furniture, the answer string's
width, the licence, even which
hazard a strict scorer meets: one
malformed string there, seven padded
ones here. A field that speaks of
'AIME' as one measure across the
years is speaking of the examination;
the numbers it computes are computed
on artifacts, and the artifacts are
not one thing.

Second, the audits diverge, and
instructively. The 2025 key, the
barer artifact, resisted: eight of
its answers still stand on borrowed
authority (Q245). The 2024 key, the
furnished one, fell entire — thirty
of thirty — and not only because its
rows carry solutions to check
against: its problems, re-derived
from their statements, closed one
after another, two of them only
after the artifact's own solutions
repaired what its problem texts had
left ambiguous (76.1.4). The pair
thus leaves the book with an
asymmetry it did not design (Q268):
the earlier year is the verified
one, and every cross-year reading
now rests on a key audited whole
beside a key audited in part.

And third, the pair's direction in
the wild. The 2024 artifact has been
downloaded forty-two thousand times
against the 2025 artifact's twelve
thousand: the older, richer
transcription is the one the field
actually holds, and the scores in
this book's chapters were read off
states of it the house never held
until today. The instrument is filed
now, both years of it. The readings
taken on it before the filing stand
as they were — attributed, and now,
at last, readings against a held
table.
# 76.2.2 — Cluster placement

AY EXTENDED — its third paper. The
founding's test (75.2.1) is passed
identically: a fixed table of
problems and verdicts, published as
data — thirty rows, held entire by
the filing, consulted at grading and
never inside the judged computation.
The differences from the founding
artifact — the furnished row, the
carried provenance, the padded key,
the derivation from a validation set
— are furniture, filed as concepts
(1.2.769-1.2.775); a new cluster on
the second year of one competition
would split the country by calendar,
not by kind. What the third resident
adds to the country is stated at
76.2.1: the pair — the measure read
at two adjacent years, so the
examination can be separated from
its transcriptions. AT HELD APART:
the citing Chapter 56's paper
reports scores against this table;
the table itself performs nothing.
J WEIGHED AND NOT JOINED — the
walk's fifth weighing since the
crossing at Chapter 71, after
Chapter 70's reference-side weighing
and Chapter 75's pipeline: this
artifact contains no mechanism at
all. Its key is consulted at
grading, after the judged
computation has ended — the AY
pattern, not J's standing condition,
which wants the reference consulted
inside the computation for a verdict
whose evidence lies beyond the
judged object. 1.2.716 remains J's
only resident, and the seam's open
question (Q242) is unchanged by a
second key. O HELD APART: this is a
judged set, not the pricing of
judges. AW HELD APART: the artifact
carries solutions — prose about its
own problems — but a survey's object
is a field's papers; these solutions
are the examination's furniture, not
a field's account of itself. AX HELD
APART: its strings are compared,
and now also read; they do not run.
