# 79.1.2 — State machines

M1, the context-aware decoding
machine: the paper's inference
machine (Section 2.2). At each token
the machine runs the model TWICE —
once on the context, query and
prefix, once on the query and prefix
alone — COMBINES the two logit
streams as (1 + α)·(with context) −
α·(without), and SAMPLES from the
adjusted distribution by the run's
standing strategy (greedy for the
conflict tasks, nucleus sampling over
the adjusted distribution for
summarization). NAMED GAPS: no state
sets α — the adjustment level
(1.2.794) is chosen per task family
outside the machine and never adapted
to the token or the instance; no
state asks whether this context
deserves its amplification — the
trust decision (1.2.799) is upstream
of the machine entire; and no state
prices the second forward pass — the
machine doubles the per-token
computation of regular decoding, a
cost the paper nowhere states.
Home: 79.1.2; concepts 1.2.791,
1.2.792, 1.2.793, 1.2.794. [stated]

M2, the entity-swap construction
machine: the instrument of Longpre et
al. that the paper spends (Section
3.1). From a question set it
IDENTIFIES the questions whose
answers are named entities, TAKES
each one's gold supporting document,
REPLACES the answer entity in the
document with a random entity, and
SCORES exact match against the
planted entity. NAMED GAPS: the
replacement is drawn at random with
no seed or draw recorded in the
account this paper gives, so the
instrument's instances cannot be
re-drawn from the record; and no
state distinguishes a model that read
the document from one that merely
complies — the machine grades
obedience to the context, and its
planted answers are false by
construction (1.2.797, Q279).
Home: 79.1.2; concepts 1.2.796,
1.2.797. [stated]
# 79.1.3 — The house check

THE HOUSE CHECK — the paper's printed
arithmetic, recomputed: fourteen
declarations through the
pre-processor's harness (p061.check),
fourteen PASS, no tension, no
failure. The results themselves —
generation runs over four model
families on five datasets — are not
rerunnable from this desk and stand
attributed at 79.1.1; what stands
verified here is every relation the
paper prints among its numbers that
its own tables can judge.

The headline survives. The abstract's
'14.3% gain for LLaMA in factuality
metrics' is Section 4.1's LLaMA-30B
CNN-DM factKB gain, 76.8 to 87.8 —
their ratio is 1.1432, the printed
14.3% exact. Section 4.1's two
conflict gains for GPT-Neo 20B close
the same way: MemoTrap 37.1 to 57.3
is the printed 54.4%, and NQ-Swap
16.1 to 36.8 is the printed 128%.

Two counts were made by hand over the
held text, outside the harness, and
are recorded as hand work. The win
census of the two results tables,
counted cell by cell: in Table 2 the
adjusted decoding beats regular
decoding in all 48 cells (eight
models, two datasets, three metrics
each); in Table 3 in 22 of 24 — the
two losses both FLAN-T5 on the
original NQ (81.8 to 80.3 at 3B, 85.5
to 82.5 at 11B), exactly the
exception the paper's own text names
and explains (79.1.4). And the
bibliography holds 34 entries,
counted against the HTML's
bibliography items (the list is
unnumbered author-year, so no
sequence declaration can run).

One property of the method itself was
verified at the coefficient level:
the independence cancellation
(1.2.795) rests on (1 + α) − α = 1,
declared and exact at the paper's
summarization setting. The remaining
two prose percentages of Section 4.1
do not close against Table 2, and the
introduction's multiplier closes
against neither of its candidate
referents in Table 3; those are
carried as caveats (79.1.4), not
failures of the tables, which stand
as printed.
# 79.1.4 — Caveats

Two of the paper's three headline
percentages disagree with its own
table. Section 4.1 gives LLaMA-30B on
CNN-DM '21% increase in ROUGE-L,
14.3% increase in factKB and 7.8%
increase in BERT-P'. Against Table 2:
factKB closes exactly (79.1.3), but
ROUGE-L 25.8 to 31.8 recomputes as
23.3%, not 21, and BERT-P 88.5 to 92.2
recomputes as 4.2%, not 7.8. No other
model's row yields the printed pair
either — LLaMA-13B's ROUGE-L gain is
20.3% and its BERT-P gain 3.9%. The
filing carries the table's numbers;
the prose pair stands as printed,
unreconciled anywhere in the paper —
the shape of percentages carried over
from an earlier draft of the table.

The introduction's multiplier has no
referent. 'CAD brings a 2.9x
improvement to LLaMA-30B on a
knowledge conflicts QA dataset': on
NQ-Swap, the conflict QA dataset, the
model's ratio is 3.93x (9.6 to 37.7);
on MemoTrap it is 1.96x (25.8 to
50.6). Neither is 2.9x, and no average
of the table's conflict ratios
produces it. The claim stands as
printed, attributed, its referent
unfound.

The adjustment-level analysis names
the wrong symbol once. Section 4.2's
text reads 'we find λ=0.5 consistently
provide robust improvements', where
the method's level is α everywhere
else in the paper (and 0.5 is the
summarization setting; the knowledge
conflict runs of the same section
spend α = 1). A typographical slip,
recorded because the sentence is the
one a reader would cite for the
level's robustness.

The paper's two trend claims are
figure-carried. That the conflict
gain grows with model size (Figure 2,
OPT at seven sizes) and that gains
rise with α on the conflict tasks
(Figure 3) stand on curves, not
tables; the house check reaches only
the numbers the text prints beside
them, and the trends stand attributed
— Q282 carries the size trend as a
question, not a finding.

Small print, recorded and not
load-bearing: Table 4's caption calls
the method 'contrast-aware decoding';
Section 4.2's heading reads
'Qualitative analyais'; Table 1's
caption, 'illustation'; Table 3
rounds GPT-Neo's 2.7B to '3B' where
Section 3.2 gives 2.7B, and shortens
its model labels ('GPT.', 'LLAMA').
And the FLAN-T5 exception of Table 3
— the method's only losses — is
explained by the paper itself: the
original NQ stands inside FLAN-T5's
instruction-finetuning data, so the
prior CAD taxes there is partly the
test itself, memorized. The
explanation is the paper's own and
stands attributed.

One version stands unread by design:
the work was held at v1 (24 May
2023), the only version the arXiv
record carries; the venue's form —
NAACL 2024, a short paper — is the
anthology's record, not this
chapter's evidence.
# 79.1.5 — Open questions, novelty

Q279, THE TRUST QUESTION. The entity-
swap test (1.2.797) pays the model
for repeating a planted falsehood,
and CAD's largest wins stand on it —
faithfulness to the context and
obedience to it are one mechanism
with two names, and the mechanism
cannot tell a corrected fact from a
planted one (1.2.799). Is there a
signal available at decoding time —
in the two distributions CAD already
computes, or in the context's own
standing — that separates evidence
deserving trust from evidence that
merely arrives as context? Or is the
trust decision permanently upstream
of the decoder, so that every
contrast of this family inherits its
answer from whoever assembled the
context?

Q280, THE CONTAMINATED PRIOR
QUESTION. The context-free prior
(1.2.792) is computed on the generated
prefix — which CAD itself generated,
under the adjusted distribution, with
the context in force. The withheld
run therefore reads a context-shaped
prefix, and the longer the generation
runs, the more of the 'prior' being
taxed is the method's own earlier
output. How much of the measured
contrast, late in a generation, is
still context against prior, and how
much is the method contrasting
against its own earlier choices? The
paper's formula spends y<t in both
terms without remarking on the
contamination.

Q281, THE ADJUSTMENT QUESTION. The
adjustment level (1.2.794) is one
scalar per task family, set by hand,
and the paper's own analysis finds
conflict gains still rising with α
past the spent value — the method's
central quantity is left at a round
number on a rising curve. Can the
right adjustment be computed at run
time from quantities the machine
already holds — the PMI itself
(1.2.793), the two distributions'
divergence, the token's standing —
the way DoLa's dynamic selection
(Chapter 74) computes its contrast's
other half at run time? A contrast
whose strength is measured, not
chosen, is the family's open half.

Q282, THE GROWTH QUESTION. On the
conflict tasks the gain grows with
model size across OPT's seven sizes
(Figure 2), which the authors read as
larger models relying more on their
prior knowledge. Two quantities could
carry the trend: the prior
strengthening with scale, or
attention to context weakening
relative to it. They predict
different futures — the first, that
every larger model needs a larger
correction to read its own input; the
second, that the correction is a
fixed tax on a shrinking share. Which
carries it, and does the trend have a
ceiling?

