# 80.1.2 — State machines

M1, the classifier-free guidance
decoding machine: the paper's
inference machine (Sections 2.2 and
3.1). At each token the machine runs
the model TWICE — once on the
conditioning prompt, once on the
reference conditioning (the dropped
prefix, or a chosen negative prompt)
— COMBINES the two next-token logit
streams as reference + γ·(conditional
− reference), and SAMPLES from the
combined distribution by the run's
standing strategy. NAMED GAPS: no
state sets γ — the guidance strength
is chosen per task family outside
the machine (near 1.0-1.5 on the
benchmarks, 1.5 for code, up to 3 in
the assistant study) and never
adapted to the token; no state fixes
the reference branch's seed — the
benchmark implementation starts it
at the prompt's last token, a
KV-cache economy the theory does not
mention (1.2.803); and no state
measures whether adherence improved
— the machine steers without a
gauge, its doubled per-token cost
priced only by the study at Section
4, outside the machine. Home:
80.1.2; concepts 1.2.800, 1.2.802,
1.2.803. [stated]

M2, the paired-preference study
machine: the instrument of the
paper's assistant evaluation
(Section 3.4, Appendix G).
ENUMERATE system prompts against
user prompts (25 claimed against 46;
the appendix lists 24 of the first —
80.1.4), SAMPLE prompt combinations
(1,740 stated), GENERATE a paired
completion for each — one at γ = 1,
one at a γ drawn at random from 1 to
6 — COLLECT blind human votes on two
questions (which completion follows
the system prompt better; which is
more relevant to the user's), and
AGGREGATE preference by γ. NAMED
GAPS: 611 votes stand over 1,740
sampled pairs, so most pairs were
never judged and the per-γ vote
counts behind the γ = 3 peak (75%
system-prompt preference) are not
reported — the peak's denominator
is not in the record; and because
each pair's guided side carries a
different random γ, the aggregate
mixes the dose with the effect it
measures. Home: 80.1.2; concepts
1.2.800, 1.2.802. [stated]
# 80.1.3 — The house check

THE HOUSE CHECK — the paper's
printed arithmetic, recomputed:
eighteen declarations through the
pre-processor's harness (p062.check),
eighteen PASS, no tension, no
failure. The results themselves —
generation runs over fifteen models
on nine benchmarks and three further
task families — are not rerunnable
from this desk and stand attributed
at 80.1.1; what stands verified here
is every relation the paper prints
among its numbers that its own
tables can judge.

The Section 4 analysis closes
exactly. Its covariance table splits
the nine tasks as the prose says:
five statistically indistinguishable
at p = .01 between guidance and a
doubled model (HellaSwag .012, PiQA
.030, ARC-c .216, BoolQ .345, ARC-e
.355), and four significant, split
two and two — LAMBADA (.000) and
SciQ (.008) for guidance, WinoGrande
(.003) and TriviaQA (.008) for size.
The headline survives its grid:
LAMBADA's LLaMA-7B rises 73.6 to
81.3 under guidance, past the 77.9
the paper attributes to PaLM-540B —
the state-of-the-art claim's own
arithmetic stands (the 77.9 itself
is another group's number and
stands attributed). Table 13's
improvement column closes entire —
137%, 189%, 189%, and the
perplexity row's 123% read as the
inverse ratio — and the
machine-translation table moves as
its prose says, Bloom's zero-shot
gain at γ = 1.10 exactly undone at
γ = 1.25. The confusion matrices'
printed accuracies recompute
exactly at γ = 1 (73) and γ = 1.25
(86) under the scoring the paper's
own account implies — the prompted
language, or any code at all for
the unspecified prompt; at the two
higher strengths the matrices
themselves sum short of the stated
hundred samples, and those are
carried as caveats (80.1.4), not
failures of the arithmetic.

Four counts were made by hand over
the held text and its extracted
tables, outside the harness, and
are recorded as hand work. The win
census of the Figure 2 grids,
counted cell by cell over the 135
paired cells: 101 wins for guidance,
30 losses, 4 ties — six benchmarks
swept or near-swept, WinoGrande
lost in all fifteen cells, and
TriviaQA net-negative, which the
paper's exception list omits
(80.1.4). The bibliography holds 85
entries standing for 79 unique
works, six pairs of them the same
work cited twice. The two HumanEval
appendix tables for the 2B and 6B
models were compared entire and are
identical, cell for cell. And the
assistant study's prompt lists were
counted in their appendix: 46 user
prompts, exact against the claim;
the system prompts list 24 against
25 claimed (80.1.4).
# 80.1.4 — Caveats

The paper's third code model was
never measured — or never printed.
Table 2's CodeGen-6B column repeats
the CodeGen-2B column in every cell,
and the appendix's own tables for
the two sizes (Tables 6 and 7) are
identical tables in the held text,
cell for cell. The HumanEval claims
therefore stand on two model sizes,
not three; the filing carries the
350M and 2B numbers, and the 6B
column as printed is the 2B column
wearing its name.

Section 3.1's exception list is
incomplete against its own grid.
'Except for ARC (challenge) and
Winogrande, the boost of
performances from CFG is nontrivial
and consistent across models and
tasks': the named exceptions are
real — ARC-c splits eleven wins to
four losses, and WinoGrande loses
in all fifteen cells — but TriviaQA
is net-negative in the same grid
(six wins, seven losses, two ties,
the losses concentrated in the
largest models) and BoolQ declines
for all four LLaMA models. A third
exception stands in the paper's own
numbers, unnamed in its prose.

'Uniformly' fails in one cell.
Section 3.3.2 claims that at low
guidance (γ ≤ 1.5) CFG increases
pass@1 uniformly; CodeGen-350M at
temperature 0.2 and γ = 1.5 scores
10.9% against its 11.0% unguided,
and at temperature 0.8 the same
strength only ties (7.9% against
7.9%). The claim is true of the
table's other low-γ cells; the
universal word is not.

The abstract's exchange rate is
flatter than the paper's own test.
'Bringing improvements equivalent
to a model with twice the
parameter-count' stands without
qualification in the abstract;
Section 4's FLOP-matched analysis
splits its nine tasks five
indistinguishable, two for guidance,
two for size (80.1.3). The filing
carries the section's version
(1.2.804).

The assistant study's census does
not close. Section 3.4 claims 25
system prompts and 46 user prompts
sampled into 1,740 combinations;
Appendix G lists the base system
prompt and 23 variants — 24 as
printed — and 25 × 46 is 1,150, not
1,740, under either count of the
system list. The user prompts
number exactly 46 as claimed. The
1,740 stands as printed, its
derivation not in the record.

Two confusion matrices sum short.
Appendix D.2 states 100 samples per
strength; the γ = 1.5 matrix sums
to 99 and γ = 1.75 to 98, the short
cells falling in the unspecified
prompt's row. Their printed
accuracies (81%, 77%) recompute
exactly if the missing completions
fell in that row's code columns;
as printed, the matrices give 80
and 75 (80.1.3).

Small print, recorded and not
load-bearing: the appendix's table
numbers tangle — Section C.2 sends
the reader to 'Table 11' for the
covariance table captioned Table 4,
while the translation tables also
stand as Table 11; Section 5.3's
defining equation prints its token
index as w_T for w_t; Table 14's
caption names GSM8K over a worked
multiple-choice item from AQuA,
whose source it spells 'Guancano';
Section 3.4 once prints 'CGF
sampling' for CFG sampling. And one
version stands unread by design:
the venue's form (ICML 2024)
renders a five-author byline, the
sixth author of the held version
absent — the filing follows the
arXiv v1 byline entire and records
the difference at 80.3.1.
# 80.1.5 — Open questions, novelty

Q283, THE STUB QUESTION. The
reference classifier-free guidance
taxes is supposed to be the model
with its conditioning removed —
but the paper's benchmark
implementation seeds the
unconditional branch with the
prompt's last token (1.2.803), so
the taxed distribution is a
one-token-conditioned stub of the
prompt, not its absence. How much
of the measured guidance is
contrast against the prompt-free
model, and how much against the
stub? The seed is an implementation
economy, chosen for the KV cache;
whether the device's gains survive
a truly empty reference — or partly
are the stub's own contribution —
is nowhere measured.

Q284, THE EXCHANGE QUESTION. The
doubling exchange (1.2.804) splits
its nine tasks five indistinguishable,
two for guidance, two for size.
What property of a task settles its
side? The split invites a reading —
guidance wins where the task is
bound by adherence to the prompt,
size wins where it is bound by
knowledge the prompt cannot supply
(TriviaQA, the size-favoured task,
is also the grid's net-negative for
guidance, 80.1.4) — but nine tasks
cannot carry the law. Does the
reading survive a wider task set,
and can a task's side be predicted
before either doubling is spent?

Q285, THE TAIL QUESTION. Guidance
zeroes some tasks' small nonzero
success rates outright (1.2.806):
the tail where rare correct samples
live is spent buying the head. Is
the tail recoverable — by mixing
guided and unguided samples, by
annealing γ across the sample
budget, by guiding the first tokens
only — or is the zeroing the same
fact as the sharpening, so that any
recovery returns the head's gains
with it? The paper reports the
inversion between pass@1 and
pass@100 and leaves the recovery
unasked.

Q286, THE AMPLIFIED-INSTRUCTION
QUESTION. The paper's own
limitations name the shadow: a
device that amplifies adherence to
a prompt amplifies adherence to any
part of it, including instructions
injected by a third party or set
against the deployment's alignment —
and the authors advocate standard
risk benchmarks for the failure
without running one. Does guidance
multiply an injected instruction's
success by the same factor it
multiplies a benign instruction's
adherence — is γ a volume knob on
the whole prompt, the malicious
part included — or does the
amplification discriminate in ways
the adherence numbers would show?
The contrast line's trust question
(Q279) asks the sibling question of
context; this one asks it of the
instruction itself.

NOVELTY BY AUTHOR — Guillaume
Sanchez: True +1 (the house check,
80.1.3), False +0, Unknown +12 (the
eight concepts 1.2.800-1.2.807, and
Q283-Q286), Fiction +0.
NOVELTY BY AUTHOR — Honglu Fan:
True +1, False +0, Unknown +12,
Fiction +0.
NOVELTY BY AUTHOR — Alexander
Spangher: True +1, False +0,
Unknown +12, Fiction +0.
NOVELTY BY AUTHOR — Elad Levi:
True +1, False +0, Unknown +12,
Fiction +0.
NOVELTY BY AUTHOR — Pawan Sasanka
Ammanamanchi: True +1, False +0,
Unknown +12, Fiction +0.
NOVELTY BY AUTHOR — Stella
Biderman: True +1, False +0,
Unknown +12, Fiction +0.
All six authors are new to the book
and their vectors are drawn fresh
at 1.5.6 (80.1.6).
# 80.1.6 — Author check #70

Four legs, all run first-hand; no
evidence on any of them. (1) The
held paper was scanned entire: no
occurrence of phext, phextura,
scroll, Exocortex, Mirrorborn,
CYOA, SBOR or Bickford — and no
occurrence of 'coordinate' either.
(2) The built tracker was scanned
for all six names: Guillaume
