# 82.1.2 — State machines

M1, the VLD scaling machine: the
paper's experiment machine
(Sections 3-4). TAKE a base
transformer, APPLY a reuse pattern
(1.2.818) at a repetition factor,
TRAIN — from scratch for the
synthetic runs, by fine-tuning
with the pattern inserted before
training for the real-world run —
and MEASURE both quantities of the
split (1.2.819): absorbed entropy
for capacity, exact-answer
accuracy for reasoning. NAMED
GAPS: no state chooses the pattern
or the factor for a task — both
are fixed outside the machine,
though the paper's own tables show
the choice can cost more than the
device gains (the inverse cycle at
×1 below its base entire, 82.1.4);
no state watches the dose for the
non-monotonicity the census later
finds — the ×3 dip is discovered
by reading the table, not by any
alarm inside the machine; and the
machine's two measures never meet
— no state compares a run's
capacity reading against its
reasoning reading, so the split is
drawn by the authors, not by the
instrument. Home: 82.1.2; concepts
1.2.817, 1.2.818, 1.2.819.
[stated]

M2, the capacity-measurement
machine: the instrument of
Sections 3.1 and 4.1. BUILD a
corpus of k uniform random tokens
over n values, TRAIN the model on
it to remember, READ the summed
entropy of the model's own
next-token distributions over the
corpus, and SUBTRACT from the
corpus's known entropy: ΔH, the
absorbed information (1.2.820).
NAMED GAPS: the machine's verdict
in this work is reported as a
figure, not numbers — no state of
the filing can be re-read from the
text layer; and the machine
measures storage of the arbitrary
by design, so it carries no state
that distinguishes a fact stored
from a noise token stored — the
split's flat half is flat under
this instrument only (Q293).
Home: 82.1.2; concept 1.2.820.
[stated]

M3, the iGSM reasoning-measurement
machine: the instrument of
Sections 3.2.1 and 4.2.1.
GENERATE problems at controlled
operation counts from the iGSM
grammar (training at fifteen
operations or fewer, validation at
exactly fifteen, and the
out-of-distribution arms at twenty
and twenty-one), TRAIN, GENERATE
solutions greedily, and GRADE the
final answers by exact match
against the generator's keys.
NAMED GAPS: the grading stands
outside the run — no state inside
the computation consults the keys,
so the machine measures reasoning
without ever steering by a verdict
(the ground of the J weighing at
82.2.2); and the census reads
final answers only — a chain that
reasons wrongly to a right number,
or rightly to a slipped one, is
counted by its last token alone.
Home: 82.1.2; concepts 1.2.818,
1.2.819. [stated]
# 82.1.3 — The house check

THE HOUSE CHECK — the paper's
printed arithmetic, recomputed:
forty-two declarations through
the pre-processor's harness
(p064.check), forty-two PASS, no
tension, no failure. The
declarations are two-term chains
throughout, under the standing
rule from leg 24: the harness's
arith parser is positional, so a
multi-term sum is not checked as
written; every sum below therefore
stands as a chain of two-term
steps, each partial sum declared
in turn. The results themselves —
training runs over GPT-2 and
LLaMA-3.2-3B — are not rerunnable
from this desk and stand
attributed at 82.1.1; what stands
verified here is every relation
the paper prints among its numbers
that its own tables can judge.

The chains close entire. Table 3's
fourteen dataset rows sum by
category — natural language 984.9,
code 397.98, mathematics 1,021.8 —
and in total to 2,404.68 million
tokens (the printed '2.3 billion'
is carried as a caveat at 82.1.4).
Table 2's five deltas recompute
exactly as signed: Math500 +5.00,
GPQA +2.52, AIME +3.34, HumanEval
+1.73, MBPP +1.86. Table 1's
load-bearing relations hold: the
headline gain 70.7 − 46.3 = 24.4;
the conceded dip 62.1 − 61.6 =
0.5; and the harms the prose does
not quote — 46.3 − 43.9 = 2.4,
21.2 − 15.8 = 5.4, 60.5 − 53.3 =
7.2, 60.5 − 59.2 = 1.3, 21.2 −
18.2 = 3.0 — each exact against
its cells (82.1.4). Table 7's
placement differences hold: 64.2 −
62.0 = 2.2 and 60.5 − 54.2 = 6.3.
The capacity design identities
close at the printed precision:
640,000 × 15.6 = 9,984,000 against
the stated ten million bits, and
2 × 5,000,000 = 10,000,000; the
LoRA share divides as a bare ratio
— 6,078,464 of 3,218,828,288 is
0.1888%, the printed 0.19%.

Three pieces of work were made by
hand over the held text, outside
the harness, and are recorded as
hand work. The full recomputation
record (p064/verify_tables.py)
carries what the declarations do
not: Table 1's monotonicity audit
entire — the cycle pattern's op15
series 54.9, 62.1, 61.6, 65.7,
70.7 is not monotone, and six
cells of the table fall below
their own baselines; the exact
entropy values behind the printed
roundings (log2 of 50,257 is
15.6170; the corpus entropy is
9,994,904 bits); Table 2's
small-denominator readings (AIME's
3.33 to 6.67 is one problem of
thirty becoming two; Math500's
scores are 152 and 177 of 500
exact; GPQA's are consistent with
the 198-question diamond subset,
59 to 64); and both worked
examples recomputed step by step
in their mod-23 arithmetic — the
Section 3.2.1 example and Appendix
E.2's long chain, every step
exact, the answers 8 and 8 as
printed (domain computation, the
pre-processor SPEC's named
boundary). The bibliography holds
fifty references, parsed and
classified by a held script (p064/
refs_frontier.py — the
pre-processor's references leg
returned 0 on this unnumbered
author–year list, its standing
shape; the script's header pattern
needed the text layer's
non-breaking spaces normalized
before it would read the 'et al.'
headers, an adaptation recorded in
the script itself), its probable
tier verified by hand against
title identity, both matches true.
And the tables were read from the
held HTML's own extraction entire
— twelve tables, the seven real
ones at indices 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10
and 11.
# 82.1.4 — Caveats

The blanket claim fails against
its own table. Section 4.3 states
that applying VLD patterns
'dramatically boosts models'
reasoning performance across all
tested configurations'. Table 1
holds six cells below their
baselines: the inverse cycle at ×1
falls below the untied base in
every column it prints (43.9
against 46.3 at op15; 17.4 against
21.8 at op20; 15.8 against 21.2 at
op21; 53.3 against 60.5 on the
eight-layer model), the inverse
cycle at ×2 is still below base at
op21 (18.2 against 21.2), and the
sequence pattern at ×1 falls below
base on the eight-layer model
(59.2 against 60.5). The boost is
real for the cycle pattern at
every dose and for all patterns at
their higher doses; as printed,
the sentence claims the weakest
pattern's worst dose too. The
filing carries the table, not the
sentence.

The corpus total is not the
printed total. Table 3's fourteen
rows sum to 2,404.68 million
tokens; Sections 3.2.2 and 4.2.2
and the table's own caption call
the corpus 'around 2.3 billion
tokens' and 'totally 2.3 billion'.
The gap is about 105 million
tokens, four and a half percent of
the printed figure — small against
the claim the corpus carries, but
the printed number is the smaller
one in every place it stands.

The headline comparison's baseline
stands in no table. Section 4.3's
'remarkable outcome' — the 50M
cycle-×2 model at 62.05% passing
the native 150M twelve-layer model
at 61.15% — pairs a prose number
the table rounds differently
(Table 1 prints 62.1 for the same
cell) with a baseline that appears
only in Figure 3(a)'s curve, in no
table of the paper. The surpassing
may be real; as printed it cannot
be recomputed, and the filing
carries it attributed, not
verified (82.1.3).

The split's storage half is
figure-only. The capacity claim —
the filing's central negative
result, that absorbed entropy
stays nearly constant under VLD —
stands in Figure 4's curves; no
capacity number is printed in any
table or in the text. The
reasoning half of the split is
tabulated entire; the storage half
cannot be re-read from the held
text, and 'nearly unchanged (with
only minor variance)' is nowhere
given a number. The filing carries
the split (1.2.819) with its two
halves at their different
standings: the reasoning half
verified against Table 1, the
capacity half attributed. Q293
takes up the instrument.

Small denominators, recorded: the
real-world leg's AIME gain is one
problem of thirty becoming two
(3.33 to 6.67), and the whole leg
is one model, one pattern (cycle),
one fine-tuning run read at five
benchmarks — the synthetic scaling
laws' transfer rests on that
single arm (Q294). The paper's
factor language also wants care in
citation: 'VLD Depth ×k' counts
added passes, so a '×2' four-layer
model is twelve layers deep, not
eight (1.2.817). Small print: the
held version is v3; the v1 of 23
Jun 2025 stood under a different
title ('The 4th Dimension for
Scaling Model Size') with six
authors — Kevin Li, Yiqun Duan and
Arindam Banerjee join the byline
by v3. The queue record's year for
this work (2023) was the walk's
own error, corrected at the
marking; the paper's record is
2025 throughout.
# 82.1.5 — Open questions, novelty

Q291, THE FIXED-POINT QUESTION.
The dose is not monotone: the
cycle pattern's third repetition
falls below its second at op15
(61.6 against 62.1) and at op21
(32.0 against 35.4), and the
paper's own Figure 3(c) shows an
effective depth of 32 scoring
below 16. Appendix I asks whether
VLD has 'inherent fixed points
beyond which performance no longer
improves'. Sharpened: is the
turning point a property of the
pattern, of the base model's
depth, or of the task's operation
count — and can it be read off the
base model before the sweep is
spent, the way the contrast line's
gap (1.2.811) still cannot be
(Q287)? A dose–response census
across bases at matched effective
depths decides the first reading;
nothing in the sixty-four papers
filed predicts a turning point for
any loop.

Q292, THE PLACEMENT QUESTION.
Table 7's ordering is the paper's
quietest surprise: on the
eight-layer cycle, repeating only
the later layers (64.2) beats
repeating all of them (62.0),
which beats no repetition (60.5),
which beats repeating only the
early layers (54.2) — the one
arrangement that harms. The
country's other members spend
their early states as references —
LoopCD's first state (1.2.535),
DoLa's premature layers — weak
sides of a contrast. Here the
early layers are not a reference
but a site, and repeating them is
worse than leaving the model
alone. Is the split's reasoning
gain (1.2.819) a property of depth
as such, or of depth added late —
computation spent near the
readout? The paper runs the
placement sweep on one backbone
at one pattern; a second family
decides whether placement is a law
of the loop or a fact of this
model.

Q293, THE MEASURE QUESTION. The
split's storage half (1.2.819)
rests on one instrument: absorbed
entropy over random tokens
(1.2.820), reported as a figure
without numbers (82.1.4). The
measure's own source — Allen-Zhu &
Li 2024 — prices capacity on
structured material, biographies
whose facts can be probed one by
one. Do the two instruments agree
that repetition leaves capacity
flat? If a structured-knowledge
measure moved under VLD while ΔH
stayed flat, the split would be an
artifact of its instrument — the
loop buying a kind of storage the
random corpus cannot see. The
paper never runs a second
instrument; the question is the
filing's, and it is decidable with
the paper's own setups.

Q294, THE TRANSFER QUESTION. The
synthetic laws are measured on
GPT-2 models of 50M to 200M
parameters trained from scratch;
the real-world leg is one 3B
model, one pattern (cycle), its
repetition inserted before a LoRA
fine-tune on a 2.3-billion-token
corpus (2,404.68 million by its
own table, 82.1.4). Are the two
legs one phenomenon — depth bought
by repetition — or is the 3B gain
adaptation around a repeated
block, a different mechanism
wearing the scaling law's name?
The legs share no model, no scale
and no training regime, and the
paper's generality claim (its
third finding) rests on their
being the same thing. A
from-scratch run at the middle
scale, or the synthetic protocol
at a second family, decides.

NOVELTY BY AUTHOR — Ruike Zhu:
True +1 (the house check, 82.1.3),
False +0, Unknown +8 (the four
concepts 1.2.817-1.2.820, and
Q291-Q294), Fiction +0.
