# 83.1.2 — State machines

M1, the evaluation machine: the
framework's run (lm_eval/evaluator
and api). ENUMERATE the named
tasks against the named model;
for each document BUILD its
context (M2), CONSTRUCT the
task's requests of its output
type, DISPATCH them to the model's
three methods (1.2.823), SCORE
the returns by the task's
process_results, AGGREGATE by the
task's aggregation, and REPORT
each metric with its bootstrap
standard error (1.2.827). NAMED
GAPS: no state verifies a task's
configuration against the
benchmark's official procedure —
the README's priority list
(agreement among trainers, then
the official implementation, then
agreement among evaluators) is a
rule for the humans who write
configs, not a state of the
machine; no state runs the
decontamination pass (M3) — it
stands outside the machine,
invoked by hand on corpora
prepared beforehand, so a run can
be scored against a contaminated
table with nothing objecting; and
the machine believes the model's
returns entire — a backend that
misreports its loglikelihoods is
scored as truth, the interface
carrying no state that audits the
answering side. Home: 83.1.2;
concepts 1.2.821, 1.2.822,
1.2.823, 1.2.827. [stated]

M2, the few-shot assembly machine:
the context builder of the task
API. DRAW the requested number of
examples from the task's fewshot
split by the seeded sampler
(1.2.825), FORMAT each by the
task's doc_to_text, JOIN them
ahead of the target document's
own text under the task's
template, and HAND the built
string to M1 as the request's
context. NAMED GAPS: the seed is
one constant for every task and
every document — no state chooses
it, and no state prices the draw:
a score's dependence on which
examples the constant happens to
pick is nowhere measured inside
the machine; and the sampler's
own header comments leave the
order of the drawn examples an
open question the code settles by
default, not by decision. Home:
83.1.2; concepts 1.2.822, 1.2.825.
[stated]

M3, the decontamination machine:
the janitor's pass over a corpus
(lm_eval/decontamination). BUILD
the word 13-grams of the test
documents a task will ask about;
INDEX the training corpus's
13-grams against them; CUT a
window of two hundred characters
around every hit in the training
text, and DROP a document whose
hits pass the dirtiness cutoff of
ten; EMIT the cleaned corpus.
NAMED GAPS: the machine sees only
exact runs of thirteen words — a
paraphrase, a translation, or a
re-typeset problem passes entire,
so the pass certifies the absence
of a shape, not of the material
(Q297); its n-gram index must be
built outside it, beforehand, per
corpus; and it is invoked by
hand, outside M1 — no state of a
run records whether the corpus it
was trained on ever passed
through it. Home: 83.1.2; concept
1.2.826. [stated]
# 83.1.3 — The house check

THE HOUSE CHECK — the work's own
claims about itself, recomputed
against the held deposit:
twenty-nine declarations through
the pre-processor's harness
(p065.check), twenty-nine PASS, no
tension, no failure. The work is a
software deposit, and its printed
numbers are its README's claims
and its library's contents; the
declarations therefore run on the
artifact itself — the deposit's
zip verified byte-for-byte against
the Zenodo record's own checksum
(md5 1edd09dc16ff3deda2f77b9efdd
d670; 1,805,987 bytes) before any
count was taken. The harness's
arith grammar evaluates each
declaration's expression entire
(the amendment of EE 629); the
declarations are written clean and
explicit regardless.

The deposit's identity closes.
The Zenodo record states version
v0.4.0 and publication date
2023-12-04, and the README's
citation block carries the concept
DOI, 10.5281/zenodo.5371628. The
README's two size claims recompute
against the library: 'over 60
standard academic benchmarks' —
sixty-seven task directories stand
in lm_eval/tasks, seven past the
printed floor; 'hundreds of
subtasks and variants' — 1,459
distinct task names and 65 group
names across 1,501 task YAML
files, 21.78 tasks to a directory.
The tree census closes entire:
2,442 files, of which the six
largest kinds (1,503 YAML, 342
JSON, 115 Python, 72 Markdown, 23
pickle, 3 YML) sum to 2,058,
leaving 384 of every other kind;
two of the 1,503 YAML files live
outside the task library; 1,432 of
the task files parse under a plain
YAML reader, the remaining
sixty-nine carrying the Jinja
templates of 83.1.4. Seven model
backends stand in lm_eval/models.

The interface closes in the code,
read from copies held by their
hashes: the model API defines
loglikelihood twice over — the
method and its rolling variant —
and generate_until once; the
multiple-choice scoring divides by
the choice's character length at
exactly one place, the acc_norm
line of 1.2.824; the janitor's
n-gram width stands at thirteen,
as filed at 1.2.826. The library
against the book's queue closes:
GSM8K — rank 36, Cobbe et al. —
stands in the library (three
files, six occurrences of its name
in the index); AIME, HumanEval,
MBPP and OlympiadBench occur zero
times among the 1,501 — the
absences carried at 83.1.4. The
citation forms close as
differences: the README block's
twenty-four authors stand seven
past CITATION.bib's seventeen, and
the Zenodo record's thirty
contributors six past the block.

One piece of work was made by hand
over the held deposit, outside the
harness, and is recorded as hand
work: the artifact census
(p065/census_artifact.py) — the
tree and library counts the
declarations read, computed by
walking the deposit and parsing
its YAML entire (the census's
first pass counted task names by
their inline form and got 841; the
parsed count, 1,459, is the filed
one, and the difference — names
carried in block lists — is
recorded in the script). The
pre-processor's references and
frontier legs returned 0 on the
README, its standing shape for a
text with no numbered bibliography;
the work's references are its
library, classified by hand at
83.3.1. No model was run from this
desk: the scores the harness
exists to produce stand attributed
wherever the filing touches them.
# 83.1.4 — Caveats

The deposit's citation apparatus
disagrees with itself in three
places. CITATION.bib — the file a
citation manager reads — still
carries the v0.0.1 record entire:
seventeen authors, September 2021,
the concept DOI. The README's
'Cite as' block carries the
twenty-four-name byline the queue
record and Chapter 56's
bibliography follow — and beneath
it the same stale fields: month
sep, year 2021, version v0.0.1.
The Zenodo record itself lists
thirty creators, the repository's
contributors, handles and all
('ben fattori', 'Fazz', 'Aflah').
Three forms, three bylines, two
years, two versions; the work a
citation names depends on which
form the citer's tool happened to
read. This filing follows the
README block's byline and the
record's date, and says so at
83.1.1; a reader following
CITATION.bib cites a different —
smaller, earlier — work under the
same title. Q295 takes up the
general question.

The version of record's library
does not hold the examinations the
book knows best. GSM8K stands in
it; HumanEval and MBPP — queue
ranks 34 and 35, the code
examinations — occur nowhere among
its 1,501 files, and neither do
the AIME and OlympiadBench
examinations this book filed as
Chapters 70, 75 and 76, which
entered the library only in later
versions. 'The harness' without a
version is a moving library: the
same citation, followed in 2023
and in 2026, lands on different
instruments holding different
examinations (Q298).

Smaller print, recorded: the
package's own version field
(pyproject.toml) reads 1.0.0, a
placeholder — the deposit's true
version lives in the Zenodo record
and the repository's tag
(c9bbec6), nowhere in the code a
user installs. Sixty-nine task
files carry Jinja templates a
plain YAML reader cannot parse:
the task as configuration
(1.2.822) is data, but data only
its own renderer reads entire.
The normalization of 1.2.824
prices length in characters — a
unit no model computes in — so
the correction of the raw
ranking's bias is itself a
convention, priced by agreement
rather than derived (Q296). And
the README's roll of users —
hundreds of papers, dozens of
companies, the Open LLM
Leaderboard's backend — stands as
the work's own account, attributed:
this desk verified the instrument,
not its adoption.
# 83.1.5 — Open questions, novelty

Q295, THE CITATION QUESTION. The
deposit's three citation forms
name three different works — a
seventeen-author v0.0.1 in
CITATION.bib, a twenty-four-author
block with v0.0.1's fields in the
README, thirty contributors in the
Zenodo record (83.1.4) — and the
scores the harness produces are
cited the same way, as 'the
harness's number', without the
version that produced them. When
an instrument is shared, its
version is part of every result
it issues: can a result be cited
apart from the version of the
procedure that produced it, and
if the field will not carry the
version, is the result a
measurement or a rumor with a
decimal point? The question is
decidable in principle — a
bibliography that pins versions
can be checked — and undecided
in practice, since the pinning
is nobody's state (83.1.2, M1's
first gap).

Q296, THE NORMALIZATION QUESTION.
The harness reports two rankings
of the same choices — acc, by raw
likelihood, and acc_norm, by
likelihood per character
(1.2.824) — and the two disagree
on some model pairs. One of them
prices length in a unit no model
computes in; the other is biased
toward the short answer by
construction. Is there a fact of
the matter a normalization
answers to — what the model
believes, independent of the
asking — or is 'which model chose
better' a property of the pair
(model, convention), so that the
field's agreement on a convention
is not a convenience but the
whole of the measurement? The
README's own priority list puts
agreement first, official
procedure second: the filing
reads that order as the field's
answer, given before the
question was asked.

Q297, THE CONTAMINATION QUESTION.
The decontamination pass
(1.2.826) certifies a corpus free
of thirteen-word runs shared
with a named test set. Everything
below that grain — a paraphrase,
a translation, a worked solution
with its numbers changed — passes
entire, and the machine's own
design concedes it: the cutoff
and window are tuned to a shape
of copying, not to copying. Is
decontamination a property a
corpus can have, or only a
procedure it can have undergone?
If the second, then every result
priced on a 'decontaminated'
corpus is priced on a procedure's
say-so, and the country this
filing extends — whose founding
device (1.2.764) makes the mirror
concession for its tables — is
built on both sides of the seam
from procedures all the way
down. A paraphrase-robust
overlap measure, run against the
janitor's own corpora, decides
how much material the concession
covers.

Q298, THE VERSION QUESTION. The
library of the version of record
holds GSM8K and lacks the code
examinations and the olympiad
examinations the book filed from
its later life (83.1.4): the
framework a bibliography cites is
a different library at every
version, and the walk reached
this work through a 2023 citation
to a deposit whose current form
names thirty creators and a
thousand more tasks. When
Chapter 56's citation is
followed, which version's library
is 'the' framework — the one the
citing paper's authors ran, the
one its readers can fetch, or
the newest? The book files the
deposit of record and dates it;
the question is whether a
software work's identity survives
its own maintenance, and it is
the filing's, left open for the
next software work the walk
takes in.

