# 85.1.2 — State machines

M1, the contrastive decoding
machine: the generation pass (the
work's sections 3 and 4). For each
position, RUN the expert and RUN
the amateur on the prefix; FILTER
the vocabulary by the plausibility
constraint (1.2.836), keeping the
tokens within alpha of the expert's
best; SCORE each survivor by the
difference of the two
log-probabilities; and BEAM-SEARCH
— width five — the continuation
that maximizes the accumulated
score, for 256 tokens. NAMED GAPS:
no state sets alpha or the
amateur's temperature — both are
constants of the run, chosen by
sweep, and the paper's own texts
disagree on the temperature's
robust interval (85.1.4); and no
state tests the machine's premise,
that the amateur's failures rhyme
with the expert's (1.2.838) — a
pair whose failures do not rhyme
runs the machine entire and is
found out only in the output's
scores. Home: 85.1.2; concepts
1.2.836, 1.2.837, 1.2.838, 1.2.841.
[stated]

M2, the paired-preference machine:
the human evaluation (the work's
section 6.2 and appendix G). PAIR
the continuations of two methods
on one prompt; COLLECT blind
preference votes from qualified
Turkers on fluency and on
coherence; AGGREGATE the votes as
preference shares per pair and
domain. Its qualification funnel
is a state of the machine: sixty
workers sat the pre-qualification
and about twenty passed. NAMED
GAPS: the shares are printed
without their denominators — no
per-cell judgment count stands in
the work, so the shares' grain is
unknowable from the text; and the
machine's wage arithmetic does not
recompute from its own printed
numbers (85.1.4). Home: 85.1.2; no
concept of its own. [stated]

M3, the beamprefix machine: the
training of an amateur (the work's
appendix M). SAMPLE prompts from
the domain; BEAM-SEARCH the expert
on each and HARVEST the degenerate
continuations; PREFIX-TUNE a small
model — a soft prompt of length
ten — on the harvest; INSTALL the
tuned model as the amateur of M1.
NAMED GAPS: no state measures
whether the tuned amateur's
failures match the expert's better
than the borrowed sibling's do —
the rhyme the machine exists to
manufacture is nowhere scored, and
its appendix rows against the
borrowed amateur are mixed
(1.2.840, Q304); and the harvest is
the expert's degeneration under one
search width, so the amateur is
trained against the failures of
beam search specifically, a
narrower mirror than the premise
wants. Home: 85.1.2; concepts
1.2.840, 1.2.837. [stated]
# 85.1.3 — The house check

Seventeen declarations ran under
the harness against the held text
and its extracted tables, and
seventeen pass; ten further checks
were worked by hand against the
held text, and ten pass — one of
the ten by verifying a negative,
carried at 85.1.4.

The work's four headline ratios
recompute. Section 6.2 prints that
its method is preferred to nucleus
sampling 2.6 times as often on
coherence and 1.4 times on fluency,
and to typical sampling 6.4 and 3.5
times. As means of the per-row
ratios of its own Table 2, the four
recompute to 2.64, 1.42, 6.43 and
3.55 — the printed values at their
printed precision. Table 2's
twenty-four preference triples each
sum to one within 0.002, and its
GPT-2 XL nucleus rows, reprinted in
the beamprefix appendix as that
appendix's contrast, are the same
numbers in both places.

The tables agree with each other
where they overlap. Table 1's
contrastive rows are the appendix's
full tables' rows in all eighteen
values; the diversity column of the
three appendix tables is the
product of its rep columns'
complements in all fifty-seven
rows, worst deviation 0.005, the
grain of the printed two decimals;
and the ablation appendix's 'full
CD' row is its main table's 1.5B
search row, cell for cell.

The appendices' arithmetic holds.
Appendix A's rankings are as the
text claims — likelihood scores the
repetitive continuation first,
contrastive scoring the human
reference first, on the printed
per-token values. Its NetMessage
token's printed contrast of 10.6
recomputes to 10.53 from the
printed probabilities, inside the
rounding envelope those printings
allow. Appendix C's identity — the
objective as pointwise mutual
information with the expert's
authorship (1.2.839) — is exact as
algebra, verified symbolically to
1.8e-15 over two thousand random
draws, its argmax coinciding with
the contrast's over five hundred
candidates. And appendix G's wage
was verified as a negative: the
printed 18 dollars an hour follows
from neither of the appendix's own
printed times (85.1.4).

The generation scores themselves —
every MAUVE, diversity, coherence
and perplexity figure — are not
recomputable from the held text and
stand attributed; the check covers
the work's internal arithmetic,
which is sound wherever the desk
could reach it, except where
85.1.4 records.
# 85.1.4 — Caveats

The ablation appendix's last table
prints one row twice. Its Context
Ablation row and its V_head
Ablation row are identical in all
nine cells — and the V_head row so
printed contradicts the main text's
own ablation table, where removing
the constraint collapses the method
(MAUVE 0.01, perplexity 2x10^5)
while the appendix's row stands at
MAUVE 0.93. One of the two ablation
rows was never printed from its own
run: the appendix's evidence for
the constraint's necessity is a
copy of its neighbour, and the
necessity rests on the main table
alone — which is, to the work's
credit, unambiguous there (85.1.3).

The human evaluation's wage does
not recompute. Appendix G prints
4.5 dollars per task, an average
completion of 14 minutes, an
assigned 20 minutes, and a rate of
18 dollars an hour. At 14 minutes
the rate is 19.29; at the assigned
20 it is 13.50. Neither printed
time yields the printed rate. The
same appendix leaves the preference
shares without denominators — no
judgment count per cell — so the
headline ratios of 85.1.3 recompute
as ratios of shares whose grain
cannot be assessed from the work.

The cross-family claim outruns its
table. Appendix H reports a GPT-J
expert with a GPT-2 small amateur
at MAUVE 0.93, 'better than GPT-2
XL's CD results' — naming no
domain; the main table prints GPT-2
XL's contrast at 0.94, 0.92 and
0.94 across its three domains, so
the appendix's figure exceeds one
of the three. The claim stands as
printed, attributed, and flagged.

Smaller print, recorded. Table 1's
caption names three scales — 1.5B,
6.7B and 13B — while its body
prints two; the 6.7B rows stand in
the appendix's full tables, where
the house check found them in
agreement. The temperature's robust
interval is printed two ways: the
text gives tau in [0.5, 1.5] as
attaining good performance, its
figure caption and the appendix
[0.5, 1.0] as robust. And the
work's own boundary stands as a
caveat on its generality: on
summarization its amateur loses to
a uniform amateur — the method
underperforms plain beam search
where both models are already good
(1.2.841) — a limit the descendants
inherited as a law (Chapter 81)
more often than as a warning.
# 85.1.5 — Open questions, novelty

Q303, THE CONSTRAINT QUESTION. The
ancestor gates its contrast by the
expert's confidence (1.2.836): only
plausible tokens compete, and the
difference's weight is one. Every
descendant filed in this book
dropped the gate and carries a
strength instead — a leash on the
step where the ancestor put a fence
at the door. The two protections
are not the same shape: the gate
can silence the contrast entire at
a position (one survivor), which no
strength short of zero can do; the
leash can grade the step, which the
gate cannot. Is there a measurement
on which the gated form beats the
leashed form — a text class where
the shared-error positions the gate
exists for are common enough to
price — or was the constraint
scaffolding the family needed only
until the leash was learned? No
filed work runs the two forms
against each other; the ancestor's
own ablation prices the gate
against nothing, not against a
strength.

Q304, THE TRAINED-AMATEUR QUESTION.
Beamprefix manufactures the weak
pole from the expert's own
degeneration (1.2.840); the
one-model descendants find theirs
inside the network — a layer, a
withheld context. Are these the
same move at different scales: the
weak pole in each case made of the
strong one's own behaviour rather
than borrowed from a smaller
sibling? The ancestor's appendix
holds the comparison its text never
makes — the trained amateur's rows
against the borrowed one's are
mixed, coherence up and MAUVE down
— so a second question rides on the
first: what property of a task
decides which weak pole it wants,
the manufactured rhyme or the
family resemblance?

Q305, THE DISTINGUISHABILITY
QUESTION. The ancestor's appendix
proves its objective maximizes
distinguishability — text most
evidently the expert's (1.2.839) —
not quality; the two coincide only
while expertise is what
distinguishes. Chapter 79 found a
case where they part inside one
model: a planted falsehood in the
context is what distinguishes, and
the contrast repeats it as
fidelity. Where do they part for
the two-model ancestor — what text
is most evidently the expert's and
least worth reading? The work's
second reading (1.2.842) assumes
the parting away: the informative
is defined as what the less
informed could not say, which is
the parting's benign form. A
measurement that separates the two
quantities on the ancestor's own
setup — distinguishability scored
against human quality, cell by
cell — is unrun in the work and in
the line.

Q306, THE RHYME QUESTION. The
ancestor states its boundary
plainly: where both models' modes
are good — its summarization tests
— the contrast fails, its BART
amateur losing to a uniform
amateur, which is plain beam
search. The whole family spends
the failure-mode mirror (1.2.838),
and the boundary says the mirror
can be empty. What property of a
task, or of a model pair, decides
whether the amateur's failures
rhyme with the expert's? The paper
offers instances on both sides —
open text rhymes, summarization
does not, the trigram's errors do
not — and no instrument: the rhyme
is everywhere assumed by the
method and nowhere measured by it.
A rhyme measure, run before the
contrast, would be the family's
first admission test.

