# 87.1.2 — State machines

M1, the Bit Diffusion training
machine: the work's Algorithm 1.
ENCODE the datum to bits and CAST
them to analog bits, shifted and
scaled to -1..1 (1.2.849); SAMPLE
a time t uniform on (0, 1) and a
noise; CORRUPT the analog bits to
x_t by the forward transition;
ESTIMATE the clean bits with the
self-conditioning input zeroed,
and with probability one half KEEP
that estimate, gradient stopped,
as the conditioning signal
(1.2.850); PREDICT the clean bits
again with the signal concatenated;
and TAKE THE REGRESSION LOSS
against the true analog bits.
Repeat to convergence. NAMED GAPS:
no state sets the one-half drop
rate — it is a constant of the
run, nowhere swept in the work;
and no state measures whether the
model's generated bits actually
concentrate at the poles — the
conjecture the whole construction
rests on is checked only after the
fact, by a histogram over finished
samples (Figure 7), never by the
machine that bets on it (Q311).
Home: 87.1.2; concepts 1.2.849,
1.2.850. [stated]

M2, the Bit Diffusion sampling
machine: the work's Algorithm 2.
DRAW the state from the standard
normal and SET the carried estimate
to zero; at each step, FORM the
current time and the next — the
next advanced by the td offset, so
the state's clock and the network's
told time part by exactly the
asymmetry of 1.2.851 — PREDICT the
clean bits with the carried
estimate concatenated (zeroed
throughout if self-conditioning is
off), STEP the state by the DDIM
or DDPM rule, and CARRY the
prediction forward as the next
step's conditioning signal; at the
end THRESHOLD the final estimate
at zero and DECODE the bits to the
discrete datum. NAMED GAPS: no
state sets the step count or the
td offset — both are swept outside
the machine, and the work's own
Table 7 shows the choice is not a
detail (the categorical run's best
cell stands at the fewest steps
tried, Q312); and no state reads
the threshold's margin — an analog
bit resting near zero decodes on
whichever side the noise left it,
and the machine keeps no count of
how many bits were read that
closely. Home: 87.1.2; concepts
1.2.849, 1.2.850, 1.2.851.
[stated]
# 87.1.3 — The house check

Thirteen declarations ran under
the harness against the held
HTML's extracted tables, and
thirteen pass; forty-two further
checks were worked by hand against
the held text, and thirty-eight
pass — the four that do not are
the check working as designed
against the work's own pages,
carried at 87.1.4.

The counting the abstract spends
is exact. CIFAR-10's datum is
32 x 32 x 3 = 3,072 analog bits —
the '3K discrete tokens' of the
abstract and the 3,072 sequential
steps the introduction charges the
autoregressive model, against this
work's 100. ImageNet 64 x 64 is
12,288 — the '12K'. The captioning
arithmetic closes: a vocabulary of
32K takes ceil(log2 32000) = 15
bits a token, and 64 tokens make
the stated 960 bits. The results
tables agree with the prose
wherever the desk could reach
them. On CIFAR-10 the categorical
section is swept entire by the
uint8 (rand) model — 6.93 against
D3PM uniform's 51.27, D3PM
absorbing's 30.97 and the
autoregressive Transformer's
12.75, the introduction's headline
comparison exact — and the
conditional uint8 model (2.72)
beats even the authors' continuous
DDPM reproduction (2.95). Table
2's prose order is the table's
order: continuous best (3.43),
then uint8 (4.84), gray code
(5.14), uint8 (rand) worst (8.76).
The captioning claim of
competitiveness measures out
exactly as hedged: at 20 steps the
model beats the autoregressive
baseline on BLEU-4 (34.7 against
33.9) and ROUGE-L (0.58 against
0.57), and never reaches it on
CIDEr (best 1.15 against 1.18). In
Table 4 the zero time difference
is the worst cell of every row,
and the offset buys +13.7 to +17.0
BLEU-4 across the four step
counts. Table 6's loss sensitivity
recomputes: the categorical
encoding under the logistic loss
collapses to 49.29 against its
6.93 under the regression loss,
and the one-hot encoding stands an
order of magnitude behind the
base-2 code. Self-conditioning
improves all four cells of Table 8
under DPM-Solver, both predictions
at both budgets. Table 9's default
row is Table 2's row entire, and
Figure 11's four caption FIDs are
Table 2's four cells exactly.

The machinery is exact. The cosine
schedule of Appendix A runs from
gamma(0) = 0.99999990 to
gamma(1) = 6.2e-9, strictly
decreasing across the interval;
the DDIM step of Algorithm 4
round-trips the forward noising
exactly — worst deviation 4.4e-16
over two thousand draws — and the
self-guidance combination of
Algorithm 6 is, term for term, the
classifier-free form of 1.2.800:
w · a + (1 - w) · b = b + w · (a -
b), to machine precision. Table
7's inversion is strict in the
printed cells: the categorical
encoding under DDIM worsens at
every step of the budget, 8.76 at
100 evaluations, 38.44 at 400,
58.45 at 1000, and its Table 2
headline is the 100-evaluation
cell — the fewest steps tried are
its best (87.1.4).

The generation scores themselves —
every FID behind the 50,000
samples, every BLEU behind the
captions — are not recomputable
from the held text and stand
attributed; the check covers the
work's internal arithmetic and the
agreement of its prose with its
tables, which is sound except
where 87.1.4 records.
# 87.1.4 — Caveats

Two of Figure 10's caption scores
disagree with the table they
illustrate. The figure prints its
unconditional CIFAR-10 samples
with FIDs of 3.14 (DDPM), 3.71
(Bit Diffusion on uint8), 3.88
(gray code) and 6.93 (uint8
(rand)); Table 1 prints 3.14 for
the DDPM reproduction and 6.93
for uint8 (rand) — exact agreement
— but 3.48 for uint8 and 3.86 for
gray code. The figure's two middle
models were therefore scored in a
run the table does not report, or
the captions carry stale numbers;
the work gives no way to tell
which, and a reader quoting the
figure quotes scores the table
contradicts. Figure 11's captions,
by contrast, agree with Table 2 in
all four cells — the disagreement
is local to Figure 10, which is
what makes it a caveat rather than
a pattern.

Table 3's captioning rows are not
Table 4's maxima, and the
operating point is never stated.
Table 4 sweeps the time difference
for each step count: its 5-step
row reaches 31.6 and its 10-step
row 34.6, where Table 3 prints
31.5 and 34.5 for the same step
counts; at 20 and 40 steps the two
tables agree exactly (34.7, 34.4).
Table 3's numbers are therefore
read at some time difference the
work does not name — a small
thing, except that the offset is
worth up to seventeen BLEU points
in the work's own sweep, so the
headline table's recipe cannot be
rebuilt from the paper.

The ImageNet headline row is three
different runs wearing one label.
Section 3.1 discloses the sweep —
samplers, step counts, time
differences — and Table 7 prints
the grid: Table 2's uint8 score
(4.84) is the DDPM sampler at 400
evaluations, its gray code score
(5.14) is DDIM at 1000, and its
uint8 (rand) score (8.76) is DDIM
at 100 — where the same encoding
under DDIM at 1000 evaluations
scores 58.45. Table 2 prints the
three bests side by side as one
model's row on one dataset, which
they are; what the row conceals is
that no single sampling recipe
earns all three, and that the
categorical best is the grid's
most fragile cell, not its most
robust.

The abstract's widest claim has a
narrower base than its wording.
It claims state-of-the-art
improved 'on both CIFAR-10 and
ImageNet 64 x 64'; the text
concedes that for ImageNet 'we
have not found other competing
methods that report FIDs', so the
ImageNet leg compares the discrete
encodings only against the
authors' own continuous DDPM —
which beats every one of them
(3.43 against 4.84, 5.14, 8.76).
The CIFAR-10 leg carries the
claim, and carries it honestly;
the ImageNet leg is a comparison
the field had not shown up for.

Smaller print, recorded. The
introduction's 'one third of the
model size of the autoregressive
model' cannot be recomputed from
the held text — the work prints
its own 51M parameters for
CIFAR-10 and nowhere prints the
autoregressive model's, so the
ratio stands attributed, as does
the 'less than 25%' training-cost
figure for self-conditioning. And
the concentration the construction
bets on (1.2.849) is evidenced by
histograms over 64 generated
images — the only direct look at
the load-bearing conjecture the
work takes (Q311).
# 87.1.5 — Open questions, novelty

Q311, THE CONCENTRATION QUESTION.
The construction bets everything
on an unenforced behaviour: no
term in the loss, no constraint in
the machine, drives an analog bit
toward -1 or 1 — the work's
conjecture is that a strong
continuous model concentrates the
values itself, and its whole
evidence is a histogram over 64
generated images (Figure 7),
read after the fact. Nothing in
the filing measures the margin
per bit, per dataset, per scale —
how close to the threshold the
mass rests, and what the decode
loses where it rests close. When
does a continuous model fail to
concentrate — a weaker model, a
larger alphabet spelled in more
bits, a datum whose bits are near
independent — and would the
failure announce itself anywhere
but in degraded samples, the one
signal that cannot distinguish a
concentration failure from every
other kind? The book's other
ports state their warrant; this
one's warrant is a picture of it
working.

Q312, THE INVERSION QUESTION. For
the categorical encoding, sampling
more makes the samples worse —
strictly, and by a factor of
nearly seven across the work's
own grid (8.76 at 100 DDIM
evaluations, 58.45 at 1000) —
while the ordinal encodings hold
or improve over the same budgets.
The work names over-smoothing
under self-conditioning as the
suspect and files two patches,
momentum and self-guidance,
neither of which restores the
100-step score at 1000 steps in
anything it prints. Three suspects
stand unseparated: the encoding
(no order for the trajectory to
lean on, 1.2.853), the memory (the
carried estimate compounding its
own smoothing, 1.2.850), and the
sampler itself. Which carries the
inversion — and is refinement
turning against itself a property
other trajectory residents should
expect when their cargo loses its
geometry?

Q313, THE MEMORY QUESTION.
Self-conditioning hands the
denoiser its own previous
estimate, and the gain is large,
generic across encodings, and
present on continuous pixels where
no bits are involved (Table 8).
But the carried estimate is a
function of states the trajectory
has already visited: it contains
no information the walk has not
touched. What, then, does the
network read in it? If the gain is
informational, the estimate must
carry something the current state
x_t does not — a commitment, a
partial decision the state leaves
open. If the gain is
computational — a second look at
the same evidence, one extra
evaluation folded into the step —
then any cheap second look should
buy it, and the book's step-count
accounting (Q310) owes the
trajectory country a recount:
self-conditioning's steps are not
the same size as the steps it is
compared against. No filed work
separates the two readings.

Q314, THE TRANSFER QUESTION. The
conclusion's promise is that the
port is generic: improvements in
continuous diffusion should cross
to discrete data through the
analog bits for free, and the
work shows one crossing — the
DPM-Solver's speedup arrives
intact (Table 7). The same table
shows an anti-crossing: the
field's most basic improvement,
more sampling steps, arrives as a
sevenfold harm on the categorical
encoding. What property of an
improvement decides whether it
crosses? A sampler that assumes
the trajectory's smoothness may
import exactly what the
categorical cargo lacks; a solver
that assumes only the trajectory's
form crosses clean. Until the
deciding property is named, the
port's genericity is a hope with
one witness on each side — and
every future resident of the
country that imports a continuous
technique inherits the question.

NOVELTY BY AUTHOR — Ting Chen:
True +1 (the house check, 87.1.3),
False +0, Unknown +9 (the five
concepts 1.2.849-1.2.853, and
Q311-Q314), Fiction +0.
NOVELTY BY AUTHOR — Ruixiang
Zhang: True +1, False +0, Unknown
+9, Fiction +0.
NOVELTY BY AUTHOR — Geoffrey
Hinton: True +1, False +0, Unknown
+9, Fiction +0.
Chen and Hinton are new to the
book; their vectors are drawn at
1.5.6 (87.1.6). Zhang returns —
he stands in the book as a
co-author of Paper 038 (Chapter
56), the very chapter whose
bibliography queued this work —
and his vector is updated, not
redrawn (87.1.6).
