# Chapter 88 — Paper 070: Evaluating Large Language Models Trained on Code (Chen et al.)

THE DELTA. Seven concepts
(1.2.854-1.2.860), no foundation
laid, no held concept revised, no
word coined: CLUSTER AY EXTENDED —
its sixth paper. The work is the
examination country's founding
table for code, filed five
chapters after its own audit: an
examination of 164 programming
problems written by hand so the
training corpus cannot already
hold them (1.2.856), graded by an
executed verdict — the candidate
run against unit tests, never
matched against a reference text
(1.2.854) — inside a sandbox built
to run untrusted code without
harm (1.2.857), and counted by
pass@k, the any-of-k measure with
its unbiased estimator (1.2.855).
Around the table the work files
the country's standing gap, the
oracle-free choice (1.2.858), and
its two sharpest limitation
findings: the chain decay
(1.2.859), capability divided by
a factor of two to three per
chained clause of the
specification, and the withheld
capability (1.2.860), misalignment
made measurable as a gap that
grows with scale. Cluster
placement is weighed at 88.2.2:
AY extended, the deliverable a
table and its measure; the
infrastructure founding weighed
under Chapter 84's restated
condition and declined again;
cluster J weighed a seventeenth
time and not joined — every
verdict in the work is spent
after the judged computation or
one stage above it, never inside
it. The house check recomputed
the work's internal arithmetic
(88.1.3): five declarations,
five pass, and twenty-eight hand
checks, five of them the check
working as designed against the
work's own pages, carried at
88.1.4.

Its provenance: rank 34 of the
reference frontier built at leg 1
of the network walk — cited by
