# 88.1.2 — State machines

M1, the HumanEval grading machine:
the work's Sections 2 and 3.2.
ASSEMBLE each problem into a
prompt — a header, a signature, a
docstring; SAMPLE n = 200
completions at the chosen
temperature under nucleus sampling
(p = 0.95), stopping at the named
stop sequences (a new class, a new
function, a comment, a branch, a
print); EXECUTE each completion in
the sandbox (1.2.857) against the
problem's unit tests; COUNT the
correct c; and ESTIMATE pass@k for
every k up to 100 by the unbiased
form (1.2.855). NAMED GAPS: no
state chooses the temperature —
it is swept outside the machine,
and its optimum moves with the
budget (0.2 for pass@1, 0.8 for
pass@100 at the 679M size), so the
machine's headline numbers are
read at settings the machine does
not contain; and no state audits
the tests — a problem whose tests
are wrong grades wrong, silently,
the fallible ground truth the
examination's descendant later
priced (1.2.834). Home: 88.1.2;
concepts 1.2.854, 1.2.855, 1.2.857.
[stated]

M2, the continuous-integration
tracing machine: the work's
Section 4.2. READ a project's CI
configuration; BUILD its
environment and INSTALL its
dependencies; RUN its integration
tests under a profiling hook that
RECORDS the inputs and outputs of
every function called; HARVEST
each recorded pair as a unit
test, assembling problems in the
examination's own form — all of
it inside the sandbox, the traced
code being untrusted. NAMED GAPS:
of millions of candidate functions
only some 40,000 were collected —
most objects captured at runtime
cannot be pickled and restored
outside the room, so the harvest
is bounded by a serialization
limit nowhere in the work's
design; and the hook sweeps in
builtin and library calls with
the project's own, so the
machine's problems skew toward
instruction-following over
algorithmics — a skew the work
itself names, and prices as a
complement to the contest
problems' puzzle nature. Home:
88.1.2; concepts 1.2.854, 1.2.857.
[stated]

M3, the self-filtering machine:
the work's Section 4.3. For each
curated problem, SAMPLE 100
solutions from the largest held
model; KEEP the problem if at
least one sample passes its unit
tests; DISCARD it as ambiguous or
too difficult if none does; and
REPEAT the verification several
times to remove stateful or
non-deterministic problems. The
survivors are the supervised
fine-tuning set from which Codex-S
is trained. NAMED GAP: the
filter's standard is the filter
model's own present reach — a
problem every sample fails may be
a sound problem standing beyond
the model, and the machine cannot
tell ambiguity from difficulty,
so the curriculum's ceiling is
set by the very capability the
curriculum exists to raise; the
verdict here is spent on the
training data of the next model,
one stage above the judged
computation (weighed for cluster
J at 88.2.2). Home: 88.1.2;
concepts 1.2.854, 1.2.855.
[stated]
# 88.1.3 — The house check

Five declarations ran under the
harness against the held HTML's
extracted tables, and five pass —
the shapes only: the
pre-processor's tables arm emptied
every cell on this dialect, so the
five tables stand at their row
counts (1, 15, 9, 3, 5, agreeing
with the held HTML's own counts
entire) and the numerics ran as
named hand work against the held
text; twenty-eight further checks
were worked by hand
(verify_tables.py), and
twenty-three pass — the five that
do not are the check working as
designed against the work's own
pages, carried at 88.1.4.

The tables are internally sound.
Table 1 is monotone in the budget
on all thirteen rows — pass@1 at
or below pass@10 at or below
pass@100 — and the Codex ladder
climbs strictly with size at every
budget, 2.00 to 28.81 at pass@1.
The prose's roundings of the table
are exact where they are
roundings: the abstract's 28.8 is
the table's 28.81; the text's 13.2
for the 300M model is its 13.17;
TabNine's 2.6 and 7.6 are its 2.58
and 7.59. Table 2's raw rows climb
with the budget on all three
tiers; its filtered pass@1 stands
above its raw pass@100 on the
introductory tier (22.78 against
20.20), as the construction
promises; and all eighteen
timeout-inclusive bracket figures
stand at or above their plain
cells. Figure 1's caption and
Section 1 agree entire — Codex-S
at 37.7, the log-probability pick
at 44.5, the test-selected pick at
77.5, at both loci.

The machinery is exact, and here
the house could do more than
compare print: it re-ran the
argument. The estimator of
equation (1), implemented as the
work's Figure 3 prints it, is
unbiased at n = 200 in simulation
— bias within 0.0035 across four
regimes (p of 0.05 and 0.10 at
k = 10; 0.01 and 0.02 at k = 100;
4,000 draws each) — while the
naive plug-in the appendix warns
against underestimates in every
regime, by 0.0905 where p is 0.01
and k is 100: Appendix A's claim
recomputed, not quoted. The
counting closes: 164 problems at
an average of 7.7 tests imply
1,262.8 tests; the training
filter's cut from 179 to 159
gigabytes is an 11.2 percent
reduction; the APPS split is
5,000 and 5,000; Section 5's
grading ran 164 problems at 10
samples — 1,640 gradings, whose
name in the text is a caveat at
88.1.4.

The generation scores themselves
— every pass rate behind the
tables, the scaling law's exponent
of -0.13, the temperature optima,
the Codex-S margins of 6.5 and
15.1 points, the tokenizer's
saving of some 30 percent — are
not recomputable from the held
text and stand attributed; the
check covers the work's internal
arithmetic and the agreement of
its prose with its tables, which
is sound except where 88.1.4
records.
# 88.1.4 — Caveats

The abstract's GPT-J figure
matches neither of the paper's
own. The abstract and the
introduction give GPT-J 11.4
percent; Table 1 prints 11.62,
and Section 3.4 rounds it to
11.6. A reader quoting the
abstract — the sentence of the
paper most quoted — understates
the strongest outside model
against the paper's own table by
a fifth of a point, in the
comparison the abstract exists to
make.

The abstract's sampling headline
has no cell. 'We solve 70.2% of
our problems with 100 samples per
problem' — but Table 1's Codex-12B
pass@100 is 72.31, and Figure 1's
test-selected figure is 77.5, for
Codex-S. The 70.2 presumably
stands at Figure 1's stated
temperature of 0.8 for the base
model, a reading the paper
nowhere states; the widest number
in the abstract cannot be rebuilt
from anything the paper prints.

The examination changes size
between the body and the
appendix. Section 2.2's HumanEval
holds 164 problems; Appendix E's
alignment evaluations rest on 'the
HumanEval dataset: 158 problems'
and evaluate 'all 158 examples'.
Six problems are unaccounted —
no subset is named, no exclusion
stated — in the appendix whose
whole method is the careful
counting of a capability's
presence.

Section 3.4's equivalences are
coarse in a way the prose does not
price. GPT-Neo-2.7B, 'roughly
equivalent' to Codex-85M, stands
1.81 points under it at pass@1
(6.41 against 8.22 — a gap of
more than a quarter of the
smaller figure); GPT-J, roughly
Codex-300M, stands 1.55 points
under (11.62 against 13.17);
TabNine, roughly Codex-12M,
stands 0.58 over (2.58 against
2.00). The parameter ratios the
sentences celebrate — thirty
times fewer, twenty times fewer —
are exact; the performance
equality they decorate is an
approximation of a point and a
half, at the scale where the
paper's own ladder moves two to
five points a doubling.

Smaller print, recorded. Section
3.4's GPT-Neo pass@100 of '21.3%'
truncates Table 1's 21.37, which
rounds to 21.4 — the prose
truncates here where elsewhere it
rounds. Section 5 grades '10
samples per problem, for a total
of 1640 problems': the 1,640 are
samples; the problems are 164.
And Table 3's Codex-S-12B pass@1
of 32.2 stands below Section 1's
37.7 — reconciled by the stated
settings (Table 3 graded at
temperature 0.8; Section 4.5's
pass@1 optimum is temperature 0),
and recorded as reconciled, not
as a disagreement: the same model
at two temperatures is two
readings, and the paper states
both temperatures.
# 88.1.5 — Open questions, novelty

Q315, THE ORACLE GAP QUESTION.
Between the best deployable pick
and the oracle's pick stand
thirty-three points (1.2.858):
44.5 percent for the sample the
model's own log-probability ranks
first, 77.5 for the sample the
tests would choose. Every selector
the founding work tries reads the
judged object's own voice, or a
sibling model's — none reads the
verdict, which is the one thing
deployment does not have. Is the
gap closable by any signal
available at the moment of choice,
or is the verdict's information
simply not in the samples, so
selection without execution has a
ceiling far under the oracle? The
examination country's later
residents spend the verdict after
the fact, at grading; no filed
work prices a selector that
approaches it from inside the
budget.

Q316, THE CHAIN QUESTION. The
chain decay (1.2.859) taxes each
added clause of a specification by
a factor of two to three. But the
synthetic blocks are independent
by construction — each clause's
implementation neither helps nor
hinders the next — so the measured
tax may be the blocks'
independence wearing the model's
name. On real specifications,
whose clauses share state,
variables and structure, does the
per-link tax shrink, hold, or
grow? And does it flatten with
scale, as the pass rates climb, or
is composition the wall that
neither sampling nor scale can
climb — the one curve in the work
that does not bend toward the
models' favour?

Q317, THE WITHHELD-CAPABILITY
QUESTION. The gap of 1.2.860 —
the model buggier after buggy
examples than it can be — grows
with model size. The work proposes
three remedies and runs none:
curate the corpus, condition on a
quality label, train on human
feedback. Is the gap removable by
any of them — a property of the
data the objective ate, priced
once and curable — or is it the
objective's own shape, fidelity to
a corpus that contains the flaw,
so that every scale-up buys the
capability and its withholding at
one price? A measurement that
separates the two readings would
price most alignment claims filed
since; none filed here makes it.

Q318, THE HAND QUESTION. The
executed verdict's founding cost
is the hand: 164 problems written
one by one, so the corpus the
models ate cannot already hold
them (1.2.856). The examination's
descendant at Chapter 84
manufactures the verdict instead —
a reference program, generated
tests by the hundred — and
inherits the reference's
departures as its own (1.2.834).
Can a verdict be manufactured at
the founding's honesty: tests
whose failures mean what
hand-written tests mean, over
problems no hand has answered
first? Until it can, every
executed verdict in the country is
either small and hand-made or
large and inherited, and the
choice between them is the
country's standing trade.

