# Chapter 90 — Paper 072: Training Verifiers to Solve Math Word Problems (Cobbe et al.)

THE DELTA. Seven concepts
(1.2.868-1.2.874), no foundation
laid, no held concept revised, no
word coined: CLUSTER AY EXTENDED —
its eighth paper. The work is the
examination country's founding
for mathematics: GSM8K, 8,500
grade-school word problems whose
key is worked solutions in
natural language (1.2.868),
written by contracted hands to a
published instruction set and
built to be trained on. On the
table the work builds the
country's first judge that is a
model: a verifier trained on a
hundred thousand of the
generator's own sampled
solutions, each labeled by its
final answer alone (1.2.869,
1.2.872), and spent by the ranked
selection (1.2.870) — a hundred
samples drawn, the judge's
favourite returned — under which
a judged 6B generator stands
where a finetuned 175B guessing
once stands, in a comparison the
work prints only as a figure.
Around the pair the work files
the token-level judge (1.2.871),
a verdict read after every token
and spent nowhere; the coverage
collapse (1.2.873), the
generator's breadth spent by the
training that sharpens it; and
the calculator annotation
(1.2.874), arithmetic executed by
override inside the key's own
solutions. Cluster J weighed a
nineteenth time and not joined
(90.2.2); the infrastructure
founding declined a fifth time.
Seven of the twelve authors
return from Paper 070; five are
new to the book. No restructuring
falls due.
# 90.1.2 — State machines

M1, the verifier-training machine:
the work's Section 4 and Appendix
E. FINETUNE the generator on the
worked examination's training
split for two epochs; SAMPLE one
hundred completions per training
problem; LABEL each by whether
its final answer matches the
key's; TRAIN the verifier one
epoch on the joint objective —
the judge's loss and the
language-model loss together.
NAMED GAPS: the labels judge only
the final answer, so flawed
reasoning with a right answer
trains as correct (1.2.872); and
no state regenerates the training
solutions as the verifier
improves — the judge is trained
once, on a fixed generator's
output.
M2, the rank-and-select machine:
the work's Sections 4 and 5.
SAMPLE one hundred completions
of the problem at temperature
0.7; SCORE each with the
verifier; RETURN the completion
the judge ranks first — or, in
the voting variant, the final
answer the top-ranked solutions
agree on. NAMED GAPS: no state
bounds the search — past roughly
four hundred completions the
output worsens as adversarial
solutions fool the judge, and the
machine cannot tell it has
crossed; and the verdict is spent
only after every candidate is
finished — no state prunes a
partial solution, though the
judge can read one (1.2.871).
M3, the calculator-override
machine: the work's Section 4 and
Appendix C. WATCH the generator's
output for a <<...>> annotation;
at the equals sign, OVERRIDE the
tokens that follow with the
evaluated result; CONTINUE
sampling. NAMED GAPS: the
annotation habit is learned from
imperfect annotations, so lines
that could be computed are
silently generated instead; and
the original instrument carried
disclosed bugs, so every score it
produced is a slight
underestimate (90.1.4).
# 90.1.3 — The house check

Seven declarations ran under the
harness, and seven pass — one
shape only: the pre-processor's
tables arm kept the work's single
table (Appendix B's
hyperparameters) at twenty-three
rows by one column with every
cell empty on this dialect, so
the table stands at its shape and
the numerics ran as named hand
work against the held text;
twenty-three further checks were
worked by hand (verify_tables.py),
and all twenty-three pass — one
of them the check working as
designed against the work's own
pages, carried at 90.1.4: the
work's headline claims stand in
its figures, with no number in
the text to recompute.

The counting closes entire. The
examination's split is 7,500 and
1,000 — 8,500, the printed total.
The bibliography counts to
twenty-nine references. The
related-dataset census the work
prints stands as printed:
Dolphin18K at 18K problems,
AQuA-RAT at 100K, Ape210K at
210K, ASDiv at 2.3K, and MathQA
carrying some thirty percent
inconsistencies in its options.
The size sweep closes: the
work's smallest training set, 175
problems against a 6B model, is
the 29.2-fold the text calls
approximately thirty times
smaller than the full set. The
no-reasoning ablation closes:
training without solution steps
costs 15.4 points, 20.6 down to
5.2, and the fallen share is the
quarter the text claims (5.2 of
20.6 is 0.2524). The error census
closes, and tightens as it
closes: Appendix A bounds
breaking errors below two percent
of problems, and the body text
stands inside its own bound, at
1.7 percent of problems still
producing disagreements. The
learning-rate table closes: the
four base rates fall strictly
with size — 1.6, 1.2 and 1.0 in
units of 10^-5 for the 3B, 6B and
12B models, 6.0 x 10^-6 at 175B —
and the fifth 1.0 x 10^-5 in the
table is the verifier's own rate,
not a fifth model's. The recipe
closes across the work: two
epochs for the generator and one
for the verifier, twenty percent
dropout, one hundred completions
a problem, the batch-size factor
of four between finetuning and
verification — each stated in the
body and again in the appendix,
and agreeing. Figure 8(c)'s three
hundred completions stand against
the headline hundred, and the
exception is declared in the note
to Table 1 itself. And the
designed negative stands as the
check's sharpest finding: the
body text's percentages are
exactly four — 20.6 and 5.2, the
ablation pair; 20, the dropout;
30, the related-dataset census —
and the claim the work is known
by, that a judged 6B matches a
finetuned 175B, exists only as
two curves in Figure 5. The work
prints its evidence as figures
and its numbers as ablations; the
check can recompute everything
the work numbers, and the work
numbers almost nothing it is
cited for.
# 90.1.4 — Caveats

The work's most-quoted claim has
no number. That a 6B generator
under verification performs
comparably to a finetuned 175B
generator is stated in the
abstract and the introduction,
and drawn in Figure 5 — and no
solve rate for any model stands
in the work's text at all: the
body's only percentages are an
ablation pair, a dropout rate
and a related-dataset census
(90.1.3). The comparison is
therefore filed attributed, not
verified: there is nothing
printed to recompute, and the
curves cannot be re-run from
this desk.

The largest model's curve is a
single run. Figure 5's caption
states that the 175B verification
result was run once, where the
other sizes are means over three
runs with standard deviations —
the comparison the work is known
by rests, at its top end, on one
draw.

The scaling claim is self-labelled
naive. The work fits its
training-set sweep log-linearly
and extrapolates to a model of
10^16 parameters for eighty
percent solve rates, calling the
extrapolation naive in its own
sentence; it is filed as the
work's hedge, not its finding.

Every number in the work ran on
a buggy calculator. Appendix C
discloses that the calculator
used in all experiments
mis-scored solutions using
certain functions, that the
discrepancy is under one percent,
and that fixing it improves
verification by about one percent
on the full set. The filed scores
are the buggy instrument's
scores, and the direction of the
error is known: they are slight
underestimates.

The judge's labels bless false
positives by construction.
Training labels come from final
answers alone (1.2.872), so the
verifier's education includes
flawed reasoning with lucky
answers — a defect the work names
itself, in its own appendix, as
exactly what a verifier should
not learn from.

The budget is not uniform. The
headline sampling budget is one
hundred completions, but Figure
8(c)'s coverage curves were drawn
at three hundred — declared in
the note to Table 1, and easy to
miss when the curves are read
against the headline procedure.
# 90.1.5 — Open questions, novelty

Q323, THE UNSPENT VALUE QUESTION.
The token-level judge (1.2.871)
is trained to read a verdict
after every token, but the work
spends only the finished
solution's final score. A search
that pruned or steered partial
solutions by the running verdict
would spend verdicts inside the
generation — the form the
boundary at 90.2.2 waits for, in
this family's own instrument.
Does any work spend it so; and if
the spending moved inside, would
a judge trained on finished
solutions' labels still price
partial ones?

Q324, THE LABEL QUESTION. The
judge's labels see only the final
answer (1.2.872), and the work's
own plots show it punishing sound
reasoning and blessing flawed
reasoning that lands right. How
much of the verifier's measured
power is judgment of reasoning,
and how much is recognition of
plausible answers? A work
labeling the reasoning itself —
process labels, step by step —
would price the difference; no
filed work does.

Q325, THE FOOLING QUESTION. The
ranked selection (1.2.870)
improves to roughly four hundred
candidates and then falls — search
finds the solutions that fool the
judge. Is the ceiling a property
of this judge (its size, its
training set) or of judged search
itself? And does the book's one
resident judge, whose verdict is
spent as reward across a whole
reinforcement run, face the same
ceiling at training scale, where
the search never stops?

Q326, THE COVERAGE QUESTION. The
generator's breadth collapses as
its accuracy grows (1.2.873), and
the work repairs both with
dropout. Is the trade a law of
finetuning — accuracy bought with
coverage — or an artifact of this
recipe? The country's other
samplers buy breadth back at test
time with budget; whether breadth
can be kept in the weights rather
than re-bought per problem is
open.

