# Chapter 93 — Paper 075: WinoGrande: An Adversarial Winograd Schema Challenge at Scale (Sakaguchi et al.)

THE DELTA. Seven concepts
(1.2.889-1.2.895), one held
concept revised (1.2.884), no
foundation laid, no word coined:
CLUSTER AY EXTENDED — its
eleventh paper. The work takes
the examination country's
smallest ancestor — the Winograd
schema, 273 expert-crafted twin
sentences — and manufactures it:
thirty-eight thousand twins
crowdsourced around seeded
topics (1.2.894), validated by
majority vote, and debiased by
AfLite, the adversarial filter
(1.2.884) the book already held
from its PIQA instance, here
founded, named and measured, and
its scroll revised in place. The
item is the twin schema
(1.2.889): one frame, one blank,
two fillers, the answer flipping
at a trigger word (1.2.890). The
filter that hardens the table
also breaks it — a third of the
debiased questions stand without
their twin (1.2.891) — and its
discards are released as the
training pool (1.2.892), on
which the headline models train.
Humans score 94.0; the best
model, 79.1. Underneath the
table stands the work's
suspicion, filed as the
wrong-reasons score (1.2.893):
that the era's near-human
numbers price the tables' leaks,
not the models' commonsense —
a suspicion it turns on its own
transfer triumphs and leaves
open. Its Exhibit A is the
polarity pattern (1.2.895), a
bias of sentence shape that no
lexical instrument can see.
Cluster J weighed a
twenty-second time and not
joined (93.2.2); the
infrastructure founding declined
an eighth time. Two authors
return from Paper 074; two are
new to the book. The filing
carries the count to 895
canonical concepts; no
restructuring falls due — Pass
#41 set the next trigger at
Paper 085 or 900 concepts.
# 93.1.2 — State machines

M1, the crowdsourcing machine:
the work's Section 2. PRIME each
worker with an anchor word drawn
from a randomly assigned WikiHow
article (1.2.894); WRITE twin
sentences of fifteen to thirty
words with at least 70 percent
word overlap, one trigger flip
and two answer options, in the
social or the physical domain;
workers qualified at 99 percent
approval and five thousand
approvals, paid $0.4 per twin;
VALIDATE each question by three
distinct workers — the majority
must choose the key, the three
must agree the options are
unambiguous, and the question
must not be answerable by word
association from the local
context alone; DISCARD the
invalid, 68 percent surviving.
NAMED GAPS: the validation's
third test asks human judges to
certify the absence of a bias
that humans demonstrably insert
— 13.5 percent of the expert
original carried it — so the
machine's own gate is porous in
exactly the place the filter is
then needed; and the seeding
flattens topics, not strategies,
the workers' crafting habits
passing through untouched.
M2, the AfLite machine: the
work's Section 3 and its first
algorithm, the founding of the
adversarial filter (1.2.884,
revised there). FINE-TUNE
RoBERTa on 6,000 instances
(5,000 training, 1,000
validation); EMBED the remaining
47,000; DISCARD the 6,000;
ENSEMBLE sixty-four logistic
regressions on random partitions
of the survivors, training size
10,000; SCORE each instance by
the fraction of the ensemble
whose prediction matches its
label; REMOVE the top five
hundred scoring at or above
0.75; ITERATE until a phase
removes fewer than five hundred
or fewer than 10,000 remain.
NAMED GAPS: the bias removed is
RoBERTa's portrait — what its
embeddings cannot express
survives by construction, the
standing gap of 1.2.884; and the
machine addresses instances, not
pairs, so it breaks twins
(1.2.891) without knowing the
pair structure exists.
M3, the fill-in scoring machine:
the work's baseline section.
REPLACE the blank with each of
the two options in turn; SCORE
the two completed sentences —
the ensemble language models by
likelihood, BERT by a classifier
over the [CLS] state of context
[SEP] option; RETURN the higher.
Its diagnostic variant, the word
association baseline, RESTRICTS
the input to the local context
around the blank. NAMED GAPS:
each option is priced in a
separate pass against the frame
alone — the machine never holds
the two candidates against each
other, the same named gap as the
paired examination's scorer
(92.1.2), Q334's kin; and the
diagnostic variant prices
exactly the leak the dataset was
built to exclude, by design.
M4, the transfer machine: the
work's Section 5. FINE-TUNE
RoBERTa on WinoGrande, train and
development splits; EVALUATE
zero-shot on the target
benchmark, or FINE-TUNE further
on the target's own splits and
then evaluate. NAMED GAP: the
machine cannot distinguish
teaching commonsense from
teaching the target's artifacts
— its gains are double-edged by
the work's own account
(1.2.893), and the work leaves
the separation open (Q339).
# 93.1.3 — The house check

Forty-eight declarations ran
under the harness and all
forty-eight pass; twenty-three
further checks were worked by
hand (verify_tables.py), and all
twenty-three pass — two of them
designed negatives, the check
working as designed against the
work's own pages, carried at
93.1.4: the collected questions
overstated against the work's
own twin count, and a borrowed
dataset's split that does not
sum to its stated whole.

The counting closes where the
work prints exact figures. The
debiased release: 9,248 training,
1,267 development, 1,767 test —
12,282 in all. The full release:
40,938 training, the same
development and test — 43,972 in
all, and the filtered residue
the difference, 31,690, printed
as 31k. The AfLite subset closes:
5,000 and 1,000 make the 6,000
the embedder was fine-tuned on,
and 53,000 less 6,000 is the
47,000 embedded. The human gaps
close at the abstract's rounding:
94.0 less 79.1 is 14.9, printed
as 15; 94.0 less 59.4 is 34.6,
printed as 35. The Winogender
claim closes by derivation: the
four cells of the work's own
RoBERTa row — 97.5, 96.7, 96.7,
97.5 — average to the abstract's
97.1, and all ten printed gender
gaps in that table recompute
from their cells.

The tables close with the
extraction's gaps named. The
third table's kept rows stand
cell-for-cell; its RoBERTa row's
two scores and its two DPR rows'
name cells were dropped by the
tables arm and are read against
the held text, where the rows
stand in order entire — RoBERTa
79.3 and 79.1 the best model
scores, its XL row in the fourth
table identical, proving the
headline model trained on the
full 40,938. The fourth table's
test accuracies rise strictly
with size. The fifth table
stands cell-for-cell but for its
two WinoGrande name cells,
dropped with their subscripts
and read against the held text.
The five claimed state-of-the-art
results stand in the sixth
table, and the two tables the
abstract does not claim are
rightly unclaimed: on PDP the
work's 87.5 stands below a
daggered 90.0, and on
SuperGLUE-WSC its 85.6 below an
ensemble's 89.0.

The designed negatives stand as
the check's sharpest findings.
The crowdsourcing section
collects '77k questions (i.e.,
38k twins)': two times 38,000 is
76,000, and the printed total
stands 1,000 above the count its
own parenthetical derives. And
the DPR section splits 'the
original training set (1,332)'
into training (1,200) and
development (122): the parts sum
to 1,322, ten problems
unaccounted in print (Q335).
# 93.1.4 — Caveats

The construction counting is
round at the top. The collected
77k questions stand against the
38k twins' derived 76k (93.1.3);
the valid share is printed as
53k where 68 percent of the
printed 77k derives 52,360; and
the filter's pool accounting
closes only at the same grain —
the 47,000 embedded less the
12,282 debiased leaves 34,718
removed, against the released
residue's 31,690, the difference
of 3,028 standing six adrift of
the development and test splits'
3,034. The exact splits close
entire; the totals they were
drawn from do not, and the filed
figures are the derivable ones.

A borrowed split does not sum.
The DPR training set is stated
at 1,332 and divided into 1,200
and 122, which make 1,322
(93.1.3). The ten missing
problems are Q335; the transfer
results that use the split are
filed as printed.

The headline range mixes
splits. The abstract's best
methods 'achieve 59.4-79.1%':
79.1 is the best model's test
score, but 59.4 is the
DPR-transfer model's development
score — its test score is 58.9.
The results section's own
account gives the range as the
learning curve's, 59 percent at
800 training instances on the
development set; the abstract
prints the two ends without the
distinction.

The debiased training split
teaches at chance, by the work's
own footnote. The headline
scores train on the full 40,938,
residue included (1.2.892); the
work's ninth footnote states
that trained on the debiased
9,248 alone, both BERT and
RoBERTa showed only chance-level
performance. No table in the
work prices the kept training
split's contribution apart from
the residue's (Q336).

The 118K figure is an
extrapolation. 'To achieve
human-level performance, current
state-of-the-art models would
need over 118K training
instances' — the largest size
trained is 40,938, the curve is
a development curve, and no
functional form for the
extension is stated (Q337).

The human score is the
validators' own instrument.
Human performance is the
majority vote of three crowd
workers per question — the same
pool and the same vote that
validated the questions — and no
independent or expert human
measurement stands in the work.
The reported model scores are
the work's, as printed, and
cannot be re-run from this desk;
the sixth table's daggered rows
stand on evaluation data the
work itself marks as not exactly
its own, and one of its COPA
figures carries the work's own
note that it could not reproduce
the leaderboard number.
# 93.1.5 — Open questions, novelty

Q335, THE TEN PROBLEMS. The work
divides DPR's original training
set, stated at 1,332 problems,
into a training split of 1,200
and a development split of 122 —
which sum to 1,322 (93.1.3). Ten
problems are unaccounted in
print: a third portion unstated,
a rounding in the stated total,
a transcription? The work does
not say, and the transfer
results of its fifth section are
computed over the divided set.

Q336, THE RESIDUE QUESTION. The
filter removes 31,690 problems
as too biased to examine with,
and the work releases them as
training data (1.2.892); the
headline models train on the
union, and the work's ninth
footnote reports chance-level
performance from the debiased
training set alone. What does
the kept 9,248 teach, that its
own baselines cannot rise off
chance on it — and is the
table's difficulty then a
property of its test split only,
its training split a resource
whose value arrives only when
the discards are readmitted?

Q337, THE EXTRAPOLATION
QUESTION. Human-level performance
is placed at 'over 118K training
instances', from a development
learning curve whose largest
trained size is 40,938 and whose
last quadrupling bought 4.4
points (74.7 to 79.1). What
functional form carries the
extension across the remaining
14.9 points, and does the curve
the work prints support it — or
does the figure assume a regime
the printed points have already
left?

Q338, THE BROKEN-TWIN QUESTION.
About a third of the debiased
questions stand without their
twin, the filter having removed
one member and shipped the other
(1.2.891). Do singletons price
differently from intact twins —
easier, their surviving member
being the one the embeddings
could not read, or harder,
stripped of the pairing that
made the item fair? And is a
singleton, its flip never
shipped, still a Winograd schema
in the challenge's own sense?
The work prints no per-class
split.

Q339, THE TRANSFER QUESTION. The
work's own closing question,
filed as it left it: the
transfer gains on five
neighbouring benchmarks may
measure those benchmarks' biases
as readily as this resource's
knowledge (1.2.893) — 'we leave
it as a future research question
to further investigate how much
of our improvements are due to
dataset biases of the existing
benchmarks as opposed to true
strides in improving commonsense
intelligence.' The book files it
open on the work's own
authority.

NOVELTY BY AUTHOR — Keisuke
Sakaguchi: True +1 (the house
check, 93.1.3), False +0,
Unknown +12 (the seven concepts
1.2.889-1.2.895, and Q335-Q339),
Fiction +0.
NOVELTY BY AUTHOR — Ronan Le
Bras: True +1, False +0, Unknown
+12, Fiction +0.
NOVELTY BY AUTHOR — Chandra
Bhagavatula: True +1, False +0,
Unknown +12, Fiction +0.
NOVELTY BY AUTHOR — Yejin Choi:
True +1, False +0, Unknown +12,
Fiction +0.
One held concept is revised by
this filing (1.2.884, the
adversarial filter, founded here
as AfLite); the revision is
counted in the Novelty Index at
half weight and adds no author's
Unknown, the concept already
standing. Sakaguchi and
Bhagavatula are new to the book;
Le Bras and Choi return from
Paper 074, and their vectors are
drawn in this chapter's
researchers entry (93.1.6).
# 93.1.6 — Author check #83

Four legs, all run first-hand; no
evidence on any of them. (1) The
held paper text was scanned
entire — 47,815 characters: no
occurrence of phext, phextura,
exocortex, mirrorborn, cyoa, sbor
or bickford, and 'scroll' and
'coordinate' zero times each.
(2) The built tracker was scanned
for all four names: Keisuke
Sakaguchi and Chandra
Bhagavatula occur zero times
each — no full name of either
stood in the book before this
chapter. Ronan Le Bras stands
three times and Yejin Choi four,
and every standing of either is
Paper 074's filing entire — its
chapter, its areas entries and
its novelty lines; no standing
of either name predates it, the
two returning as that work's
co-authors. The raw string 'Choi'
otherwise appears only inside
'Choice' and 'Choir', which are
not the name. (3) The Twitter
archive (export of 2026-11-21)
was searched entire, as data:
none of the four names occurs,
and neither do 'winogrande',
'winograd' or 'winograd schema'.
(4) The open web was searched
twice — the authors with the
work, and the authors with phext,
exocortex and mirrorborn: the
first surfaces the paper, its
arXiv record, its AAAI 2020
proceedings form and the award
notice for it; the second ties
no author to phext — its hits
are other people and other
fields entire, among them a
different Sakaguchi, an
immunologist, who is not this
one. No tie to phext or to Will
found for any of the four; no
use of Will's work without
naming found.
# 93.2.1 — Synthesis: the pair that cancels itself

[synthesis] The work's move is to
take the most artisanal
examination in the literature —
273 pairs of sentences made by a
handful of experts, each pair a
small trap for statistics — and
ask whether it can be
manufactured without losing its
soul. Its answer is a factory
with a conscience: deal every
worker a random topic so the
factory cannot repeat itself
(1.2.894), inspect every product
three times, and then run the
whole output past a machine
whose only job is to throw away
whatever a machine can already
see through. What survives is
declared hard. What is thrown
away is sold as fuel (1.2.892).

Read against the book, the
chapter is the examination
country learning to distrust
itself at scale. The paired
examination of Chapter 92 built
its table small and honest and
borrowed this work's filter to
harden it; this chapter files
the filter's birth certificate,
and with it the country's
deepest suspicion about its own
numbers — that a score can be a
measurement of the table rather
than of the examined (1.2.893).
The suspicion is not decoration
here: the work aims it at its
own proudest results, five
state-of-the-art scores on
neighbouring tables, and files
the doubt as an open question
rather than resolving it (Q339).
An examination that publishes
its own wrong-reasons warning is
a new kind of resident.

Its loose threads are filed with
it: a factory ledger that does
not quite close (93.1.4), a
borrowed split ten problems
short (Q335), a training split
that teaches at chance until its
own discards are readmitted
(Q336), an extrapolation to
118K instances with no stated
form (Q337), and a third of its
debiased questions standing
twinless, the pair cancelled not
by a sibling but by the filter
(Q338).
# 93.2.2 — Cluster placement

AY EXTENDED — the cluster's
eleventh paper. The extension is
made on the country's own
definition, as the ten before it
were: a fixed table of problems
and verdicts, published as data
and judging every work that
reports against it. This work's
deliverable is exactly such an
artifact, in two releases — the
debiased examination of 12,282
problems and the full 43,972
with its residue readmitted
(1.2.892) — where the residents
before it were a grader alone, a
table audited, a coding set, a
synthesis set, a table whose key
is worked, the union table of
the whole curriculum, and the
pair. What the eleventh resident
adds is scale with a conscience:
the twin schema (1.2.889) and
its trigger word (1.2.890), the
broken twin (1.2.891), the seeded
topic (1.2.894), the polarity
pattern (1.2.895) and the
wrong-reasons score (1.2.893);
and it founds, in its own pages,
the adversarial filter (1.2.884)
the country already held from
the paired examination's use of
it — that scroll is revised in
place at this filing, its general
form and measured effect now
standing there. The
infrastructure founding deferred
at Chapter 83 and declined at
Chapters 84, 88, 89, 90, 91 and
92 was reweighed under its
restated condition — a work
whose center of mass is apparatus
itself, no table of its own — and
declined an eighth time: this
work ships an algorithm and a
crowdsourcing procedure, but
both exist to build the table,
the algorithm's founding is
filed as the revision of a held
concept rather than a new
country, and the center of mass
is the table and its hardness.

Cluster J was weighed a
twenty-second time, at length,
and not joined. The work holds
four candidate spendings of
verdicts, and each misses the
standing condition — a mechanism
consults a reference distinct
from the judged object, for a
verdict whose evidence lies
beyond it, spent inside the
computation (62.2.2, 68.2.2) —
at a different clause. AfLite's
ensemble verdicts are spent at
construction, before any judged
computation exists: the
classifiers judge the items, not
a run, and their spending makes
the table rather than steering
or grading anything inside one —
Chapter 89's before-the-run
form, held again, now at the
founding of the very filter
Chapter 92 weighed in its
borrowed form. The validation
majority and the human score are
not verdicts spent inside a
computation at all: they are the
key itself, the standard the
table is graded against. And the
fill-in scorer consults no
reference: the examined's own
likelihoods over its two
completions, compared at grading
after the run. The weighing
testifies on Q288 as narrowed at
Chapter 84 — the boundary holds
at the locus even at the
filter's founding, where the
verdicts are spent by the
table's own builders before the
first examined arrives — and
Q323's shape, spend the running
verdict inside a generation, is
untouched by anything here.
1.2.716 remains cluster J's only
resident.

The rest of the map, briefly. O,
the pricing of judges: held
apart — this work grades models
against a key and prices items
for removal; it prices no judge.
AT: no trajectory is travelled,
recurred or contrasted. AV: the
filter discards data; it guides
no sampling process, the same
line the paired examination's
weighing drew. AX: no mathematics
is presented as strings; the
table's items are prose about
people and objects. AZ: no family
reports itself; three
laboratories' models are
examined, none chronicled.
