convolutions paper — and Grave
et al.'s cache paper collides
with rank 35 only on the words
'language models'. The walk's
connection to this work remains
inbound, from Chapter 56.
# Chapter 95 — Paper 077: HellaSwag: Can a Machine Really Finish Your Sentence? (Zellers et al.)

THE DELTA. Six concepts
(1.2.902-1.2.907), one held
concept revised (1.2.884), no
foundation laid, no word coined:
CLUSTER AY EXTENDED — its
twelfth paper, and the
examination rebuilt against its
solver. One year after the
predecessor examination — SWAG,
by an overlapping team — fell to
BERT at 86.7 percent against a
human 88, its makers return with
the controls run first: the
ending-only model keeps all but
11.9 points of the full score,
and the shuffled-context model
loses less than ten, so the
predecessor was in large part an
examination of its answers
(1.2.905). The rebuild generates
its wrong answers with a
finetuned language model
(1.2.907) and keeps only the ones
a discriminator — BERT-Large
itself, the solver — cannot tell
from the real endings, at the
length where generated text is
ridiculous to humans and
plausible to models (1.2.902).
Whole categories are withheld
from training to make the
zero-shot part of every split
(1.2.904); human validation keeps
the questions people answer
reliably; and the finished table
holds every model below fifty
percent against a human 95.6,
the filter's own victim
strongest among them — the
hardness transfers from the one
model the set was hardened
against to all the rest
(1.2.906). The work is the
country's co-evolving benchmark
(1.2.903) in its first full
instance, and it founds, in its
own appendices, the parent form
of the adversarial filter
(1.2.884) the country learned
from the filter's lightest
descendant at Chapter 93 — that
scroll is revised in place at
this filing. Cluster J weighed a
twenty-fourth time and not
joined (95.2.2). Four authors
return from earlier papers; one,
Farhadi, is new to the book. The
filing carries the count to 907
canonical concepts; no
restructuring falls due — Pass
#42 set the next trigger at
Paper 090 or 920 concepts.
# 95.1.2 — State machines

M1, the adversarial filtering
machine in its parent form: the
work's Section 4 and Appendix A,
the founding of the adversarial
filter (1.2.884, revised there).
SCRAPE the contexts (ActivityNet
captions; 80,000 WikiHow
articles, at most three sentences
on each side); FINE-TUNE the
generator on each source (GPT:
two epochs on WikiHow, five on
ActivityNet) and SAMPLE candidate
endings at two sentences — random
sampling, nucleus p=0.98 on
WikiHow; ASSIGN each context its
generated negatives (k=9 for
ActivityNet, k=5 for WikiHow);
SPLIT the set 80/20; RE-INITIALIZE
the discriminator (BERT-Large)
from its pretrained checkpoint
and FINE-TUNE it three epochs on
the training portion, its
hyperparameters randomized each
iteration (Appendix D); SCORE the
held-out portion; REPLACE the
endings the discriminator finds
easy with fresh generations;
ITERATE until the discriminator's
held-out accuracy converges.
NAMED GAPS: the convergence
criterion is nowhere stated — no
threshold, no patience, no cap —
so the machine's halting is a
judgment its operators made and
its readers cannot re-run (Q345);
and the discriminator is one
model family throughout, so the
bias removed is its portrait —
the per-iteration re-initialization
and randomized hyperparameters
re-roll the hand, but within the
one family, the standing gap of
1.2.884 narrowed and not closed.
M2, the human validation machine:
the work's Section 4.3 and
Appendix E. SHOW each surviving
question with six endings — the
real one and five generated;
COLLECT the workers' judgments;
COMBINE them under a Naive-Bayes
worker model, each worker's
reliability estimated from how
often the endings they rate best
are the real ones; KEEP the real
ending with the three
least-preferred wrong ones;
REPEAT for a second round on
ActivityNet, where human accuracy
rises from 60 to 94 percent, and
KEEP the best 25,000 ActivityNet
and 45,000 WikiHow questions.
NAMED GAPS: the machine selects
for questions humans answer
reliably, which is also a
selection on the questions' kind,
and the work does not price the
selection's effect on the table's
coverage; and the per-worker
accuracies behind the model are
not printed, so the 94 cannot be
decomposed from this desk.
M3, the zero-shot split machine:
the work's Section 4.4. DIVIDE
each source's category inventory
into an in-domain half and a
zero-shot half; ASSIGN the
finished questions to validation
and test at 10,000 each — 5,000
in-domain and 5,000 zero-shot,
dividing by source as 3.2 and
6.8 thousand (validation) and
3.5 and 6.5 (test). NAMED GAP:
the categories are the sources'
own — an activity labeler's
taxonomy and a how-to site's —
so a withheld category is only as
unseen as that taxonomy is
honest, and the machine inherits
both taxonomies' grain entire
(1.2.904).
M4, the four-way scoring machine:
the work's Section 5. ENCODE each
context with each of its four
endings in a separate pass;
SCORE one logit per ending;
RETURN the highest; the human
reference is the majority vote of
five independent workers solving
the same problems. NAMED GAPS:
the four candidates are priced in
separate passes and never held
against each other except at the
final comparison — the same named
gap as the paired examination's
scorer (92.1.2), Q334's kin; and
the appendix's account of the
human side gives the workers five
endings against the models' four
while calling the tasks the same
(Q344).
# 95.1.3 — The house check

Forty-four declarations ran
under the harness and all
forty-four pass; twenty-six
further checks were worked by
hand (verify_tables.py), and all
twenty-six pass — one of them
the designed negative the check
was built to catch, the check
working as designed against the
work's own pages, carried at
95.1.4: the appendix that gives
the human evaluators five endings
while calling their task the
models' own four-way one.

The counting closes where the
work prints exact figures. The
splits: 5,000 in-domain and 5,000
zero-shot make each 10,000; by
source, 3.2 and 6.8 thousand make
the validation ten, 3.5 and 6.5
the test ten. The kept sets sum
to the stated whole: 25,000
ActivityNet and 45,000 WikiHow
make the printed 70,000. The
investigation's headline
difference closes exactly: 86.7
less 74.8 is the printed 11.9 —
the ending-only control's slip,
to the tenth.

Table 1's overall column is the
mean of its in-domain and
zero-shot parts at the rounding
grain. Exact: BERT-Base
validation (42.9 and 36.1 average
to the printed 39.5), ESIM+ELMo
validation (33.6), the human
validation row (95.7). Within
half a grain everywhere else
checked, and the source-weighted
form closes beside it: BERT-Large
test weights to 47.345 against
the printed 47.3, BERT-Base test
to exactly 40.5, the human
validation row to exactly 95.7.
One cell stands a full grain
outside, carried at 95.1.4. The
caption's two gap claims close
against the table it captions:
the in-domain gap is 45.9 points
on test and 45.4 on validation —
the printed "over 45"; the
zero-shot gap is 50.7 and 52.5 —
the printed "50".

The ablation's printed 4.3 is
the test fold's difference (40.5
less 36.2, the frozen-encoder row
against BERT-Base); the
validation fold's difference is
3.6, unprinted. The zero-shot
drop, printed as "roughly 5%", is
4.7 points on the test fold. The
transfer section's printed twelve
closes by derivation: 46.7 less
34.6 is 12.1. The compute
estimate's own arithmetic closes:
a billion GPU-hours at 8,760
hours to a year is 114,155
GPU-years, over the printed
hundred thousand. The length
ratio closes: 41 tokens against
13 is 3.15, the printed "three
times as many opportunities".

What the harness could not
recompute — the figure-carried
numbers (the 34.6 transfer score
itself, the 69 and 28 percent
cross-scores, the 63 percent
filter ablation, the filtering
curves) and every score requiring
a training run — stands
attributed in the chapter, not
here; no model was run from this
desk.
# 95.1.4 — Caveats

Appendix F describes the human
evaluation as "the exact same
task as is given to the models"
and, in the same breath, gives
the workers five endings to
choose among; the models' task,
stated at Section 5, is four-way.
One of the appendix's two
sentences misdescribes the
evaluation it documents (Q344).

One cell of Table 1 stands
outside its own column's
arithmetic: the LSTM+ELMo
validation overall prints 31.7
while its in-domain and zero-shot
parts (33.2 and 30.4) average
31.8 — a tenth of a point below
both the split mean and the
source-weighted mean, where every
other checked row closes within
half a grain (95.1.3).

Figure 5's caption and Section
4.2's text disagree in grain
about the one-sentence filter:
the caption has the lengths
converging "to random, ~40%, and
~50%", the text gives the
one-sentence case as 35% when it
converges, "slightly higher than
random". The two-sentence choice
is unaffected; the convergence
criterion itself is nowhere
stated (Q345).

Section 5's sentence that GPT
"outperforms BERT on WikiHow"
holds on the printed table only
against BERT-Base (37.7 test);
against BERT-Large, the work's
headline model, GPT trails on
WikiHow on both folds (39.8 and
40.5 against 42.9 and 45.0), as
it does on ActivityNet — where
the same sentence says the
reverse is true.

The human headline (95.6%)
rests on 200 examples per source
per validator count (Appendix
F), combined by majority vote;
the per-worker accuracy behind
the vote is not printed.

The billion-GPU-hours estimate
is an extrapolation of a
log-linear fit, not a
measurement; its printed form is
a straight line drawn through
the era's models' scores and
extended to the human line
(Q346).
# 95.1.5 — Open questions, novelty

Q344, THE FIVE ENDINGS. Appendix F
gives the human evaluators five
endings, and must pick the best one,
while calling the task "the exact
same task as is given to the models"
— whose task, stated at section 5,
is four-way. Which form did the
human evaluation actually take, and
what does the answer do to the
95.6% headline: measured against
five endings it is the stronger
result, against four the comparable
one, and the work's own appendix
asserts both at once.

Q345, THE CONVERGENCE GRAIN. The
filter runs until the discriminator's
accuracy converges, but no criterion
is stated — no threshold, no
patience, no cap — and the two
statements of the one-sentence limit
disagree in grain: the caption's
"to random" against the text's 35%,
"slightly higher than random". What
separates a converged filter from a
stopped one, and would the surviving
dataset change under the other
reading of the limit?

Q346, THE BILLION HOURS. The closing
estimate draws a log-linear line
through the printed models' scores
and extends it to the human line,
pricing the crossing at a billion
GPU-hours. The fit's residuals are
not printed and the line's slope is
carried by the few largest models.
Is the billion a property of the
task, or of the five points the line
was drawn through — and what does
the estimate become when the next
generation of models moves the
points?

Q347, THE TRANSFER OF HARDNESS. A
set filtered against one
discriminator defeats the rest, and
a set filtered against the weaker
sibling still holds the stronger to
63%. What carries the hardness
across architectures — the surviving
questions' length, their topics, or
the specific deceptions the
generator learned against the first
filter — and is the transfer
symmetric, hardness against a weak
filter bounding hardness against a
strong one?

NOVELTY BY AUTHOR — Rowan Zellers:
True +1 (the house check, 95.1.3),
False +0, Unknown +10 (the six
concepts 1.2.902-1.2.907, and
Q344-Q347), Fiction +0.
NOVELTY BY AUTHOR — Ari Holtzman:
True +1, False +0, Unknown +10,
Fiction +0.
NOVELTY BY AUTHOR — Yonatan Bisk:
True +1, False +0, Unknown +10,
Fiction +0.
NOVELTY BY AUTHOR — Ali Farhadi:
True +1, False +0, Unknown +10,
Fiction +0.
NOVELTY BY AUTHOR — Yejin Choi:
True +1, False +0, Unknown +10,
Fiction +0.
One held concept is revised by
this filing (1.2.884, the
adversarial filter, gaining its
parent algorithm's founding form);
the revision is counted in the
Novelty Index at half weight and
adds no author's Unknown, the
concept already standing. Farhadi
is new to the book; Zellers and
Bisk return from Paper 074,
Holtzman from Paper 067, and Choi
from Papers 074 and 075, and their
vectors are drawn in this
chapter's researchers entry
(95.1.6).
