# Chapter 96 — Paper 078: Think you have Solved Question Answering? Try ARC, the AI2 Reasoning Challenge (Clark et al.)

Peter Clark, Isaac Cowhey, Oren
Etzioni, Tushar Khot, Ashish
Sabharwal, Carissa Schoenick and
Oyvind Tafjord (Allen Institute
for Artificial Intelligence),
arXiv:1803.05457v1 — the only
version, submitted 2018-03-14.
A question set, a text corpus,
and baselines, assembled to move
question answering past retrieval:
7,787 natural, grade-school
science questions authored for
human standardized tests,
partitioned into a Challenge Set
(2,590) and an Easy Set (5,197)
by two solvers' joint failure;
a companion corpus of 14M science
sentences; and a battery of
baselines, none of which scores
significantly above random on
the Challenge Set.

THE DELTA. The examination
country gains its thirteenth
paper and its earliest-dated
member, and with it the country's
founding gesture, which the later
filings all presuppose: before a
set can be filtered, audited, or
rebuilt against its solver, it
must be gathered and partitioned.
ARC's partition is by failure —
two simple solvers run once over
harvested human items, and the
questions both miss become the
challenge. The adversarial
machinery filed later in this
country (1.2.884) is this gesture
iterated and learned; here it
stands in its first form, one
pass, no iteration, both halves
published. The work also brings
the third authorship mode — the
harvested examination (1.2.909),
items written for people and
gathered, beside the hand-written
and crowd-written sets already
held — and the first examination
that ships its own library, the
companion corpus (1.2.911),
whose measured failure to help
is itself a finding: coverage
without retrievability (1.2.912).
The tie-credit rubric (1.2.910)
is the small mechanism that makes
a set defined by failure still
scorable by the solvers who
defined it. Chapter 56, which
cites this work, spends the
Challenge Set as a measuring
instrument; this chapter is the
instrument's construction record.

Six concepts are coined at
1.2.908-1.2.913; no held concept
is revised. Five state machines
are stated in the work. Four
questions are filed (Q348-Q351).
State machines stated in the
work (five):

The failure-partition machine:
run the retrieval solver and the
co-occurrence solver over every
question; a question enters the
Challenge Set if and only if
both answer it incorrectly, and
the Easy Set otherwise; the
train/dev/test partitions are
then drawn within each set.

The corpus-mining machine:
instantiate roughly 100
hand-written query templates
over 80 science topics (the
worked example: 360 terms under
2 templates make 720 queries),
run them through a search
engine, collect the top
documents, de-duplicate, strip
markup, lists and tables, chunk
into sentences, and augment
with the AristoMini corpus —
14M sentences out.

The entailment answering
machine: convert the question
and one option into a
fill-in-the-blank assertion;
retrieve the top-8 premises for
it (a premise must contain the
answer choice; premises over
300 characters, or containing
negation or noise, are
filtered); score each premise
for entailment of the
assertion; the option's score
is the maximum over its
premises; answer with the
argmax option. (The DGEM
variant builds a graph of
OpenIE tuples in place of raw
premises.)

The span-overlap answering
machine: retrieve sentences
with the question (and option)
as query, concatenate them
into one paragraph, run the
reading model to select a
span, and answer with the
option whose lemmatized
non-stopword tokens overlap
the span most.

The tie-credit scoring machine:
one point for naming the key;
1/k points for a k-way tie
that includes it; zero
otherwise — applied uniformly
over the test sets to produce
the work's Table 6.
The house check, run against
the held text before
integration: 37 of 37 harness
declarations pass, with the
hand checks beside them.

