Caveats, standing on the
record beside the filing:

The Easy Set's "generally
between 55% and 65%" excludes
two of the eight non-defining
systems below the band — the
table solver at 36.15 and the
span model at 50.11 — and the
defining pair above it, at
74.48 and 77.82. The sentence
describes the middle of the
table, not its range.

The introduction's "scoring up
to 61%" on the Easy Set stands
against Table 6's retrieval
row at 62.55; which baselines
the sentence governs is Q350.

One described baseline has no
printed score: the OpenIE
variant of the entailment
model is given a construction
and a section, and no row in
Table 6.

The Challenge Set's difficulty
is indexed to its two defining
solvers, both of their era: the
partition is a fact about 2018
retrieval and co-occurrence,
and the set's hardness for any
later solver is an empirical
question the work does not
pretend to settle. Its grade
profile, though, matches the
Easy Set's nearly cell for
cell — whatever the partition
measures, it is not the human
difficulty axis.

The knowledge-type and
reasoning-type classifications
rest on a sample of 100
questions and judgments the
work itself calls subjective;
the atlas is an impression,
offered as one.

The harvest's breadth is
narrower than its source list:
one supplier contributes
65.85% of all questions, and
about 43% of the set is the
publicly releasable part of a
single earlier competition's
question pool.
Open questions filed by this
chapter:

Q348, THE TWO OR THREE MODELS.
Section 1's contribution list
announces "two baseline neural
models" and in the same breath
lists "Three neural baseline
models, DecompAttn, BiDAF, and
DGEM"; the abstract also says
three, and the methods sections
describe three. Which count is
the error — and does any
printed number depend on the
roster, or is the contradiction
confined to the prose?

Q349, THE DUPLICATED WORD.
Footnote 3's list of question
words absent from the corpus is
headed "Only 11 question words"
and prints eleven tokens, one
of them — Quickgrow — twice:
ten distinct words. The 99.8%
vocabulary claim survives
either reading. What is the
exact numerator — and is the
eleventh token a word the
typesetting lost, or a word
the list duplicated?

Q350, THE SIXTY-ONE PERCENT.
The introduction reports the
baselines "scoring up to 61%"
on the Easy Set, while Table 6
prints the retrieval solver
over the companion corpus at
62.55. Under the reading that
excludes the retrieval solvers,
the maximum is 60.81, which
rounds to the printed figure.
Which baselines does the
sentence govern?

Q351, THE ATLAS'S SIZES. The
knowledge-type and
reasoning-type distributions
are drawn from a hand
classification of 100 sampled
questions, under judgments the
work itself calls subjective,
and their category sizes stand
only in the figures, never
printed numerically. The
examination's atlas cannot be
recomputed from the printed
record: what were the counts?

