# Chapter 99 — Paper 081: Pasteur's Quadrant: Basic Science and Technological Innovation (Stokes)

Donald E. Stokes (professor of
politics and public affairs in the
Woodrow Wilson School, Princeton),
'Pasteur's Quadrant: Basic Science
and Technological Innovation',
Brookings Institution Press,
Washington, D.C., released August 1,
1997 (ISBN 0815781784 hardcover,
0815781776 paperback). The author
died on January 26, 1997, with the
book in progress; it appeared after
his death. Queue rank 49 — a
mention-node (m:stokes||1997) mined
from the citing chapter's held
documentation, resolved by the ranks
48-50 sweep to this work, its title
taken from the work's own record at
this filing.

HELD SCOPE, STATED PLAINLY: this is
a book, and its body text is not
publicly readable. The Archive's
scanned copy is loan-restricted; no
posted text of the book or any
chapter was found; the snippet route
refused the pass. The filing rests
on what could be held entire: the
publisher's own description, the
book's contents (five chapters, then
Notes and Index), its catalogue
record, three verbatim fragments of
its text quoted with page anchors in
citing scholarship (pp. 2, 24, 103),
the quadrant table as rendered in
the reference literature, and the
citing record's operative statement
of the framework. Claims beyond that
basis are attributed in this chapter,
never verified; the house check
(99.1.3) runs on the held text and
on nothing else.

THE DELTA: cluster AS gains its
third paper — and its first source
from outside the book's own century.
Chapter 55 founded the cluster on
SciNet, whose task dimensions carry
the Pasteur clause in a single line
of 1.2.518 — "Pasteur's quadrant
(Stokes 1997)" — borrowed, as its
documentation says, "After Stokes
(1997)". This filing holds the
source: the quadrant model itself
(1.2.927), the category it exists to
name (1.2.928), the paradigm it
opposes (1.2.929), and the settlement
it proposes (1.2.930). No held
concept is revised: 1.2.518 states
no construction of Stokes's — one
clause naming a dimension — and the
ground on which Paper 080 revised
1.2.519 is absent (weighed at
99.2.2). No foundation is laid: the
model's two axes are stated inside
the quadrant concept itself, and
nothing in the held text presents a
level-zero definition apart from the
model. Three machines are stated
(99.1.2). Four questions open
(Q362-Q365, 99.1.5).
# 99.1.2 — The formal core: three stated machines

THE QUADRANT CLASSIFIER: the work's
one mechanism, stated entire in its
table. Input: a body of research.
Two questions are put to it — is it
guided by a quest for fundamental
understanding? is it guided by
considerations of use? The two
answers sort the research into the
four cells of Pasteur's quadrant
(1.2.927): Bohr's (understanding
without use), Edison's (use without
understanding), Pasteur's (both),
and the unnamed remainder (neither).
The machine replaces a sort the
older paradigm ran on one question —
where on the line from basic to
applied does this stand? — and its
output is a placement, not a score:
the book grades neither axis, and
the graded scales the citing record
runs (1.2.518: each axis 1 to 5, the
quadrant cut at 4) are the citing
record's addition, stated as such
where they stand.

THE EXEMPLAR MACHINE: each named
cell is anchored by one paradigmatic
career, and the classification is
taught and defended through the
anchor. Bohr: the model of the atom,
use not a consideration. Edison:
"the applied investigator wholly
uninterested in the deeper scientific
implication of his discoveries" —
the book's page 24 gives the measure:
"Edison gave five years to creating
his utility empire, but no time at
all to the basic physical phenomena
underlying his emerging technology".
Pasteur: the fundamental yet
use-inspired studies — fermentation,
spoilage, disease — by which he laid
the foundations of microbiology.
The machine's moving part is the
choice of exemplars: a cell exists,
for the reader, because a career
fills it.

THE COMPACT MACHINE: the argument's
output mechanism. Input: the
interactive view — technology
increasingly science-based, science
increasingly technology-based, the
choice of problems inspired by
societal needs (the publisher's
statement). Operation: recognize
use-inspired basic research
(1.2.928) as a standing category of
the settlement, rather than an
anomaly the linear model (1.2.929)
misprices. Output: a renewed compact
between science and government
(1.2.930), framed to fund and defend
all three named cells as what they
are. The book gives the machine a
chapter of its own (its fourth) and
its ground in democratic theory (its
fifth); the terms it outputs stand
in the unheld body, and are not
restated here.
# 99.1.3 — House check: what the held text closes

Run against the held text (7,723
bytes: the publisher's description,
the book's contents, its catalogue
record, three verbatim fragments,
the citing record's operative
statement, and the quadrant table as
rendered): 33 harness declarations
and 18 hand checks, all passing.

What closes entire. The identity:
author, title, publisher and release
date (August 1, 1997) agree across
the publisher's page, the Archive's
catalogue, and an independent
bibliography entry (Ramaley 2007:
"Washington, DC: Brookings
Institution Press, 1997"). The
structure: the contents list five
chapters and two closing parts —
Stating the Problem; The Rise of the
Modern Paradigm; Transforming the
Paradigm; Renewing the Compact
between Science and Government;
Basic Science and American Democracy;
then Notes and Index — and the
census of the held text reads those
five titles back as its section map,
the only numbered structure the held
record has. The bibliography's place
closes arithmetically: references at
pages 153-173 is a span of 21 pages.
The fragments stand in page order
(p. 2, p. 24, p. 103) and each is
verbatim in its citing source. The
citing record's thresholds are
internally consistent: Bohr
(understanding 4 or 5, use below 4),
Edison (the reverse), Pasteur (both
4 or 5), neither (both below 4) —
four cells, no overlaps, no gaps on
the 1-to-5 scales. The queue stands
preserved at 624 records, with rank
48 marked deferred and rank 50
closed as a non-work before this
filing.

Two designed items. FIRST, THE
BOOK'S LENGTH: the records of the
work do not agree on its extent. The
reference literature cites it at
page 196; retail records give 200
pages; Google Books' records for the
same ISBN give 180 pages. The
disagreement stands in the held
record entire and is filed, not
resolved (Q363). SECOND, THE FOURTH
CELL: the work's table, as rendered,
names three classes of research and
leaves the neither-goal cell blank;
the citing record's operationalization
completes the model with a fourth
label — "neither" — and graded axes
the book does not state. The book's
most-used form is thus partly its
borrowers' work (Q362).

Attributed, not verified: everything
the publisher's description asserts
about the argument's interior; the
fragments' contexts beyond their
quoting sentences; the book's dynamic
claims (an interactive view is the
publisher's summary phrase); and the
whole of the book's evidence — its
body, where any of this could be
checked, is the one thing not held.
# 99.1.4 — Caveats

1. THE HELD TEXT IS PARTIAL, AND
THE FILING SAYS SO WHEREVER IT
MATTERS. The publisher's description,
the contents, the catalogue record,
three fragments, and the citing
record's use: that is the whole
basis. The book's own development —
its history of the paradigm, its
reading of Pasteur, its compact's
terms — stands unheld, and this
chapter files the framework and its
place in the book, not the book's
interior argument. A reader who
needs the interior needs the book.

2. THE AUTHOR IS DEAD BEFORE THE
BOOK. Stokes died on January 26,
1997, aged 69, at work on the book;
it was released on August 1, 1997.
Who carried the manuscript through
the press is not in the held record
(Q365). The text the world cites is
a posthumous text, and its finishing
hand is unrecorded here.

3. THE MODEL AS USED IS NOT QUITE
THE MODEL AS PRINTED. The field's
working form — four named cells,
graded axes, quadrant thresholds —
was completed by its borrowers;
SciNet's version (1.2.518) cuts each
axis at 4 on a 1-to-5 scale and
names the fourth cell "neither".
Nothing in the held text shows the
book grading an axis or naming the
fourth cell. Attributing the
completed form to Stokes alone
overstates the held record.

4. THE EXEMPLARS ARE THE BOOK'S
CHOICE. Bohr, Edison and Pasteur
anchor the cells by the book's
reading of their careers; the
Edison of page 24 — five years to
the utility empire, none to the
phenomena — is a characterization in
service of the model, and historians
of any of the three could contest
the portrait without touching the
model's logic. The filing holds the
portraits as the book's, not as
settled biography.

5. ONE COUNTRY, ONE SETTLEMENT.
The compact the book renews is the
American postwar settlement, and
its fifth chapter's ground is
American democracy. The quadrant
travels — the citing record runs it
on the world's research tasks — but
the policy argument is addressed to
one country's arrangements, and the
filing does not generalize it.
# 99.1.5 — Open questions, and novelty by author

Q362 — THE UNNAMED CELL: the work's
table, as rendered in the reference
literature, names three classes of
research and leaves the fourth cell —
neither a quest for understanding nor
considerations of use — blank. The
citing record (1.2.518) supplies
"neither" and runs the cell as a
standing outcome. Does the book's own
text name the cell, dismiss it, or
leave it empty on purpose — and what,
on the book's account, lives there?

Q363 — THE EXTENT: the records of the
book disagree on its length — cited
at page 196, retailed at 200 pages,
catalogued by Google Books at 180
pages for the same ISBN — while its
bibliography is placed at pages
153-173. What does the physical book
actually hold? A copy in hand decides;
the records, unreconciled, cannot.

Q364 — THE DYNAMIC FORM: the
publisher's statement gives the book
an "interactive view of science and
technology", and its page 103 speaks
of support flowing between the cells.
The static table is what the field
borrowed. Does the book state a
dynamic form of the model — research
moving between quadrants as it
matures, support circulating — that
the citing record's one-shot
classification dropped?

Q365 — THE POSTHUMOUS TEXT: the
author died in January 1997 with the
book in progress (his university's
obituary has him "at work on" it);
the book appeared in August. Who
carried it through the press, and
does the published text bear an
editor's or a collaborator's hand
that the record nowhere names?

NOVELTY BY AUTHOR (standing basis —
the filing's own increments only):
Donald E. Stokes — True +1,
Unknown +8.
