# 100.2.1 — Synthesis: the spent theorem, filed at its source [synthesis]

[synthesis] Chapter 54 proved the
comparison by spending a theorem it
did not hold: the finite-level
globalization that identifies a
weight-by-weight globalization with
the smooth Casselman-Jacquet
functor stood in its proof as a
citation, [CWYZ26], one line in a
borrowed bibliography. This filing
reverses the direction of the debt.
The tracker now holds the spent
theorem at its source, with the
whole theory that proves it — and
the citing chapter's chain, read
backwards, turns out to have been
standing on the deepest part of
this work, the place where algebra
and analysis are identified level
by finite level.

[synthesis] The work's own shape
rhymes with its subject. It is a
paper about closure — about what
must be added to a subspace before
quotients behave — written into a
registry whose whole discipline is
the same operation performed on
claims: nothing enters unclosed,
nothing is quotiented away
silently, and every completion is
labelled with what it cost. Its
central discovery is that the
obvious completion and the obvious
intersection meet exactly — the
submodule is stable, the quotient
is surjective, and the two functors
are one machine seen from its two
ends. The Bernstein-Zelevinsky
filtration it delivers is the
payoff the p-adic theory promised
the real one forty-nine years ago;
the walk files it in the hundredth
chapter, in the country its
borrower founded.
# 100.2.2 — Cluster placement: AR extended; J weighed a twenty-ninth time

CLUSTER AR IS EXTENDED — its
second paper. The kinship is
weighed, not assumed, and it is
not a kinship of vocabulary: the
citing relation runs from the
cluster's founding chapter into
this work's body. Chapter 54
founded AR on Paper 036, whose
proof chain consumes this work's
finite-level globalization theorem
(cited there as [CWYZ26]) at the
chain's centre; the objects are
AR's own held ground — Casselman-
Wallach representations (1.2.496),
Harish-Chandra modules (1.2.499),
globalization (1.2.500), the
Casselman-Jacquet functor itself
(1.2.507) — and this work is the
theory of that functor, built by
four authors one of whom (Jun Yu)
co-authored the founding paper.
The cluster's country acquires its
foundation layer: Chapter 54 filed
what the functor does inside a
comparison; this chapter files
what the functor is.

NO HELD CONCEPT IS REVISED. The
candidate was 1.2.507, which files
the Casselman-Jacquet functor as
Paper 036 used it — the algebraic
completion and its smooth form at
the minimal parabolic, with q_pi
stated as the canonical surjection.
Weighed against Paper 080's
revision of 1.2.519 and held:
there, the held scroll stated a
construction under the index's
name that was the port's, not the
founder's. Here the scroll states
the construction truly, for its
paper's setting; this work
generalizes the parabolic, adds
the submodule functor the older
filing never needed, and CONFIRMS
the stated surjection (1.2.934)
rather than correcting it. The
general theory files beside the
held concept as 1.2.931-1.2.938,
linked, not merged — under the
loss test, folding this work's
eight concepts into 1.2.507 would
bury both papers' claims inside
one scroll that could no longer
state either cleanly.

CLUSTER J IS WEIGHED A TWENTY-NINTH
TIME AND NOT JOINED. The work
contains no mechanism that consults
a reference distinct from the
judged object, for a verdict spent
inside the computation: its
functors transform the object
itself, and its theorems are
proved, not consulted. The nearest
approach is the inverse limit of
1.2.931 — a completion assembled
from the object's own quotients,
each a quotient of the object, not
a reference outside it; and the
imprimitivity theorem it cites (du
Cloux, inside 1.2.937's proof) is a
tool taken off the shelf, not a
verdict spent inside a judged run.
Q288 as narrowed is not met; the
shape Q323 names — a verdict spent
on finished candidates — is absent
entire: nothing here is a candidate
and nothing is selected. 1.2.716
remains the cluster's only
resident.

THE INFRASTRUCTURE ALTERNATIVE IS
WEIGHED UNDER CHAPTER 84'S RESTATED
CONDITION AND DECLINED, THE
THIRTEENTH TIME. The work is
infrastructure in the plain sense —
a functor theory built to carry a
research program (Q370) — but the
condition as restated asks for an
examination's apparatus, the
standard instrument for judging
models; a Casselman-Jacquet
functor judges nothing, examines
nothing, and no examination in the
book runs on it. The filing stands
in AR. Neighbours held apart: AX
(first strings) — this work's
filtrations filter a representation,
not a string, and its mathematics
is analysis, not execution; the
p-adic Bernstein-Zelevinsky works
in its own bibliography [BZ76,
BZ77] are its cited ancestors, not
a cluster.
