# 101.1.2 — The formal core: four stated machines

THE SETTING AND THE THEOREMS. G is a
real linear reductive group, P = L_P U_P
and Q = LN parabolics, X = G/P the real
flag variety, E_F the homogeneous
tempered bundle of a Casselman-Wallach
representation F of L_P. The work proves
three theorems of increasing reach:
Theorem C, the orbitwise supported
extension (1.2.939's engine); Theorem B,
the minimal-parabolic case for
principal-series coefficients, all
parameters; and Theorem A, the general
statement — surjectivity of restriction
to a Q-stable Nash open, with
contravariant exactness in the
coefficients. The filing's concepts
carry the proof's joints: the normal
presentation (1.2.940), the fibre's
polynomial densities (1.2.941), the
comparison factor (1.2.942), the
continuation (1.2.943), the good
parameters (1.2.944), the pole removal
(1.2.945), and the coefficient lifting
(1.2.946).

STATE MACHINES (charter rule 13) — four,
all stated by the work, drawn here:
M1 — the orbit induction machine of
Sections 7-8. States: a Q-orbit O_w;
EXTENDED ON ONE ORBIT (Theorem C, at
good parameters by the regularized
pairing, at bad ones by M3); LIFTED
through the finite orbit filtration to
the Q-stable open, then to X. Moves:
each step preserves supports, so the
induction terminates at the whole flag
variety. The gap named: the machine runs
only because the orbit set is finite and
each extension is supported — an
infinite stratification would stall it
at the first limit.
M2 — the normal-order recursion of
Section 3. States: a supported
distribution of normal order at most r;
SPLIT (by the normal presentation,
1.2.940) into a normal derivative of an
order-zero distribution plus a remainder
of lower order; RECURSED on the
remainder. The gap named: the sections
s_r are chosen, not canonical — the
machine terminates, but its path depends
on choices the theorem does not price
(Q371).
M3 — the parameter-line machine of
Sections 6-8. States: a density at a
parameter lambda; DEFORMED along the
line lambda_0 + tau nu_0, nu_0 the one
transverse direction (1.2.944);
REGULARIZED by the Mellin pairing at
good parameters; POLE-REMOVED at bad
ones by descent along the orbit
filtration (1.2.945). The gap named: at
a bad parameter the machine proves the
extension exists and gives no formula
for it — the value there is an
induction's output, not a pairing's.
M4 — the coefficient-lifting machine of
Section 9. States: a supported
distribution with coefficients in F;
REGULARIZED ALONG THE FIBRE (Proposition
9.3: a base coefficient tensored with a
polynomial density, 1.2.941); LIFTED
along a coefficient embedding with
support and normal order preserved
(Proposition 9.1, 1.2.946). The gap
named: the machine's last road runs on
the strict exactness of CJ^infty
consumed from the work's reference [10]
— the theorem Paper 082 filed — and
outside the Casselman-Wallach country
that road does not exist, the same
boundary Paper 082's own machines named.
Tracker total after this filing: 315
state machines (278 stated, 37
inferred).
