# 101.1.5 — Open questions, and novelty by author

Q371 — THE EXTENSION'S CANONICITY: at
good parameters the extension is a
regularized pairing; at bad parameters
it is a deformation's value, reached by
induction; and the normal presentation's
sections are chosen, not canonical
(101.1.2, M2). Do the constructions
agree where more than one applies — is
the extension of Theorem A independent
of the resolution, the frames, and the
chosen sections? The work proves
existence everywhere and prices
uniqueness nowhere; an extension
operator, or a counterexample pair of
extensions differing by a distribution
supported on the boundary, decides.

Q372 — THE BAD SET'S SHARPNESS: Theorem
6.3 bounds the bad parameters inside a
locally finite union of hyperplanes —
the CANDIDATE poles of the continuation
(1.2.943). Is every hyperplane of H_w an
actual pole for some density, or is the
true bad set strictly smaller? The work
needs only the containment; the
sharpness question is the difference
between the bad set as a bound and the
bad set as a fact.

Q373 — BEYOND NASH: every analytic step
runs in the Nash category — tempered
partitions of unity, Nash frames, the
resolution's Nash data. Is Nash load-
bearing, or does the extension theorem
hold for semianalytic, or merely real-
analytic, Q-stable opens? The work's
methods do not say; a single analytic-
but-not-Nash open where extension fails
would prove the category is the
theorem's real hypothesis.

Q374 — THE TRIANGLE'S THIRD SIDE: Paper
036 spends this work for a direct proof
of its (3.4); this work spends Paper 036
for a cohomological proof of its Theorem
B, beside its own analytic one; and both
stand on Paper 082's exactness. Do the
analytic extension here and the
cohomological proof there produce the
same maps — and read together, do the
three filings close into one argument
about distributions on flag varieties,
or do the works merely cite each other
at the joints? The walk has now filed
all three corners; the question is the
record's to answer as the cluster grows.

Q375 — THE DERIVED SHADOW: Theorem A(ii)
is exactness at the level of modules —
the functor F |-> S'(W, E_F|_W)^[n]
takes short exact sequences to short
exact sequences. Does it have derived
functors with content — a homological
shadow computing something in the family
of the Schwartz homologies the work
cites (its reference [9], queue rank 54)
— or is order-zero exactness the whole
story? The Jacquet-functor theory of
Paper 082 met its own version of this
question at the Artin-Rees boundary
(Q368); the two questions may be one.

NOVELTY BY AUTHOR (standing basis — the
filing's own increments only): Junyan
Xiao — True +1, Unknown +13.
