# 103.1.4 — Caveats

The corrected version. The version
of record is not the version the
citing chapter's bibliography names:
Chapter 54 cites the journal form of
2021, and v2 (2026) corrects it.
Two of the corrections touch named
theorems — Theorem 1.15 (the
finite-rank automatic extension
theorem) and Proposition 7.11 (the
finite-dimensional vanishing
criterion beneath it, filed here as
1.2.960 and 1.2.961) — and three
theorems are 'slightly improved'
(1.12, 1.13, 8.5). What the published
versions of the two corrected
results asserted, the held text does
not say: the v2 comment names the
mistakes' locations, not their
content, and the journal text is not
held. Whether any citer of 2021–2026
spent the faulty forms stands open
as Q381. The citation the walk
follows is untouched by all of it:
Chapter 54 spends only Propositions
8.2–8.3 — Borel's lemma — which
appear in neither the corrected nor
the improved list, and which stand
in v2 at the numbers the citation
gives (103.1.3). A second matter the
record holds without settling: the
correction's source. The
acknowledgements thank Jun Yu for
pointing the mistakes out — Jun Yu
is the citing chapter's co-author
and the center of the cluster this
filing extends (103.2.2); the
record states it as the work's own
sentence, and infers nothing from
it.

The held form. The text is held as
arXiv HTML: prose and statements
entire, but displays are extracted
linearly and some of the work's
argument lives in them — the
filtration of 1.2.959, for instance,
is read from the prose around its
displayed quotient, not from the
display itself. One raw citation
artifact stands in Proposition
7.11's proof (103.1.3): a pointer
the HTML form failed to render,
filed as an artifact of the form.
The PDF is not held; nothing filed
rests on a display the extraction
could have mangled, and the
paragraph rule re-verified every
anchored claim against the built
tracker after integration.

The setting's breadth. The theory
runs for almost linear Nash groups —
wider than the reductive ground of
the cluster's four papers, and the
work itself warns that for a general
Nash group the homology is 'totally
different' from smooth homology
(1.2.954). The concepts are filed at
the work's own generality; the
cluster's consumption of the theory
(Chapters 54, 100–102) is at
reductive groups, where the work's
own identifications hold. Nothing in
the filing narrows the theory to the
cluster's ground, and nothing
extends the cluster's theorems
beyond it.

The Hausdorff seam. The work is
plain that its homology may be
non-Hausdorff — non-closed subspaces
in the coinvariants of relatively
projective terms — and that its only
general Hausdorff certificate is
finite-dimensionality. The concepts
are filed with that seam open
(Q380); a reader who needs separated
homology spaces from this theory has
the question the work itself leaves,
not an answer the filing invents.
# 103.1.5 — Open questions from this paper

Q380 — HAUSDORFFNESS IN GENERAL. The
work's only general certificate that
its Schwartz homology is Hausdorff is
finite-dimensionality (Proposition
1.9); outside that case the spaces
may carry non-closed subspaces and
the theory proceeds anyway. Is there
a wider natural class of
representations — or a weaker
hypothesis on the group — under
which H^S_i(G; V) is always
Hausdorff, or is non-Hausdorffness
generic outside finite dimension?
The work does not say. Decisive
evidence: a theorem closing the
question either way, or a worked
infinite-dimensional example with
non-Hausdorff homology exhibited
explicitly. Filed as the standing
gap of the resolution machine
(103.1.2, M2).

Q381 — THE CORRECTION'S BLAST
RADIUS. The version of record
corrects Theorem 1.15 and
Proposition 7.11 against the
published version of 2021, without
stating what the published forms
asserted. What did the faulty forms
claim, and does any work of
2021–2026 cite or spend them — in
particular, does any member of this
cluster's citing chain rely on the
published Proposition 7.11 or
Theorem 1.15 anywhere? The held
record settles only the walk's own
exposure: Chapter 54 spends
Propositions 8.2–8.3, untouched.
Decisive evidence: the journal text
of the two results compared against
v2, and a citation census of the
published version across the window.

Q382 — THE FINITE-ORBIT HYPOTHESIS.
The automatic extension theorems
(1.2.960) require the closed
complement Z to carry finitely many
G-orbits; the proof peels orbits one
at a time. Is the hypothesis
essential — does extension by zero
fail to induce the stated maps when
Z has infinitely many orbits — or is
it an artifact of the peeling proof,
removable by a limit argument that
keeps the vanishing degrees?
Decisive evidence: a counterexample
with infinitely many orbits, or a
strengthened theorem without the
hypothesis. Filed as the standing
gap of the Borel filtration machine
(103.1.2, M5).

Q383 — BEYOND ALMOST LINEAR. The
work's identifications — Schwartz
homology equals smooth homology
equals H_i(g, K; V) — are proved for
almost linear Nash groups, and the
work itself warns the general Nash
case is 'totally different'. Which
step of the construction is the
almost-linearity load-bearing for:
the coinvariant identity (1.2.955),
the de Rham resolution (M4), or the
moderate-growth pricing of Smod_G —
and does some weaker structure on G
(a linear envelope, a Nash
compactification with controlled
growth) recover part of the package?
Kin to Q373, which asks the same
shape of question of Paper 083's
finiteness. Decisive evidence: a
counterexample at a named step, or
an extension theorem under a weaker
hypothesis.

Novelty increments for this filing
(charter rule 9): Yangyang Chen —
True +1, Unknown +12 (eight
concepts, four questions); Binyong
Sun — True +1, Unknown +12 (the
same, carried whole per co-author).
# 103.1.6 — Author check #93: Yangyang Chen and Binyong Sun (charter rule 11)

NO EVIDENCE. The check ran in its
four standing legs. Leg one, the
held text entire: it holds none of
the house's terms — no phext,
phextura, exocortex, mirrorborn,
cyoa, sbor, bickford, scroll, or
coordinate — a work of pure
representation theory, published
years before the house's names
existed in print; the leg's value
here is that it ran, and its result
is recorded. Leg two, the tracker:
the names 'Yangyang Chen' and
'Binyong Sun' stand nowhere in the
built tracker before this filing —
zero occurrences each; both authors
are new to the book, and this filing
is their first entry into it. The
work itself, however, already stood
in the book three ways before it was
filed: as a frontier record at
100.3.1 and 101.3.1 under the
initials 'Chen-Sun', and as the
unnamed theory inside Q375
(101.1.5), which asks whether Paper
083's functor has derived functors
'in the family of the Schwartz
homologies' — this filing is the
theory that question names, held at
its source. The bare surname 'Sun'
stands 31 times in the tracker
before this filing, every one of
them another person. Leg three,
Will's own Twitter archive entire —
21 files, 168,563,526 characters:
no Chen, no Sun in any of the
checked forms, no Schwartz homology,
no Nash group, no Jiangnan, no
Zhejiang — nothing. Leg four, the
web: an exact-title search returned
the work's arXiv record and its
scholarly mirrors, and nothing
connecting either author to the
house's names; the namesakes the
searches returned are other people
in other fields. No leg returns
evidence that either author has
encountered phext, the exocortex, or
this house in any form. The verdict
stands as the check's standard form:
NO EVIDENCE — recorded as the state
of the record, not as a fact about
the authors.
# 103.2.1 — Synthesis: the cluster's homological substrate, held at its source, filed [synthesis]

Five filings in, the representation-
theory cluster has a shape the walk
did not plan: each new paper has
been the previous paper's missing
premise. Paper 036 compared
homologies across the divide and
cited its foundation (Paper 082) and
its coefficient manuscript (Paper
083); Paper 084 was the comparison's
prior case; and this filing is the
theory all four presuppose — the
derived coinvariants of the analytic
category, with its own foundations
laid in the open: the identity that
starts it (1.2.955), the resolutions
that define it (1.2.956), the lemma
that computes it (1.2.957), and the
extension theorems that spend it
(1.2.960). The walk came here by
citation, not by topic: Chapter 54's
text spends exactly two propositions
of this work — Borel's lemma — and
those two propositions turned out to
be the hinge of the work's longest
proof, the filtration under its
automatic extensions. A citation
chain is a reading order the field
wrote itself; the cluster is what
that order looks like when it is
followed to the bottom.

Three testimonies the filing
records. First, Q375's: Paper 083
asked whether its functor has
derived functors 'in the family of
the Schwartz homologies the work
cites' — this filing holds the cited
theory at its source, and the
question's family is now a filed
construction (1.2.954). The evidence
is filed; Q375 stays open — it asks
about the functor's derived theory,
not about Schwartz homology's
existence, and nothing here settles
it (the P060 pattern). Second, the
correction's: the cluster's central
figure appears inside this work's
acknowledgements as the reader who
caught its two mistakes — the
network the walk is tracing is not
only a citation graph but a working
community that audits itself, and
the version of record is its audit
made public. Third, the shape's:
extension across a stable open,
priced at the boundary, has now been
filed three times in the cluster —
for tempered distributions (Paper
083), for Schwartz sections (this
filing), and implicitly under
Chapter 54's filtration facts, which
cite this work for exactly the
exhaustivity that makes the shape
run. The cluster's deepest shared
construction is not any single
theorem but that shape, and this
filing is where the shape is proved
in its greatest generality.

The filing also crosses the book's
restructure line: 961 canonical
concepts, against Pass #44's 960.
The walk files and stops here, as it
did at each prior crossing; the
restructure is its own pass.
# 103.2.2 — Cluster placement: AR extended; J weighed a thirty-second time, not joined; no revision

Cluster AR — the representation
theory of real reductive groups,
founded at Chapter 54 (Paper 036,
Xiao and Yu) — is EXTENDED, its
fifth paper. The placement is
weighed on two grounds, both from
the work's own facts. Consumption:
the founding paper spends this work
in its body, for the Borel lemma
beneath its orbit filtration's
normal-symbol sequence — the
cluster's first paper already runs
on this work's machinery, and the
citing chain (036 cites it; 082 and
083 cite 036 and each other; 084 is
036's cited prior case) makes this
filing the figure's fifth side, the
substrate beneath the closed
triangle. Ground: the work's
category Smod_G, its moderate
growth, its (g, K)-homology
identification and its Whittaker
example are the founding paper's own
ground — real groups, Frechet
representations, homology against
the compact subgroup — at wider
generality (almost linear Nash
groups rather than reductive ones).
The width is recorded, not smoothed
(103.1.4): the cluster gains a
member whose theory strictly
contains the ground its other four
papers share.

Cluster J is weighed a thirty-second
time and not joined. The theory's
functors transform their judged
object — homology is a derived
construction on the representation
itself, and every identification the
work proves (Shapiro's lemma,
Theorem 7.7) equates two computations
of the same object, spending no
verdict on it. The automatic
extension criterion comes nearest in
shape: a stabilizer's homology is
checked and the verdict spent on
whether the open part's homology
extends — but the checked homology
is the object's own local data at
the boundary, computed by the theory
itself, not a reference consulted
from beyond the judged computation;
the locus is inside, the reference
clause fails, as at Chapter 84's
narrowing and against Q323's named
shape. The infrastructure-founding
alternative is declined a sixteenth
time under Chapter 84's restated
condition: the work founds a theory
the cluster consumes, stated and
consumed as mathematics, not as
apparatus.

No revision in place. Three held
concepts were weighed entire:
1.2.496 (the Casselman–Wallach
representation class) states Paper
036's class truly — this work's
Smod_G is a different category,
priced by Nash functions rather than
by embedding in a Casselman–Wallach
representation, and the work never
claims the two coincide; 1.2.498
(Lie algebra homology) states the
Chevalley–Eilenberg object of Paper
036's nilradical truly — this work's
Theorem 7.7 identifies ITS homology
with H_i(g, K; V), a theorem about
its own construction, filed inside
1.2.954 rather than restated over
Paper 036's scroll; 1.2.505 (the
smooth Bruhat filtration) states
Paper 036's filtration truly — the
Borel-lemma facts beneath it are
cited BY Paper 036's text TO this
work, which is a citation, not a
restatement, and 1.2.959 files the
cited propositions at their source
without touching the citing scroll.
The Paper 080 ground — a held scroll
stating another work's construction
as its own paper's — is absent in
all three.
