# 106.1.2 — The formal core: five stated machines

THE SETTING AND THE THEOREMS. A
representation is a continuous action
on a complete locally convex space
(Definition 1.1), and the work's
question is the unitarity problem
(1.2.986): which admissible
representations carry an invariant
Hermitian form, and when is it
positive definite. The flexibility of
the definition forces the theory of
globalization: one Harish-Chandra
module, four canonical completions
(1.2.985), measured by norms canonical
up to polynomial factors (1.2.987) and
read off K-type expansions as growth
conditions on sequences (1.2.988).
Real parabolic induction is realized
in the compact picture (1.2.989),
where globalization becomes regularity
of functions on K. The complex
geometry enters with the measurable
flag varieties (1.2.990): over X = G/L
the Dolbeault complex, quotiented to
Hausdorffness (1.2.991), carries
cohomological parabolic induction —
maximal by Wong's theorem (1.2.992),
minimal by Bratten's, through compact
supports and Serre duality (1.2.994,
1.2.995) — and the dominance
conditions govern vanishing and the
transfer of unitarity from the Levi
factor (1.2.993). Section 9 closes the
arc: forms are maps, maps are kernels,
kernels are cohomology classes on Y x
Y^op, and a unitary representation is
built by completing one (1.2.996).
Five stated state machines are drawn
out.

M1, THE GLOBALIZATION CHAIN (Sections
1 and 4). State: an admissible
Harish-Chandra module X^K.
Transitions: complete by growth
against the canonical norm family —
exponentially decreasing to X^omega,
rapidly decreasing to X^infty, slowly
increasing to X^-infty, subexponential
to X^-omega (1.2.988). Output: the
chain of Theorem 1.5, each term
depending only on the infinitesimal
class. Named gap: the chain's
canonicity is entirely Theorem 4.17's
— the norms are chosen from a Hilbert
globalization and agree only up to
polynomial factors in |mu|; the
machine has no norm-free input, and
the SL(2,R) example of Section 3,
where the growth is read off Taylor
coefficients directly, is the only
place the choice is invisible.

M2, COMPACT-PICTURE INDUCTION (Section
5). State: an admissible
representation of a Levi factor L, the
Levi chosen by the Cartan involution
as L = P cap theta P. Transition:
induce and realize on functions on K;
the Hilbert globalization is the L^2
sections (Proposition 5.11), and the
four globalizations are the four
regularity classes of (5.15). Output:
an induced representation whose
analytic content is function
regularity on a compact group. Named
gap: the machine's Levi is theta's
choice — a different Cartan involution
conjugates the picture, and the work's
structure package (Proposition 5.3) is
what makes the choice harmless; the
machine does not run on a parabolic
whose Levi is picked by hand.

M3, COHOMOLOGICAL INDUCTION, MAXIMAL
(Section 7). State: an admissible
(q,L)-module V in its maximal
globalization, over a very nice q.
Transition: form the Dolbeault complex
over X = G/L, take cohomology through
the maximal Hausdorff quotient
(1.2.991). Output: admissible
representations, each the maximal
globalization of its Harish-Chandra
module (Theorem 7.21). Named gap: the
machine is a derived functor because
the underived one fails — plain
sections vanish or collapse in the
ranges that matter (Propositions 7.2
and 7.4) — and its closed range is
Wong's theorem, cited, its proof
elsewhere; the work states the machine
and spends it.

M4, THE MINIMAL DUAL MACHINE (Section
8). State: an admissible (q,L)-module
V in its minimal globalization.
Transition: form the compactly
supported Dolbeault complex and take
H_c, Serre duality (Theorem 8.13)
exchanging it with M3's output.
Output: admissible representations,
each the minimal globalization of its
module (Corollary 8.14). Named gap:
the duality runs on the functional
analysis of Section 8 — reflexivity
and nuclearity (Proposition 8.7), the
dual-of-cohomology identification
(Proposition 8.12), the Banach
closed-range theorem — and where those
hypotheses fail the machine's output
is a cohomology space, not yet a
globalization.

M5, THE HERMITIAN-FORM MACHINE
(Section 9). State: a Harish-Chandra
module X^K, or a (q,L)-module E over Y
= G/L. Transition: read a form as a
map to the Hermitian dual (Theorem
9.16), a map as a kernel (Theorems 9.3
and 9.8), a kernel as a cohomology
class on the product with the opposite
manifold (Corollary 9.18). Output: the
invariant forms on cohomological
induction as the G^Delta-invariant
real classes in H^{(n,n),(p,p)}(Y x
Y^op, F^-omega), and at Corollary 9.20
a unitary representation, completed
from H_c^{0,r}. Named gap: the machine
identifies the distinguished class but
does not write it down — the class of
Corollary 9.20 has no explicit
representative in the work, which is
its Question 10.1 (Q394); the
distinguished line of forms on V^omega
is only L^Delta-invariant, so it
defines no subbundle off the diagonal.
