# 106.1.4 — Caveats

Four caveats ride on this filing.
First, the version: the book holds the
author's own final version of 2
January 2008 and files it as the
version of record because it is the
text of the published chapter — the
citing records cite the LNM form, the
page count and contents match the
chapter entire, and the author's page
describes this version's revisions and
its resetting by the volume's editor —
but no comparison against Springer's
printed pages was run; if the printed
chapter differs from the author's
version anywhere, the difference is
not in the book's hands. The fetch
itself is on the record: two HTTPS
attempts from this machine returned
empty replies and two from the ranch
node failed on certificates before the
plain-HTTP fetch succeeded once; the
held file's identity is verified by
its contents, not by its channel.
Second, the work is lectures, and says
so by its shape: it omits proofs by
design ('by virtue of omitting proofs'
is its own phrase at Definition 9.5),
cites its load-bearing theorems to the
literature — Wong's closed range to
[Won99], the transfer theorem to the
author's 1984 paper, Casselman-Wallach
to [Cas89] and its companions — and
files its last section as questions
and conjectures. The concepts of
Chapter 106 are filed as the work
states them; where the work spends a
cited theorem, the concept text says
whose theorem it is. Third, the work's
own Section 10 slip about the minimal
globalization's letter (106.1.3) sits
inside its report of Bratten's results
on Conjecture 10.3 — the one place
where the slip could mislead a reader
about which globalization a proved
result covers; the filing records the
slip and files the question (Q398)
rather than silently choosing a
reading. Fourth, testimony on Q180:
Chapter 54's question asks whether the
comparison holds in the full
generality of Vogan's Conjecture 10.3,
and this filing now holds that
conjecture entire at its source (Q395)
— its four parts, its author's own
remarks on their difficulty, and
Bratten's partial results as reported.
The conjecture stands open in the work
itself, so the filing testifies to its
exact shape and does not close Q180:
what would close it is a proof or a
counterexample, and neither is in this
text.
# 106.1.5 — Open questions left by Paper 088

Q394. The work's Question 10.1: in the
setting of Corollary 9.20, give a
simple geometric description of the
distinguished G^Delta-invariant
Dolbeault cohomology class in
H^{(n,n),(s,s)}(Y x Y^op, F^omega)
that Corollary 9.18 identifies with
the positive definite form on
H_c^{0,r} — perhaps an explicit
representative (2n,2s)-form. The
difficulty is named in the work: the
distinguished line inside F^omega, the
invariant form on V^omega, is only
L^Delta-invariant, so it defines no
one-dimensional subbundle off the
diagonal of Y x Y^op. The work relates
the question to Frobenius reciprocity
for Dolbeault cohomology, as the
Shapovalov form's construction rests
on the universal mapping property of
Verma modules. Decisive evidence: an
explicit representative, or a
reciprocity theorem from which one is
computed. Filed as the standing gap of
the form machine (106.1.2, M5).

Q395. The work's Conjecture 10.3 — the
conjecture Chapter 54's body names at
[Vog08] and Q180 stands open on, here
held entire at its source. In four
parts: (1) the u-cohomology complexes
Hom(wedge^p u, X^g) have the closed
range property for every canonical
globalization g; (2) the cohomology of
a canonical globalization is the
canonical globalization of the
cohomology, H^p(u, X^g) = [H^p(u,
X^K)]^g; (3) and (4) two
first-quadrant spectral sequences,
with E2 terms built from Ext over L
and over G, abutting in common — the
precise form of the Frobenius
reciprocity Question 10.2 asks for.
The work reports: generalizations of
(1) and (2) for the minimal
globalization are Bratten's in
[Bra98], for arbitrary parabolic
subalgebras with real theta-stable
Levi factors; (1) for the maximal
globalization follows by duality; and
the generalization of (2) cannot
extend to real parabolics with
distribution or maximal globalizations
— the work's own counterexample
argument, through Harish-Chandra's
subquotient theorem, stands in the
text. The author calls the specific
(1) 'fairly easy to prove' and has
'not thought carefully' about the
rest. Decisive evidence: proofs of
(1)-(4) as stated, or a failure of
closed range in some globalization.
Filed against 1.2.985 and 1.2.989;
testimony on Q180 stands at 106.1.4 —
the filing holds the conjecture's
shape and does not close the citing
chapter's question.

Q396. The work's Conjecture 10.4: for
admissible X^K and Y^K, the standard
complex Hom_K(wedge^p (g/k),
L(X^omega, Y^-omega)) has the closed
range property, and its cohomology is
Ext_{g,K}(X^K, Y^K) — likewise with
omega replaced by infinity. The work's
own caveat stands in the text: results
at least very close may be found in
Chapter 9 of [BW80], unchecked by the
author against this statement.
Decisive evidence: the deduction from
Borel-Wallach written out, or a proof
or counterexample of the closed range
property. Filed against 1.2.996: the
Ext groups the conjecture names are
the home of the spectral sequences of
Conjecture 10.3(3)-(4).

Q397. After Corollary 9.14 the work
observes that on Y1 x Y2 the diagonal
group G^Delta has finitely many
orbits, that the Frobenius reciprocity
isomorphisms of the parabolic case
(Corollary 9.12) are replaced by
spectral sequences, and that 'one
should be able to find some reasonable
and interesting statements. We leave
this task to the reader.' What are the
statements? No conjecture is
formulated; the task is the reader's
entire. Decisive evidence: a stated
and proved reciprocity for
intertwining operators between
Dolbeault cohomology representations,
of the kind the parabolic case has at
Corollary 9.12. Filed against 1.2.992
and 1.2.996.

Q398. Section 10 reports Bratten's
generalizations of Conjecture
10.3(1)-(2) 'in the case of the
minimal globalization (the case g =
infinity)' — but the work's own
Section 4 gives the minimal
globalization the letter omega
(Definition 4.21) and infinity the
smooth globalization (Definition
4.19), and Conjecture 10.3 itself
quantifies 'for g = omega, g =
infinity, and so on'. Which
globalization did Bratten's [Bra98]
prove (1) and (2) for — the minimal,
as the sentence names it, or the
smooth, as its letter says? The two
readings cover different theorems: the
sentence's next clause derives (1) for
the maximal globalization by a duality
argument, and duality pairs minimal
with maximal, which supports the name
against the letter. Decisive evidence:
[Bra98] itself, on this filing's
frontier at would-be rank 682. Filed
unresolved from the held text; no
filed claim rests on the reading.

Novelty increments for this filing
(charter rule 9): David A. Vogan Jr. —
True +1, Unknown +17 (twelve concepts,
five questions).
