# 107.1.2 — The formal core: filtrations, vanishing, and the Whittaker functor

Six stated machines.

M1 THE STRATIFICATION MACHINE
(Sections 2-3). Input: a smooth
manifold with a finite subanalytic
stratification, a coefficient space
E. The machine first builds the
relative Schwartz space S(U, E) —
sections flat on the closed complement
— and its dual, the tempered
distributions (1.2.997); then the
spaces T(Y, E) of tempered
distributions supported on a stratum,
filtered by transversal degree
(1.2.998); then the filtration of
C^infty(E) by flatness along the
Z_p, the unions of strata of smaller
dimension. Output: the graded object,
computed entire by Theorems 3.1 and
3.4 — the sum over strata (orbits) of
the duals of the T(Y, E), each with
its transversal filtration (1.2.999).
The machine is exact: every short
exact sequence it produces is the
input of the next chapter's spectral
sequence.

M2 THE BRUHAT MACHINE (Section 4).
The stratification machine specialized
to the flag variety X = P\G under N:
the strata are the Bruhat cells, the
filtration is flatness along the
unions of smaller cells, and the
output is the Bruhat filtration of
the smooth principal series with its
graded pieces J(w, sigma) (1.2.1000),
each cell's piece computed as a
tensor product over the cell by
Lemmas 4.2-4.4 (1.2.1001).

M3 THE TWISTED-REGULAR VANISHING
ENGINE (Section 5). Input: a
unipotent group, a character eta, the
twisted regular representation on a
Schwartz quotient space. The engine
runs the Fourier transform in the
abelian case and inducts downward on
dim N through central one-dimensional
subalgebras, the Hochschild-Serre
spectral sequence carrying each step
(Proposition 5.4). Output: the
homology and cohomology tables of
Theorems 5.6 and 5.12 — finite,
bounded by dim U, and zero unless
eta is central-trivial and purely
imaginary in real part (1.2.1002,
1.2.1003) — and, on the open cell,
the single surviving functional,
the Jacquet integral (1.2.1003).

M4 THE COHOMOLOGICAL EXTENSION
MACHINE (Sections 1 and 6). Input:
the graded pieces of M2 and the
tables of M3. The machine assembles
the filtration's spectral sequence:
every piece off the open cell dies by
M3, because the character restricts
nontrivially to the cell's conjugate
nilpotent algebra; the sequence
collapses to the open cell. Output:
the restriction isomorphism of Lemma
6.3 and the dimension theorems 6.1
and 6.2 (1.2.1004, 1.2.1005) — the
introduction's SL(2, R) argument,
where the extension across the point
at infinity is checked by hand
against the residues at eta = 0,
grown to all real groups.

M5 THE HOLOMORPHIC FAMILY MACHINE
(Section 7 and Appendix B). Input:
the principal series with its
inducing parameter lambda free in
a^*. The machine forms the sheaf of
Whittaker vectors over a^*, proves
cohomology commutes with germs
(Lemma 7.4, on Appendix B's theory of
holomorphic vector-valued functions),
and outputs Theorem 7.1 — the sheaf
is free of rank dim sigma — and
Theorem 7.2, the Jacquet integral's
holomorphic continuation to all of
a^* (1.2.1006).

M6 THE WHITTAKER FUNCTOR MACHINE
(Sections 8-9). Input: any object of
M^infty(G), the smooth representations
of moderate growth and finite length.
The machine embeds the object in a
principal series (Lemma 8.4's
criterion), where M4 has already
computed the functor, and outputs
Theorem 8.2 — Psi_eta exact,
finite-dimensional, nonzero exactly
at Re eta = 0 — and, for linear
quasisplit groups, Theorems 9.1 and
9.2: multiplicity one (1.2.1007).
