# 107.1.4 — Caveats

The version. The book holds the
published version itself, from the
first author's own page — the
proceedings text as the volume
prints it, its copyright page and
classification standing in the
extraction. No second copy was
fetched to compare against: the
identification closes instead on the
citing records, which cite this
chapter at these pages in this
volume, and on the text's own
stamps. A printing variant elsewhere
would not be seen from here; none is
suspected — the citing chain is
three chapters deep and every spend
lands at its number in the held text.

The extraction. The PDF's mathematics
extracts imperfectly: integrals print
as a stray letter, the orthogonal
sign and the infinity sign ride as
separate glyphs, and French titles in
the bibliography carry their accents
as combining marks. The chapter's
claims are therefore filed from the
theorems' statements as the
extraction gives them, with the
formulas' shapes checked against
their uses in the citing chapters;
a glyph-level reading of any single
display is the held PDF's business,
not this filing's.

The mathematics. As at every
pure-mathematics filing, the proofs
stand attributed to the work; the
house check (107.1.3) verifies the
record — identity, version, structure,
cross-reference, bibliography, the
citation chain — and not the proofs.
The theorems' consequences the book
already spends (the Bruhat filtration
in Papers 036, 083, 084 and 086) were
verified at their spends, in their
own chapters.

The unpublished neighbours. Two of
the work's thirty references are
unpublished objects — Harish-Chandra's
handwritten notes on the Whittaker
integral [17] and the first author's
1982 letter to Harish-Chandra [8] —
and one was 'to appear' at printing
[5]. The filing records them as the
bibliography prints them; Q400
carries what the work leaves open
about the unpublished pair, Q401
about the third.
# 107.1.5 — Open questions left by Paper 089

Q399. The work's own promise, in its
introduction: 'The theory of the
Bruhat filtration, in particular,
deserves a fuller treatment. It might
serve, in fact, as the cornerstone of
a reasonably elegant exposition of
the subject from its beginnings. We
hope to return to these ideas in a
subsequent paper.' Did the return
appear — an exposition of the theory
of Whittaker vectors from its
beginnings, built on this filtration?
The citing cluster spends the
filtration's consequences in four
chapters and nowhere names a sequel.
Decisive evidence: a subsequent
paper by any of the three authors
taking up the Bruhat filtration as
its cornerstone, or the authors'
bibliographies showing none. Filed
as the work's standing promise,
against 1.2.1000.

Q400. The bibliography leans twice on
objects no reader can consult:
reference [17], 'Harish-Chandra, On
the theory of the Whittaker integral.
Handwritten notes. Unpublished' — no
date, no repository named — and
reference [8], 'W. Casselman, Letter
to Harish-Chandra, November 1982'.
The work's subject is the theory
Harish-Chandra founded; its
dedication is to his memory; and two
of its sources stand outside the
public record entire. What do the
notes contain, and where are they
kept? Decisive evidence: the notes
themselves, or a catalogue record of
them (the Harish-Chandra papers are
held in archives; a shelf record
would close the location half).
Filed against 1.2.1006: the
holomorphic continuation the work
proves is the theory the notes'
title names.

Q401. Reference [5] stands in the
bibliography as 'Mladen Bozicevic, A
property of Lie group orbits. To
appear in Journal of Lie Theory' —
the orbit-subanalyticity the third
section's machinery quietly needs,
cited ahead of its publication. Did
it appear, and in the form cited?
Decisive evidence: the published
paper, or the journal's record
showing none under that title.
Filed against 1.2.999: the
stratification machine's strata are
orbits, and the citation is the
work's own pointer to where their
tameness is proved.

Q402. Every main theorem of the work
assumes eta nondegenerate — a
character of N nontrivial on each
simple root space — or, at the
functor's level, a general character
whose real part vanishes. The
introduction's model case shows what
the excluded side looks like: at
eta = 0 the SL(2, R) functional is
meromorphic in s with poles, its
residues the derivatives of the
delta function at the identity —
a theory of residues, not of
holomorphic families. What is
Psi_eta for degenerate eta on
M^infty(G) — is there an exact
functor, a finite dimension, a
multiplicity statement, when eta
vanishes on some simple root space?
The partial Whittaker models the
question names are outside this
work entire. Decisive evidence: an
extension of Theorem 8.2 to
degenerate characters, or a
counterexample to exactness there.
Filed against 1.2.1007.

