# 109.2.1 — Synthesis: the theorem at its source [synthesis]

[synthesis] Read whole, the held
record is the comparison country's
ground floor. Chapter 54 founded the
country on a theorem it could state
only by pointing here — the
globalization whose 'existence and
uniqueness are guaranteed' by a
theorem in a book the house could
not open — and nine further chapters
built on the pointing: functors,
filtrations, Schwartz spaces, the
substrate itself. The walk has now
arrived at the source, and the source
turns out to be a single theorem with
an unusually honest paper trail: one
equivalence of categories, proved by
building two completions from
opposite ends — the largest the
analysis allows, the smallest the
duality allows — and showing they
meet. The author's own later guide,
written twenty-two years on for
another man's birthday volume, is
the rare source that grades itself:
the argument 'rather convoluted',
misprints 'in unfortunate places', a
cleaner version in a colleague's
hands, and the book itself out of
print while remaining, by its
author's flat statement, 'the unique
reference for the general result'.
That the general result is general
in exactly the direction the field's
other proofs are not — the nonlinear
groups, the covering groups whose
representations the linear theory
cannot see — is the book's quiet
claim to permanence, and it stands
in the record unchallenged by any
citing chapter. The country was
founded spending this theorem twice;
it now holds the theorem, its two
completions, its keystone lemma, and
its author's own account of where
its proof is weak — everything, that
is, except the book's pages, which
remain where the century put them:
in libraries, under a lending lock,
cited by section number by everyone
who builds on them.
