# Chapter 111 — Paper 093: Canonical Extensions of Harish-Chandra Modules to Representations of G (Casselman)

Paper 093 is W. Casselman, 'Canonical
extensions of Harish-Chandra modules
to representations of G', Canadian
Journal of Mathematics, vol. 41
(1989), no. 3, pp. 385-438 (received
March 3, 1988; Zbl 0702.22016; MR
1013462; DOI 10.4153/CJM-1989-019-5)
— the paper that proves the
Casselman-Wallach globalization
theorem: up to canonical topological
isomorphism, exactly one smooth
representation of G of moderate
growth overlies a finitely generated
Harish-Chandra module, the assignment
is functorial and exact, and every
(g, K)-map between such
representations extends uniquely to a
continuous G-map, with closed range.
The work the queue carried as a work
record (rank 63, key w:casselman|1989,
its title's tail carrying the
journal's doubled letter and its
author standing as initials, cited by
Chapter 54), identified on held
records entire: Chapter 54's
bibliography carries it as [Cas89] in
full form, and its body spends it
once, jointly with Wallach's book —
'the Casselman-Wallach globalization,
whose existence and uniqueness are
guaranteed by the Casselman-Wallach
globalization theorem [Cas89] and
[Wal92, Chapter 11]'. Paper 086's
text carries it as [Cas89] and spends
it three times: for the very class
its generalized Jacquet modules are
defined on — 'Let V be a finite-
length moderate growth Frechet
representation of G (see Casselman
[Cas89, p. 391])' — and for the
principal series' natural structure
of that same kind; its bibliography
prints the work's Zbl 0702.22016 and
MR 1013462. Chapters 104 and 106
carry it in their frontiers. THE FULL
TEXT IS HELD: the published paper
entire, 54 pages (pp. 385-438),
fetched once from the publisher's own
service and read entire (held text
183,411 bytes); no partial-text
clause stands in this chapter. The
paper is, in its own words, 'a report
on what has been to a great extent
joint work with Nolan Wallach', and
'essentially a sequel to [38]' —
Wallach's 1983 asymptotic expansions,
standing in the walk's would-be
frontier at 667.
