# 111.1.4 — Caveats

The extraction governs first. The
held text is a text extraction of the
publisher's PDF; its prose is clean
and its mathematical displays are
not — the filing quotes and rests on
prose throughout, and where a
construction's statement lives in a
display (the seminorm bound of
1.2.1034, the conditions (M1)-(M4) of
1.2.1036), the concept is filed from
the paper's surrounding prose, which
states the content in words.

The byline governs second. The paper
is signed by one author and reports
work 'to a great extent joint with
Nolan Wallach': Wallach's [38] holds
the only written proof of its Theorem
7.6, and Section 8's results are 'due
almost entirely to Wallach'. The book
files the paper under Casselman's
name, as Chapter 54 and Paper 086
cite it, and carries the joint
character here and at 1.2.1042; no
concept in this filing is assigned to
Wallach that the paper assigns to the
paper itself.

Priority inside the paper is the
paper's own account, filed as such:
Theorem 3.2 'apparently due
originally' to a 1976 Doklady note
(Q421); Theorem 7.6 conjectured in
correspondence in 1975, proved by the
author in 1978, written by Wallach,
and proved independently by
Kashiwara, unpublished (Q419). The
filing repeats the paper's hedges
where the paper hedges.

The namesake governs last. The same
author published a second paper in
the same journal a volume earlier —
the Schwartz space introduction,
cited here as the 1987 preprint [11],
published at vol. 40 (1989), pp.
285-320 — a different work, kept
apart at 111.3.1; and the queue's
three Casselman name-nodes (ranks
71-73) carry no titles and resolve to
neither work.
# 111.1.5 — Open questions left by Paper 093

Q418. The direct proof (1.2.1042).
The paper states that despite the
'down-to-earth nature of these
assertions, no direct proof in terms
of, say, the growth orders of matrix
coefficients is known, even for
SL2(R)' — its own proof of closed
range and automatic continuity runs
the long way, through the agreement
of four extension functors. Does a
direct proof exist now, in the
literature since 1989?

Q419. Kashiwara's proof (1.2.1040).
Theorem 7.6 in the case n acts
trivially was proved independently by
Kashiwara, by hyperfunction
techniques — 'not, as far as I know,
published', the paper says. Was it
ever published, and does the
hyperfunction proof extend to the
general case the paper's proof
covers?

Q420. The sequel program (111.1.1).
The introduction promises subsequent
papers: Bruhat's thesis refined by
the globalization, the theory of
Whittaker models sharpened,
asymptotics made more explicit, a new
proof of the automatic continuity
theorem, and an improvement of the
Paley-Wiener theorem to non-K-finite
functions. Which of the program
appeared, and in what form?

Q421. Prischepionok's priority
(1.2.1036). Theorem 3.2 — every
Harish-Chandra module has matrix
coefficients — is 'apparently due
originally' to the paper's reference
[33], a 1976 Doklady note in Russian.
The hedge 'apparently' stands in the
paper itself: what does the priority
record actually hold?

Q422. The Oshima compactification's
reference (1.2.1044). Sections 3 and
11 run their asymptotics on a partial
compactification of G 'attributed to
Oshima', for which the author knows
'only the partial reference [32]' —
and [32] is Oshima's Fourier analysis
paper, not the compactification.
Where does the compactification's own
published account stand?
