# 111.2.1 — Synthesis: the theorem its citers spend in a clause [synthesis]

[synthesis] Read whole, the paper is
an agreement proved four ways. The
cluster's filings have run for twelve
papers on a theorem none of them
states at its source: that a
Harish-Chandra module has exactly one
good globalization. Chapter 54 spends
it inside a subordinate clause,
jointly cited; Paper 086 builds its
whole subject on the class the
theorem delimits; the book's concept
of the globalization (1.2.500) was
filed from the citing chapter's use.
At its source the theorem is not one
construction but a coincidence of
constructions: a closure inside an
induced representation, a quotient of
induced representations, and two
extensions read off the module's own
matrix coefficients — built from
unrelated materials, each computable,
none obviously the same — and the
theorem is that they coincide, that
the canonical injection between the
principal-series pair is an
isomorphism, that regularity is
universal. The proof's two halves
divide the field the way the
cluster's papers divide it: the
tempered side falls to asymptotics of
coefficients, the general side to an
induction on the Langlands rank run
through analytic families. And the
paper is candid about its own
strangeness: a report on joint work
under one name, a sequel whose
predecessor holds its keystone's only
written proof, a central theorem
whose natural direct proof its author
says does not exist. The book's
globalization concept now stands with
its founding paper filed beside it,
in the founding paper's own
vocabulary — Harish-Chandra
representations, regularity, Frobenius
extensions — and the cluster's later
vocabulary (Casselman-Wallach,
HF^mod) reads, in this light, as the
names the field chose for the paper's
proposals.
# 111.2.2 — Cluster placement: AR extended; J weighed and not joined; no revision

Cluster AR extends to thirteen
papers. The placement is weighed from
the work's own construction, not its
citations: the paper proves the
globalization theorem that Chapter
54's founding proof runs on, in the
same representation-theoretic ground
— (g, K)-modules, Jacquet functors,
completions at a parabolic's
nilradical — and its constructions
are the ones the cluster's later
papers presuppose (Paper 086's ground
class is this paper's p. 391;
Chapter 109's book is its sequel's
companion). No other cluster's ground
touches it.

Cluster J is weighed a fortieth time
and not joined, against the standing
test (77.2.2, as narrowed at Chapter
84; Q323's shape): the paper's
functors construct and compare
extensions of the judged module
itself — closures and quotients of
the object, measured against the
object's own completions. No
mechanism in the work consults a
reference distinct from the judged
object for a verdict spent inside a
computation; the comparisons are
proved isomorphisms between the
object's own extensions, not verdicts
returned by an outside standard.
1.2.716 remains J's only resident.

The infrastructure-founding
alternative is weighed a twenty-
fourth time under Chapter 84's
restated condition and declined: the
work is a theorem of its field, not
an examination's or an apparatus's
founding — nothing in it is built to
be re-run by strangers as a measure.

No revision: 1.2.500 states the
globalization theorem at the level
the citing chapter spends it, under
both warrants' names; 1.2.1021 states
the book's automatic continuity in
the book's strength; 1.2.1022 the
exposition's moderate-growth form.
Each held scroll states its own
paper's form truly; this paper's
constructions file beside them.
