# 112.1.4 — Caveats

The partial text governs first. This
filing rests on the book's printed
record, its review, its author's
later exposition, and its citing
records — not on the book's body.
Every concept is filed at the level
those witnesses support: the
definition and the norm from the
exposition's and the review's own
statements of them; the Jacquet
module, the classification, and the
fundamental series from the printed
contents' architecture; the spent
propositions from the citing texts'
own quotations of what they spend.
Where a construction's internal form
lives only in the body — Proposition
4.2.3's statement, the duality
properties' proofs, the
classification's proof — the filing
records the spend and the place, and
files the gap as a question (Q423),
not as a claim.

The contents govern second. The
printed contents are held entire,
but their extraction garbles at the
edges: section 1.4 prints 'compact 9
groups', section 4.1 '(g, K>module',
Chapter 5's heading 'Chapters.',
section 6.2 '(f,/iC)-modules', and
section 8.7's title truncates at
'The completeness of the nt'. The
filing reads the contents through
those artifacts and rests no claim on
a garbled cell; section 8.7's full
title stands unverified on the held
record (Q426).

The review governs third. The
two-volume plan is filed from the
review's opening, the only held
witness that states it: the book's
own introduction, where the plan
would stand in the author's words, is
listed in the contents at xiii and is
not held (Q425). The review is a
contemporary's account, filed as
such; the sequel's contents, held at
Paper 091, corroborate the plan's
shape — its Chapter 13 is the
Plancherel theorem the review
announced as Part II's culmination.

The namesake governs last. The queue
carries the two volumes as separate
records (ranks 61 and 64) and one
joint name-node (rank 73,
n:casselman+wallach), which resolves
to neither volume. The author's 1983
asymptotic-expansions paper and his
2014 exposition are different works,
kept apart at 112.3.1; the exposition
is a witness here, not the work.
