# 112.2.1 — Synthesis: the ground floor, filed last [synthesis]

[synthesis] Read whole, the cluster
has been citing this book for
fourteen papers without holding it.
Chapter 54's main proof runs its
load-bearing step on the book's
Proposition 4.2.3; Paper 082 takes
its scale from the book's appendix
and its finiteness vocabulary from
its fourth chapter; Paper 086 takes
the duality its whole seventh section
runs on from the book's pages, cited
as 'well-known properties' — the
citation form a field uses for the
ground it no longer argues about. The
book is the treatise's ground floor:
the definitions, norms, and Jacquet
theory the second volume's theorem
stands on, written four years before
the theorem, in a volume whose own
review already knew the plan — this
one lays the groundwork, the next
culminates in Plancherel. The walk's
order filed the theorem first (Paper
091), its article second (Paper 093),
and the ground floor last, because
the queue's order is the citing
chapters' bibliography order, and a
bibliography lists the guarantee
before the ground. What the filing
changes is not any held claim — no
scroll is revised — but the citation
chain's footing: the three spends now
stand on a filed source, with the
source's own architecture (its ten
chapters, its appendices, its
section-level map) on the record, and
the unheld body named as unheld. The
ground was always load-bearing; now
it is also addressed.
