# 116.2.1 — Synthesis: the third leg of the sentence [synthesis]

[synthesis] Read whole, this is
the filing where one sentence
of the founding chapter stands
filed entire. Chapter 54's
completion proof spends three
works in a single breath — the
Artin-Rees property from
Stafford and Wallach, the
exactness of completion from
Nastasescu and Van Oystaeyen,
and, in the sentence beside
them, finite generation over
U(n) from this paper, jointly
with the lemma Hecht and Schmid
built from it — and the queue
had laid those three works at
three consecutive ranks, 66,
67, 68, without remarking that
they were one argument's legs.
The walk has now filed them in
the queue's order, which is the
reverse of the proof's: the
theorem first, the treatise
second, and the restriction
paper last, though the proof
runs from the restriction
outward. What this paper is, in
the cluster's economy, is the
point where admissibility
becomes algebra: a
representation's finiteness,
which the group states in
K-types, is handed to a
nilpotent algebra whose ring is
Noetherian, and everything the
cluster later proves about
homology, filtrations, and
