# Chapter 117 — Paper 099: Groupes réductifs (Borel & Tits)

Paper 099 is A. Borel and J. Tits,
'Groupes réductifs', Publications
Mathématiques de l'IHÉS, tome 27,
1965, pp. 55-151 (DOI
10.1007/BF02684375; received 1
November 1964) — the founding
account of the relative theory of
reductive groups: the structure of
a connected reductive group read
relative to a field of definition
k, its maximal split tori, its
k-roots, its relative Weyl group,
its parabolic subgroups over k.
The work the queue carried as a
work record (rank 69, key
w:borel|1965, its title field
truncated to its page range ',
55–151' and its authors field
carrying the title and the journal
entire), cited by Chapter 54.
Identified on held records entire:
Chapter 54's bibliography carries
it in full form as [BT65], and its
body spends it once — Proposition
3.11, the product decomposition of
a unipotent group over a partition
of its roots — for the
multiplication assertion. The
pair's own sequel stands apart on
the record: the 'Compléments à
l'article Groupes réductifs' of
1972 is carried as its own entry,
[6], in Paper 084's bibliography,
and Paper 083's frontier had
already kept the two apart in the
house's words — its Borel at rank
69 is the 1965 paper, not
Borel-Tits 1972. THE TEXT IS HELD
ENTIRE, and no partial-text clause
is needed: the journal's official
archive, Numdam, serves the
article, and the filing rests on
its 97 pages entire — the
introduction's own programme,
§§0-14 as its contents print them,
and its bibliography of forty
entries. Every claim in this
chapter rests on that held text.
The archive scan's OCR layer
garbles the paper's notation
passim — its k prints as A, its
root systems as bare ciphers — and
the filing reads the mathematics
through the section structure and
the introduction's summaries,
quoting the scan only where the
reading is certain; the caveat
stands at 117.1.4.
