its year null — is identified from
Chapter 54's bibliography, which
carries the work in full form as
[WY26] with its arXiv URL, and
from arXiv's own record: the
abstract page naming the work,
both authors, and the subject
math.RT, its history listing one
version only, v1, submitted 10
June 2026. The text is held entire
— arXiv's HTML of the paper, its
six sections and its bibliography
of thirty-five entries — and no
partial-text clause is needed: the
introduction's programme
(§§1.1-1.6, stating every main
result), the paper's own date line
(August 24, 2026), and the
acknowledgements all stand in it.
The spends close on the held
bytes: Chapter 54's text carries
the tag [WY26] nine times in all —
its bibliography entry; the
statement that the three
comparisons are also shown in
[WY26]; §2's presentation as an
update of [CWYZ26] and [WY26]; and
the three numbered spends of its
§2's ground — Definition 4.1,
Lemma 4.4, Definition 4.16 and
(4.4) for the complete category
and its globalization;
Propositions 5.2 and 4.18 for the
algebraic comparison and the
Casselman-Jacquet compatibility;
Proposition 4.13 and Theorem 5.3
for the homology comparison — each
standing in the held text at its
numbered place. Its
acknowledgements thank Kaidi Wu
for the collaborations leading to
[CWYZ26] and [WY26]. Thirty-two
checkfile declarations and
twenty-seven hand checks pass on
the held bytes (the harness on
ni-knight; the hand checks beside
it): the held files' hashes and
sizes, the queue record's fields
and its neighbours', the citing
contexts, the author forms on the
raw and flattened bases, the
paper's structure points, and the
filing arithmetic; the proofs
attributed, the check verifying
the record of the claims and not
the mathematics.
# 118.1.4 — Caveats

The extraction's dress governs
first. The held text is arXiv's
HTML read through a crude
tag-strip: the mathematics stands
in a TeX-ish dress, its fraktur
and script letters spelled out as
Unicode, its displays linearised.
The filing reads each statement
through the section structure, the
introduction's own summaries
(§§1.1-1.6, which state every main
result), and the statements'
numbered places, and files the
constructions' content, not their
glyphs; where the introduction and
a section statement differ in
dress, the section statement
governs. A later filing holding
the source TeX stands to re-weigh
the quotations, not the
constructions.

The spectral
assumption governs second. Theorem
1.5's cases are exactly the cases
where the assumption ♠ — every
principal series admits a coarse
spectral filtration — has been
verified, by Wu-Zhang [WZ25] for
GL_n and the orthogonal Levis. The
paper is explicit that the general
case runs through ♠, and ♠ in
general is not proved in it. The
filing therefore records Theorem
1.5 as testimony on Q180 for the
named families, not as the
conjecture's settlement: the
general real reductive case, and
Vogan's Conjecture 10.3 in its
full generality, stand open
(Q453).

The companions govern
third. Two of the paper's
load-bearing references are the
authors' own: [CWYZ26], Paper 082,
already filed; and [WZ25],
Wu-Zhang, which stands in the
walk's would-be pool at rank 703
and supplies both the smooth
complete category and the
verification of ♠; a third,
[WZ26], is forthcoming and does
not yet exist to be filed. This
chapter's concepts name those
debts where they occur; nothing
here rests on a text the book does
not hold or has not placed.

The
citing chain's dress governs last.
Chapter 54 presents its §2 as an
update of this paper's §§4-5 — the
descendant cites the ancestor as a
draft of itself, and both are
papers of 2026. The filing states
the spends that stand in the held
bytes (118.1.3) and files the rest
of the theory as this paper's own
constructions.
# 118.1.5 — Open questions left by Paper 100

Q453. The remainder of Q180
(1.2.1090). The comparison
conjecture now stands proved for
GL_n(ℝ) and GL_n(ℂ) at arbitrary
parabolics and for quasi-split
O_{2n} at the parabolics P_t, in
each case through the spectral
assumption ♠ verified for those
groups by [WZ25]. Does every
principal series of a general real
reductive group admit a coarse
spectral filtration — is ♠ a
theorem waiting for its proof, or
does some group's principal series
refuse one? And beyond the linear
groups: the conjecture for
non-linear real reductive groups,
and Vogan's Conjecture 10.3 in
full generality, stand open. A
proof of ♠ in general, or a
principal series without one,
decides the shape of what
remains.

Q454. The highest
derivative's symmetry (1.2.1092).
Conjecture 3.23: for irreducible
π, the socle and co-socle
filtrations of the highest
derivative π⁻ coincide, their
lengths are symmetric, and π⁻ has
a unique irreducible quotient and
a unique irreducible submodule.
The paper verifies it in three
example classes —
finite-dimensional, monomial, and
a depth-two product. Does it hold
for every irreducible
Casselman-Wallach representation
of GL_n, and is the length of π⁻ a
known invariant (a multiplicity, a
dimension of a Whittaker space) in
disguise?

Q455. Homogeneity for
all irreducibles (1.2.1095).
Conjecture 3.37: every irreducible
π is homogeneous, and π|_{M_n} is
indecomposable. Proved here for
the unitary case (through
Kirillov's conjecture) and the
generic case (through the
Gelfand-Graev representation). The
remaining irreducibles — neither
unitary nor generic — are the
conjecture's open ground: does
some non-unitary, non-generic
irreducible split under the
mirabolic, or does homogeneity
hold entire?

Q456. The nuclearity
boundary (1.2.1098). The spectral
exact sequence fails for the
non-nuclear E^∞ of §3.6, and the
authors report Paper 082's
conjecture that it holds under
nuclear hypotheses. Is nuclearity
of π sufficient for the sequence 0
→ 𝒮(N̂ ∖ {0})·π → π → CJ^∞(π) → 0
— and is it necessary, or is there
a weaker condition (a closedness
of 𝒮(𝒪)·π orbit by orbit) that is
exactly the boundary?

Q457. The
forthcoming computation
(1.2.1097). The transitivity of §6
is proved so that the
Casselman-Jacquet functor can be
computed on standard objects from
maximal parabolic cases, and the
authors announce the computation
for parabolic induced
representations as [WZ26],
forthcoming. When it appears, what
does CJ^∞ take the (co)standard
objects to — and does the citing
chain's own use of the completion
(Chapter 54's §6.2 Bruhat pieces
above all) spend the computation,
or only the functor?
