# 118.2.1 — Synthesis: the descendant that answers [synthesis]

[synthesis] Read whole, this is
the filing where the walk stops
excavating and arrives at the
cluster's present tense. For
nineteen papers the queue's order
ran backwards through the
comparison-theorem country — from
the founding chapter of 2026 down
through its sources to a memoir of
1965 — and the hundredth paper is
the founding chapter's own
sibling: written by the founder's
co-author and his collaborator,
cited by the founding chapter as a
draft of its own §2, and holding
the answer to the question the
founding chapter left open. The
shape is worth naming. Chapter 54
spends this paper's §4 and §5 the
way a paper spends its own lemmas,
because that is what they are: the
complete category, the
globalization, the finite-length
dévissage were built in this work
and borrowed, with
acknowledgement, into the proof
the cluster filed first. The walk
has now filed the borrowing and
the borrowed-from in the wrong
order, which is the literature's
order — a bibliography is a list
of debts in the order they were
incurred this year, not the order
they were built. And the paper's
own gesture is the cluster's
gesture at one remove: the
Casselman-Jacquet functor
completes a representation by an
inverse limit, gathering all its
finite quotients into one object
that remembers them; the
comparison theorem then says the
completion loses nothing the
homology could see. A reference
network walked to its hundredth
paper is the same construction on
the literature — and this paper,
which proves the completion
faithful for the general linear
groups, is a fitting hundredth:
the point where the gathering
starts to answer back.
# 118.2.2 — Cluster placement: AR extended; J weighed and not joined; Q180 testimony filed; no revision

Cluster AR extends to twenty
papers. The placement is weighed
from the work's own construction,
not its citations: the paper is
the comparison-theorem country
built out — the complete
categories in which the
Casselman-Jacquet completion
lives, the category equivalence
that moves the comparison between
the algebraic and smooth sides,
and the proof of Casselman's
comparison conjecture for the
general linear groups. Its senior
author co-wrote the cluster's
founding chapter, and the founding
chapter's proof runs on this
paper's §4 and §5 by number. It
stands where Papers 082, 084 and
090 stand, and for their reason:
the cluster's central theorem and
its machinery, filed at its
source. No other cluster's ground
touches it.

Cluster J is weighed a
forty-seventh time and not joined,
against the standing test (77.2.2,
as narrowed at Chapter 84; Q323's
shape): the paper's mechanisms are
completions and transports
performed on the judged object
itself — a representation is
completed by its own inverse
limit, globalized from its own
quotients, decomposed over its own
orbits — and no mechanism consults
a reference distinct from the
judged object for a verdict spent
inside the computation. The
category equivalence of 1.2.1087
moves the judged object between
its two dresses; it does not judge
it. The comparison itself is a
theorem proved about the objects,
not a verdict consulted by them.
1.2.716 remains J's only
resident.

The
infrastructure-founding
alternative is weighed a
thirty-first time under Chapter
84's restated condition and
declined: the work is a research
paper of its field — its
categories are constructions in a
proof, not an examination or an
apparatus built to be re-run by
strangers as a measure.

Q180
receives testimony and stands
open. Theorem 1.5 (1.2.1090)
proves the comparison conjecture
for GL_n(ℝ) and GL_n(ℂ) at
arbitrary parabolics and for
quasi-split O_{2n} at the
parabolics P_t — the largest
single advance on the question
since the founding chapter — and
the question's own terms (general
real reductive groups; Vogan's
Conjecture 10.3 in full
generality) remain unproved. By
the standing precedent (Papers 088
and 090), testimony is filed and
the question is not closed by it;
Q453 names the remainder.

No
revision: 1.2.495 states the
comparison theorem as Paper 036
proves it, for real linear
reductive groups; this paper's
GL_n proof is a different route to
an overlapping case, and the held
scroll states its own paper's
theorem truly — the new proof
files beside it as 1.2.1090.
1.2.947 and 1.2.1008 state the
minimal-parabolic cases at their
sources (Papers 084 and 090); the
general case's advance does not
touch their statements. 1.2.1012
states Paper 090's
principal-series reduction, the
ancestor of this paper's Step 1,
and stands as its paper's. 1.2.507
states the Casselman-Jacquet
functor as Paper 036 defines it;
this paper's contribution — that
the algebraic completion lands in
ℋ𝒞(𝔤, K_L) and intertwines with
the smooth one under the
equivalence — files as
1.2.1086-1.2.1087, the held scroll
untouched. 1.2.935 states Paper
082's Artin-Rees lemma for
Casselman-Wallach representations;
this paper's exactness of CJ rests
on the classical Artin-Rees of
Paper 098, a different warrant,
and the held scroll stands.
1.2.1047 states the Jacquet module
of an admissible (𝔤, K)-module at
Wallach's book; nothing here
restates it. Each held scroll
states its own paper's
construction truly; this work's
constructions file beside them.
