certification; 1.2.1154 direct
wBH's failure under negative
correlation; 1.2.1155 the exact-
calibrated threshold.

The chain: separation (Assumption
2.1) makes the certificate and the
tests conditionally independent
given the learning field H; the
routing identity (Lemma 4.6) then
prices the routed FDP as the
certificate-weighted mixture of
its branches. The certificate
(3.6) is an intersection-union
test of the checked correlations'
signs at threshold tau (3.5),
sound at beta and complete above
the margin Delta (Theorem 4.12).
Nonnegative checked correlations
give one-sided Gaussian PRDS
(Corollary 4.10), under which wBH
controls FDR (Proposition 4.4);
the fallback branches control at
qcirc/cm under arbitrary
dependence (Proposition 4.2, Lemma
4.1); Theorem 4.7's budget
identity qcirc + (1 - qcirc) beta
= q closes the routed guarantee.
Certification's price is matched:
Corollary 4.15 above, Theorems
4.16 and 4.18 below, at gamma^-2
{log(d + 1) + log(1/beta)}.
Example 4.5 is the standing
warning: uncertified wBH at
correlation -0.3 already violates,
strictly.
# 123.1.3 — The house check: Example 4.5 recomputes to the printed digits; the budget identity and the sample-size shape close

The house check ran at intake, in
the p110 working directory
(hand_check.py, 20 assertions, 20
pass):

EXAMPLE 4.5 AGREES TO THE PRINTED
DIGITS. The paper's direct-
integration value FDR{wBH(W, q)}
approximately 0.1005506 at m = 2,
q = 0.1, W = (0.7, 1.3),
correlation -0.3, both nulls true,
recomputes by bivariate-normal
integration to 0.10055059951... —
agreement at every printed digit,
and strictly above q, as claimed.
The example's routed bound also
closes: 0.01(0.1006) +
0.99(0.0608) = 0.06119... < 0.0612
< q.

THE BUDGET IDENTITY (3.1) closes
exactly at three (q, beta) pairs:
qcirc + (1 - qcirc) beta = q to
machine precision. THE SAMPLE-SIZE
SHAPE (Corollary 4.15) verifies
numerically at four (d, gamma,
beta) settings: at N_C = ceil(4
log(d/beta)/{sqrt(1 + gamma) -
1}^2), the paper's own Delta of
(3.5) satisfies Delta <= gamma,
and the closed-form bound ceil(4(1
+ sqrt(2))^2 gamma^-2 log(d/beta))
dominates the sufficient N_C — the
claimed rate gamma^-2 log(d/beta)
is the bound's own shape. THE ALL-
PAIRS COUNTS close: m = 20, 50,
100 give d = 190, 1225, 4950, the
abstract's 'up to 4,950 checked
pairs'. TABLE 1 is internally
consistent: Cert-wBY mean FDP
0.0480 < q = 0.10 with its
interval's upper endpoint 0.0542 <
q; the power gain over direct wBY
is 0.5445 - 0.3734 = 0.1711 as the
text's framing requires. The HBN
primary setting's ratio
s_J(Gamma)/Delta = 0.5611 lies in
(0, 1), outside Corollary 4.20's
comparison region, exactly as the
paper states at A.4.

Not recomputed, stated plainly:
the Monte Carlo experiments of §5
and Appendix A (the paper's code
release is announced as
forthcoming, not held); the chi-
square mixture arguments of
Theorems 4.16/4.18 were read for
structure and spending, not re-
derived line by line. The
checkable joints close; the
simulation body stands attributed.
# 123.1.4 — Caveats, and what would settle the claim

(1) The shared-Gamma assumption is
an assumption, not a consequence
of splitting: certification and
testing scores must carry the same
population correlation matrix, and
the paper says so itself (§2.2).
Site differences, family
dependence, and unknown marginal
scales can break it; the HBN
analysis estimates variances in
the learning sample and lacks
family identifiers, so the paper
files it as implementation
illustration, not verified FDR
control — the filing follows the
paper's own grading.

(2) The sign condition is
sufficient, not necessary: some
negative correlations are
compatible with wBH control
(diagnostic wBH stayed below q in
all six fixed-budget regimes), so
the certificate's regime is a
certified subset of the valid set,
priced at 1.2.1153. What the full
valid set is stands open (Q481).

(3) The minimax bounds match in
rate, not in constants: the
experiments show acceptance
reaching 1 well below the
sufficient bound (at r = 1/2 of
Corollary 4.15's N_C, acceptance
already 0.9869-0.9996), so the
sufficient constant is
conservative by the paper's own
numbers; the minimax constant is
not estimated.

(4) AI assistance is declared in
the paper (ChatGPT used to audit
arguments and derivations in
revision); the author takes
responsibility for the statements.
As at Paper 104, the filing's
answer is its two-layer shape:
checkable joints verified at
123.1.3, the rest attributed.
# 123.1.5 — Open questions, Paper 105 (Q478–Q481)

Q478 — Certification under
covariance shift: the certificate
assumes certification and testing
scores share one Gamma. What
soundness survives when the
testing Gamma differs from the
certification Gamma by a priced
amount — is there a robust
certificate whose budget identity
absorbs a stated shift, or does
shift break routing at any price?

Q479 — Calibration after variance
estimation: Proposition 2.7's
contrasts need known marginal
variances; studentizing on the
same subjects needs a separate
validity argument the paper does
not give. Does a studentized
certificate keep the soundness
budget, and at what sample-size
price?

Q480 — The exact threshold in use:
tau_ex (1.2.1155) accepts whenever
the explicit certificate accepts,
but the paper leaves their
numerical comparison to future
work. How much acceptance — and
routed power — does exact
calibration actually buy across
the transition grid of §5?

Q481 — The shape of the valid set:
the sign condition is sufficient
for wBH under one-sided Gaussian
tests, not necessary (123.1.4).
What characterizes the Gaussian
laws under which learned weighted
BH controls FDR — and can a
certificate certify a strictly
larger, still checkable regime at
the same minimax price? The
comparisons with compound e-values
and conditional calibration, also
left open by the paper, belong to
this question.

NOVELTY BY AUTHOR — Soyeong Oh:
True +2, False +0, Unknown +20,
Fiction +0.
