# 124.2.1 — Synthesis [synthesis]

Paper 106 files a new kind of
object for this book: not a
paper but a corpus — 180 claimed
theorems with an apparatus
attached. The book's answer to
it is the audit's shape, and the
shape is the filing's real
content: a machine-produced
proof corpus cannot grade
itself, and a Lean kernel's
verdict, however clean, certifies
that a declaration follows — it
does not certify that the
declaration is the conjecture
whose name it carries. The one
serious divergence in 180 items
was exactly there: not a broken
proof but a changed hypothesis,
|E(G)| become |E(Ḡ)|, invisible
to every instrument that reads
only the repo. What catches it
is the oldest move in this
book's discipline — read the
primary source, compare the
statements, recompute what can
be recomputed.

The corpus otherwise earns its
filing. Its scope discipline is
its norm: prize cases claimed as
prize cases, partials labelled
partial, conditional corollaries
fenced off with their premise
named, one external audit
genuine. Against that background
the two stale paragraphs and the
one altered statement stand out
cleanly — which is what an audit
is for. The book files the
collection in cluster BC, its
own country, under the standing
that governs the whole country:
claims stand at the strength of
the comparison, and provisional
Lean is a declaration to
compare, never evidence
(1.2.1160).

The filing also crosses a line
the book set for itself: 1,160
canonical concepts, reached at
this filing's fifth concept.
Pass #55 stands due.
# 124.2.2 — Cluster ruling:
cluster BC founded

FOUNDED: cluster BC — the claim
corpus: combinatorics and number
theory as claimed theorems with
a verification apparatus. The
collection is its first paper.
The book held no combinatorics
or number-theory country; the
subject alone would earn the
founding, and the apparatus
fixes the country's law (the
three-column standing, 1.2.1160).

WEIGHED AND HELD APART. AY (the
examinations country): the
EvalPlus shape is genuinely
present in the apparatus — an
artifact auditing itself, a
catalogue checking its own
claims — but nothing in this
work is measured against a
held-out answer key; the subject
is a corpus of claimed theorems,
not an examination, and AY has
no slot for it. AX (math
structure as such): the work is
a corpus with an apparatus, not
a mathematical structure; its
formal core is a catalogue
grammar, not a geometry. BA: no
— nothing here is communication
against a rank bound; the
apparatus's verdicts are not
communication complexity.

CLUSTER J, WEIGHED A
FIFTY-SECOND TIME, NOT JOINED.
The collection's verification
apparatus is a mechanism that
consults references — Lean
declarations, the kernel — and
the temptation is to file it
beside 1.2.716. The standing
test fails on the locus: the
kernel's verdict is spent inside
the PUBLICATION computation (may
this claim be promoted to the
catalogue?), not inside the
mathematical computation it
certifies; the proof object,
once written, consults nothing.
The falsifiable condition is
named: a filing whose derivation
itself runs the checker as a
stated step of the method would
meet Q323's shape. This work's
method produces proofs the
checker afterwards reads. 1.2.716
remains J's only resident.

THE INFRASTRUCTURE ALTERNATIVE,
DECLINED A THIRTY-SIXTH TIME
under Chapter 84's restated
condition: the candidate is the
collection's verification
apparatus itself — a method for
grading claims, not a substrate
this book's other work runs on.
# 124.3.1 — References and
frontier: the collection's
sources, at audit depth

THE FALLBACK, STATED. The
collection carries no
bibliography in this book's
sense: its references are its
180 entries' primary sources,
and they were censused at audit
depth — each full-depth record
names its primary source (the
arXiv paper, the OEIS entry, the
Wall listing), and each doc-depth
record carries its package's
source documents. No refs leg is
run against a bibliography that
does not exist; the census that
was run is the audit's.

THE NAMESAKE SCAN. The 180
catalogue names were scanned
against the reference queue's
624 records (name keys): five
catalogue items carry a lead
surname that also stands among
the queue's keys — Han (cat.
37), Liu and Zhu (77), Johnson
(118), Wang (127, 133) — among
the commonest surnames in the
literature; every one is a
namesake collision, not the same
work (the queue's records are
the filed chapters' own
bibliographies), and four
further raw hits are title words
("Spectrum"), not names. No
catalogue source is already
filed, in-queue, or pool-standing
under its own identity.

THE POOL. The would-be pool
stands unchanged at 1,098. The
ground: the collection's sources
enter this book through the
audit's records, not through the
frontier instrument, and no
would-be ranks are minted for
them. (Paper 105's un-run census
is not repeated here as a
silence: this leg's fallback and
its ground stand on the record,
as 123.3.1's defect taught.)
